(window.CONTENT_PARTS = window.CONTENT_PARTS || {})["notes-primary"] = {"abg-interpretation":"<h2 id=\"a-systematic-approach\">A systematic approach</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Patient and context:</strong> FiO<sub>2</sub>, ventilation, clinical picture. Is the sample arterial?</li><li><strong>Oxygenation:</strong> PaO<sub>2</sub> relative to FiO<sub>2</sub> (expected PaO<sub>2</sub> in kPa ≈ FiO<sub>2</sub> (%) − 10 in healthy lungs; <strong>P/F ratio</strong> in kPa: &lt; 40 mild, &lt; 26.7 moderate, &lt; 13.3 severe ARDS-range impairment); calculate the <strong>A–a gradient</strong> if needed.</li><li><strong>pH:</strong> acidaemia (&lt; 7.35) or alkalaemia (&gt; 7.45)?</li><li><strong>Primary process:</strong> PaCO<sub>2</sub> (respiratory) versus HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> / base excess (metabolic), in the direction that explains the pH.</li><li><strong>Compensation:</strong> appropriate? (see acid–base note; compensation never overcorrects).</li><li><strong>Anion gap</strong> (corrected for albumin) in metabolic acidosis; <strong>delta ratio</strong> (Δ anion gap / Δ HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>) to look for a mixed picture.</li><li><strong>Other values:</strong> lactate, glucose, Na<sup>+</sup>, K<sup>+</sup>, Cl<sup>−</sup>, ionised Ca<sup>2+</sup>, Hb, COHb, MetHb.</li><li><strong>Synthesis:</strong> a diagnosis, with causes and actions.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"worked-patterns\">Worked patterns</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Gas</th><th>Interpretation</th><th>Typical causes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>pH 7.21, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 8.9 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 26, BE +1</td><td><strong>Acute respiratory acidosis</strong> (no metabolic compensation yet)</td><td>Opioid overdose, residual neuromuscular block, exhaustion</td></tr><tr><td>pH 7.36, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 8.0 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 33, BE +8</td><td><strong>Chronic (compensated) respiratory acidosis</strong></td><td>COPD with CO<sub>2</sub> retention</td></tr><tr><td>pH 7.12, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 3.2 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 8, BE −19, lactate 9</td><td><strong>Raised anion gap metabolic acidosis</strong> with respiratory compensation</td><td>Septic or haemorrhagic shock, ischaemic bowel</td></tr><tr><td>pH 7.30, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 4.3 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 16, Cl<sup>−</sup> 116, lactate 1.1</td><td><strong>Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis</strong></td><td>Large volumes of 0.9% saline; diarrhoea</td></tr><tr><td>pH 7.52, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 6.2 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 37, K<sup>+</sup> 2.9</td><td><strong>Metabolic alkalosis</strong> with respiratory compensation</td><td>Vomiting / nasogastric losses, diuretics</td></tr><tr><td>pH 7.51, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 3.4 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 20</td><td><strong>Acute respiratory alkalosis</strong></td><td>Pain, anxiety, over-ventilation, early PE, pregnancy (chronic)</td></tr><tr><td>pH 7.25, PaCO<sub>2</sub> 6.8 kPa, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 22</td><td><strong>Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis</strong> (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> lower than expected for acute respiratory acidosis)</td><td>Cardiac arrest, severe pneumonia with shock</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pitfalls\">Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Venous samples:</strong> pH about 0.03–0.04 lower and PCO<sub>2</sub> about 0.8 kPa higher than arterial; PO<sub>2</sub> is not interpretable.</li><li><strong>Air bubbles</strong> raise PO<sub>2</sub> towards ~20 kPa and lower PCO<sub>2</sub>; <strong>delay</strong> (cells keep metabolising) lowers PO<sub>2</sub> and raises PCO<sub>2</sub>; <strong>excess heparin</strong> dilutes and lowers PCO<sub>2</sub>.</li><li><strong>Temperature:</strong> analysers measure at 37°C (alpha-stat).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> interpret gases quickly and name a likely cause; calculate the anion gap and P/F ratio.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> you are handed a gas from a deteriorating patient in recovery: talk through it aloud, then say what you will do.</li></ul></div>","acid-base":"<h2 id=\"definitions\">Definitions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>pH = −log<sub>10</sub>[H<sup>+</sup>].</strong> Normal arterial pH 7.35–7.45, i.e. [H<sup>+</sup>] <strong>35–45 nmol/L</strong> (pH 7.4 = 40 nmol/L). A strong acid dissociates completely; a weak acid partially.</li><li><strong>Buffers</strong> resist changes in pH and work best within about one pH unit of their <strong>pKa</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-bicarbonate-buffer\">The bicarbonate buffer</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Henderson–Hasselbalch:</strong> <strong>pH = 6.1 + log<sub>10</sub>([HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>] / (0.23 × PCO<sub>2</sub> in kPa))</strong> (0.03 × PCO<sub>2</sub> if in mmHg).</li><li>Although its pKa (6.1) is far from 7.4, it is the most important extracellular buffer because it is an <strong>open system</strong>: CO<sub>2</sub> is regulated by the lungs and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> by the kidneys.</li><li>Other buffers: <strong>haemoglobin</strong> (histidine imidazole groups; the most important non-bicarbonate buffer in blood), plasma proteins, phosphate (important intracellularly and in urine), bone.</li><li><strong>Isohydric principle:</strong> all the buffer pairs in a solution share the same [H<sup>+</sup>], so they are in equilibrium with one another; measuring one pair (bicarbonate) describes them all.</li><li><strong>Renal handling:</strong> the kidney reclaims filtered bicarbonate (proximal tubule, carbonic anhydrase) and excretes H<sup>+</sup> bound to <strong>phosphate</strong> (titratable acid) and <strong>ammonia</strong> (as NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>), generating new bicarbonate. The liver also contributes, through lactate metabolism and the balance between ureagenesis and glutamine production.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"derived-variables\">Derived variables</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Standard bicarbonate:</strong> HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> after equilibration at PCO<sub>2</sub> 5.3 kPa and 37°C: removes the respiratory component.</li><li><strong>Base excess:</strong> the amount of strong acid (mmol/L) needed to return blood to pH 7.4 at PCO<sub>2</sub> 5.3 kPa and 37°C. Normal −2 to +2; negative values indicate metabolic acidosis. <strong>Standard base excess</strong> is calculated for extracellular fluid (Hb about 50 g/L) and is more representative of the whole body.</li><li><strong>Anion gap = (Na<sup>+</sup> + K<sup>+</sup>) − (Cl<sup>−</sup> + HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>)</strong>, normally about 12–16 mmol/L with K<sup>+</sup> (8–12 without). Unmeasured anions are mostly <strong>albumin</strong>: correct for low albumin (add about 2.5 mmol/L per 10 g/L fall).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"four-primary-disorders-and-their-compensation\">Four primary disorders and their compensation</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Disorder</th><th>Primary change</th><th>Compensation</th><th>Expected compensation (rules of thumb)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Metabolic acidosis</td><td>↓ HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup></td><td>Hyperventilation ↓ PCO<sub>2</sub></td><td>PaCO<sub>2</sub> (mmHg) ≈ 1.5 × HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> + 8 (± 2) (Winter's formula)</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolic alkalosis</td><td>↑ HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup></td><td>Hypoventilation ↑ PCO<sub>2</sub> (limited by hypoxia)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory acidosis</td><td>↑ PCO<sub>2</sub></td><td>Renal HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> retention (days)</td><td>Acute: HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> ↑ ~1 per 10 mmHg; chronic: ~3.5–4 per 10 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory alkalosis</td><td>↓ PCO<sub>2</sub></td><td>Renal HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> excretion</td><td>Acute: ↓ ~2 per 10 mmHg; chronic: ↓ ~4–5 per 10 mmHg</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Compensation <strong>does not overcorrect</strong>; if the pH is on the \"other side\", suspect a mixed disorder.</p>\n<h2 id=\"metabolic-acidosis-the-anion-gap\">Metabolic acidosis: the anion gap</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Raised gap:</strong> lactate, ketones (diabetic, alcoholic, starvation), renal failure (sulphate, phosphate), toxins (methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate).</li><li><strong>Normal gap (hyperchloraemic):</strong> large volumes of 0.9% saline, GI bicarbonate loss (diarrhoea, fistulae, ileal conduit), renal tubular acidosis, acetazolamide.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-stewart-physicochemical-approach-in-brief\">The Stewart (physicochemical) approach in brief</h2>\n<p>pH is determined by three independent variables: <strong>PCO<sub>2</sub></strong>, the <strong>strong ion difference</strong> (SID ≈ Na<sup>+</sup> + K<sup>+</sup> − Cl<sup>−</sup> − lactate; normal about 40 mEq/L) and <strong>total weak acids</strong> (mainly albumin and phosphate). Bicarbonate is a dependent variable. It explains why 0.9% saline (SID 0) causes acidosis and why hypoalbuminaemia causes alkalosis.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Henderson–Hasselbalch calculations; base excess; anion gap with albumin correction; expected compensation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpret a blood gas from a septic patient step by step, and explain what you would do about it.</li></ul></div>","acute-pain-management":"<h2 id=\"assessment\">Assessment</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Numerical rating scale</strong> (0–10), verbal rating scales; <strong>functional assessment</strong> (can the patient cough, breathe deeply and mobilise?) is often more useful than the score alone.</li><li><strong>Special groups:</strong> children (FLACC, faces scales), cognitive impairment and dementia (PAINAD, Abbey pain scale), communication difficulties and learning disability (involve carers), the unconscious (physiological and behavioural signs).</li><li>The <strong>biopsychosocial model</strong>: anxiety, catastrophising, expectations, culture and previous experience affect pain.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"multimodal-analgesia\">Multimodal analgesia</h2>\n<ul><li>Combine drugs and techniques with different mechanisms to improve analgesia and reduce opioid side effects: <strong>paracetamol</strong>, <strong>NSAIDs</strong> (if not contraindicated), <strong>opioids</strong> (oral where possible; PCA when needed), <strong>local and regional anaesthesia</strong> (infiltration, peripheral blocks, neuraxial), and adjuncts (dexamethasone, ketamine, α<sub>2</sub>-agonists, gabapentinoids in selected patients, IV lidocaine in some settings).</li><li><strong>Rescue analgesia:</strong> a protocol for severe pain (titrated IV opioid with monitoring, or a regional technique), after checking for new causes of pain (compartment syndrome, surgical complications).</li><li><strong>Epidural and PCA management:</strong> monitoring, side effects (sedation, respiratory depression, hypotension, pruritus, nausea, motor block), and when to call the anaesthetist.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-acute-pain-service\">The acute pain service</h2>\n<p>A multidisciplinary team (nurses, anaesthetists, pharmacists) that reviews patients with complex analgesia, supports wards with protocols and education, audits outcomes, and plans transition to oral analgesia and discharge (including safe opioid prescribing and deprescribing).</p>\n<h2 id=\"chronic-pain-and-opioid-tolerance\">Chronic pain and opioid tolerance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Chronic pain</strong> (pain for more than 3 months) is common. Many patients take long-term opioids, gabapentinoids or antidepressants.</li><li><strong>Opioid-tolerant patients:</strong> continue usual opioids (or equivalent) perioperatively to prevent withdrawal, anticipate higher requirements, maximise non-opioid and regional techniques, consider ketamine; <strong>buprenorphine</strong> and <strong>methadone</strong> need specific plans (usually continued); involve the pain team; plan discharge analgesia and follow-up.</li><li><strong>Limitations of pain medicine:</strong> opioids have poor long-term effectiveness for chronic non-cancer pain and carry risks of dependence; acute pain management aims to restore function, not to abolish all pain.</li><li>Poorly controlled acute pain is a risk factor for <strong>chronic post-surgical pain</strong>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> multimodal principles; PCA side effects; pain assessment tools for special groups.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining an analgesia plan to a patient who takes 60 mg oral morphine a day and is having a laparotomy.</li></ul></div>","ageing-obesity":"<h2 id=\"ageing\">Ageing</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Change</th><th>Implication</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cardiovascular</td><td>Stiffer arteries (↑ systolic BP, wide pulse pressure), diastolic dysfunction, reduced β-receptor responsiveness, conduction disease</td><td>Exaggerated hypotension at induction; poor tolerance of tachycardia and fluid overload</td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory</td><td>↑ closing capacity, ↓ compliance of chest wall, ↓ PaO<sub>2</sub> (a rule of thumb: about 100 − age/3 mmHg breathing air), blunted responses to hypoxia and hypercapnia</td><td>Hypoxaemia; sensitivity to respiratory depressants</td></tr><tr><td>Renal</td><td>↓ GFR (about 1 mL/min per year after 40), ↓ concentrating ability</td><td>Reduced drug clearance; creatinine underestimates impairment (low muscle mass)</td></tr><tr><td>CNS</td><td>↓ neurones and neurotransmitters, <strong>↓ MAC (about 6% per decade after 40)</strong></td><td>Postoperative delirium and cognitive dysfunction</td></tr><tr><td>Body composition</td><td>↑ fat, ↓ muscle and total body water</td><td>Larger Vd for lipid-soluble drugs, smaller for water-soluble ones</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>Thinner skin, osteoporosis, impaired thermoregulation, polypharmacy</td><td>Positioning injury, hypothermia, drug interactions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Frailty</strong> (reduced physiological reserve, often measured with the Clinical Frailty Scale) predicts complications and mortality better than age alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"obesity\">Obesity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>BMI = weight (kg) / height (m)<sup>2</sup>.</strong> Obese ≥ 30; class III ≥ 40. <strong>Central (android, visceral) fat</strong> carries more cardiovascular and metabolic risk than peripheral (gynaecoid) fat, so waist circumference predicts morbidity better than BMI.</li><li><strong>Energy balance:</strong> <strong>leptin</strong> from adipose tissue acts on the hypothalamus to reduce appetite and raise energy expenditure; obesity is usually a leptin-resistant state.</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> ↓ FRC and ERV (especially supine), atelectasis, ↑ oxygen consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> production, ↑ work of breathing, <strong>obstructive sleep apnoea</strong> (screen with STOP-BANG) and obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Rapid desaturation; potential difficult mask ventilation.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> ↑ blood volume and cardiac output, hypertension, LV hypertrophy, pulmonary hypertension (OSA), arrhythmias.</li><li><strong>Other:</strong> diabetes, reflux, fatty liver, VTE risk, difficult venous access, pressure injuries.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"size-scalars-for-drug-dosing\">Size scalars for drug dosing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Ideal body weight (IBW)</strong> (Devine): men 50 kg + 0.9 kg/cm above 152 cm; women 45.5 kg + 0.9 kg/cm above 152 cm. A simple alternative: height (cm) − 100 (men) or − 105 (women).</li><li><strong>Lean body weight (LBW)</strong>: fat-free mass; formulae (e.g. Janmahasatian) plateau at high BMI.</li><li><strong>Adjusted body weight</strong> = IBW + 0.4 × (actual − IBW).</li><li>General principles: <strong>induction agents and opioids</strong> dosed on <strong>LBW</strong> (then titrated); <strong>suxamethonium</strong> on <strong>total body weight</strong> (increased plasma cholinesterase and ECF); <strong>non-depolarising NMBs</strong> on <strong>IBW</strong> (or LBW); <strong>sugammadex</strong> on actual or adjusted weight per product guidance; LMWH per local obesity protocols. TCI models must be used within their validated ranges.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> changes in closing capacity and MAC with age; weight scalars and which drugs use which.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning induction for a patient with a BMI of 48 and OSA: position, pre-oxygenation, drug doses and extubation.</li></ul></div>","airway-assessment":"<h2 id=\"four-difficulties-to-predict\">Four difficulties to predict</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Difficult facemask ventilation:</strong> beard, obesity, age &gt; 55, edentulous, OSA/snoring, neck radiotherapy, Mallampati IV, limited jaw protrusion.</li><li><strong>Difficult supraglottic airway:</strong> reduced mouth opening, upper airway pathology, obesity, fixed cervical spine flexion.</li><li><strong>Difficult laryngoscopy and intubation:</strong> previous difficulty (the best predictor), <strong>Mallampati III–IV</strong>, <strong>reduced mouth opening</strong> (&lt; 3 cm), <strong>short thyromental distance</strong> (&lt; 6 cm), reduced <strong>jaw protrusion</strong> (upper lip bite test), <strong>limited neck movement</strong>, prominent incisors, large tongue, obesity, pregnancy, airway pathology (tumour, abscess, trauma, burns).</li><li><strong>Difficult front-of-neck access:</strong> obesity, short or fixed neck, previous radiotherapy or surgery, goitre, tumour — <strong>identify the cricothyroid membrane</strong> (palpation or ultrasound) before induction when difficulty is expected.</li></ol>\n<p>No single test is reliable; combinations are better, and even then many difficult airways are unanticipated. <strong>Always have a plan and backups.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"grading-laryngoscopy\">Grading laryngoscopy</h2>\n<p><strong>Cormack–Lehane:</strong> grade 1 (most of the glottis visible), 2 (posterior glottis / arytenoids only), 3 (epiglottis only), 4 (no laryngeal structures). Record the grade, the device used, and any adjuncts (bougie, video laryngoscope) in the anaesthetic chart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"airway-devices\">Airway devices</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oropharyngeal airways</strong> (size: incisors to the angle of the mandible) and <strong>nasopharyngeal airways</strong> (avoid with base-of-skull fracture and coagulopathy).</li><li><strong>Supraglottic airways:</strong> first-generation (classic LMA) and <strong>second-generation</strong> devices with a drain tube and higher seal pressure (i-gel, ProSeal, LMA Supreme), preferred for rescue and for many routine cases. Contraindications: high aspiration risk, need for high ventilation pressures, some surgical positions and procedures.</li><li><strong>Tracheal tubes:</strong> cuffed tubes, sizes (typical adult 7.0–8.0 mm ID), reinforced, RAE preformed tubes, microlaryngeal tubes, double-lumen tubes.</li><li><strong>Laryngoscopes:</strong> Macintosh (curved), McCoy (levering tip), Miller (straight: infants), and <strong>video laryngoscopes</strong> (standard-geometry or hyperangulated blades; hyperangulated blades need a stylet). DAS 2025 promotes video laryngoscopy as a first-line tool for competent users.</li><li><strong>Bougie and stylet</strong>; <strong>flexible bronchoscope</strong> for awake or asleep fibreoptic intubation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"making-a-plan\">Making a plan</h2>\n<ul><li>Plan A (the primary technique), B (rescue oxygenation with a second-generation SGA), C (facemask ventilation, then wake up or proceed), D (front-of-neck access) — and state them in the team brief.</li><li>Consider <strong>awake tracheal intubation</strong> when difficulty is predicted and awake techniques are feasible.</li><li>Optimise <strong>pre-oxygenation</strong> (head-up, tight seal, end-tidal O<sub>2</sub> ≥ 0.9; HFNO for apnoeic oxygenation), positioning (\"sniffing\" or ramped in the obese), and the right team and equipment.</li><li><strong>Confirm tracheal placement with sustained waveform capnography</strong> every time (\"no trace = wrong place\").</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> predictors of each type of difficulty; Cormack–Lehane grades; device choices.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> assess a patient's airway at preassessment, explain your findings and plan to a supervisor, and tell the patient what to expect.</li></ul></div>","anaesthesia-mechanisms":"<h2 id=\"historical-lipid-theories\">Historical: lipid theories</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Meyer–Overton (c. 1900):</strong> potency correlates with lipid (olive oil) solubility across agents of very different structure, suggesting a common hydrophobic site.</li><li><strong>Problems:</strong> stereoisomers with the same lipid solubility have different potencies; some lipid-soluble compounds are not anaesthetic (non-immobilisers); the <strong>cut-off effect</strong> (potency stops increasing beyond a certain chain length); and pressure reversal is only partial.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"current-view-protein-targets\">Current view: protein targets</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Effect</th><th>Agents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor</strong> (ligand-gated Cl<sup>−</sup> channel)</td><td>Positive allosteric modulation → increased Cl<sup>−</sup> entry, hyperpolarisation, inhibition</td><td><strong>Propofol, etomidate, thiopental</strong>, benzodiazepines (hypnotic component), volatiles</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NMDA receptor</strong> (glutamate)</td><td>Antagonism</td><td><strong>Ketamine, N<sub>2</sub>O, xenon</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Two-pore domain K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> (e.g. TREK-1)</td><td>Activation → hyperpolarisation</td><td>Volatiles, N<sub>2</sub>O, xenon</td></tr><tr><td>Glycine receptors (spinal cord)</td><td>Potentiation</td><td>Volatiles (immobility)</td></tr><tr><td>HCN channels, nicotinic receptors</td><td>Various</td><td>Volatiles</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"where-in-the-nervous-system\">Where in the nervous system</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Unconsciousness and amnesia:</strong> cortex, thalamus (thalamocortical disconnection), hippocampus (amnesia at low concentrations), arousal systems in the brainstem and hypothalamus (propofol acts via GABA<sub>A</sub> in the tuberomammillary nucleus).</li><li><strong>Immobility</strong> (the endpoint of MAC) is largely <strong>spinal</strong>: MAC is unchanged in animals when the brain is removed.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"components-of-anaesthesia\">Components of anaesthesia</h2>\n<p>Hypnosis, amnesia, analgesia (antinociception), immobility and autonomic stability. Different drugs contribute differently, which is why balanced anaesthesia combines them. <strong>Accidental awareness</strong> (NAP5: about 1 in 19,000 general anaesthetics reported, higher with neuromuscular block, TIVA errors, caesarean section and cardiac surgery) occurs when hypnosis is inadequate while the patient is paralysed.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Meyer–Overton and its failings; the main receptor targets of each agent; the spinal site of immobility.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining to a worried patient how anaesthesia works and how you prevent awareness.</li></ul></div>","anaesthetic-machine":"<h2 id=\"the-high-intermediate-and-low-pressure-systems\">The high-, intermediate- and low-pressure systems</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>High pressure:</strong> cylinders → yoke → Bourdon gauge → pressure regulator (down to ~4 bar).</li><li><strong>Intermediate pressure:</strong> pipelines (4 bar) and regulated cylinder gas → non-return valves → <strong>oxygen failure warning device</strong> → flow control valves. The <strong>oxygen flush</strong> (35–75 L/min) draws from here, bypassing flowmeters and vaporisers.</li><li><strong>Low pressure:</strong> flowmeters → back bar (vaporisers) → <strong>pressure relief valve</strong> (about 30–40 kPa, protecting the machine) → <strong>common gas outlet</strong>.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"flowmeters\">Flowmeters</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Variable-orifice (Thorpe tube) rotameters:</strong> a bobbin rises in a tapered glass tube; read at the <strong>top of a bobbin</strong> or the <strong>middle of a ball</strong>. Low flows are laminar (viscosity-dependent), high flows turbulent (density-dependent), so each tube is calibrated for one gas. Errors: static electricity, dirt, tubes not vertical, bobbin sticking.</li><li><strong>Oxygen is added last</strong> (downstream) on the back bar, so a crack in an upstream tube leaks other gases rather than oxygen.</li><li>Many modern machines have <strong>electronic flow control</strong> and virtual flowmeters, with a backup flowmeter.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"safety-features\">Safety features</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygen failure alarm:</strong> audible (at least 7 s), powered by the oxygen supply itself, triggered when oxygen pressure falls below about 2 bar, and shuts off other gases (so air or N<sub>2</sub>O cannot flow without oxygen).</li><li><strong>Hypoxic guard / anti-hypoxic device:</strong> mechanically or electronically links N<sub>2</sub>O and O<sub>2</sub> flows so the mixture cannot fall below <strong>25% oxygen</strong>.</li><li><strong>Minimum oxygen flow</strong> (basal flow) when the machine is on.</li><li>Colour-coded, touch-coded oxygen control knob (larger, fluted, on the left in the UK).</li><li>Vaporiser interlock; keyed fillers; pin index and NIST; non-return valves.</li><li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> inspired oxygen with alarm, airway pressure (disconnection and high pressure), capnography, vapour analysis.</li><li><strong>Scavenging</strong>, <strong>APL valve</strong>, and a <strong>self-inflating bag and oxygen cylinder</strong> as backup.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-machine-check\">The machine check</h2>\n<p>Follow the Association of Anaesthetists' current guidance for checking anaesthetic equipment: a full check at the start of each session (power, gas supplies and pipelines with <strong>tug test</strong>, oxygen analyser, flowmeters, vaporisers, breathing system leak and function, ventilator, scavenging, suction, monitors, airway equipment and alternative means of ventilation), and a shorter check between cases. Record it in a logbook. Take particular care with the <strong>two-bag test</strong> and after changing breathing systems or vaporisers.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> order of flowmeters; hypoxic guard; oxygen failure device; pressure relief valve; flush.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the oxygen analyser shows 16% inspired oxygen though you set 50%: step through the causes and actions.</li></ul></div>","anaphylaxis-management":"<h2 id=\"recognition\">Recognition</h2>\n<p>Sudden <strong>hypotension</strong> (the commonest presenting feature), tachycardia or bradycardia, <strong>bronchospasm</strong> (high airway pressure, falling or sloping ETCO<sub>2</sub>), desaturation, angioedema, urticaria or flushing (often absent in severe cases), and cardiac arrest. Consider it whenever there is unexplained collapse after drug administration (antibiotics, neuromuscular blockers, chlorhexidine, patent blue dye, colloids, latex).</p>\n<h2 id=\"immediate-management-follow-the-qrh-and-rcuk-guidance\">Immediate management (follow the QRH and RCUK guidance)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Call for help</strong>; declare anaphylaxis; note the time.</li><li><strong>Remove likely triggers</strong>: stop drug or colloid infusions, remove chlorhexidine-containing items; maintain anaesthesia with a volatile if an IV agent may be the trigger (or vice versa).</li><li><strong>Airway and oxygen:</strong> 100% O<sub>2</sub>; secure the airway if needed.</li><li><strong>Adrenaline:</strong> IV titrated boluses in the monitored anaesthetised adult (for example 50 µg, repeated as needed), or IM (0.5 mg in adults) if no IV access. Start an <strong>adrenaline infusion</strong> if repeated boluses are needed. In children, use weight-based doses.</li><li><strong>Fluids:</strong> rapid crystalloid boluses (large volumes may be needed).</li><li><strong>Cardiac arrest:</strong> start CPR and follow ALS; adrenaline per the ALS algorithm.</li><li><strong>Refractory hypotension:</strong> add other vasopressors (noradrenaline, <strong>vasopressin</strong>, metaraminol), glucagon for patients on β-blockers, consider ECMO where available.</li><li><strong>Bronchospasm:</strong> salbutamol, volatile, magnesium.</li><li><strong>Second-line drugs</strong> (antihistamines, steroids) are not life-saving and should not delay adrenaline.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"after-the-event\">After the event</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Tryptase</strong> samples (as soon as possible after resuscitation, at 1–2 hours, and at 24 hours or later).</li><li>Admit to a critical care area as needed; biphasic reactions can occur.</li><li><strong>Refer to a specialist allergy clinic</strong> (the anaesthetist is responsible for the referral), <strong>inform the patient</strong> (in writing), their GP and surgeon, document clearly in the notes, and <strong>report to the MHRA</strong> (Yellow Card) and locally.</li><li>Future anaesthetics: avoid all possible triggers until investigated.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> adrenaline dosing routes; NAP6 triggers; tryptase timing.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> run an anaphylaxis simulation, then explain to the patient afterwards what happened and what they must do next.</li></ul></div>","anat-airway":"<h2 id=\"nose-and-pharynx\">Nose and pharynx</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Nasal cavity:</strong> septum (Little's area anteriorly bleeds easily), three turbinates; the floor runs horizontally backwards — pass nasal tubes <strong>along the floor</strong>, not upwards.</li><li><strong>Pharynx:</strong> nasopharynx (adenoids), oropharynx, laryngopharynx (hypopharynx, including the piriform fossae), from the skull base to <strong>C6</strong> where it becomes the oesophagus at the cricopharyngeus.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-larynx-c3-c6-in-adults\">The larynx (C3–C6 in adults)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Cartilages:</strong> unpaired <strong>thyroid</strong>, <strong>cricoid</strong> (the only complete ring; at <strong>C6</strong>), epiglottis; paired <strong>arytenoids</strong>, corniculate, cuneiform.</li><li><strong>Membranes:</strong> thyrohyoid membrane (pierced by the internal laryngeal nerve), <strong>cricothyroid membrane</strong> (between thyroid and cricoid; about 10 mm high in adults; the site for emergency front-of-neck access), cricotracheal ligament.</li><li><strong>Vocal cords</strong> attach from the arytenoids to the back of the thyroid cartilage. The glottis is the narrowest part of the adult airway.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"muscles\">Muscles</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Action</th><th>Nerve</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Posterior cricoarytenoid</strong></td><td>The <strong>only abductor</strong> (opens the cords)</td><td>Recurrent laryngeal</td></tr><tr><td>Lateral cricoarytenoid, transverse and oblique arytenoids</td><td>Adduction</td><td>Recurrent laryngeal</td></tr><tr><td>Thyroarytenoid (vocalis)</td><td>Relaxes/shortens cords</td><td>Recurrent laryngeal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cricothyroid</strong></td><td><strong>Tenses</strong> the cords (pitch)</td><td><strong>External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"innervation\">Innervation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Sensory:</strong> above the cords — <strong>internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve</strong> (vagus). Below the cords — <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong>. Posterior tongue and oropharynx — <strong>glossopharyngeal nerve</strong>. Nose — trigeminal branches.</li><li><strong>Motor:</strong> all intrinsic muscles by the <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> except cricothyroid.</li><li>The <strong>left recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> loops under the aortic arch (at risk in thoracic surgery and with mediastinal masses); the right loops under the subclavian artery. Both are at risk in thyroidectomy.</li><li><strong>Nerve injuries:</strong> unilateral RLN palsy → hoarse voice (cord in paramedian position); <strong>bilateral RLN palsy → stridor and airway obstruction</strong> (cords adducted); SLN injury → weak, low-pitched voice.</li><li><strong>Laryngospasm:</strong> reflex adduction mediated by the superior laryngeal nerve (sensory) and recurrent laryngeal nerve (motor).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"landmarks\">Landmarks</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Cricoid pressure:</strong> on the cricoid cartilage (C6), about 10 N when awake and 30 N after loss of consciousness, compressing the oesophagus against the vertebral body (evidence for its effectiveness is debated; release if it impedes laryngoscopy or ventilation).</li><li><strong>Front-of-neck access:</strong> identify the cricothyroid membrane by palpation (laryngeal handshake) or ultrasound <strong>before</strong> induction in predicted difficult airways. DAS recommends a <strong>scalpel–bougie–tube</strong> technique for emergency surgical cricothyroidotomy.</li><li><strong>Airway blocks</strong> for awake intubation: glossopharyngeal (posterior tongue), superior laryngeal (at the greater horn of the hyoid), and transtracheal injection through the cricothyroid membrane; or topical local anaesthesia (spray-as-you-go).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> nerve supply by region; the only abductor; the cricoid's level and shape; nerve injury patterns.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning topical anaesthesia for an awake fibreoptic intubation, naming the nerves blocked.</li></ul></div>","anat-head-neck-eye":"<h2 id=\"brain-and-blood-supply\">Brain and blood supply</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Subdivisions:</strong> cerebral hemispheres (frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital), diencephalon (thalamus, hypothalamus), brainstem (midbrain, pons, medulla), cerebellum.</li><li><strong>Circle of Willis:</strong> internal carotid arteries and the basilar artery (from the two vertebral arteries) joined by the anterior and posterior communicating arteries; gives off the anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries. An anastomosis that may compensate for occlusion, though often incomplete. Berry aneurysms occur at its junctions (subarachnoid haemorrhage).</li><li><strong>Venous drainage:</strong> dural venous sinuses to the internal jugular veins; head-down position and jugular compression raise ICP.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cranial-nerves-anaesthetic-relevance\">Cranial nerves (anaesthetic relevance)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Function</th><th>Anaesthetic relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I Olfactory</td><td>Smell</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>II Optic</td><td>Vision</td><td>Postoperative visual loss (prone spine surgery, ischaemic optic neuropathy)</td></tr><tr><td>III Oculomotor</td><td>Most eye muscles, levator palpebrae, pupil constriction (parasympathetic)</td><td><strong>Fixed dilated pupil</strong> with uncal herniation</td></tr><tr><td>IV Trochlear</td><td>Superior oblique</td><td>Eye blocks</td></tr><tr><td>V Trigeminal</td><td>Facial sensation (V1, V2, V3); muscles of mastication</td><td>Oculocardiac and nasal reflexes (afferent); trigeminal neuralgia procedures</td></tr><tr><td>VI Abducens</td><td>Lateral rectus</td><td>Long intracranial course: palsy with raised or low ICP (e.g. post-dural puncture headache)</td></tr><tr><td>VII Facial</td><td>Facial expression; taste (anterior two-thirds)</td><td>Facial nerve monitoring in parotid surgery (avoid NMB); nerve stimulator site</td></tr><tr><td>VIII Vestibulocochlear</td><td>Hearing, balance</td><td>PONV; N<sub>2</sub>O and middle ear</td></tr><tr><td>IX Glossopharyngeal</td><td>Posterior tongue and pharynx sensation; carotid sinus</td><td>Gag reflex (afferent); airway block</td></tr><tr><td>X Vagus</td><td>Larynx (SLN, RLN), parasympathetic to heart and gut</td><td>Laryngospasm; bradycardia; aortic arch baroreceptors</td></tr><tr><td>XI Accessory</td><td>Sternocleidomastoid, trapezius</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>XII Hypoglossal</td><td>Tongue muscles</td><td>Tongue deviation; injury with LMA or laryngoscopy (rare)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-orbit-and-eye\">The orbit and eye</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Extraocular muscles</strong> arise from the annulus of Zinn (the common tendinous ring) and form a <strong>cone</strong>: intraconal (retrobulbar) versus extraconal (peribulbar) blocks. <strong>Sub-Tenon's block</strong> injects under Tenon's capsule (fascial sheath of the eyeball) with a blunt cannula; the safest common technique.</li><li>Globe axial length &gt; 26 mm (high myopia) increases the risk of globe perforation with needle blocks.</li><li><strong>Oculocardiac reflex:</strong> traction on extraocular muscles (especially medial rectus in strabismus surgery) or pressure on the globe → <strong>bradycardia</strong>, arrhythmias, even asystole. Afferent: <strong>trigeminal (V1, ciliary ganglion)</strong>; efferent: <strong>vagus</strong>. Stop the stimulus; atropine or glycopyrronium.</li><li><strong>Intraocular pressure</strong> 10–20 mmHg; see drugs and the eye.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"autonomic-ganglia-of-the-head-and-neck\">Autonomic ganglia of the head and neck</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Sympathetic:</strong> superior, middle and inferior cervical ganglia (the inferior fuses with T1 as the <strong>stellate ganglion</strong>, at C7 anterior to the neck of the first rib and transverse process).</li><li><strong>Parasympathetic:</strong> ciliary (III), pterygopalatine (VII), submandibular (VII), otic (IX) ganglia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"endocrine-anatomy-brief\">Endocrine anatomy (brief)</h2>\n<p>The <strong>pituitary</strong> sits in the sella turcica below the optic chiasm; the <strong>thyroid</strong> lies at C5–T1 anterior to the trachea with the recurrent laryngeal nerves in the tracheo-oesophageal grooves and the parathyroids posteriorly; the <strong>adrenals</strong> sit on the upper poles of the kidneys (medulla surrounded by cortex).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> cranial nerve functions; circle of Willis; orbit and cone; oculocardiac reflex arc.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> bradycardia to 35/min during squint surgery in a child: explain and manage.</li></ul></div>","anat-heart-vessels":"<h2 id=\"the-heart\">The heart</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Chambers and valves:</strong> tricuspid and pulmonary valves (right), mitral and aortic (left). The <strong>right ventricle</strong> is anterior (most of the anterior surface); the <strong>left atrium</strong> is posterior (next to the oesophagus: why TOE views it well).</li><li><strong>Conducting system:</strong> SA node (upper right atrium near the SVC) → AV node (interatrial septum, near the coronary sinus) → bundle of His → left and right bundle branches → Purkinje fibres.</li><li><strong>Coronary arteries:</strong> the <strong>left main stem</strong> divides into the <strong>LAD</strong> (anterior LV, anterior two-thirds of the septum) and <strong>circumflex</strong> (lateral LV); the <strong>RCA</strong> supplies the RV, inferior LV (in right-dominant hearts, about 70–85%), SA node (~60%) and AV node (~80–90%). ECG territories: anterior V1–V4 (LAD), lateral I, aVL, V5–6 (circumflex), inferior II, III, aVF (RCA usually).</li><li><strong>Venous drainage:</strong> coronary sinus to the right atrium; Thebesian veins directly into the chambers.</li><li><strong>Pericardium:</strong> fibrous and serous layers; tamponade physiology.</li><li><strong>Innervation:</strong> sympathetic (T1–T4 cardiac accelerator fibres) and vagus.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"great-vessels-and-neck-veins\">Great vessels and neck veins</h2>\n<ul><li>The <strong>aortic arch</strong> gives off the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and left subclavian arteries.</li><li><strong>Internal jugular vein (IJV):</strong> runs in the carotid sheath <strong>lateral to the common carotid artery</strong> (with the vagus posterior between them), deep to sternocleidomastoid, joining the subclavian to form the brachiocephalic vein behind the sternoclavicular joint. The right IJV gives the most direct route to the SVC. Ultrasound shows the IJV as compressible and variable in size, and the carotid as pulsatile and round.</li><li><strong>Subclavian vein:</strong> passes over the first rib anterior to the scalene anterior (the artery is behind it); lower infection risk but higher pneumothorax risk and non-compressible.</li><li><strong>Femoral vein:</strong> in the <strong>femoral triangle</strong> (inguinal ligament, sartorius, adductor longus), <strong>medial to the artery</strong> (\"NAVY\" from lateral: nerve, artery, vein, Y-fronts); below the inguinal ligament.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"peripheral-access-and-arteries\">Peripheral access and arteries</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Antecubital fossa:</strong> from lateral to medial — <strong>biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve</strong>; the median cubital vein lies superficial; the <strong>bicipital aponeurosis</strong> separates the vein from the artery. Beware accidental arterial cannulation.</li><li><strong>Radial artery:</strong> lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon at the wrist; collateral supply via the ulnar artery and palmar arches. Others: ulnar, brachial, femoral, dorsalis pedis.</li><li><strong>Intraosseous access:</strong> proximal tibia (1–2 cm below the tibial tuberosity, medially), proximal humerus (greater tubercle), distal femur in children. All drugs and fluids can be given; flush and use pressure for fluids.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> coronary territories; IJV relations; femoral triangle; antecubital fossa contents; IO sites.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> talking through ultrasound-guided right IJV cannulation, including complications and how to confirm venous placement.</li></ul></div>","anat-plexuses":"<h2 id=\"brachial-plexus-c5-t1\">Brachial plexus (C5–T1)</h2>\n<p><strong>Roots</strong> (between scalenus anterior and medius) → <strong>trunks</strong> (upper C5–6, middle C7, lower C8–T1; in the posterior triangle above the clavicle) → <strong>divisions</strong> (behind the clavicle) → <strong>cords</strong> (lateral, posterior, medial — named by their relation to the <strong>axillary artery</strong>) → <strong>branches</strong> (in the axilla). Mnemonic: \"Real Teenagers Drink Cold Beer\".</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Terminal branch</th><th>From</th><th>Supplies (key)</th><th>Injury sign</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Musculocutaneous</strong> (C5–7)</td><td>Lateral cord</td><td>Biceps, brachialis; lateral forearm skin</td><td>Weak elbow flexion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Axillary</strong> (C5–6)</td><td>Posterior cord</td><td>Deltoid; \"regimental badge\" skin</td><td>Weak abduction</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Radial</strong> (C5–T1)</td><td>Posterior cord</td><td>Extensors; dorsum of hand (first web space)</td><td><strong>Wrist drop</strong> (e.g. humeral fracture, compression)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Median</strong> (C5–T1)</td><td>Lateral + medial cords</td><td>Forearm flexors, thenar muscles; lateral palm and 3½ fingers</td><td>Loss of thumb opposition; carpal tunnel</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ulnar</strong> (C8–T1)</td><td>Medial cord</td><td>Intrinsic hand muscles; medial 1½ fingers</td><td>Claw hand; commonest perioperative nerve injury (elbow)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Other branches: <strong>suprascapular nerve</strong> (upper trunk), long thoracic (C5–7, serratus anterior), phrenic (C3–5, close to the plexus in the neck).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Blocks:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Level</th><th>Covers</th><th>Main issues</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Interscalene</strong></td><td>Roots/trunks (C5–7)</td><td><strong>Shoulder</strong> and upper arm</td><td><strong>Phrenic nerve block (almost 100%)</strong>, Horner's, hoarseness; spares C8–T1 (ulnar)</td></tr><tr><td>Supraclavicular</td><td>Trunks/divisions</td><td>Whole arm below the shoulder</td><td>Pneumothorax risk; phrenic block less common</td></tr><tr><td>Infraclavicular</td><td>Cords</td><td>Elbow, forearm, hand</td><td>Deep; vascular</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Axillary</strong></td><td>Terminal branches</td><td>Elbow, forearm and hand</td><td>Musculocutaneous must be blocked separately (it leaves the sheath high)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lumbar-plexus-l1-l4-and-sacral-plexus-l4-s4\">Lumbar plexus (L1–L4) and sacral plexus (L4–S4)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Lumbar plexus</strong> (in psoas): iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1–2), <strong>lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh</strong> (L2–3), <strong>femoral</strong> (L2–4: quadriceps, anterior thigh, and the <strong>saphenous nerve</strong> to the medial leg), <strong>obturator</strong> (L2–4: adductors, a small patch of medial thigh).</li><li><strong>Femoral nerve</strong> lies <strong>lateral</strong> to the femoral artery, deep to the fascia iliaca (not inside the femoral sheath).</li><li><strong>Fascia iliaca block</strong> (below or above the inguinal ligament) spreads under fascia iliaca to reach the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves (± obturator): used for <strong>hip fractures</strong>. PENG block targets articular branches to the hip.</li><li><strong>Sacral plexus:</strong> <strong>sciatic nerve</strong> (L4–S3): leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis, divides (usually in the popliteal fossa) into <strong>tibial</strong> and <strong>common peroneal (fibular)</strong> nerves. Common peroneal injury (at the fibular head, e.g. from lithotomy stirrups) → <strong>foot drop</strong>. Also: pudendal (S2–4), posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh.</li><li><strong>Ankle block:</strong> five nerves — tibial, deep peroneal, superficial peroneal, sural and saphenous.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"trunk\">Trunk</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Intercostal nerves</strong> (T1–T11) run in the <strong>subcostal groove below each rib</strong>, between internal and innermost intercostal muscles, with the vein and artery above.</li><li>The <strong>anterior abdominal wall</strong> is supplied by T7–T12 (thoracoabdominal nerves) and L1 (iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal), which run in the plane <strong>between internal oblique and transversus abdominis</strong> — the target of the <strong>TAP block</strong>. The <strong>rectus sheath block</strong> targets the terminal branches behind the rectus muscles.</li><li><strong>Inguinal region:</strong> ilioinguinal, iliohypogastric and genital branch of genitofemoral nerves (inguinal hernia field block).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> plexus structure and branches; injury signs; which nerves each block covers and misses.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing a block for a shoulder arthroscopy in a patient with COPD, and explaining the phrenic nerve issue.</li></ul></div>","anat-spine-neuraxis":"<h2 id=\"vertebral-column\">Vertebral column</h2>\n<ul><li>7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 fused sacral, 4 coccygeal vertebrae. Curvatures: cervical and lumbar lordosis, thoracic and sacral kyphosis (the highest point of the lumbar lordosis at about <strong>L3</strong> and the lowest of the thoracic kyphosis at about <strong>T5–6</strong> influence hyperbaric spinal spread in the supine patient).</li><li>Thoracic spinous processes are steeply angled (paramedian approach helps); lumbar spinous processes are horizontal.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ligaments-from-skin-to-dura-in-the-midline\">Ligaments (from skin to dura in the midline)</h2>\n<p><strong>Skin → subcutaneous tissue → supraspinous ligament → interspinous ligament → ligamentum flavum</strong> (the \"give\" / loss of resistance) <strong>→ epidural space → dura (and arachnoid) → subarachnoid space (CSF).</strong> Posterior and anterior longitudinal ligaments lie within the canal on the vertebral bodies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spinal-cord-and-meninges\">Spinal cord and meninges</h2>\n<ul><li>The cord ends at about <strong>L1–L2</strong> in adults (the conus medullaris; as low as L3 in some), and at about <strong>L3 at birth</strong>. The <strong>dural sac ends at S2</strong> in adults (S3–4 in neonates). Below the conus, the <strong>cauda equina</strong> floats in CSF — why spinals are performed below L2–3 in adults.</li><li><strong>Tuffier's line</strong> (the intercristal line joining the iliac crests) usually crosses <strong>L4 or the L4–5 interspace</strong>, but clinical estimation of level is often inaccurate (frequently a level higher than thought). Ultrasound improves accuracy.</li><li><strong>Meninges:</strong> dura mater, arachnoid mater (the subdural space between them is a potential space: accidental subdural injection causes patchy, high, slow-onset block), pia mater (with denticulate ligaments).</li><li><strong>Epidural space contents:</strong> fat, <strong>epidural veins</strong> (valveless, <strong>Batson's plexus</strong>; engorged in pregnancy and with raised intra-abdominal pressure), lymphatics, spinal nerve roots, arteries. It is widest in the lumbar region (about 5–6 mm) and narrow in the cervical region.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"blood-supply-of-the-cord\">Blood supply of the cord</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>One anterior spinal artery</strong> (supplying the anterior two-thirds) and <strong>two posterior spinal arteries</strong>, reinforced by segmental (radicular) arteries. The largest, the <strong>artery of Adamkiewicz</strong>, usually arises on the left between <strong>T9 and T12</strong> and supplies the lower anterior cord: at risk in aortic surgery.</li><li><strong>Anterior spinal artery syndrome:</strong> paralysis and loss of pain and temperature below the level with preserved proprioception and vibration (dorsal columns). Causes: hypotension, aortic cross-clamping, compression.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"dermatomes-and-key-sensory-levels\">Dermatomes and key sensory levels</h2>\n<ul class=\"md-tags\"><li>C4 clavicles</li><li>T4 nipples</li><li>T6 xiphisternum</li><li><strong>T10 umbilicus</strong></li><li>L1 inguinal ligament</li><li>L4 medial calf/knee</li><li>L5 dorsum of foot and big toe</li><li>S1 lateral foot and little toe</li><li>S2–4 perineum.</li></ul>\n<p>Block heights for surgery (approximate): caesarean section <strong>T4</strong> (bilaterally to touch); inguinal hernia T10; TURP T10; hip and lower limb L1–T12; perineal S1–S5.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sacrum-and-caudal-space\">Sacrum and caudal space</h2>\n<p>The <strong>sacral hiatus</strong> (failure of fusion of the 5th sacral laminae) is covered by the <strong>sacrococcygeal ligament</strong>, between the two sacral cornua at the apex of an equilateral triangle with the posterior superior iliac spines. It is easily palpable in children (caudal block) but variable in adults.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> layers traversed; ends of cord and sac by age; Tuffier's line; blood supply; dermatomes.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient with a new motor block 12 hours after an epidural was stopped: what anatomy explains your concern and what do you do (see neurological monitoring guidance)?</li></ul></div>","anat-trachea-lungs":"<h2 id=\"trachea-and-bronchi\">Trachea and bronchi</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Trachea:</strong> from <strong>C6</strong> to the <strong>carina at about T4–T5</strong> (the sternal angle); about <strong>10–12 cm</strong> long and 1.5–2 cm wide in adults; 16–20 C-shaped cartilage rings, open posteriorly (trachealis muscle next to the oesophagus).</li><li><strong>Right main bronchus:</strong> <strong>shorter, wider and more vertical</strong> (about 25° from the vertical) — hence endobronchial intubation and aspiration favour the right. The <strong>right upper lobe bronchus arises only about 2–2.5 cm from the carina</strong> (sometimes from the trachea), relevant to right-sided double-lumen tubes.</li><li><strong>Left main bronchus:</strong> about 4.5–5 cm long, angled about 45°; passes under the aortic arch. Left-sided double-lumen tubes are preferred for most thoracic surgery.</li><li>Bronchial tree: about 23 generations to the alveoli; conducting zone (to terminal bronchioles, generation ~16) and respiratory zone.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"lungs\">Lungs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Right lung:</strong> 3 lobes (upper, middle, lower), <strong>10 bronchopulmonary segments</strong>; oblique and horizontal fissures.</li><li><strong>Left lung:</strong> 2 lobes (upper with lingula, lower), <strong>8–10 segments</strong>; oblique fissure.</li><li><strong>Blood supply:</strong> pulmonary arteries (deoxygenated) and <strong>bronchial arteries</strong> (from the aorta; nutritive; some drain into pulmonary veins — part of the normal shunt).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pleura-and-surface-markings\">Pleura and surface markings</h2>\n<ul><li>Visceral and parietal pleura; the <strong>parietal pleura</strong> is sensitive (intercostal and phrenic nerves).</li><li>Surface markings (pleural reflections): mid-clavicular line at rib 8, mid-axillary line at rib 10, posteriorly at rib 12. The lungs extend about two ribs higher. The <strong>pleura rises above the clavicle</strong> in the root of the neck (pneumothorax risk with supraclavicular and interscalene blocks and subclavian lines).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"diaphragm\">Diaphragm</h2>\n<ul><li>Main muscle of inspiration; <strong>phrenic nerve C3–5</strong> (\"keeps the diaphragm alive\"). Openings: IVC at T8, oesophagus at T10, aorta at T12.</li><li>Phrenic nerve block is almost inevitable with <strong>interscalene</strong> brachial plexus block (hemidiaphragmatic paresis).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"emergency-landmarks\">Emergency landmarks</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Chest drain:</strong> the <strong>\"triangle of safety\"</strong>: anterior border of latissimus dorsi, lateral border of pectoralis major, a line at the level of the nipple (5th intercostal space), with the apex below the axilla. Insert <strong>just above a rib</strong> to avoid the neurovascular bundle, which runs under each rib (vein, artery, nerve from above down).</li><li><strong>Needle decompression / thoracostomy:</strong> 2nd intercostal space mid-clavicular line, or (increasingly preferred in adults, because of chest wall thickness) the <strong>4th–5th intercostal space anterior to the mid-axillary line</strong>. Finger thoracostomy in ventilated trauma patients.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> tracheal length and carina level; right versus left bronchus; segments; pleural reflections; diaphragm openings.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why a patient desaturated after a tube was advanced, and confirming a right-sided double-lumen tube position with a bronchoscope.</li></ul></div>","antiarrhythmics":"<h2 id=\"vaughan-williams-classification\">Vaughan Williams classification</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Action</th><th>Examples</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td><strong>Na<sup>+</sup> channel block</strong></td><td>Ia: quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide; <strong>Ib: lidocaine</strong>; <strong>Ic: flecainide</strong></td><td>Ia: ↑ AP duration; Ib: ↓ AP duration (ischaemic tissue, ventricular); Ic: marked slowing of conduction (avoid in structural heart disease)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>II</strong></td><td><strong>β-blockade</strong></td><td>Bisoprolol, esmolol, metoprolol, propranolol</td><td>Slow SA and AV nodes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>III</strong></td><td><strong>K<sup>+</sup> channel block</strong> (↑ AP duration and refractory period)</td><td><strong>Amiodarone</strong>, sotalol</td><td>QT prolongation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IV</strong></td><td><strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup> channel block</strong></td><td>Verapamil, diltiazem</td><td>Slow AV node</td></tr><tr><td>Unclassified</td><td></td><td><strong>Adenosine, digoxin, magnesium, atropine</strong></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Many drugs have multiple actions (amiodarone has class I–IV effects; sotalol II and III).</p>\n<h2 id=\"key-drugs\">Key drugs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Amiodarone:</strong> broad spectrum (AF, VT, <strong>VF/pulseless VT after the third shock in cardiac arrest</strong>, per RCUK). IV via a central line if possible (phlebitis), in glucose. Very long half-life (weeks), large Vd, iodine-containing. Long-term toxicity: <strong>thyroid</strong> (hypo- or hyper-), pulmonary fibrosis, hepatitis, corneal deposits, photosensitivity, grey skin, neuropathy. Inhibits CYP (↑ warfarin, digoxin). Hypotension and bradycardia when given IV.</li><li><strong>Adenosine:</strong> A<sub>1</sub> agonist → transient <strong>AV node block</strong> (half-life ~10 s). Terminates <strong>AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia</strong> and reveals atrial activity in flutter. Rapid bolus via a large vein with a flush. Chest tightness, flushing, dyspnoea, brief asystole. Caution in asthma; effect <strong>enhanced by dipyridamole</strong>, reduced by theophylline; lower doses via central lines and in heart transplant recipients.</li><li><strong>Digoxin:</strong> inhibits Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase → ↑ intracellular Ca<sup>2+</sup> (inotropy) and ↑ vagal tone (AV slowing). Rate control in AF with heart failure. Narrow therapeutic index; toxicity worsened by <strong>hypokalaemia</strong>, hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia, renal failure, amiodarone; causes any arrhythmia, nausea, visual disturbance. Treatment: digoxin-specific antibody fragments.</li><li><strong>Magnesium:</strong> torsades de pointes; adjunct in AF and digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Lidocaine:</strong> ventricular arrhythmias (alternative to amiodarone in cardiac arrest).</li><li><strong>β-blockers and verapamil/diltiazem:</strong> rate control; never give IV verapamil for a broad-complex tachycardia of uncertain origin or with a β-blocker.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"perioperative-arrhythmias\">Perioperative arrhythmias</h2>\n<p>Look for and correct causes first: hypoxia, hypercapnia, electrolytes (K<sup>+</sup>, Mg<sup>2+</sup>, Ca<sup>2+</sup>), light anaesthesia or pain, hypovolaemia, drugs, myocardial ischaemia, surgical stimulation (vagal reflexes). Unstable tachyarrhythmias need <strong>synchronised DC cardioversion</strong> (RCUK algorithm).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> classification and examples; adenosine pharmacology; amiodarone side effects; digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> new fast AF under anaesthesia with hypotension: priorities and treatment options.</li></ul></div>","anticholinergics-cholinergics":"<h2 id=\"muscarinic-receptors\">Muscarinic receptors</h2>\n<p>M<sub>1</sub> (CNS, gastric parietal cells), <strong>M<sub>2</sub> (heart: bradycardia)</strong>, <strong>M<sub>3</sub> (smooth muscle, glands, endothelium)</strong>, M<sub>4</sub> and M<sub>5</sub> (CNS).</p>\n<h2 id=\"antimuscarinics-compared\">Antimuscarinics compared</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Atropine</strong></th><th><strong>Glycopyrronium</strong></th><th><strong>Hyoscine (scopolamine)</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Tertiary amine (crosses BBB and placenta)</td><td><strong>Quaternary</strong> (does not cross)</td><td>Tertiary</td></tr><tr><td>Heart rate</td><td>↑↑ (initial paradoxical slowing at low doses)</td><td>↑ (less, smoother)</td><td>↑ (less)</td></tr><tr><td>Antisialagogue</td><td>++</td><td>+++</td><td>+++</td></tr><tr><td>Central effects</td><td>Excitation, confusion in the elderly</td><td><strong>None</strong></td><td><strong>Sedation, amnesia</strong>, antiemetic (motion sickness), confusion</td></tr><tr><td>Onset / duration</td><td>Rapid, shorter</td><td>Slower, longer (matches neostigmine)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Uses</td><td>Bradycardia, organophosphate poisoning</td><td>Reversal with neostigmine; drying secretions</td><td>Motion sickness, secretions, bowel spasm (hyoscine butylbromide is quaternary: no central effects)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other effects of antimuscarinics: bronchodilation, reduced gut motility, urinary retention, mydriasis and cycloplegia (glaucoma risk with atropine), reduced sweating (hyperthermia in children).</p>\n<h2 id=\"central-anticholinergic-syndrome\">Central anticholinergic syndrome</h2>\n<p>Agitation, confusion, hallucinations, hyperthermia, dry flushed skin, mydriasis, tachycardia — after atropine, hyoscine, tricyclics, antihistamines. Treatment: supportive; <strong>physostigmine</strong> (a tertiary anticholinesterase) in severe cases.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cholinergic-agonists\">Cholinergic agonists</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Direct:</strong> pilocarpine (glaucoma), bethanechol, carbachol.</li><li><strong>Indirect</strong> (anticholinesterases): see reversal of neuromuscular block. <strong>Cholinergic crisis</strong> (excess ACh): muscarinic signs (<strong>DUMBELS</strong>: diarrhoea, urination, miosis, bradycardia/bronchorrhoea/bronchospasm, emesis, lacrimation, salivation) plus nicotinic weakness and paralysis.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> tertiary versus quaternary; comparisons of the three antimuscarinics; features of anticholinergic and cholinergic syndromes.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing an anticholinergic to go with neostigmine for an elderly patient, and explaining why.</li></ul></div>","anticoagulants":"<h2 id=\"parenteral-anticoagulants\">Parenteral anticoagulants</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Unfractionated heparin (UFH)</strong></th><th><strong>Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH)</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Binds <strong>antithrombin</strong>; inactivates thrombin (IIa) and Xa equally</td><td>Antithrombin-mediated; mainly <strong>anti-Xa</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Kinetics</td><td>Short half-life (~1–2 h); IV infusion or SC</td><td>Longer (~4–6 h); predictable; SC once or twice daily; renally excreted</td></tr><tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>APTT (or ACT during bypass)</td><td>Usually none; <strong>anti-Xa</strong> in renal impairment, obesity, pregnancy</td></tr><tr><td>Reversal</td><td><strong>Protamine</strong> (1 mg per 100 units, fully)</td><td>Protamine only partially</td></tr><tr><td>HIT risk</td><td>Higher</td><td>Lower</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Fondaparinux</strong> (synthetic pentasaccharide, indirect anti-Xa) is not reversed by protamine. <strong>Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia</strong> (type II, immune): platelet fall of &gt; 50% at 5–10 days with <strong>thrombosis</strong>; stop all heparin and use an alternative (e.g. argatroban).</p>\n<h2 id=\"warfarin\">Warfarin</h2>\n<ul><li>Inhibits <strong>vitamin K epoxide reductase</strong> → reduced II, VII, IX, X (and proteins C and S: transient hypercoagulability when started). Monitored by <strong>INR</strong>.</li><li>Highly protein-bound, CYP2C9-metabolised: many interactions (enzyme inhibitors ↑ INR; inducers ↓).</li><li><strong>Reversal:</strong> for urgent surgery or major bleeding, <strong>prothrombin complex concentrate</strong> plus <strong>IV vitamin K</strong>; for elective surgery, stop about 5 days before and check INR; bridge with LMWH only in high thrombotic risk (e.g. mechanical mitral valve, recent VTE).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"direct-oral-anticoagulants-doacs\">Direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Target</th><th>Renal elimination</th><th>Reversal agent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Dabigatran</strong></td><td>Thrombin (IIa)</td><td>~80%</td><td><strong>Idarucizumab</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban</strong></td><td>Xa</td><td>~25–50%</td><td><strong>Andexanet alfa</strong> (licensed for apixaban/rivaroxaban major bleeding); PCC often used</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Stopping time before surgery depends on <strong>renal function</strong> and the bleeding risk of the procedure (typically 1–2 days for low risk, longer for high risk and neuraxial block, and longer for dabigatran with renal impairment). Bridging is not needed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"antiplatelet-drugs\">Antiplatelet drugs</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Typical stop before neuraxial / high-risk surgery (guide)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Irreversible COX-1</td><td>Not usually needed for neuraxial block</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clopidogrel</strong></td><td>Irreversible P2Y<sub>12</sub> (pro-drug, CYP2C19)</td><td>7 days</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prasugrel</strong></td><td>Irreversible P2Y<sub>12</sub></td><td>7 days</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ticagrelor</strong></td><td>Reversible P2Y<sub>12</sub></td><td>5 days</td></tr><tr><td>GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors (tirofiban, eptifibatide, abciximab)</td><td>Block aggregation</td><td>Hours to days</td></tr><tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>PDE inhibitor, adenosine reuptake</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"neuraxial-block-and-anticoagulation\">Neuraxial block and anticoagulation</h2>\n<p>Timing of needle insertion and catheter removal relative to anticoagulant doses is set out in the Association of Anaesthetists/OAA/RA-UK guidance (e.g. about <strong>12 h after prophylactic LMWH</strong>, 24 h after treatment doses). <strong>Always check the current guideline and local policy</strong>, and assess renal function and the patient's overall risk.</p>\n<h2 id=\"vte-prophylaxis\">VTE prophylaxis</h2>\n<p>NICE NG89: assess every surgical patient for VTE and bleeding risk; use mechanical (graduated compression stockings, intermittent pneumatic compression) and pharmacological prophylaxis as indicated; early mobilisation.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms; monitoring tests; reversal agents; half-lives and renal dependence.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient on apixaban with eGFR 40 needs a hip fracture repair tomorrow: can you do a spinal, and when?</li></ul></div>","antiemetics":"<h2 id=\"classes\">Classes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Examples</th><th>Site / receptor</th><th>Side effects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>5-HT<sub>3</sub> antagonists</strong></td><td>Ondansetron, granisetron, palonosetron</td><td>CTZ and vagal afferents</td><td>Headache, constipation, <strong>QT prolongation</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Corticosteroids</strong></td><td><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> (e.g. 4–8 mg IV at induction)</td><td>Uncertain (central)</td><td>Hyperglycaemia; perineal burning with rapid IV injection; debated wound infection risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D<sub>2</sub> antagonists</strong></td><td>Droperidol, haloperidol; metoclopramide (also prokinetic); domperidone</td><td>CTZ</td><td><strong>Extrapyramidal effects</strong> (dystonia, especially in young women; treat with procyclidine), sedation, QT prolongation, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (rare); avoid metoclopramide in bowel obstruction and Parkinson's disease</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antihistamines (H<sub>1</sub>)</strong></td><td>Cyclizine, promethazine</td><td>Vomiting centre, vestibular</td><td>Sedation, dry mouth, <strong>tachycardia</strong>, injection pain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antimuscarinics</strong></td><td>Hyoscine (transdermal patch)</td><td>Vestibular, vomiting centre</td><td>Dry mouth, blurred vision, confusion in the elderly</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NK<sub>1</sub> antagonists</strong></td><td>Aprepitant, fosaprepitant</td><td>Substance P receptors in the nucleus tractus solitarius and CTZ</td><td>Long-acting; interactions (CYP3A4, oral contraceptives)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Others</strong></td><td>Propofol (TIVA), dexmedetomidine, ondansetron + dexamethasone combinations</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"risk-and-prophylaxis-fourth-consensus-guidelines\">Risk and prophylaxis (Fourth consensus guidelines)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Apfel score (adults):</strong> female, non-smoker, history of PONV or motion sickness, postoperative opioids. Each adds risk (roughly 10%, 20%, 40%, 60%, 80% for 0–4 factors).</li><li><strong>Reduce baseline risk:</strong> regional anaesthesia where appropriate, <strong>propofol TIVA</strong>, avoid N<sub>2</sub>O and volatile agents in high-risk patients, <strong>opioid-sparing</strong> multimodal analgesia, adequate hydration.</li><li><strong>Multimodal prophylaxis:</strong> give <strong>two agents</strong> from different classes to patients with one or two risk factors, and three or more when risk is high; many centres now give two agents to most patients having general anaesthesia.</li><li><strong>Rescue:</strong> use an antiemetic from a <strong>different class</strong> from those already given (repeating the same drug within about 6 hours is ineffective).</li><li>Children: POVOC score (surgery ≥ 30 min, age ≥ 3 years, strabismus surgery, history of PONV).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> receptors targeted by each drug; side effects; Apfel factors.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a young woman with severe PONV previously, having a laparoscopic gynaecological procedure: build and justify a plan.</li></ul></div>","antihypertensives":"<h2 id=\"classes\">Classes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Examples</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Perioperative points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ACE inhibitors / ARBs</strong></td><td>Ramipril, lisinopril; losartan, candesartan</td><td>Block angiotensin II production or its AT<sub>1</sub> receptor</td><td><strong>Hypotension after induction</strong> that responds poorly to phenylephrine; many units omit on the morning of surgery (especially if used for hypertension alone); cough and angioedema with ACEi; hyperkalaemia; AKI risk with hypovolaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Calcium channel blockers</strong></td><td>Amlodipine, nifedipine (dihydropyridines: vascular); verapamil, diltiazem (cardiac: rate)</td><td>L-type channel block</td><td>Continue; verapamil + β-blocker → heart block</td></tr><tr><td><strong>β-blockers</strong></td><td>Bisoprolol, atenolol, metoprolol, propranolol</td><td>β<sub>1</sub> (± β<sub>2</sub>) antagonism</td><td><strong>Continue</strong> (withdrawal → rebound tachycardia and ischaemia); do not start high doses just before surgery (POISE); mask signs of hypovolaemia and hypoglycaemia; bronchospasm with non-selective agents</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thiazide / loop diuretics</strong></td><td>Indapamide, bendroflumethiazide; furosemide</td><td>See diuretics</td><td>Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, hypovolaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>α-blockers</strong></td><td>Doxazosin; <strong>phenoxybenzamine</strong> (irreversible, non-selective)</td><td>α<sub>1</sub> antagonism</td><td>Postural hypotension; phenoxybenzamine for phaeochromocytoma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Centrally acting</strong></td><td>Clonidine, methyldopa (pregnancy)</td><td>α<sub>2</sub> agonism</td><td>Rebound hypertension if clonidine stopped</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Direct vasodilators</strong></td><td>Hydralazine, minoxidil</td><td>Arteriolar dilatation</td><td>Reflex tachycardia</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"acute-control-in-theatre\">Acute control in theatre</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Labetalol</strong></td><td>α<sub>1</sub> + β blockade (β:α about 3:1 IV)</td><td>Bolus titration; pre-eclampsia; avoid in asthma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Esmolol</strong></td><td>Short-acting β<sub>1</sub> (red cell esterases; t<sub>½</sub> ~9 min)</td><td>Tachycardia and pressor response to intubation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hydralazine</strong></td><td>Arteriolar dilator</td><td>Pre-eclampsia; slow onset, reflex tachycardia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GTN</strong></td><td>NO donor; venodilator &gt; arteriolar</td><td>Also coronary vasodilator; tachyphylaxis; methaemoglobinaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sodium nitroprusside</strong></td><td>NO donor; arterial and venous</td><td>Very potent; <strong>cyanide toxicity</strong> (treat with hydroxocobalamin, sodium thiosulfate); protect from light</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Magnesium</strong></td><td>Ca<sup>2+</sup> antagonism, catecholamine release inhibition</td><td>Pre-eclampsia, phaeochromocytoma</td></tr><tr><td>Deepening anaesthesia / analgesia, remifentanil</td><td></td><td>Often the right first step</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"hypertension-and-surgery\">Hypertension and surgery</h2>\n<ul><li>Elective surgery can usually proceed if blood pressure in primary care is below <strong>160/100 mmHg</strong> (joint AAGBI/BHS guidance, 2016); very high readings in clinic should be confirmed and assessed for end-organ damage.</li><li>Intraoperative targets: avoid falls below about 20% of baseline (or MAP &lt; 65 mmHg) in at-risk patients.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms; nitroprusside toxicity; perioperative management of each class.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> managing hypotension after induction in a patient who took ramipril and amlodipine that morning.</li></ul></div>","antimicrobials":"<h2 id=\"classes\">Classes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Examples</th><th>Anaesthetic relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>β-lactams</strong></td><td>Cell wall synthesis (penicillin-binding proteins); bactericidal</td><td>Penicillins, co-amoxiclav, piperacillin–tazobactam, cephalosporins (cefuroxime, ceftriaxone), carbapenems (meropenem)</td><td>Allergy (true penicillin allergy much rarer than reported; cross-reactivity with cephalosporins low); <strong>co-amoxiclav a common NAP6 trigger</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Glycopeptides</strong></td><td>Cell wall (Gram-positive, including MRSA)</td><td>Vancomycin, <strong>teicoplanin</strong></td><td>Vancomycin: <strong>\"red man\" syndrome</strong> with fast infusion (histamine); nephrotoxic. <strong>Teicoplanin had the highest anaphylaxis rate in NAP6</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong></td><td>30S ribosome; bactericidal</td><td>Gentamicin</td><td>Nephro- and <strong>ototoxic</strong>; <strong>potentiate neuromuscular block</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Macrolides</strong></td><td>50S ribosome</td><td>Erythromycin, clarithromycin</td><td><strong>CYP3A4 inhibition</strong> (↑ midazolam, alfentanil); QT prolongation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tetracyclines</strong></td><td>30S</td><td>Doxycycline</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quinolones</strong></td><td>DNA gyrase</td><td>Ciprofloxacin</td><td>Seizure threshold ↓; QT; tendinopathy; CYP1A2 inhibition (theophylline)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nitroimidazoles</strong></td><td>DNA damage (anaerobes)</td><td>Metronidazole</td><td>Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lincosamides</strong></td><td>50S</td><td>Clindamycin</td><td><em>C. difficile</em>; potentiates NMB</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oxazolidinones</strong></td><td>50S</td><td>Linezolid</td><td>Weak MAOI (serotonin syndrome)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antifolates</strong></td><td>Folate synthesis</td><td>Trimethoprim, co-trimoxazole</td><td>Hyperkalaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antifungals</strong> (azoles)</td><td>Ergosterol synthesis</td><td>Fluconazole</td><td>CYP inhibition</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"surgical-antibiotic-prophylaxis\">Surgical antibiotic prophylaxis</h2>\n<ul><li>Give IV <strong>within 60 minutes before incision</strong> (so that tissue levels are adequate at incision); re-dose in long procedures or major blood loss; a single dose is usually enough.</li><li>Choice according to <strong>local policy</strong> (likely organisms, resistance, allergy). Tourniquet surgery: give before inflation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"stewardship\">Stewardship</h2>\n<p>Use antibiotics only when needed, choose narrow spectrum where possible, the right dose and duration, review at 48–72 hours (\"start smart, then focus\"). Resistance: MRSA, ESBL-producing Gram-negatives, carbapenemase producers, <em>C. difficile</em> infection after broad-spectrum use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"infection-prevention\">Infection prevention</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hand hygiene</strong> (the WHO \"five moments\"), gloves and aprons, aseptic non-touch technique for IV access, full asepsis (hat, mask, gown, gloves, drape) for central lines and neuraxial procedures, single-use equipment, safe sharps disposal.</li><li><strong>Healthcare-associated infections:</strong> surgical site infection, catheter-related bloodstream infection, ventilator-associated pneumonia, catheter-associated UTI, <em>C. difficile</em>. <strong>Prions</strong> (vCJD) resist standard sterilisation: single-use instruments for high-risk tissues.</li><li><strong>Hospital immunisation:</strong> staff hepatitis B vaccination and immunity checks, influenza and other vaccines per occupational health policy. Needlestick injuries: first aid, report, assess for post-exposure prophylaxis (HIV PEP ideally within hours).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms; drug interactions with anaesthetic agents; timing of prophylaxis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient with a \"penicillin allergy\" label needs prophylaxis for arthroplasty: how do you decide what to give?</li></ul></div>","benzodiazepines-sedation":"<h2 id=\"benzodiazepines\">Benzodiazepines</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> bind the <strong>benzodiazepine site</strong> (between α and γ subunits) of the GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor → increase the <strong>frequency</strong> of Cl<sup>−</sup> channel opening (barbiturates increase the duration).</li><li><strong>Effects:</strong> anxiolysis, sedation, <strong>anterograde amnesia</strong>, anticonvulsant, muscle relaxation (spinal). Respiratory depression is <strong>synergistic with opioids</strong>.</li><li><strong>Midazolam:</strong> water-soluble in the ampoule (ring open at pH ~3.5), lipid-soluble at body pH; hydroxylated by <strong>CYP3A4</strong> (inhibited by erythromycin, azoles) to active 1-hydroxymidazolam (accumulates in renal failure); elimination half-life about 2 h, longer in the elderly and obese.</li><li><strong>Diazepam:</strong> long half-life with active metabolites (desmethyldiazepam).</li><li><strong>Lorazepam:</strong> glucuronidated (no active metabolites); used in status epilepticus.</li><li><strong>Remimazolam:</strong> ultra-short-acting ester benzodiazepine (tissue esterases).</li><li><strong>Reversal:</strong> <strong>flumazenil</strong> (competitive antagonist) has a shorter duration than most benzodiazepines → re-sedation; may precipitate seizures in dependent patients or tricyclic overdose.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"2-agonists\">α<sub>2</sub>-agonists</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Clonidine</strong> and <strong>dexmedetomidine</strong> (much more α<sub>2</sub>-selective): sedation resembling natural sleep (locus coeruleus), analgesia, sympatholysis, reduced anaesthetic requirement, reduced shivering and emergence agitation, with <strong>little respiratory depression</strong>.</li><li>Side effects: bradycardia and hypotension (initial hypertension with rapid loading), rebound hypertension on stopping clonidine.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"procedural-sedation\">Procedural sedation</h2>\n<p><strong>Levels (continuum):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Response</th><th>Airway and breathing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Minimal (anxiolysis)</td><td>Normal response to voice</td><td>Unaffected</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Moderate (\"conscious\") sedation</strong></td><td>Purposeful response to voice or light touch</td><td>No intervention needed; adequate spontaneous ventilation</td></tr><tr><td>Deep sedation</td><td>Purposeful response only to repeated or painful stimulation</td><td>May need airway support</td></tr><tr><td>General anaesthesia</td><td>Unrousable</td><td>Often needs airway support</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Patients can slip unintentionally into a deeper level: those giving sedation must be able to rescue from the next level.</li><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> ASA status, airway, OSA, fasting, comorbidities, frailty, drug history; consent and explanation (reassurance reduces drug requirement).</li><li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> as for anaesthesia when deep sedation is used; <strong>capnography</strong> is recommended for moderate and deep sedation (Association of Anaesthetists 2021 monitoring standards); pulse oximetry, BP, ECG as indicated. Scoring tools (e.g. RASS) quantify depth.</li><li><strong>Complications:</strong> airway obstruction, hypoventilation, hypoxaemia, hypotension, aspiration, paradoxical agitation (children, elderly). Keep reversal agents, oxygen, suction and airway equipment ready.</li><li><strong>Discharge:</strong> recovery criteria; a responsible adult for day cases; no driving or legal decisions for 24 hours.</li><li>Particular risks: the <strong>frail and elderly</strong> (smaller doses, slower titration), the critically ill, and patients needing transfer.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanism at GABA<sub>A</sub>; midazolam chemistry and metabolism; flumazenil limitations; levels of sedation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> asked to sedate an elderly patient with COPD for a cardioversion in the emergency department: plan, monitoring and rescue.</li></ul></div>","blood-haemostasis":"<h2 id=\"primary-haemostasis\">Primary haemostasis</h2>\n<p>Vessel injury → vasoconstriction → <strong>platelet adhesion</strong> to exposed collagen via <strong>von Willebrand factor</strong> (binding platelet <strong>GPIb</strong>) → <strong>activation</strong> (shape change; release of ADP, thromboxane A<sub>2</sub>, serotonin) → <strong>aggregation</strong> via <strong>GPIIb/IIIa</strong> receptors cross-linked by fibrinogen. Normal platelet count 150–400 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L; lifespan 7–10 days.</p>\n<h2 id=\"coagulation-the-cell-based-model\">Coagulation: the cell-based model</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Initiation:</strong> <strong>tissue factor</strong> (TF) on subendothelial cells binds <strong>factor VIIa</strong>, activating small amounts of IX and X → a little thrombin.</li><li><strong>Amplification:</strong> thrombin activates platelets and factors <strong>V, VIII and XI</strong>.</li><li><strong>Propagation:</strong> on the activated platelet surface, the <strong>tenase (IXa–VIIIa)</strong> and <strong>prothrombinase (Xa–Va)</strong> complexes generate a burst of thrombin, which converts <strong>fibrinogen to fibrin</strong>; <strong>factor XIIIa</strong> cross-links it.</li><li>The classical <strong>extrinsic</strong> (TF, VII) and <strong>intrinsic</strong> (XII, XI, IX, VIII) pathways remain useful for interpreting laboratory tests.</li><li><strong>Vitamin K-dependent factors:</strong> II, VII, IX, X (and proteins C and S). Calcium is required for most steps (citrate anticoagulates stored blood).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"natural-anticoagulants-and-fibrinolysis\">Natural anticoagulants and fibrinolysis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Antithrombin</strong> inactivates thrombin and Xa (accelerated about 1000-fold by heparin). <strong>Protein C</strong> (activated by thrombin–thrombomodulin) with <strong>protein S</strong> inactivates Va and VIIIa. <strong>Tissue factor pathway inhibitor</strong> limits initiation.</li><li><strong>Fibrinolysis:</strong> <strong>tPA</strong> converts plasminogen to <strong>plasmin</strong>, which breaks down fibrin into fibrin degradation products (<strong>D-dimers</strong>). <strong>Tranexamic acid</strong> blocks lysine-binding sites on plasminogen.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"tests\">Tests</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Measures</th><th>Prolonged by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PT / INR</strong></td><td>Extrinsic and common (VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen)</td><td>Warfarin, liver disease, vitamin K deficiency, DIC</td></tr><tr><td><strong>APTT</strong></td><td>Intrinsic and common (XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II)</td><td>Unfractionated heparin, haemophilia, lupus anticoagulant (in vitro)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fibrinogen (Clauss)</strong></td><td>Normal ~2–4 g/L; ≥ 2 g/L is a common target in obstetric haemorrhage</td><td>Dilution, consumption (DIC, obstetric haemorrhage)</td></tr><tr><td>Anti-Xa assay</td><td>Heparin effect (LMWH, UFH) and some DOACs</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Viscoelastic tests (TEG, ROTEM)</strong></td><td>Whole-blood clot formation, strength and lysis</td><td>Guide targeted treatment in major haemorrhage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Viscoelastic tests show the <strong>time to start clotting</strong> (factor deficiency), <strong>clot strength</strong> (fibrinogen and platelets) and <strong>lysis</strong> (hyperfibrinolysis), within minutes. Their use in obstetric haemorrhage is supported by a national consensus.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-disorders\">Common disorders</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Haemophilia A (VIII) and B (IX):</strong> X-linked; prolonged APTT; factor replacement before surgery with haematology input.</li><li><strong>Von Willebrand disease:</strong> the commonest inherited bleeding disorder; mucosal bleeding; <strong>desmopressin</strong> releases vWF in type 1.</li><li><strong>DIC:</strong> widespread activation with consumption of factors and platelets and secondary fibrinolysis (sepsis, obstetric emergencies, trauma).</li><li><strong>Liver disease:</strong> reduced synthesis of both pro- and anticoagulant factors (\"rebalanced\" haemostasis): INR overstates bleeding risk.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> vitamin K-dependent factors; what PT and APTT measure; interpreting a viscoelastic trace.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a bleeding patient after major surgery with a coagulation screen and ROTEM result: interpret and prioritise treatment.</li></ul></div>","blood-rbc-hb":"<h2 id=\"red-cells\">Red cells</h2>\n<ul><li>Produced in bone marrow under the control of <strong>erythropoietin</strong> (about 90% from peritubular interstitial cells of the kidney and 10% from the liver, released in response to reduced oxygen delivery); lifespan about <strong>120 days</strong>; removed by the spleen.</li><li>No nucleus or mitochondria: energy from <strong>anaerobic glycolysis</strong> (Embden–Meyerhof). The <strong>Rapoport–Luebering shunt</strong> produces <strong>2,3-DPG</strong>; the <strong>pentose phosphate pathway</strong> produces NADPH to protect against oxidative damage (<strong>G6PD deficiency</strong> → haemolysis with oxidant drugs and infection). <strong>Methaemoglobin reductase</strong> keeps iron in the ferrous (Fe<sup>2+</sup>) state.</li><li>Haematinics: <strong>iron, vitamin B<sub>12</sub> and folate</strong> (B<sub>12</sub> and folate deficiency → macrocytic anaemia; nitrous oxide inactivates B<sub>12</sub>-dependent methionine synthase).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"haemoglobin\">Haemoglobin</h2>\n<ul><li>Tetramer of four globin chains, each with a haem group (protoporphyrin + <strong>Fe<sup>2+</sup></strong>) binding one O<sub>2</sub>. Adult <strong>HbA = α<sub>2</sub>β<sub>2</sub></strong> (~97%), HbA<sub>2</sub> = α<sub>2</sub>δ<sub>2</sub>, <strong>HbF = α<sub>2</sub>γ<sub>2</sub></strong> (higher affinity; P50 ~2.5 kPa).</li><li><strong>Sickle haemoglobin (HbS):</strong> a β-chain glutamate → valine substitution; polymerises when deoxygenated → sickling, vaso-occlusion and haemolysis. Avoid hypoxia, dehydration, hypothermia, acidosis and stasis; sickle cell disease patients need specialist perioperative planning, often including transfusion.</li><li><strong>Thalassaemias:</strong> reduced synthesis of α or β chains.</li><li><strong>Methaemoglobin (Fe<sup>3+</sup>)</strong> cannot carry oxygen; causes include nitrates, prilocaine (o-toluidine), dapsone. Pulse oximetry reads around 85% whatever the true saturation. Treat with <strong>methylthioninium chloride (methylene blue)</strong> unless G6PD deficient.</li><li><strong>Carboxyhaemoglobin:</strong> see oxygen transport.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"iron\">Iron</h2>\n<ul><li>Absorbed in the <strong>duodenum</strong> as Fe<sup>2+</sup> (enhanced by acid and vitamin C); regulated by <strong>hepcidin</strong>, which rises in inflammation and blocks absorption and release (the anaemia of chronic disease). Transported by <strong>transferrin</strong>, stored as <strong>ferritin</strong> (an acute phase protein; low ferritin is diagnostic of iron deficiency, but normal or high ferritin does not exclude it when inflammation is present: check transferrin saturation).</li><li><strong>Preoperative iron deficiency anaemia</strong> should be treated (often with IV iron when time is short), as part of patient blood management.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"anaemia\">Anaemia</h2>\n<ul><li>WHO definition: Hb &lt; 130 g/L in men and &lt; 120 g/L in non-pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Acute adaptations:</strong> increased cardiac output (lower viscosity, sympathetic activity), increased oxygen extraction. <strong>Chronic:</strong> raised 2,3-DPG (right shift), increased plasma volume.</li><li>Perioperative anaemia is associated with worse outcomes and more transfusion; restrictive transfusion thresholds (e.g. 70 g/L, or 80 g/L with cardiovascular disease) are generally recommended.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"polycythaemia\">Polycythaemia</h2>\n<p>Primary (polycythaemia vera) or secondary (hypoxia, altitude, smoking, EPO-secreting tumours). Raised viscosity increases thrombotic risk; in polycythaemia vera, venesection before elective surgery reduces complications.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> red cell metabolism and G6PD; Hb variants and their P50; causes of methaemoglobinaemia; iron studies.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient listed for major surgery with a Hb of 98 g/L: what investigations and optimisation would you arrange?</li></ul></div>","blood-transfusion":"<h2 id=\"blood-groups\">Blood groups</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Antigens on red cells</th><th>Antibodies in plasma</th><th>UK frequency (approx.)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>O</td><td>None</td><td>Anti-A and anti-B</td><td>~45%</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>A</td><td>Anti-B</td><td>~40%</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>B</td><td>Anti-A</td><td>~10%</td></tr><tr><td>AB</td><td>A and B</td><td>None</td><td>~5%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>ABO antibodies are <strong>naturally occurring IgM</strong>, which fix complement: ABO-incompatible transfusion causes <strong>intravascular haemolysis</strong> and can be fatal.</li><li><strong>Group O</strong> red cells are the universal donor for red cells (O RhD-negative in emergencies for women of childbearing potential and unknown group); <strong>AB plasma</strong> is the universal plasma donor.</li><li><strong>Rhesus D:</strong> about 85% RhD-positive. Anti-D is IgG and develops only after exposure (transfusion or pregnancy); it can cross the placenta → <strong>haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn</strong>. Anti-D immunoglobulin prevents sensitisation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"compatibility-testing\">Compatibility testing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Group and screen:</strong> determines ABO and RhD group and screens the plasma for irregular antibodies.</li><li><strong>Crossmatch:</strong> serological (patient plasma against donor cells) or <strong>electronic issue</strong> when the screen is negative and valid samples exist. Emergency O-negative is available immediately; group-specific blood within about 10–15 minutes.</li><li><strong>Two independent samples</strong> (or a second check) are usually needed before group-specific blood is issued, to prevent wrong-blood-in-tube errors, which are a major cause of ABO incompatibility.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"components\">Components</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Storage</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Red cells</td><td>2–6°C, up to 35 days</td><td>Hct ~0.5–0.7 in SAG-M; one unit raises Hb by about 10 g/L in an adult</td></tr><tr><td>Platelets</td><td>20–24°C with agitation, up to 7 days</td><td>Highest risk of bacterial contamination</td></tr><tr><td>Fresh frozen plasma</td><td>−25°C or below</td><td>Contains all coagulation factors; 12–15 mL/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Cryoprecipitate</td><td>Frozen</td><td>Rich in fibrinogen, VIII, vWF, XIII</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Changes in stored red cells: rising K<sup>+</sup>, falling pH and 2,3-DPG, microaggregates, cold. Citrate binds calcium.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transfusion-reactions-and-complications\">Transfusion reactions and complications</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Acute haemolytic (ABO incompatible):</strong> fever, hypotension, loin pain, haemoglobinuria, DIC; under anaesthesia may present only as hypotension, tachycardia and bleeding. <strong>Stop the transfusion</strong>, keep the line, resuscitate, check the patient ID against the unit, inform the lab, send samples.</li><li><strong>Febrile non-haemolytic reactions</strong> (cytokines), <strong>allergic reactions</strong> (urticaria) up to <strong>anaphylaxis</strong> (IgA-deficient patients with anti-IgA).</li><li><strong>TACO</strong> (transfusion-associated circulatory overload): the commonest serious cause of transfusion-related morbidity; elderly and cardiac patients; hypertension, pulmonary oedema.</li><li><strong>TRALI:</strong> acute lung injury within 6 hours, from donor antibodies against recipient leucocytes.</li><li><strong>Bacterial contamination</strong> (platelets), <strong>delayed haemolytic reaction</strong> (days to weeks), <strong>transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease</strong> (prevented by irradiation in at-risk patients).</li><li><strong>Massive transfusion:</strong> hypothermia, hypocalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, dilutional coagulopathy, acidosis.</li><li><strong>Transfusion-transmitted infection</strong> (HIV, hepatitis B and C, HTLV, syphilis, bacterial contamination, and the theoretical risk of variant CJD) is now very rare in the UK because of donor selection, testing and leucodepletion.</li><li><strong>Safe transfusion practice:</strong> positive patient identification at every step (sampling, collection, administration), bedside checks against the prescription and the compatibility label, observations before, 15 minutes into and after each unit, and completion within 4 hours of leaving controlled storage.</li><li>Serious hazards are reported to the UK haemovigilance scheme (SHOT) and MHRA (SABRE).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ABO antibodies and their type; storage of components; features of TRALI versus TACO.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"The patient becomes hypotensive 10 minutes after the second unit of blood is started under GA\": run the emergency and explain it to the consultant.</li></ul></div>","breathing-systems":"<h2 id=\"ideal-properties\">Ideal properties</h2>\n<p>Low resistance and dead space, efficient use of fresh gas, allows spontaneous and controlled ventilation, humidification, scavenging, lightweight, safe.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mapleson-classification\">Mapleson classification</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Description</th><th>FGF to prevent rebreathing (SV)</th><th>FGF (IPPV)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A (Magill; Lack = coaxial)</strong></td><td>FGF at the bag end, APL valve at the patient end</td><td><strong>~ alveolar minute ventilation (≈ 70–80 mL/kg/min)</strong> — the most efficient for SV</td><td>Very inefficient (&gt; 3 × MV)</td><td>Lack: expiratory limb inside the inspiratory tube</td></tr><tr><td>B, C</td><td>FGF and APL valve at the patient end</td><td>High (1.5–2 × MV)</td><td></td><td>C (\"Waters\") used for resuscitation and transfers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D (Bain = coaxial)</strong></td><td>FGF at the patient end, APL valve at the bag end</td><td><strong>2–3 × MV</strong> (150–200 mL/kg/min)</td><td><strong>~70 mL/kg/min</strong> — efficient for IPPV</td><td>Bain: fresh gas in the <strong>inner</strong> tube; check for inner tube disconnection (Pethick test)</td></tr><tr><td>E (Ayre's T-piece)</td><td>No bag or valve</td><td>2–3 × MV</td><td>Intermittent occlusion</td><td>For children</td></tr><tr><td><strong>F (Jackson-Rees)</strong></td><td>T-piece with an open-ended bag</td><td>2–3 × MV (minimum ~3 L/min)</td><td></td><td>Children under ~20 kg; the bag shows breathing and allows CPAP</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Memory aid for efficiency in spontaneous breathing: <strong>A &gt; D &gt; C &gt; B</strong> (\"All Dogs Can Bite\"); for controlled ventilation: <strong>D &gt; B &gt; C &gt; A</strong> (\"Dogs Bite Can't Accept\").</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-circle-system\">The circle system</h2>\n<ul><li>Components: fresh gas inlet, <strong>unidirectional inspiratory and expiratory valves</strong>, reservoir bag, APL valve, <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> absorber</strong> canister, Y-piece.</li><li>Allows <strong>low-flow</strong> anaesthesia (conserves volatile, heat and humidity; reduces pollution and cost). At low flows, inspired concentrations differ from those set: monitor inspired and expired gases.</li><li><strong>CO<sub>2</sub> absorbents:</strong> soda lime (mainly calcium hydroxide, with water and small amounts of sodium hydroxide) or lime without strong bases. Reaction: CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>, then neutralised by hydroxides → carbonates + water + <strong>heat</strong>. An indicator dye changes colour (e.g. white → violet) when exhausted. Granule size 4–8 mesh balances surface area and resistance.</li><li><strong>Hazards:</strong> exhausted absorbent (rising inspired CO<sub>2</sub>), <strong>compound A</strong> (sevoflurane), <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> (desiccated absorbent with desflurane, historically), fires, valves sticking (rebreathing), leaks and misconnections; nitrogen and other gases accumulate at low flows early in a case.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"resuscitation-devices\">Resuscitation devices</h2>\n<p><strong>Self-inflating bag–valve–mask</strong> devices (e.g. Ambu) work without a gas supply; with a reservoir and 15 L/min oxygen they deliver about 85–90% oxygen. The non-rebreathing valve prevents exhaled gas returning.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the Mapleson table and FGFs; Bain versus Lack; circle system chemistry; hazards.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the capnograph baseline rises steadily during a long case on a circle: work out the cause and fix it.</li></ul></div>","capnography":"<h2 id=\"measurement\">Measurement</h2>\n<ul><li>CO<sub>2</sub> absorbs <strong>infrared light</strong> strongly at about <strong>4.3 µm</strong>. The sample chamber is compared with a reference; absorption is proportional to CO<sub>2</sub> partial pressure.</li><li><strong>Sidestream:</strong> gas is drawn (about 50–200 mL/min) to the monitor. Slight delay; can also measure other gases; water traps needed; the sampled gas should be returned to the circuit or scavenged.</li><li><strong>Mainstream:</strong> the sensor sits in the airway; instant response but adds weight and dead space.</li><li>Collision broadening by N<sub>2</sub>O causes small errors (corrected by modern monitors).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-normal-capnogram\">The normal capnogram</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Phase I:</strong> inspiratory baseline (dead space gas, ~0).</li><li><strong>Phase II:</strong> rapid expiratory upstroke (mixing of dead space and alveolar gas).</li><li><strong>Phase III:</strong> alveolar plateau, slightly upsloping; <strong>end-tidal</strong> value at its end.</li><li><strong>Phase 0 (or IV):</strong> inspiratory downstroke.</li><li><strong>α angle</strong> (between II and III) increases with airway obstruction; <strong>β angle</strong> (between III and inspiration) increases with rebreathing.</li><li>Normal P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> − P<sub>ET</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> gap: about <strong>0.5–1 kPa</strong>, widened by increased <strong>alveolar dead space</strong> (low cardiac output, PE, hypovolaemia, high PEEP, COPD).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"abnormal-traces\">Abnormal traces</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Trace</th><th>Causes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>No trace / flat line</strong></td><td>Oesophageal intubation, disconnection, apnoea, total airway obstruction, cardiac arrest (very low), sampling line failure — <strong>\"no trace = wrong place\"</strong> until proven otherwise; a trace is also seen during effective CPR</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sudden fall in ETCO<sub>2</sub></strong></td><td>Massive PE (thrombus, air, fat), cardiac arrest, sudden severe hypotension, disconnection or leak</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gradual fall</strong></td><td>Hyperventilation, hypothermia, falling cardiac output</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gradual rise</strong></td><td>Hypoventilation, rising temperature, <strong>malignant hyperthermia</strong>, CO<sub>2</sub> insufflation (laparoscopy), sepsis, tourniquet release</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Raised baseline (inspired CO<sub>2</sub>)</strong></td><td><strong>Rebreathing</strong>: exhausted soda lime, faulty expiratory valve, inadequate FGF in a Mapleson system</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sloping \"shark fin\" upstroke</strong></td><td><strong>Bronchospasm</strong>, COPD, kinked or obstructed tube</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cleft (\"curare cleft\") in plateau</strong></td><td>Spontaneous breathing efforts against the ventilator (returning neuromuscular function)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oscillations on plateau</strong></td><td>Cardiogenic oscillations</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"capnography-in-practice\">Capnography in practice</h2>\n<p>Mandatory for all general anaesthetics and for moderate and deep sedation, in recovery until the airway is removed, and for <strong>all intubated patients</strong> (in ICU, ED and transfer). In cardiac arrest it confirms tube placement, shows CPR quality and can signal return of spontaneous circulation (a sudden rise).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> infrared principle; phases; causes of each abnormal trace; the arterial–end-tidal gap.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> after intubation the capnograph shows three small waveforms and then a flat line: what has happened and what do you do?</li></ul></div>","cardiac-drugs":"<h2 id=\"ischaemic-heart-disease\">Ischaemic heart disease</h2>\n<p><strong>Goal:</strong> improve the oxygen supply–demand balance (reduce heart rate, preload, afterload and contractility; increase coronary flow) and prevent thrombosis.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Nitrates</strong> (GTN, isosorbide mononitrate)</td><td>NO donors → cGMP → venodilation (↓ preload, wall stress), coronary dilation</td><td>Tolerance with continuous use; headache; hypotension with PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>β-blockers</strong></td><td>↓ Heart rate and contractility; longer diastole</td><td>Continue perioperatively</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Calcium channel blockers</strong></td><td>Vasodilation (dihydropyridines); rate control (verapamil, diltiazem)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nicorandil</strong></td><td>K<sub>ATP</sub> opener with nitrate activity</td><td>Ulceration (mouth, GI)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ivabradine</strong></td><td>Blocks I<sub>f</sub> (funny current) in the SA node → slows rate without negative inotropy</td><td>Sinus rhythm only</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ranolazine</strong></td><td>Late Na<sup>+</sup> current inhibitor</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antiplatelets</strong></td><td>Aspirin; P2Y<sub>12</sub> inhibitors (clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor)</td><td><strong>Dual antiplatelet therapy after coronary stents</strong>: do not stop without cardiology advice (stent thrombosis risk)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Statins</strong></td><td>HMG-CoA reductase inhibition; plaque stabilisation</td><td>Continue perioperatively</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"heart-failure-with-reduced-ejection-fraction\">Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction</h2>\n<p>The mainstays that improve survival (NICE NG106, ESC):</p>\n<ul><li><strong>ACE inhibitor</strong> (or ARB) or <strong>sacubitril/valsartan</strong> (neprilysin inhibitor + ARB: raises natriuretic peptides; vasodilation — expect hypotension);</li><li><strong>β-blocker</strong> (bisoprolol, carvedilol);</li><li><strong>mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist</strong> (spironolactone, eplerenone: hyperkalaemia);</li><li><strong>SGLT2 inhibitor</strong> (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin): risk of <strong>euglycaemic ketoacidosis</strong> perioperatively — usually withheld for a period before surgery according to current guidance;</li><li><strong>loop diuretics</strong> for congestion; digoxin, ivabradine and devices (CRT, ICD) for selected patients.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"perioperative-implications\">Perioperative implications</h2>\n<ul><li>Continue β-blockers and statins; do not start high-dose β-blockers de novo just before surgery.</li><li>Plan for vasodilator-induced hypotension (ACEi/ARB/ARNI) and fluid balance issues (diuretics).</li><li>Check potassium and renal function.</li><li>For patients with stents or recent MI, timing of elective surgery and antiplatelet management need cardiology and surgical agreement (ESC 2022 guidance).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms of each antianginal; drugs that improve prognosis in heart failure; SGLT2 inhibitor risks.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> preassessment of a patient on dual antiplatelet therapy after a stent six months ago who needs a cholecystectomy.</li></ul></div>","cardiac-output-measurement":"<h2 id=\"fick-principle\">Fick principle</h2>\n<ul><li>The amount of a substance taken up by an organ per unit time equals the arterial–venous difference × blood flow.</li><li><strong>CO = VO<sub>2</sub> / (CaO<sub>2</sub> − CvO<sub>2</sub>).</strong> Example: 250 mL/min / (200 − 150 mL/L) = 5 L/min.</li><li>Requires mixed venous blood (pulmonary artery) and measurement of oxygen consumption; the reference method in a steady state. Indirect Fick using CO<sub>2</sub> (partial rebreathing) is also used.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"indicator-dilution-stewart-hamilton\">Indicator dilution (Stewart–Hamilton)</h2>\n<ul><li>A known amount of indicator is injected and its concentration measured downstream over time: <strong>CO = amount of indicator / area under the concentration–time curve</strong> (∫C dt). Recirculation must be excluded (extrapolate the curve).</li><li><strong>Thermodilution (pulmonary artery catheter):</strong> cold saline injected into the right atrium; a <strong>thermistor</strong> at the catheter tip measures the temperature change in the pulmonary artery. <strong>A smaller area under the curve = a higher cardiac output.</strong> Errors: tricuspid regurgitation, intracardiac shunts, injection technique. Continuous versions use a heating filament.</li><li><strong>Transpulmonary thermodilution</strong> (e.g. PiCCO): central venous injection, detection in a femoral arterial line; calibrates pulse contour analysis and gives extravascular lung water and global end-diastolic volume.</li><li><strong>Lithium dilution</strong> (LiDCO): lithium injected, detected by an ion-selective arterial electrode; calibrates pulse power analysis. Not with lithium therapy or some neuromuscular blockers (interference).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"arterial-waveform-analysis\">Arterial waveform analysis</h2>\n<p>Stroke volume is estimated from the shape and area of the arterial pressure waveform (pulse contour or pulse power), either calibrated (PiCCO, LiDCO) or uncalibrated (population-based algorithms, e.g. FloTrac). Less accurate with arrhythmias, aortic regurgitation, damping problems, and rapid changes in vascular tone. Also give pulse pressure and stroke volume variation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"doppler-and-echocardiography\">Doppler and echocardiography</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oesophageal Doppler:</strong> a probe in the distal oesophagus measures descending aortic blood velocity; <strong>stroke distance</strong> (velocity–time integral) × aortic cross-sectional area (from a nomogram) = stroke volume (only about 70% of cardiac output goes to the descending aorta; the algorithm corrects). <strong>Corrected flow time</strong> relates to preload; peak velocity relates to contractility. Widely used for goal-directed fluid therapy.</li><li><strong>Echocardiography (TTE, TOE):</strong> stroke volume from the LVOT velocity–time integral × LVOT area; also shows function, filling and valves.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-methods\">Other methods</h2>\n<p>Thoracic electrical bioimpedance and bioreactance (changes in thoracic impedance with blood flow), and CO<sub>2</sub> rebreathing (indirect Fick).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate CO by Fick; the Stewart–Hamilton equation and thermodilution curves; Doppler principles.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing a cardiac output monitor for a high-risk laparotomy and interpreting its numbers.</li></ul></div>","cell-physiology":"<h2 id=\"cells-and-homeostasis\">Cells and homeostasis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Homeostasis</strong> (Claude Bernard's <em>milieu intérieur</em>; Cannon): the maintenance of a stable internal environment by negative feedback (sensor → integrator → effector).</li><li><strong>Organelles:</strong> nucleus (DNA; transcription to mRNA), ribosomes and rough ER (protein synthesis; translation), smooth ER (lipid synthesis, drug metabolism: the \"microsomal\" enzymes), Golgi (packaging), mitochondria (oxidative phosphorylation; own DNA, inherited maternally), lysosomes, peroxisomes, cytoskeleton.</li><li><strong>Gene expression:</strong> DNA → (transcription) → mRNA → (translation) → protein, regulated by transcription factors; steroid and thyroid hormones act here.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-cell-membrane\">The cell membrane</h2>\n<ul><li>A <strong>phospholipid bilayer</strong> with cholesterol and proteins (the fluid mosaic model): lipid-soluble substances (O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, anaesthetic agents) cross easily; ions and polar molecules need channels or carriers.</li><li>Cell junctions: tight junctions (barriers such as the blood–brain barrier), gap junctions (electrical coupling in cardiac and smooth muscle), desmosomes (mechanical strength).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"transport-across-membranes\">Transport across membranes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Energy</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Simple diffusion</td><td>None; down the gradient (Fick's law)</td><td>O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, lipid-soluble drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Facilitated diffusion</td><td>None; carrier or channel; saturable</td><td>Glucose via GLUT transporters</td></tr><tr><td>Primary active transport</td><td>ATP directly</td><td><strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase</strong>, H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase, Ca<sup>2+</sup>-ATPase, P-glycoprotein</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary active transport</td><td>Uses a gradient set up by primary transport</td><td>SGLT (Na<sup>+</sup>–glucose), Na<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> exchange, Na<sup>+</sup>/Ca<sup>2+</sup> exchange</td></tr><tr><td>Endocytosis / exocytosis</td><td>ATP</td><td>Neurotransmitter release</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Fick's law:</strong> rate of diffusion ∝ (area × concentration gradient × solubility) / (thickness × √molecular weight) — the square-root term is Graham's law.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"receptors\">Receptors</h2>\n<p>Drugs and hormones act through four main receptor families (detail in <a href=\"#notes/pd-receptors\">Pharmacodynamics</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Speed</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Ligand-gated ion channels</strong> (ionotropic)</td><td>Milliseconds</td><td>Nicotinic ACh, GABA<sub>A</sub>, NMDA, 5-HT<sub>3</sub></td></tr><tr><td><strong>G-protein coupled receptors</strong> (seven transmembrane domains)</td><td>Seconds</td><td>Adrenoceptors, muscarinic, opioid. G<sub>s</sub> ↑ cAMP; G<sub>i</sub> ↓ cAMP; G<sub>q</sub> → phospholipase C → IP<sub>3</sub> and DAG</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Enzyme-linked</strong> (tyrosine kinase)</td><td>Minutes</td><td>Insulin, growth factors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intracellular (nuclear)</strong></td><td>Hours</td><td>Steroid and thyroid hormones: alter gene transcription</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Second-messenger cascades <strong>amplify</strong> the signal: one occupied receptor can activate many G-proteins and generate many cAMP molecules.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"enzymes\">Enzymes</h2>\n<ul><li>Biological catalysts that lower activation energy; activity depends on temperature, pH and cofactors.</li><li><strong>Michaelis–Menten:</strong> rate = V<sub>max</sub>[S] / (K<sub>m</sub> + [S]). K<sub>m</sub> is the substrate concentration at half V<sub>max</sub>. At low [S] the rate is proportional to [S] (<strong>first-order</strong>); when saturated it is constant (<strong>zero-order</strong>), e.g. ethanol, phenytoin and high-dose aspirin.</li><li>Inhibition: competitive (raises apparent K<sub>m</sub>), non-competitive (lowers V<sub>max</sub>), irreversible (organophosphates, aspirin on COX).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"protective-mechanisms\">Protective mechanisms</h2>\n<p>Skin and mucosal barriers, mucociliary clearance, gastric acid, inflammation, the immune system, coagulation, and pain as a warning signal.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> transport types with examples; Fick's law; receptor families and their second messengers; Michaelis–Menten and zero-order kinetics.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why phenytoin levels can rise disproportionately with a small dose increase.</li></ul></div>","cns-drugs":"<h2 id=\"antiepileptics\">Antiepileptics</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Anaesthetic relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Phenytoin</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup> channel block</td><td><strong>Enzyme inducer</strong>; zero-order kinetics; arrhythmias and hypotension with rapid IV; resistance to NMBs with chronic use</td></tr><tr><td>Carbamazepine</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup> channel block</td><td>Enzyme inducer; hyponatraemia</td></tr><tr><td>Sodium valproate</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup> channels, ↑ GABA</td><td>Enzyme inhibitor; thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction; teratogenic</td></tr><tr><td>Levetiracetam</td><td>SV2A binding</td><td>Few interactions; IV form (useful perioperatively)</td></tr><tr><td>Lamotrigine</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup> channel block</td><td>Rash</td></tr><tr><td>Gabapentin, pregabalin</td><td>α<sub>2</sub>δ subunit of Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</td><td>Also for neuropathic pain; sedation, respiratory depression with opioids</td></tr><tr><td>Benzodiazepines</td><td>GABA<sub>A</sub></td><td>First-line in status epilepticus (then levetiracetam, phenytoin or valproate, then anaesthesia)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Continue antiepileptics perioperatively (omitting doses risks seizures); many anaesthetic drugs lower (propofol, thiopental, benzodiazepines) or occasionally raise (high-dose local anaesthetics, pethidine's metabolite, sevoflurane at high concentration, tramadol) the seizure threshold.</p>\n<h2 id=\"antidepressants\">Antidepressants</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>SSRIs</strong> (sertraline, citalopram, fluoxetine): hyponatraemia (SIADH), platelet dysfunction and bleeding, <strong>serotonin syndrome</strong> with tramadol, pethidine, fentanyl (rarely), methylene blue, ondansetron (rarely), linezolid. Citalopram prolongs QT.</li><li><strong>Tricyclics</strong> (amitriptyline): anticholinergic, α-blocking, Na<sup>+</sup> channel blocking; <strong>overdose → wide QRS, arrhythmias, seizures, hypotension</strong> (treat with sodium bicarbonate); interactions with sympathomimetics.</li><li><strong>MAOIs</strong> (phenelzine, tranylcypromine; moclobemide is reversible): <strong>hypertensive crisis with indirect sympathomimetics</strong> (ephedrine, metaraminol partly) and tyramine; <strong>serotonin syndrome with pethidine and tramadol</strong> (and possibly other serotonergic drugs). Use direct-acting vasopressors in reduced doses; decisions about stopping should be made with psychiatric input.</li><li><strong>SNRIs</strong> (venlafaxine, duloxetine), mirtazapine.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Serotonin syndrome:</strong> agitation, confusion, <strong>clonus and hyperreflexia</strong>, tremor, hyperthermia, sweating, tachycardia, diarrhoea. Stop serotonergic drugs; supportive care; benzodiazepines; cyproheptadine; cooling. (Distinguish from neuroleptic malignant syndrome — rigidity, slower onset, dopamine antagonists — and malignant hyperthermia.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"antipsychotics-and-lithium\">Antipsychotics and lithium</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Antipsychotics:</strong> sedation, hypotension (α-blockade), QT prolongation, extrapyramidal effects, <strong>neuroleptic malignant syndrome</strong> (fever, rigidity, autonomic instability, raised CK).</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> narrow therapeutic index; renal excretion (<strong>levels rise with dehydration, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors and thiazides</strong>); potentiates NMBs; nephrogenic DI, hypothyroidism. Consider withholding 24 h before major surgery and restarting when eating and drinking with normal renal function (with psychiatric input).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"parkinson-s-disease-drugs\">Parkinson's disease drugs</h2>\n<p>Levodopa (with a dopa-decarboxylase inhibitor), dopamine agonists, MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline), COMT inhibitors. <strong>Do not miss doses</strong> (risk of rigidity, dysphagia, aspiration, neuroleptic malignant-like syndrome): use a nasogastric route or a <strong>rotigotine patch</strong> if nil by mouth. <strong>Avoid metoclopramide, droperidol and haloperidol</strong>; use ondansetron or domperidone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cns-stimulants\">CNS stimulants</h2>\n<p>Amphetamines and methylphenidate (ADHD), modafinil: sympathomimetic, raised anaesthetic requirement acutely; interactions with vasopressors.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> enzyme inducers among antiepileptics; features of serotonin syndrome; lithium toxicity triggers; MAOI interactions.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient on phenelzine needs urgent surgery: which drugs will you avoid and why?</li></ul></div>","consent-capacity-safeguarding":"<h2 id=\"consent\">Consent</h2>\n<ul><li>Valid consent is <strong>voluntary</strong>, given by a person with <strong>capacity</strong>, who is <strong>adequately informed</strong>. It is a <strong>process</strong> (a dialogue), not a signature. Anaesthetists usually record consent in the anaesthetic record rather than on a separate form.</li><li><strong>Material risks (Montgomery, 2015):</strong> disclose risks that <strong>a reasonable person in the patient's position</strong> would want to know, or that <strong>this particular patient</strong> would attach significance to; discuss reasonable alternatives (including doing nothing). Use accessible numbers and patient information (e.g. RCoA leaflets).</li><li>Consent can be withdrawn at any time. Emergencies: treat in the patient's best interests if they lack capacity and consent cannot wait.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"capacity-mental-capacity-act-2005-england-and-wales\">Capacity (Mental Capacity Act 2005, England and Wales)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Presume capacity</strong>; do not treat someone as lacking capacity because they make an unwise decision; take all practicable steps to help them decide.</li><li><strong>Two-stage test:</strong> is there an impairment or disturbance of the mind or brain? If so, does it mean the person <strong>cannot understand, retain, use or weigh</strong> the relevant information, or <strong>communicate</strong> their decision?</li><li>Capacity is <strong>decision-specific and time-specific</strong>.</li><li>If a patient lacks capacity: act in their <strong>best interests</strong> (consider their past and present wishes, beliefs and values, and consult family and carers), choose the <strong>least restrictive</strong> option, and check for an advance decision to refuse treatment or a lasting power of attorney for health and welfare. An <strong>IMCA</strong> is appointed for serious decisions when there is no one else to consult. <strong>Deprivation of Liberty</strong> safeguards may apply.</li><li>Patients with <strong>learning disability, autism or dementia</strong> need reasonable adjustments (e.g. hospital passports, familiar carers, quiet environments, premedication planning).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"children-and-young-people\">Children and young people</h2>\n<p>Those with parental responsibility consent for young children. <strong>Gillick-competent</strong> children under 16 can consent for themselves. Young people of 16–17 are presumed to have capacity (MCA applies from 16). Refusal by a competent young person can in rare situations be overridden by the courts or those with parental responsibility — seek senior and legal advice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"safeguarding\">Safeguarding</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> be alert to signs of non-accidental injury (inconsistent history, delayed presentation, particular injury patterns); follow local child protection procedures — escalate to the safeguarding lead and do not investigate yourself.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> the principles are <strong>empowerment, prevention, proportionality, protection, partnership and accountability</strong>. Recognise abuse and neglect (physical, sexual, financial, psychological, neglect, domestic abuse, modern slavery) and refer.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"professional-duties\">Professional duties</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Good Medical Practice (GMC):</strong> knowledge and skills, safety and quality, communication and partnership, and trust.</li><li><strong>Duty of candour:</strong> be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong, apologise, explain and support; organisations have a statutory duty of candour for notifiable safety incidents.</li><li><strong>Raising concerns:</strong> through the line manager, clinical governance, the Freedom to Speak Up Guardian, or the GMC; you are protected when raising concerns in good faith.</li><li><strong>Governance and data:</strong> incident reporting, audit, complaints, clinical guidelines; data protection (UK GDPR, Caldicott principles, confidentiality).</li><li><strong>Employment law:</strong> contracts, the Working Time Regulations (average 48-hour week with opt-out, rest requirements), rota rules; <strong>equality and diversity</strong> legislation (Equality Act 2010: protected characteristics).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Montgomery; MCA principles and test; Gillick competence; duty of candour.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> consenting a confused elderly patient for a hip fracture repair, involving the family appropriately.</li></ul></div>","critical-incidents":"<h2 id=\"general-approach\">General approach</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Recognise, call for help early, declare the problem</strong>, and use the <strong>Quick Reference Handbook (QRH)</strong> with a reader. Use a structured approach (e.g. <strong>ABCDE</strong>, or a \"COVER ABCD\" check of the circuit, oxygen, ventilation, endotracheal tube, review of monitors and equipment, then airway, breathing, circulation, drugs).</li><li>Allocate roles, share the mental model, verbalise plans, and re-evaluate.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"unexpected-desaturation\">Unexpected desaturation</h2>\n<p><strong>100% O<sub>2</sub></strong> and hand-ventilate to feel compliance. Then work from machine to patient: oxygen supply and analyser, circuit leaks and disconnection, tube position (oesophageal, endobronchial, displaced, blocked, kinked), breathing (bronchospasm, pneumothorax, aspiration, atelectasis, pulmonary oedema), circulation (low cardiac output, PE), equipment (probe position, perfusion). Confirm with capnography.</p>\n<h2 id=\"high-airway-pressure\">High airway pressure</h2>\n<p><strong>Equipment:</strong> kinked or blocked tube or circuit, tube too far in, valve failure. <strong>Patient:</strong> bronchospasm, light anaesthesia and coughing, chest wall rigidity (opioids), pneumothorax, endobronchial intubation, aspiration, abdominal insufflation or steep Trendelenburg. Disconnect and hand-ventilate; pass a suction catheter down the tube.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hypotension\">Hypotension</h2>\n<p>Rate (brady- or tachyarrhythmia), preload (hypovolaemia, bleeding, IPPV, aortocaval compression, pneumothorax, tamponade, embolism), afterload (anaesthetic vasodilation, neuraxial block, sepsis, anaphylaxis), contractility (ischaemia, drugs, acidosis). Check the measurement, reduce anaesthetic depth, fluids, vasopressors, and look for the cause.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-scenarios-see-qrh-cards\">Other scenarios (see QRH cards)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> light anaesthesia and pain, hypercapnia, hypoxia, drug error, pre-eclampsia, phaeochromocytoma, raised ICP (Cushing's).</li><li><strong>Arrhythmias:</strong> correct oxygenation, ventilation and electrolytes; treat per RCUK peri-arrest algorithms.</li><li><strong>Convulsions:</strong> exclude hypoxia, hypoglycaemia and local anaesthetic toxicity; benzodiazepine; protect the airway.</li><li><strong>Gas embolism</strong> (e.g. laparoscopy, sitting craniotomy, central lines): sudden fall in ETCO<sub>2</sub>, hypotension, desaturation: flood the field, stop insufflation, 100% O<sub>2</sub>, left lateral head-down, aspirate via CVC, support circulation. <strong>Fat embolism</strong> after long-bone fractures; <strong>bone cement implantation syndrome</strong>.</li><li><strong>Inadvertent intra-arterial injection:</strong> leave the cannula in, flush with saline, analgesia, consider heparin and sympathetic block, seek vascular advice.</li><li><strong>Accidental decannulation of a tracheostomy</strong> or extubation in the prone position: follow the relevant algorithms.</li><li><strong>Raised ICP / brainstem herniation:</strong> head-up, hyperventilate briefly, osmotherapy, urgent neurosurgical input.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"afterwards\">Afterwards</h2>\n<p>Debrief the team; tell the patient what happened and apologise (<strong>duty of candour</strong>); document fully; report through the local incident system; discuss at morbidity and mortality meetings; arrange follow-up and support for staff.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> causes of each abnormal monitor reading; first actions.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a SimMan scenario — you are called to theatre where the SpO<sub>2</sub> is 82% and airway pressure is high; lead the team, then debrief.</li></ul></div>","cvs-bp-control":"<h2 id=\"the-basics\">The basics</h2>\n<p><strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> (strictly, MAP − CVP = CO × SVR). MAP ≈ diastolic + ⅓ pulse pressure at normal heart rates.</p>\n<h2 id=\"short-term-the-baroreceptor-reflex-seconds\">Short-term: the baroreceptor reflex (seconds)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>High-pressure baroreceptors</strong> in the <strong>carotid sinus</strong> (glossopharyngeal nerve, IX) and <strong>aortic arch</strong> (vagus, X) fire in proportion to stretch.</li><li>Signals go to the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius</strong>; a fall in pressure reduces firing → less vagal (parasympathetic) output and more sympathetic output → tachycardia, increased contractility, vasoconstriction and venoconstriction.</li><li>The reflex <strong>resets</strong> within days in chronic hypertension. It is blunted by volatile agents, propofol, age, diabetic autonomic neuropathy and β-blockers.</li><li><strong>Low-pressure receptors</strong> in the atria and great veins respond to volume: stretch releases <strong>ANP</strong> and reduces ADH and sympathetic output. The <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong> is a rise in heart rate with atrial stretch.</li><li><strong>Other short-term inputs:</strong> peripheral <strong>chemoreceptors</strong> (carotid and aortic bodies) raise blood pressure in hypoxia and hypercapnia; pain raises it through the <strong>somatosympathetic reflex</strong>.</li><li><strong>Local endothelial control:</strong> <strong>nitric oxide</strong> (from L-arginine by NO synthase; the main tonic vasodilator) and prostacyclin dilate; <strong>endothelin-1</strong> is the most potent endogenous vasoconstrictor.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"medium-and-long-term-minutes-to-days\">Medium and long term (minutes to days)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system:</strong> renal hypoperfusion, sympathetic β<sub>1</sub> stimulation and low distal tubular sodium release renin → angiotensin II (vasoconstriction, thirst, ADH and aldosterone release) → sodium and water retention.</li><li><strong>ADH (vasopressin):</strong> released for raised osmolality and (at larger volume losses) hypovolaemia; V<sub>1</sub> receptors cause vasoconstriction, V<sub>2</sub> receptors retain water.</li><li><strong>Pressure natriuresis:</strong> the kidney's long-term set point for pressure.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-valsalva-manoeuvre-forced-expiration-against-a-closed-glottis-40-mmhg\">The Valsalva manoeuvre (forced expiration against a closed glottis, ~40 mmHg)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Blood pressure</th><th>Heart rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I (onset)</td><td>Brief <strong>rise</strong> (raised intrathoracic pressure is transmitted to the aorta and pushes pulmonary venous blood into the left heart)</td><td>Reflex slight fall</td></tr><tr><td>II (strain)</td><td><strong>Falls</strong> as venous return drops, then partly recovers with sympathetic vasoconstriction</td><td><strong>Rises</strong></td></tr><tr><td>III (release)</td><td>Brief further fall (sudden drop in intrathoracic pressure)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>IV (recovery)</td><td><strong>Overshoot</strong> as restored cardiac output meets constricted vessels</td><td>Reflex <strong>bradycardia</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A <strong>square-wave response</strong> (BP rises and stays up with no overshoot or heart rate change) occurs in heart failure; loss of the phase IV overshoot and bradycardia indicates <strong>autonomic neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"response-to-haemorrhage\">Response to haemorrhage</h2>\n<ul><li>Baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction (skin, gut, kidney) preserve flow to the brain and heart; venoconstriction mobilises blood from venous reservoirs.</li><li><strong>Transcapillary refill:</strong> lower capillary hydrostatic pressure draws interstitial fluid into the vessels (can restore about 0.5–1 L over hours).</li><li>Hormonal: catecholamines, ADH, RAAS, cortisol. Erythropoiesis restores red cells over weeks.</li><li>Blood pressure is often maintained until about 30% of blood volume is lost in healthy young patients, so a normal BP does not exclude major haemorrhage. Children and fit young adults may decompensate abruptly; β-blockers mask tachycardia.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> baroreceptor nerves; phases of the Valsalva; which hormones act on which receptors.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a bleeding trauma patient with a \"normal\" blood pressure: explain the compensation and why you are worried.</li></ul></div>","cvs-cardiac-ap":"<h2 id=\"ventricular-myocyte-action-potential\">Ventricular myocyte action potential</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Event</th><th>Main current</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>Resting potential <strong>about −90 mV</strong></td><td>K<sup>+</sup> efflux through inward rectifier (I<sub>K1</sub>) channels</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>Rapid depolarisation</td><td>Fast <strong>Na<sup>+</sup></strong> influx (voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels)</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Early partial repolarisation</td><td>Transient K<sup>+</sup> outflow (I<sub>to</sub>)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Plateau</strong> (~200 ms)</td><td><strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup> influx through L-type channels</strong> balanced by K<sup>+</sup> efflux</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Repolarisation</td><td>Delayed rectifier K<sup>+</sup> currents (I<sub>Kr</sub>, I<sub>Ks</sub>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The long plateau gives a long <strong>absolute refractory period</strong>, so cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised and has time to relax and refill.</li><li><strong>Hyperkalaemia</strong> makes the resting potential less negative, inactivating some Na<sup>+</sup> channels, slowing conduction (wide QRS) and eventually causing ventricular fibrillation or asystole.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pacemaker-sa-node-action-potential\">Pacemaker (SA node) action potential</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>No stable resting potential.</strong> Phase 4 is a slow diastolic depolarisation from about −60 mV, due mainly to the <strong>\"funny\" current (I<sub>f</sub>, HCN channels; Na<sup>+</sup> and K<sup>+</sup>)</strong> plus T-type Ca<sup>2+</sup> current.</li><li><strong>Phase 0</strong> in nodal tissue is caused by <strong>L-type Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> influx (slower upstroke), not Na<sup>+</sup>.</li><li>Sympathetic stimulation (β<sub>1</sub>, cAMP) steepens phase 4 → faster rate. Vagal stimulation (M<sub>2</sub>, ACh → more K<sup>+</sup> conductance) hyperpolarises and flattens phase 4 → slower rate.</li><li><strong>Hierarchy of pacemakers:</strong> SA node 60–100/min, AV node 40–60/min, ventricular (Purkinje) 20–40/min.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"conduction\">Conduction</h2>\n<p>SA node → atria → <strong>AV node</strong> (slow conduction, about 0.1 s delay, allowing ventricular filling; the delay comes from small cells with few gap junctions and a Ca<sup>2+</sup>-dependent upstroke. Elsewhere the atria and ventricles are electrically insulated by the fibrous skeleton, so the AV node is the only normal route) → bundle of His → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres (fastest conduction, about 4 m/s).</p>\n<h2 id=\"excitation-contraction-coupling\">Excitation–contraction coupling</h2>\n<ul><li>The action potential spreads down <strong>T-tubules</strong> (in cardiac muscle short, wide and at the Z-lines, each meeting one SR cisterna as a <em>diad</em>; skeletal muscle has <em>triads</em>); L-type channels (dihydropyridine receptors) let in a small amount of Ca<sup>2+</sup>, which triggers a much larger release from the <strong>sarcoplasmic reticulum via ryanodine receptors (RyR2)</strong>: <strong>calcium-induced calcium release</strong>.</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds <strong>troponin C</strong>, moving tropomyosin to expose myosin-binding sites on actin; cross-bridges cycle using ATP.</li><li>Relaxation: Ca<sup>2+</sup> is pumped back into the SR by <strong>SERCA</strong> (regulated by phospholamban) and expelled by the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/Ca<sup>2+</sup> exchanger</strong>.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong> inhibits Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase, raising intracellular Na<sup>+</sup> and hence Ca<sup>2+</sup> (reduced exchange) → positive inotropy.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ions for each phase; why nodal tissue has a slow upstroke; effect of potassium on the ECG.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining how a drug (atropine, a β-blocker, amiodarone) changes heart rate or rhythm, from the ion channel up.</li></ul></div>","cvs-cardiac-cycle":"<h2 id=\"phases-left-ventricle\">Phases (left ventricle)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> mitral valve closes (S1); pressure rises with no change in volume until it exceeds aortic pressure.</li><li><strong>Ejection:</strong> aortic valve opens; rapid then reduced ejection. Stroke volume ~70 mL from an end-diastolic volume of ~120 mL (<strong>ejection fraction ≈ 60%</strong>).</li><li><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> aortic valve closes (S2, with the dicrotic notch on the arterial trace); pressure falls with no change in volume.</li><li><strong>Filling:</strong> mitral valve opens; rapid passive filling, diastasis, then <strong>atrial systole</strong> (about 20% of filling at rest, rising towards 40% in tachycardia when passive filling time is short; more important in the stiff or hypertrophied ventricle and lost in atrial fibrillation).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Heart rate:</strong> as rate rises, <strong>diastole shortens much more than systole</strong>. Filling time falls, and so does coronary perfusion of the left ventricle, which happens mainly in diastole.</p>\n<p><strong>Heart sounds:</strong> S1 (AV valves close), S2 (semilunar valves close; physiological splitting on inspiration). <strong>S3</strong> in early rapid filling: normal in the young and in pregnancy, otherwise a sign of a dilated, volume-loaded ventricle. <strong>S4</strong> with atrial systole into a <strong>stiff ventricle</strong> (hypertrophy, ischaemia); absent in AF.</p>\n<p><strong>The CVP waveform:</strong> a wave (atrial contraction), c wave (tricuspid bulging during isovolumetric contraction), x descent (atrial relaxation and descent of the valve ring), v wave (atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve), y descent (tricuspid opening). Cannon a waves occur in complete heart block and junctional rhythms; large v waves in tricuspid regurgitation; absent a waves in AF.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-pressure-volume-loop\">The pressure–volume loop</h2>\n<ul><li>Plotted with <strong>LV volume on the x-axis and pressure on the y-axis</strong>; the loop runs anticlockwise.</li><li>Bottom-left corner = mitral valve opens; bottom-right = mitral closes (end-diastolic point); top-right = aortic opens; top-left = aortic closes (end-systolic point).</li><li><strong>Width = stroke volume.</strong> <strong>Area = stroke work.</strong></li><li>The <strong>end-systolic pressure–volume relationship (ESPVR)</strong> is the line joining end-systolic points under different loading: its slope (<strong>end-systolic elastance</strong>) is a relatively load-independent index of <strong>contractility</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>end-diastolic pressure–volume relationship (EDPVR)</strong> describes passive filling: a steeper curve means a stiffer ventricle (diastolic dysfunction).</li><li><strong>Arterial elastance</strong> (Ea ≈ end-systolic pressure / stroke volume) represents afterload.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect on the loop</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>↑ Preload</td><td>Wider loop; end-diastolic point moves right along the EDPVR</td></tr><tr><td>↑ Afterload</td><td>Taller, narrower loop; smaller stroke volume</td></tr><tr><td>↑ Contractility</td><td>Steeper ESPVR; wider loop with smaller end-systolic volume</td></tr><tr><td>Aortic stenosis</td><td>Very high systolic pressures; steep EDPVR (hypertrophy)</td></tr><tr><td>Mitral regurgitation</td><td>No true isovolumetric phases; large end-diastolic volume</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> identify points on a loop; what the ESPVR slope means; ejection fraction and load-dependence; the effect of heart rate on diastolic time; S3 and S4.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> draw a PV loop and show the effect of a vasopressor, a fluid bolus and an inotrope; explain why ejection fraction is load-dependent.</li></ul></div>","cvs-cardiac-output":"<h2 id=\"determinants\">Determinants</h2>\n<p><strong>CO = HR × SV.</strong> Resting adult CO ≈ 5 L/min; cardiac index ≈ 2.5–4 L/min/m<sup>2</sup>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Preload:</strong> ventricular wall stretch at end-diastole (approximated by end-diastolic volume; pressures such as CVP are poor surrogates).</li><li><strong>Afterload:</strong> the wall stress the ventricle must generate during ejection. <strong>Laplace:</strong> wall stress ∝ <strong>P × r / (2 × wall thickness)</strong>, so a dilated ventricle faces higher afterload at the same pressure, and hypertrophy reduces wall stress.</li><li><strong>Contractility:</strong> the intrinsic strength of contraction at a given preload and afterload; increased by sympathetic stimulation and inotropes, reduced by ischaemia, acidosis, hypoxia and many anaesthetic drugs.</li><li><strong>Heart rate:</strong> increasing rate raises output until diastolic filling time (and coronary perfusion time) becomes limiting; the <strong>Bowditch (staircase) effect</strong> gives a small inotropic effect with higher rates.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"frank-starling-mechanism\">Frank–Starling mechanism</h2>\n<ul><li>Within physiological limits, <strong>greater end-diastolic fibre length → greater force of contraction</strong> (optimal actin–myosin overlap and increased calcium sensitivity of troponin).</li><li>Starling curves: stroke volume against preload. Sympathetic stimulation and inotropes shift the curve up and left; heart failure shifts it down and right.</li><li>On the steep part, a fluid bolus raises stroke volume (<strong>fluid responsive</strong>); on the flat part it only raises filling pressures and causes oedema.</li><li><strong>Anrep effect:</strong> a sudden rise in afterload first reduces stroke volume, but contractility then increases over a few minutes, partly restoring it (homeometric autoregulation).</li><li><strong>Ventriculo-arterial coupling:</strong> stroke volume depends on the match between ventricular elastance (E<sub>es</sub>) and arterial elastance (E<sub>a</sub>); mechanical efficiency is best at roughly E<sub>es</sub> ≈ 2 × E<sub>a</sub>. See the <a href=\"#notes/cvs-cardiac-cycle\">pressure–volume loop</a>.</li><li>Moment-to-moment control of rate and resistance is by the <a href=\"#notes/cvs-bp-control\">baroreceptor reflex</a>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"venous-return-guyton\">Venous return (Guyton)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Venous return = (mean systemic filling pressure − right atrial pressure) / resistance to venous return.</strong> Mean systemic filling pressure is about 7 mmHg; about 70% of blood volume is in the veins.</li><li>At steady state, cardiac output equals venous return. Venoconstriction (sympathetic tone, vasopressors) raises mean systemic filling pressure and venous return; anaesthetic vasodilation lowers it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-fluid-challenge\">The fluid challenge</h2>\n<ul><li>Give a defined bolus (for example 250 mL crystalloid over 5–10 minutes) and measure a <strong>stroke volume</strong> response (a rise of 10% or more is commonly used) rather than relying on blood pressure or CVP.</li><li>Dynamic indices (pulse pressure or stroke volume variation during controlled ventilation, passive leg raising) predict response better than static pressures, within their limitations.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Laplace and afterload; Starling curves; the Guyton venous return equation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"This patient's blood pressure is 80/40 after induction. Talk me through the causes in terms of preload, afterload, contractility and rate, and what you would do.\"</li></ul></div>","cvs-fetal-circulation":"<h2 id=\"the-fetal-circulation\">The fetal circulation</h2>\n<ul><li>Oxygenated blood leaves the <strong>placenta</strong> in the <strong>umbilical vein</strong> (PO<sub>2</sub> only about 4 kPa, but saturation about 80% thanks to <strong>fetal haemoglobin</strong>, which has a <strong>P50 of about 2.5 kPa</strong> and does not bind 2,3-DPG well).</li><li><strong>Ductus venosus:</strong> about half of umbilical venous blood bypasses the liver to the IVC.</li><li><strong>Foramen ovale:</strong> the better-oxygenated IVC stream is directed across the atrial septum to the left atrium → left ventricle → <strong>brain and coronary arteries</strong>.</li><li><strong>Ductus arteriosus:</strong> most right ventricular output bypasses the high-resistance fluid-filled lungs from the pulmonary artery to the descending aorta.</li><li>Deoxygenated blood returns to the placenta in the <strong>two umbilical arteries</strong>.</li><li>Pulmonary vascular resistance is <strong>high</strong> and systemic (placental) resistance <strong>low</strong> before birth.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-placenta\">The placenta</h2>\n<ul><li>Gas exchange by <strong>diffusion</strong>, aided by the double Bohr effect (fetal CO<sub>2</sub> loss increases fetal affinity; maternal CO<sub>2</sub> gain decreases maternal affinity) and the double Haldane effect.</li><li>Transfer of drugs depends on lipid solubility, ionisation, protein binding and molecular size. Most anaesthetic drugs cross; <strong>neuromuscular blockers (quaternary, ionised)</strong> and heparin do not cross to any significant degree. <strong>Ion trapping:</strong> weak bases such as local anaesthetics become more ionised in the more acidic fetus, especially when it is acidotic.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"changes-at-birth\">Changes at birth</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>First breaths</strong> expand the lungs; alveolar PO<sub>2</sub> rises and PVR falls dramatically.</li><li><strong>Clamping the cord</strong> removes the low-resistance placenta, so SVR rises.</li><li>Left atrial pressure now exceeds right, closing the <strong>flap of the foramen ovale</strong> (functional closure at once; anatomical closure over months; probe-patent in about a quarter of adults).</li><li>The <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> constricts within hours to days in response to higher PO<sub>2</sub> and falling prostaglandins (functional closure in 1–3 days). NSAIDs such as indometacin or ibuprofen promote closure; prostaglandin E infusions keep it open in duct-dependent congenital heart disease.</li><li>The <strong>ductus venosus</strong> closes over days.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Transitional circulation:</strong> hypoxia, acidosis, hypothermia or sepsis in the neonate can raise PVR again and reopen right-to-left shunting (persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the three shunts and their fate; fetal haemoglobin P50; which drugs cross the placenta.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining to a colleague why avoiding hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypothermia matters in a neonate having surgery.</li></ul></div>","cvs-microcirculation":"<h2 id=\"structure\">Structure</h2>\n<p>Arterioles (the main site of <strong>resistance</strong>, under neural, humoral and local control) → capillaries (exchange) → venules. Precapillary sphincters and arteriolar tone control how many capillaries are perfused; in exercising muscle, recruitment can increase flow many times over.</p>\n<h2 id=\"starling-s-equation\">Starling's equation</h2>\n<p><strong>Jv = Kf [(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Jv: net fluid filtration; Kf: filtration coefficient (surface area × permeability).</li><li>Pc and Pi: capillary and interstitial hydrostatic pressures.</li><li>πc and πi: plasma and interstitial oncotic pressures (plasma about 25 mmHg, mainly <strong>albumin</strong>).</li><li>σ: the reflection coefficient for protein (1 = impermeable, 0 = freely permeable).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>The revised Starling principle:</strong> the endothelial <strong>glycocalyx</strong> (a gel layer of glycoproteins and proteoglycans on the luminal surface) means the relevant oncotic gradient is between plasma and the small space just beneath the glycocalyx, not the whole interstitium. In most capillaries there is <strong>net filtration along the whole length, with little or no reabsorption</strong>; the filtrate returns via <strong>lymph</strong> (about 8 L/day enters the lymphatics; roughly half is reabsorbed in lymph nodes, so about 4 L/day returns to the circulation via the thoracic and right lymphatic ducts). The glycocalyx is damaged by sepsis, surgery, hyperglycaemia and rapid volume loading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"causes-of-oedema\">Causes of oedema</h2>\n<ul><li>↑ capillary hydrostatic pressure: heart failure, venous obstruction, fluid overload.</li><li>↓ plasma oncotic pressure: hypoalbuminaemia (liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition).</li><li>↑ permeability / glycocalyx damage: sepsis, burns, inflammation, ARDS.</li><li>Lymphatic obstruction.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"functions-of-the-endothelium\">Functions of the endothelium</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Vascular tone:</strong> releases <strong>nitric oxide</strong> (from L-arginine by eNOS, raising cGMP in smooth muscle) and <strong>prostacyclin</strong> (vasodilators), and <strong>endothelin-1</strong> (vasoconstrictor).</li><li><strong>Haemostasis:</strong> normally anticoagulant (thrombomodulin, heparan sulphate, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, tPA); becomes procoagulant when damaged (von Willebrand factor, tissue factor).</li><li>Barrier function, inflammation (adhesion molecules), angiogenesis, and metabolism (ACE in the lung).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the Starling equation and its terms; the glycocalyx; nitric oxide synthesis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why a septic patient develops oedema despite a normal albumin, and the implications for fluid therapy.</li></ul></div>","cvs-special-circulations":"<h2 id=\"share-of-cardiac-output-at-rest-approximate\">Share of cardiac output at rest (approximate)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Flow</th><th>% of CO</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Liver and gut</td><td>~1.3–1.5 L/min</td><td>~25–30%</td><td>Dual supply</td></tr><tr><td>Kidneys</td><td>~1.1–1.2 L/min</td><td>~20–25%</td><td>Highest flow per gram</td></tr><tr><td>Brain</td><td>~750 mL/min</td><td>~15%</td><td>~50 mL/100 g/min</td></tr><tr><td>Heart</td><td>~250 mL/min</td><td>~5%</td><td>~70–80 mL/100 g/min</td></tr><tr><td>Muscle</td><td>~1 L/min</td><td>~20%</td><td>Up to 20-fold increase in exercise</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"coronary\">Coronary</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>LV perfusion occurs mainly in diastole</strong> because systolic intramyocardial pressure compresses subendocardial vessels. <strong>Coronary perfusion pressure ≈ aortic diastolic pressure − LVEDP.</strong> The RV is perfused through systole and diastole.</li><li>Myocardial <strong>oxygen extraction is already ~70% at rest</strong>, so extra demand must be met by <strong>increased flow</strong> (metabolic autoregulation: adenosine, NO, low PO<sub>2</sub>, K<sup>+</sup>, CO<sub>2</sub>; opening of <strong>ATP-sensitive K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> as ATP falls is a key vasodilator mechanism). Sympathetic α-constriction is normally overridden by this metabolic dilation.</li><li>Tachycardia shortens diastole (reduces supply) while increasing demand; the subendocardium is most vulnerable.</li><li>Anatomy: left main → LAD and circumflex; RCA supplies the SA node in about 60% and the AV node in about 80–90% (right dominance).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cerebral\">Cerebral</h2>\n<ul><li>Autoregulation keeps flow roughly constant between a <strong>MAP of about 50–150 mmHg</strong> (shifted right in chronic hypertension; impaired after brain injury and by volatile agents at high concentration).</li><li><strong>CO<sub>2</sub> is the most powerful regulator:</strong> flow changes by about <strong>15 mL/100 g/min per kPa</strong> of PaCO<sub>2</sub> (roughly 2–4% per mmHg) between about 3 and 10 kPa. Hypoxia (PaO<sub>2</sub> below ~6.7 kPa) greatly increases flow.</li><li><strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong> (or − CVP if higher).</li><li>Flow–metabolism coupling: flow follows metabolic rate (reduced by propofol, barbiturates and hypothermia).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"renal\">Renal</h2>\n<ul><li>High flow serves filtration, not oxygen need: extraction is low overall, but the <strong>medulla</strong> is relatively hypoxic and vulnerable.</li><li>Autoregulation (MAP about 80–180 mmHg) by the <strong>myogenic response</strong> and <strong>tubuloglomerular feedback</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hepatic\">Hepatic</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Portal vein ~75% of flow</strong> (but ~50% of oxygen) and <strong>hepatic artery ~25% of flow</strong> (~50% of oxygen).</li><li>The <strong>hepatic arterial buffer response</strong>: when portal flow falls, hepatic arterial flow rises (adenosine washout).</li><li>No true autoregulation of portal flow. Flow is reduced by IPPV, PEEP, hypocapnia, sympathetic stimulation and neuraxial blockade with hypotension.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> percentages of cardiac output; coronary perfusion pressure; CO<sub>2</sub> reactivity; hepatic dual supply.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient with ischaemic heart disease becomes tachycardic under anaesthesia: explain why this is dangerous and what you would do.</li></ul></div>","defibrillation-pacing":"<h2 id=\"defibrillators\">Defibrillators</h2>\n<ul><li>A <strong>capacitor</strong> is charged from a DC source (via a transformer and rectifier) and discharged through the patient; an <strong>inductor</strong> shapes and prolongs the discharge. Stored energy = <strong>½CV<sup>2</sup></strong>; not all stored energy is delivered.</li><li>Defibrillation depolarises a <strong>critical mass of myocardium</strong> simultaneously, allowing the SA node to resume control.</li><li><strong>Biphasic</strong> waveforms (current reverses direction) achieve defibrillation at <strong>lower energies</strong> than monophasic and are now standard (typically 120–200 J initially, per manufacturer; RCUK recommends at least 150 J for the first shock with biphasic devices, then fixed or escalating energies).</li><li><strong>Thoracic impedance</strong> (typically 50–150 Ω) reduces current: reduced by correct pad size and position (right infraclavicular and left mid-axillary apex, or anteroposterior), good contact (gel pads), shocking at end-expiration, and firm paddle pressure. Modern devices measure and compensate for impedance.</li><li><strong>Synchronised cardioversion</strong> delivers the shock on the <strong>R wave</strong> to avoid the vulnerable period on the T wave (which could induce VF): used for tachyarrhythmias with a pulse.</li><li><strong>Safety:</strong> remove oxygen sources during shock (fire), no one touching the patient or bed, \"stand clear\" call; minimise pauses in compressions (charge during compressions). <strong>AEDs</strong> analyse rhythm and advise shocks.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pacemakers\">Pacemakers</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Generic code (NBG):</strong> position I <strong>chamber paced</strong> (O, A, V, D); II <strong>chamber sensed</strong> (O, A, V, D); III <strong>response to sensing</strong> (O none, T triggered, I inhibited, D dual); IV <strong>rate modulation</strong> (R); V multisite pacing.</li><li>Example: <strong>DDDR</strong> — dual chamber pacing and sensing, dual response, rate adaptive. <strong>VOO</strong> — asynchronous ventricular pacing (as with a magnet on many pacemakers).</li><li><strong>ICDs</strong> detect and treat VT/VF (antitachycardia pacing, shocks). A <strong>magnet</strong> usually <strong>suspends tachyarrhythmia therapies</strong> in ICDs but <strong>does not</strong> change pacing mode (behaviour varies by device and must be confirmed).</li><li><strong>Perioperative management:</strong> know the device type, indication, pacing dependence and last check; involve the cardiac physiologist; deactivate ICD therapies for surgery with monopolar diathermy above the umbilicus (with external defibrillator pads on); consider reprogramming pacing-dependent patients to asynchronous mode; use bipolar diathermy or keep monopolar current away from the device; recheck the device afterwards. <strong>MRI</strong> needs specific protocols (MR-conditional devices).</li><li><strong>Temporary pacing:</strong> transcutaneous, transvenous or epicardial; threshold testing; microshock precautions.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> capacitor energy; monophasic versus biphasic; impedance; NBG code; magnet effects.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient with an ICD is listed for a laparotomy: plan the device management with the team.</li></ul></div>","diabetes-drugs":"<h2 id=\"insulins\">Insulins</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th>Onset / duration (approx.)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rapid-acting analogues</td><td>Aspart, lispro, glulisine</td><td>10–20 min / 3–5 h</td></tr><tr><td>Short-acting (soluble)</td><td>Actrapid, Humulin S</td><td>30 min / 6–8 h; used IV in VRIII</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate (isophane)</td><td>Humulin I, Insulatard</td><td>1–2 h / 12–18 h</td></tr><tr><td>Long-acting analogues</td><td>Glargine, detemir, degludec</td><td>Flat profile, ~24 h (degludec &gt; 40 h)</td></tr><tr><td>Mixtures</td><td>Novomix 30, Humalog Mix</td><td>Biphasic</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Type 1 diabetes: never stop basal insulin</strong> — without it, ketoacidosis follows within hours. Continue long-acting basal insulin, usually at about <strong>80%</strong> of the dose, around surgery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"non-insulin-drugs\">Non-insulin drugs</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Perioperative risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Metformin</strong></td><td>↓ hepatic gluconeogenesis, ↑ insulin sensitivity</td><td>Lactic acidosis (rare; renal impairment, contrast, hypoperfusion)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sulphonylureas</strong> (gliclazide)</td><td>Close K<sub>ATP</sub> channels → insulin release</td><td><strong>Hypoglycaemia</strong> when fasting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SGLT2 inhibitors</strong> (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin)</td><td>Glycosuria</td><td><strong>Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis</strong> (ketones high, glucose may be near normal) — usually stopped for a defined period before surgery (per current guidance)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GLP-1 receptor agonists</strong> (semaglutide, liraglutide, tirzepatide as a GIP/GLP-1 agonist)</td><td>↑ insulin, ↓ glucagon, <strong>delayed gastric emptying</strong></td><td><strong>Residual gastric contents and aspiration risk</strong> despite fasting; follow current multisociety guidance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DPP-4 inhibitors</strong> (sitagliptin)</td><td>↑ incretins</td><td>Low hypoglycaemia risk</td></tr><tr><td>Pioglitazone</td><td>PPAR-γ agonist</td><td>Fluid retention, heart failure</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"perioperative-principles-association-of-anaesthetists-2015-updated-multidisciplinary-guidance-2025\">Perioperative principles (Association of Anaesthetists 2015; updated multidisciplinary guidance 2025)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Optimise before elective surgery:</strong> the 2015 guidance suggested referral for optimisation if HbA1c was above about <strong>69 mmol/mol</strong> (8.5%); check the current guideline and local pathway.</li><li><strong>Short starvation</strong>, first on the list; aim for <strong>capillary glucose 6–10 mmol/L</strong> (up to 12 acceptable).</li><li><strong>Modify</strong> medication using a <strong>drug-by-drug</strong> protocol; resume when eating.</li><li><strong>Variable-rate IV insulin infusion (VRIII)</strong> with a glucose-containing fluid (and K<sup>+</sup>) for long starvation (more than one missed meal), poorly controlled diabetes, or type 1 patients who cannot use their usual regimen; continue basal insulin.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycaemia (&lt; 4 mmol/L):</strong> treat promptly (IV glucose if unconscious or fasting).</li><li>Watch for <strong>DKA</strong> (type 1 and SGLT2 users) and <strong>hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state</strong>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> insulin types; mechanisms and specific risks of each drug class.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a type 1 diabetic on a basal–bolus regimen is listed for an afternoon hernia repair: write the plan and explain it to them.</li></ul></div>","diabetes-perioperative":"<h2 id=\"before-surgery\">Before surgery</h2>\n<ul><li>Identify diabetes, its type, treatment, control (HbA1c), complications (cardiac, renal, autonomic neuropathy — gastroparesis and cardiovascular instability, \"stiff joint syndrome\" with difficult laryngoscopy) and hypoglycaemia awareness.</li><li>Optimise control where time allows; plan for a <strong>short fast</strong> and a <strong>first-on-the-list</strong> slot; provide written instructions for medication; involve the diabetes team for complex regimens.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"on-the-day\">On the day</h2>\n<ul><li>Check <strong>capillary glucose</strong> on arrival and at least hourly during surgery (more often if on insulin infusion). Aim for about <strong>6–10 mmol/L</strong> (up to 12 acceptable). Check ketones in type 1 diabetes and SGLT2 inhibitor users if unwell or glucose high.</li><li>Medication is adjusted <strong>drug by drug</strong> following the current guideline (e.g. hold sulphonylureas while fasting; continue long-acting basal insulin at a reduced dose; SGLT2 inhibitors are usually stopped beforehand; consider GLP-1 agonist implications for gastric emptying).</li><li><strong>Variable-rate intravenous insulin infusion (VRIII)</strong> when more than one meal will be missed, control is poor, or the usual regimen cannot be used: soluble insulin with a substrate fluid containing glucose and potassium (e.g. glucose–saline with KCl). <strong>Continue basal insulin</strong> alongside a VRIII.</li><li>Avoid excessive dexamethasone doses in poorly controlled patients (hyperglycaemia); watch for hypoglycaemia in recovery.</li><li>Return to the usual regimen when eating and drinking; overlap the first subcutaneous insulin dose before stopping the VRIII.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"emergencies\">Emergencies</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>DKA</strong></th><th><strong>HHS</strong></th><th><strong>Hypoglycaemia</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Features</td><td>Glucose &gt; 11 (or known diabetes; may be normal with SGLT2i), <strong>ketones ≥ 3 mmol/L</strong>, pH &lt; 7.3 or HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> &lt; 15</td><td>Very high glucose (often &gt; 30), hyperosmolality &gt; 320 mOsm/kg, severe dehydration, little ketosis; older type 2 patients</td><td>Glucose &lt; 4 mmol/L; under anaesthesia signs are masked (sweating, tachycardia)</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Fluids, <strong>fixed-rate IV insulin</strong> (0.1 unit/kg/h), potassium, glucose once &lt; 14, treat the cause</td><td>Slow fluid replacement, cautious sodium correction, low-dose insulin later, VTE prophylaxis</td><td>IV glucose (e.g. 75–100 mL of 20% glucose) if fasting or unconscious; oral carbohydrate if able; review the cause</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> target glucose; VRIII principles; DKA versus HHS; SGLT2 inhibitor risks.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> manage a type 2 diabetic on metformin and gliclazide listed for an afternoon hernia repair, and explain it to the patient.</li></ul></div>","diathermy-lasers-fires":"<h2 id=\"surgical-diathermy\">Surgical diathermy</h2>\n<ul><li>Uses <strong>high-frequency alternating current (~0.4–1 MHz; radio frequency)</strong>. At these frequencies current does not stimulate nerves or muscle (no time for depolarisation) or cause VF, but it heats tissue where <strong>current density</strong> is high.</li><li><strong>Cutting:</strong> continuous sine wave; <strong>coagulation:</strong> pulsed (damped) waveform.</li><li><strong>Monopolar:</strong> current passes from the fine active electrode (high current density → heating) through the patient to a large <strong>patient return electrode</strong> (\"plate\"), where current density is low. <strong>Burns</strong> occur if the plate is poorly applied (small contact area), or if current finds an alternative path to earth (e.g. ECG electrodes, contact with metal). Modern isolated (floating) generators and plate-contact monitoring reduce this.</li><li><strong>Bipolar:</strong> current passes only between the tips of the forceps: no plate needed; low power; preferred for extremities, near pacemakers, and in circumcision and digital surgery.</li><li><strong>Implanted devices:</strong> monopolar diathermy may inhibit or reprogramme pacemakers and trigger ICD shocks. Use bipolar where possible, keep the current path away from the device, and follow device-management guidance (e.g. reprogramming or a magnet as advised).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"lasers\">Lasers</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>L</strong>ight <strong>A</strong>mplification by <strong>S</strong>timulated <strong>E</strong>mission of <strong>R</strong>adiation: <strong>monochromatic</strong> (single wavelength), <strong>coherent</strong> (in phase) and <strong>collimated</strong> (parallel) light.</li><li>A lasing medium is \"pumped\" to create a population inversion; photons stimulate emission of identical photons, amplified between mirrors.</li><li>Examples: <strong>CO<sub>2</sub></strong> (10,600 nm, infrared; absorbed by water; superficial precise cutting, e.g. airway surgery); <strong>Nd:YAG</strong> (1064 nm; deeper penetration; coagulation); argon, KTP, diode.</li><li><strong>Classes 1–4</strong> by hazard; surgical lasers are <strong>class 4</strong> (hazard to eyes, skin and fire risk).</li><li><strong>Safety:</strong> designated laser protection supervisor, warning signs, restricted access, <strong>wavelength-specific eye protection</strong> for staff and patient, covered windows, matt instruments, damp swabs.</li><li><strong>Airway surgery:</strong> use <strong>laser-resistant tracheal tubes</strong> (metal or foil-wrapped) with cuffs filled with <strong>saline (± dye)</strong>; keep FiO<sub>2</sub> as low as possible (ideally 0.25–0.3 or less) and avoid N<sub>2</sub>O; or use tubeless techniques (jet ventilation, apnoea). If an <strong>airway fire</strong> occurs: stop the laser, stop gas flow, remove the tube, pour saline, then ventilate with air, re-intubate and bronchoscopy.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"fires-and-explosions\">Fires and explosions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Fire triangle:</strong> <strong>fuel</strong> (drapes, skin preparations with alcohol, tubes, hair), <strong>oxidiser</strong> (oxygen, <strong>N<sub>2</sub>O</strong>, which supports combustion), and <strong>ignition source</strong> (diathermy, lasers, fibreoptic light sources, sparks).</li><li>Prevention: let <strong>alcoholic skin preparations dry</strong> fully, avoid oxygen pooling under drapes (particularly head and neck surgery with nasal oxygen), use the lowest FiO<sub>2</sub> needed, and communicate with the surgical team.</li><li>Flammable anaesthetic agents (ether, cyclopropane) are historical; the zone of risk was within 25 cm of the gas outlet.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> why high-frequency current is safe; monopolar versus bipolar; laser properties; fire triangle.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> managing an airway fire during laser surgery on the vocal cords.</li></ul></div>","difficult-airway-cico":"<h2 id=\"principles-das\">Principles (DAS)</h2>\n<p>The DAS guidelines (2025 update, superseding 2015) set out a structured, stepwise approach. The consistent principles are:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygenation is the priority</strong>, not intubation. Maintain oxygenation throughout (pre-oxygenation, apnoeic oxygenation).</li><li><strong>Limit attempts</strong> at each technique to avoid airway trauma and swelling; each attempt should be the best possible (optimise position, device, adjuncts such as a bougie, external laryngeal manipulation, video laryngoscopy, adequate neuromuscular block).</li><li><strong>Declare the difficulty</strong> early and <strong>call for help</strong>; use clear, shared language.</li><li>After failed intubation, <strong>rescue oxygenation with a second-generation supraglottic airway</strong>; if that fails, attempt <strong>facemask ventilation</strong> (two-person technique, adjuncts).</li><li>If oxygenation is possible, <strong>stop and think</strong>: wake the patient up (reverse, e.g. sugammadex after rocuronium), continue surgery via the SGA if necessary and safe, or intubate through the SGA with a flexible scope, or perform a tracheostomy/cricothyroidotomy.</li><li><strong>Can't intubate, can't oxygenate (CICO):</strong> <strong>declare it</strong> and proceed to <strong>emergency front-of-neck access</strong> without delay: ensure neuromuscular block, extend the neck, and perform a <strong>scalpel–bougie–tube cricothyroidotomy</strong> (a transverse stab incision through the cricothyroid membrane, rotate the blade, pass a bougie, railroad a small cuffed tracheal tube of the size set out in the DAS guidance). Use a vertical skin incision first if the membrane is impalpable.</li><li><strong>After the event:</strong> document, explain to the patient, complete an airway alert (letter to the patient and GP, entry in the notes), report as an incident, and ensure follow-up.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Always read the current DAS guideline and algorithm</strong> (BJA 2026 publication and DAS website): the details (number of attempts, specific steps) are set there, and local departments teach and rehearse them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"obstetric-and-critical-care-variations\">Obstetric and critical care variations</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Obstetrics (OAA/DAS):</strong> a pre-planned decision about whether to <strong>wake or proceed</strong> with surgery via the SGA, depending on maternal and fetal factors; failed intubation drills are practised by the whole team.</li><li><strong>Critically ill patients (DAS/ICS/FICM/RCoA 2018):</strong> a checklist, pre-oxygenation with CPAP/NIV or HFNO, video laryngoscopy, and early front-of-neck access, recognising that physiological difficulty (hypoxaemia, hypotension) adds risk.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"nap4-lessons\">NAP4 lessons</h2>\n<p>NAP4 (2011) found that airway complications were more likely in ICU and ED, with obesity, with failure to use capnography, and with poor planning. Waveform capnography is now mandatory wherever patients are intubated.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the sequence of plans; SGA types for rescue; FONA technique and equipment.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a simulated CICO: declare it, lead the team through front-of-neck access, then debrief and explain what happened to the patient.</li></ul></div>","diuretics":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Site</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Uses</th><th>Effects and side effects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</strong> (acetazolamide)</td><td>Proximal tubule</td><td>↓ HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> reabsorption</td><td>Glaucoma, altitude sickness, metabolic alkalosis, idiopathic intracranial hypertension</td><td><strong>Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis</strong>, hypokalaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Osmotic</strong> (mannitol)</td><td>Proximal tubule and descending limb (water-permeable segments)</td><td>Filtered but not reabsorbed; holds water in the tubule</td><td>Raised ICP, (historically) renal protection</td><td>Initial plasma volume expansion (heart failure), then hypovolaemia, hypernatraemia; crosses a damaged BBB</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Loop</strong> (furosemide, bumetanide)</td><td><strong>Thick ascending limb</strong></td><td>Block <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/2Cl<sup>−</sup></strong> co-transporter</td><td>Heart failure, oedema, hyperkalaemia, hypercalcaemia</td><td>Most potent; <strong>hypokalaemia</strong>, hypomagnesaemia, <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong>, hyponatraemia, hypocalcaemia, <strong>ototoxicity</strong> (with aminoglycosides)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thiazides</strong> (bendroflumethiazide, indapamide)</td><td>Distal convoluted tubule</td><td>Block <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>−</sup></strong> co-transporter</td><td>Hypertension, oedema, nephrogenic DI (paradoxically)</td><td>Hypokalaemia, <strong>hyponatraemia</strong>, <strong>hypercalcaemia</strong>, hyperglycaemia, hyperuricaemia (gout)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Potassium-sparing: aldosterone antagonists</strong> (spironolactone, eplerenone)</td><td>Collecting duct</td><td>Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism</td><td>Heart failure, cirrhosis, hyperaldosteronism</td><td><strong>Hyperkalaemia</strong>, gynaecomastia (spironolactone)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Potassium-sparing: ENaC blockers</strong> (amiloride, triamterene)</td><td>Collecting duct</td><td>Block epithelial Na<sup>+</sup> channels</td><td>With thiazides or loops to limit K<sup>+</sup> loss</td><td>Hyperkalaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SGLT2 inhibitors</strong> (dapagliflozin)</td><td>Proximal tubule</td><td>Glycosuria and natriuresis</td><td>Diabetes, heart failure, CKD</td><td>Euglycaemic ketoacidosis, genital infections</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Why loop and thiazide diuretics cause hypokalaemia:</strong> more Na<sup>+</sup> and fluid reach the collecting duct, where Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption through ENaC drives K<sup>+</sup> secretion, amplified by the secondary aldosterone rise.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> match each diuretic to its site and transporter; electrolyte effects (especially calcium with loop versus thiazide).</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient on furosemide and spironolactone with a potassium of 6.1 mmol/L before surgery.</li></ul></div>","drug-chemistry":"<h2 id=\"ionisation-and-pka\">Ionisation and pKa</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>pKa</strong> is the pH at which a drug is 50% ionised.</li><li><strong>Weak acids</strong> are <strong>more ionised when pH &gt; pKa</strong>; <strong>weak bases</strong> are <strong>more ionised when pH &lt; pKa</strong>.</li><li>Henderson–Hasselbalch: for a base, <strong>log([BH<sup>+</sup>]/[B]) = pKa − pH</strong>; for an acid, <strong>log([A<sup>−</sup>]/[HA]) = pH − pKa</strong>.</li><li><strong>Only the un-ionised form crosses lipid membranes</strong> readily.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical consequences</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Local anaesthetics</strong> are weak bases (pKa about 7.6–8.1): the closer the pKa to tissue pH, the more un-ionised drug is available and the <strong>faster the onset</strong> (lidocaine pKa 7.9 is faster than bupivacaine 8.1). Infected (acidic) tissue increases ionisation and reduces effectiveness. Adding bicarbonate speeds onset.</li><li><strong>Ion trapping:</strong> a weak base crosses into a more acidic compartment, becomes ionised and is trapped (fetal acidosis increases fetal local anaesthetic and opioid levels; morphine concentrates in gastric acid).</li><li><strong>Opioids:</strong> alfentanil (pKa ~6.5) is about 90% un-ionised at pH 7.4 → faster onset than fentanyl (pKa ~8.4, &lt;10% un-ionised), despite lower lipid solubility.</li><li><strong>Thiopental</strong> (pKa ~7.6) is a weak acid formulated at high pH as the sodium salt.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"quaternary-ammonium-compounds\">Quaternary ammonium compounds</h2>\n<p>Permanently charged (N<sup>+</sup> with four carbon groups) regardless of pH: <strong>do not cross the blood–brain barrier or placenta</strong> and are poorly absorbed orally (neuromuscular blockers, <strong>glycopyrronium</strong>, neostigmine). Compare <strong>atropine</strong> (tertiary amine: crosses the BBB → central effects).</p>\n<h2 id=\"protein-binding\">Protein binding</h2>\n<ul><li>Acidic drugs bind mainly to <strong>albumin</strong>; basic drugs to <strong>α<sub>1</sub>-acid glycoprotein</strong> (an acute phase protein, raised after surgery and trauma) as well as albumin.</li><li>Only the <strong>free fraction</strong> is active and can be metabolised or filtered.</li><li>Displacement matters for drugs that are highly bound (&gt;90%) with a small Vd and low extraction (e.g. warfarin, phenytoin), especially if the free level cannot redistribute. Hypoalbuminaemia increases the free fraction of phenytoin.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"isomerism\">Isomerism</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Structural isomers:</strong> same formula, different structure (<strong>isoflurane and enflurane</strong>).</li><li><strong>Stereoisomers:</strong> same structure, different spatial arrangement. <strong>Enantiomers</strong> are mirror images with a chiral centre, designated R/S (absolute configuration) or +/− (optical rotation).</li><li>A <strong>racemic mixture</strong> is 50:50. Enantiomers can differ in potency and toxicity:<ul><li><strong>Levobupivacaine</strong> (S) and <strong>ropivacaine</strong> (S) are less cardiotoxic than racemic bupivacaine.</li><li><strong>S-ketamine</strong> (esketamine) is about twice as potent as R-ketamine.</li><li><strong>Cisatracurium</strong> is one of ten atracurium stereoisomers: more potent, less histamine release.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Tautomerism:</strong> dynamic structural isomers that change with the environment (<strong>midazolam</strong> has an open ring at pH &lt; 4, water-soluble in the ampoule, and closes at physiological pH, becoming lipid-soluble). Strictly this is a pH-dependent ring opening with loss of water, not true tautomerism, but it is usually taught under this heading.</li><li><strong>Geometric (cis–trans) isomers</strong> arise around a double bond or ring: <strong>mivacurium</strong> is a mixture of cis–trans and trans–trans isomers (the cis–cis isomer is weak).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate the ionised fraction; predict onset from pKa; examples of each isomer type; quaternary compounds.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why a block into an abscess cavity may fail, or why bicarbonate is added to lidocaine for a top-up epidural.</li></ul></div>","drug-interactions":"<h2 id=\"adverse-drug-reactions\">Adverse drug reactions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Type A (augmented):</strong> predictable from pharmacology and dose-related (e.g. hypotension with propofol, respiratory depression with opioids). The commonest type.</li><li><strong>Type B (bizarre):</strong> unpredictable, idiosyncratic (anaphylaxis, MH, blood dyscrasias, pulmonary fibrosis with amiodarone or bleomycin).</li><li>Others: chronic (C), delayed (D, e.g. teratogenicity), end-of-use (E, withdrawal), failure of therapy (F).</li><li>Report suspected reactions to the MHRA via the <strong>Yellow Card</strong> scheme.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"interactions\">Interactions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Pharmaceutical:</strong> in the syringe or line (thiopental precipitates with acidic drugs such as suxamethonium or rocuronium; blood and calcium-containing fluids).</li><li><strong>Pharmacokinetic:</strong> absorption, distribution (protein binding displacement), metabolism (induction or inhibition), elimination (NSAIDs reduce lithium clearance).</li><li><strong>Pharmacodynamic:</strong> at the receptor or physiological level — <strong>additive</strong> (effects sum), <strong>synergistic</strong> (more than additive: midazolam with propofol or opioids), <strong>antagonistic</strong> (naloxone with opioids), or potentiation (one drug with no effect of its own increases another's).</li><li><strong>Isobologram:</strong> plots combinations giving an equal effect. A straight line = additive; bowed towards the origin = synergy; bowed away = antagonism.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"drug-errors\">Drug errors</h2>\n<p>Common causes: similar-looking ampoules and names, unlabelled syringes, calculation errors, wrong route (e.g. IV–intrathecal errors), fatigue and interruptions. Prevention: <strong>standardised labelling</strong> (colour-coded ISO labels), reading the ampoule, prefilled syringes, <strong>non-Luer (NRFit) connectors for neuraxial devices</strong>, double-checking high-risk drugs, drug-trolley organisation, electronic prescribing, reporting and learning from incidents.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drugs-and-the-eye\">Drugs and the eye</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Intraocular pressure (IOP)</strong> is raised by suxamethonium (transient), ketamine (slight), coughing, straining, hypercapnia, hypoxia, venous congestion; lowered by most IV and volatile agents, hyperventilation.</li><li><strong>Glaucoma drugs:</strong> timolol (β-blocker: bradycardia, bronchospasm), acetazolamide (acidosis, hypokalaemia), pilocarpine, prostaglandin analogues. <strong>Ecothiopate</strong> (organophosphate eye drops, now rare) prolongs suxamethonium.</li><li>Atropine can precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma in susceptible eyes (rare with standard doses).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"recreational-drugs-alcohol-and-tobacco\">Recreational drugs, alcohol and tobacco</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Substance</th><th>Anaesthetic relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Tobacco</strong></td><td>↑ COHb (↓ O<sub>2</sub> content; stopping for 12–24 h helps), ↑ airway reactivity and secretions, impaired wound healing; CYP1A2 induction. Stopping at any time before surgery helps; longer abstinence reduces pulmonary complications</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alcohol</strong></td><td>Acute: ↓ MAC, aspiration risk. Chronic: ↑ MAC and enzyme induction, liver disease, cardiomyopathy, <strong>withdrawal</strong> (seizures, delirium tremens) — prescribe benzodiazepine regimens and thiamine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cocaine</strong></td><td>Sympathomimetic (↓ reuptake): hypertension, myocardial ischaemia and arrhythmias, vasospasm; avoid β-blockers alone in acute toxicity; benzodiazepines</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Amphetamines, MDMA</strong></td><td>Sympathetic stimulation, hyperthermia, hyponatraemia (MDMA), serotonin syndrome</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Opioids</strong> (heroin, methadone, buprenorphine)</td><td>Tolerance (higher analgesic needs), withdrawal, venous access difficulty, blood-borne viruses; continue substitution therapy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cannabis</strong></td><td>Airway reactivity (smoked), tachycardia, possible ↑ anaesthetic requirement; hyperemesis syndrome</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ketamine (recreational)</strong></td><td>Tolerance; urological damage</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nitrous oxide (recreational)</strong></td><td>B<sub>12</sub> deficiency: subacute combined degeneration</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> classify reactions; interpret an isobologram; list pharmaceutical incompatibilities; effects of drugs on IOP.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> preassessment of a patient who uses cocaine and drinks heavily, with a plan for the day of surgery and afterwards.</li></ul></div>","ecg-interpretation":"<h2 id=\"recording-the-ecg\">Recording the ECG</h2>\n<ul><li>The ECG records the <strong>potential differences on the body surface</strong> generated by cardiac depolarisation and repolarisation (about 1 mV).</li><li><strong>Silver/silver chloride electrodes</strong> with conductive gel reduce skin impedance and polarisation.</li><li><strong>Leads:</strong> bipolar limb leads (I, II, III), augmented unipolar limb leads (aVR, aVL, aVF) and six chest leads (V1–V6) give 12 views. In theatre, <strong>lead II</strong> (rhythm, P waves, inferior ischaemia) and <strong>V5</strong> or <strong>CM5</strong> (lateral ischaemia) are commonly monitored.</li><li><strong>Filters:</strong> <strong>monitoring mode</strong> uses a narrow bandwidth (about 0.5–40 Hz) to reduce baseline wander and interference, which <strong>distorts ST segments</strong>; <strong>diagnostic mode</strong> (0.05–150 Hz) is needed to assess ST changes accurately.</li><li>Differential amplifiers with high common-mode rejection reduce mains interference; diathermy causes gross artefact.</li><li>Paper speed 25 mm/s: 1 small square = 0.04 s; 1 large square = 0.2 s. Rate = 300 / number of large squares between R waves.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"normal-values\">Normal values</h2>\n<p>PR 0.12–0.20 s; QRS &lt; 0.12 s; <strong>QTc</strong> &lt; about 0.44 s (men) / 0.46 s (women) (Bazett: QTc = QT/√RR); axis −30° to +90°.</p>\n<h2 id=\"changes-to-recognise\">Changes to recognise</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Causes and relevance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ST elevation</strong></td><td>Acute MI (territorial, reciprocal changes), pericarditis (widespread, saddle-shaped, PR depression), LV aneurysm, early repolarisation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ST depression / T inversion</strong></td><td>Ischaemia (subendocardial), digoxin (reverse tick), LVH strain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hyperkalaemia</strong></td><td>Peaked T waves → flattened P waves, prolonged PR → broad QRS → sine wave → VF/asystole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hypokalaemia</strong></td><td>Flattened T waves, U waves, ST depression, prolonged QU; arrhythmias</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hypocalcaemia / hypercalcaemia</strong></td><td>Long QT / short QT</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Long QT</strong></td><td>Torsades de pointes; drugs (ondansetron, droperidol, macrolides, antipsychotics, methadone), electrolytes, congenital</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Heart block</strong></td><td>1st degree (PR &gt; 0.2 s); 2nd degree Mobitz I (Wenckebach: progressive PR lengthening) and <strong>Mobitz II</strong> (dropped beats without PR change: risk of complete block); <strong>3rd degree</strong> (AV dissociation)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bundle branch block</strong></td><td>RBBB (RSR' in V1); LBBB (broad, notched R in V5–6; new LBBB may indicate MI)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tachyarrhythmias</strong></td><td>Narrow regular (sinus tachycardia, SVT, flutter with 2:1 block at ~150/min), narrow irregular (<strong>AF</strong>), broad regular (<strong>VT</strong> until proven otherwise), broad irregular (AF with BBB, polymorphic VT)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pre-excitation (WPW)</strong></td><td>Short PR, delta wave; risk of fast AF down the accessory pathway (avoid AV nodal blockers in pre-excited AF)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Territories:</strong> inferior (II, III, aVF) — usually the <strong>right coronary artery</strong>; anterior and septal (V1–V4) — the <strong>left anterior descending</strong>; lateral (I, aVL, V5–V6) — the <strong>circumflex</strong> (or diagonal branches). The left ventricle, having the greatest muscle mass, dominates the QRS. Posterior and far-lateral territory is poorly seen on the standard 12 leads: posterior infarction shows as <strong>ST depression with tall R waves in V1–V3</strong> (confirm with posterior leads V7–V9).</p>\n<p>Management of peri-arrest arrhythmias follows the <strong>RCUK</strong> bradycardia and tachycardia algorithms, based on <strong>adverse features</strong> (shock, syncope, myocardial ischaemia, heart failure).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> monitoring versus diagnostic filters; electrode principles; intervals; coronary territories; recognising the patterns in the table.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpreting a 12-lead ECG at preassessment and deciding whether surgery can proceed.</li></ul></div>","elderly-hip-day-remote":"<h2 id=\"hip-fracture\">Hip fracture</h2>\n<ul><li>Surgery <strong>on the day of or the day after admission</strong> (NICE CG124); correct reversible problems quickly, but do not delay for tests that will not change management.</li><li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> paracetamol, cautious opioids, and a <strong>fascia iliaca</strong> (or femoral) block from the emergency department.</li><li><strong>Anaesthesia:</strong> spinal or general anaesthesia — neither is clearly superior for mortality; the Association of Anaesthetists 2020 guideline emphasises avoiding hypotension, avoiding deep sedation with spinals, cautious drug dosing, and an experienced anaesthetist.</li><li><strong>Bone cement implantation syndrome:</strong> hypoxia, hypotension, arrhythmias, raised pulmonary vascular resistance or cardiac arrest around cementation. Reduce risk by identifying high-risk patients, communicating with the surgeon before cementing, vigilant monitoring, and adequate fluid loading; treat with oxygen, fluids, vasopressors and CPR. Other orthopaedic complications: fat embolism, compartment syndrome.</li><li><strong>Frail and older patients:</strong> delirium prevention (avoid anticholinergics and benzodiazepines, maintain hearing aids and glasses, adequate analgesia), pressure care, thromboprophylaxis, early mobilisation, orthogeriatric input.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"day-surgery\">Day surgery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Patient selection</strong> is based on physiological status rather than arbitrary age or BMI limits: stable comorbidity, understanding and a responsible adult escort and carer for 24 hours (for GA), suitable home circumstances.</li><li><strong>Anaesthetic technique:</strong> short-acting drugs (propofol, remifentanil, sevoflurane or desflurane where appropriate), multimodal analgesia with regional or local techniques, PONV prophylaxis, and spinal anaesthesia with short-acting agents (e.g. chloroprocaine, prilocaine).</li><li><strong>Preassessment</strong> is often nurse-led with protocols and anaesthetic review of complex patients; consider medical (stable comorbidity), surgical (short, low blood loss, manageable pain) and social factors (escort, carer, telephone, distance from hospital).</li><li><strong>Discharge criteria:</strong> stable observations, controlled pain and nausea, mobile, having passed urine where relevant, written instructions and a contact number. Patients should not drive, operate machinery or make important decisions for 24 hours after GA or sedation (and should check with their insurer and the DVLA guidance for driving after surgery).</li><li><strong>Unplanned admission</strong> is most often due to pain, PONV, surgical complications (bleeding, extended surgery), drowsiness, urinary retention or social reasons: track it as a quality measure.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"positioning-and-procedures\">Positioning and procedures</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Positioning hazards:</strong> nerve injuries (ulnar at the elbow, common peroneal in lithotomy, brachial plexus with arm abduction &gt; 90°), pressure injuries, eye injury (prone), compartment syndrome, cardiovascular changes (sitting, prone, lateral), and airway access.</li><li><strong>Tourniquets:</strong> pressure about 50–75 mmHg above systolic for the arm and 100–150 mmHg for the leg (or by limb occlusion pressure); limit time (about 1.5–2 hours); complications include nerve injury, ischaemia, pain, and metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia and a fall in blood pressure on release.</li><li><strong>Laparoscopy:</strong> CO<sub>2</sub> pneumoperitoneum (raised intra-abdominal pressure → reduced FRC and compliance, raised PaCO<sub>2</sub>, reduced venous return, vagal reflexes), and Trendelenburg or reverse Trendelenburg effects; risks of gas embolism, subcutaneous emphysema and pneumothorax.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"remote-sites-mri-ct-radiology-endoscopy-ed\">Remote sites (MRI, CT, radiology, endoscopy, ED)</h2>\n<ul><li>The same standards of monitoring, equipment, assistance and recovery as in theatre; unfamiliar environments and limited access increase risk.</li><li><strong>MRI:</strong> the powerful static magnetic field makes <strong>ferromagnetic objects dangerous projectiles</strong>; implants and devices must be checked (MR safe, MR conditional or MR unsafe); use MR-conditional equipment and monitoring; burns from cables; noise (ear protection); limited access to the patient; quench risk. Contrast agents (gadolinium) and iodinated contrast in CT (allergy-like reactions, nephropathy).</li><li><strong>Radiation safety:</strong> IR(ME)R roles (referrer, practitioner, operator), lead protection, distance.</li><li>Anaesthesia in the emergency department carries the risks of an unprepared patient (full stomach, head injury, sepsis, airway obstruction).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> NICE hip fracture timing; BCIS features; day-case criteria; MRI safety.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning anaesthesia for a 90-year-old with dementia and a hip fracture, including a discussion with the family.</li></ul></div>","electricity-safety":"<h2 id=\"principles\">Principles</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Ohm's law: V = I × R.</strong> Power = V × I = I<sup>2</sup>R. Impedance is the total opposition to alternating current (resistance plus reactance).</li><li><strong>Capacitor:</strong> two conducting plates separated by an insulator; stores charge (Q = CV; energy = ½CV<sup>2</sup>). <strong>Impedance falls as frequency rises</strong>: capacitors pass high-frequency AC and block DC. Used in defibrillators and filters.</li><li><strong>Inductor:</strong> a coil; <strong>impedance rises with frequency</strong> (opposes changing current). Used in defibrillators to shape and prolong the discharge, and in filters.</li><li>UK mains: <strong>230 V AC at 50 Hz</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"electric-shock\">Electric shock</h2>\n<ul><li>Current flows if a person completes a circuit (e.g. between a live conductor and <strong>earth</strong>).</li><li>Effects depend on <strong>current</strong> (not voltage alone), path, duration and frequency. <strong>50 Hz is especially dangerous</strong> for inducing VF.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Current through the body (mains, hand to hand)</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 mA</td><td>Tingling (threshold of perception)</td></tr><tr><td>5–15 mA</td><td>Pain; cannot let go (tetany)</td></tr><tr><td>50 mA</td><td>Respiratory muscle spasm, pain, possible fainting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>100 mA</strong></td><td><strong>Ventricular fibrillation</strong></td></tr><tr><td>&gt; 5 A</td><td>Sustained contraction, burns</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Microshock:</strong> if current is applied <strong>directly to the heart</strong> (via a pacing wire, CVC guidewire or fluid-filled catheter), as little as <strong>~100–150 µA</strong> may cause VF. Hence strict leakage current limits for cardiac equipment.</li><li><strong>Burns</strong> result from current density (heat = I<sup>2</sup>Rt), e.g. at a diathermy plate or where skin touches earthed metal.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"protection-and-equipment-classes\">Protection and equipment classes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Protection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>Accessible metal parts <strong>earthed</strong>; fuse blows if a live wire touches the casing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>II</strong></td><td><strong>Double insulated</strong>; no earth wire</td></tr><tr><td><strong>III</strong></td><td>Powered by <strong>safety extra-low voltage</strong> (≤ 24 V AC / 50 V DC)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type (applied parts)</th><th>Maximum patient leakage current (normal)</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>≤ 100 µA</td><td>Not for direct cardiac connection</td></tr><tr><td><strong>BF</strong></td><td>≤ 100 µA, <strong>floating</strong> (isolated) applied part</td><td>Most monitoring (ECG, NIBP)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CF</strong></td><td><strong>≤ 10 µA</strong>, floating</td><td><strong>Direct cardiac connection</strong> (e.g. invasive pressure, cardiac output)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(Symbols: B is a figure; BF a figure in a square; CF a heart in a square. Defibrillation-proof versions show paddles around the symbol.)</p>\n<p>Further safety: <strong>isolating transformers</strong> (the patient circuit is not referenced to earth), <strong>equipotential earthing</strong> in theatres, <strong>residual current circuit breakers</strong> (detect imbalance between live and neutral and cut the supply within milliseconds), antistatic flooring, regular maintenance and testing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"interference\">Interference</h2>\n<ul><li>Sources: mains (50 Hz), diathermy (high frequency), other equipment, movement.</li><li>Reduction: <strong>differential amplifiers</strong> with high <strong>common mode rejection ratio</strong>, filters (notch filters at 50 Hz), screened (shielded) cables, good electrode contact, keeping leads short and together.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> equipment classes and types with leakage limits; microshock thresholds; capacitor and inductor behaviour with frequency.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient with a temporary pacing wire in situ: what electrical precautions do you take?</li></ul></div>","endocrine-glands":"<h2 id=\"hormone-signalling\">Hormone signalling</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Peptide and catecholamine hormones</strong> act on cell surface receptors (G-protein coupled or tyrosine kinase). <strong>Steroid and thyroid hormones</strong> cross the membrane and act on <strong>intracellular (nuclear) receptors</strong>, changing gene transcription (slower onset, longer effect).</li><li>Most axes use <strong>negative feedback</strong>; oxytocin in labour is an example of positive feedback.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pituitary\">Pituitary</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Anterior</strong> (adenohypophysis; hypothalamic releasing hormones via the portal circulation): ACTH, TSH, GH, prolactin, FSH, LH.</li><li><strong>Posterior</strong> (neurohypophysis; axons from the hypothalamus): <strong>ADH</strong> and <strong>oxytocin</strong>.</li><li>Anatomy: in the sella turcica, below the optic chiasm (bitemporal hemianopia), with the cavernous sinuses laterally. Acromegaly (excess GH) causes airway difficulty (macroglossia, prognathism, laryngeal changes) and OSA.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"adrenal-gland\">Adrenal gland</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Hormone</th><th>Controlled by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Zona glomerulosa</td><td><strong>Aldosterone</strong></td><td>Angiotensin II, K<sup>+</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Zona fasciculata</td><td><strong>Cortisol</strong></td><td>ACTH</td></tr><tr><td>Zona reticularis</td><td>Androgens</td><td>ACTH</td></tr><tr><td>Medulla</td><td><strong>Adrenaline</strong>, noradrenaline</td><td>Preganglionic sympathetic fibres (ACh)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Cortisol</strong> (about 20 mg/day, far more under stress): gluconeogenesis, anti-inflammatory, permissive for catecholamine vasoconstriction. <strong>Adrenal insufficiency</strong> (Addison's, or suppression by exogenous steroids) → hypotension resistant to vasopressors, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia; give <strong>hydrocortisone</strong>. Patients on long-term steroids need perioperative steroid cover.</li><li><strong>Phaeochromocytoma:</strong> catecholamine-secreting tumour; requires preoperative <strong>α-blockade first</strong>, then β-blockade.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"thyroid\">Thyroid</h2>\n<ul><li>Iodide trapping → thyroglobulin iodination (thyroid peroxidase) → <strong>T<sub>4</sub></strong> (mostly) and <strong>T<sub>3</sub></strong> (more active; formed peripherally by deiodination). Highly protein-bound.</li><li>Increase metabolic rate, oxygen consumption, heat production, heart rate (upregulated β receptors).</li><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism:</strong> risk of <strong>thyroid storm</strong> perioperatively (fever, tachycardia, AF, agitation) — make euthyroid before elective surgery. <strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> sensitivity to anaesthetic drugs, hypothermia, hypoventilation.</li><li>Anatomy: the <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerves</strong> run close to the inferior thyroid arteries (at risk in thyroidectomy); the <strong>parathyroids</strong> lie behind.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"calcium-homeostasis\">Calcium homeostasis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>PTH</strong> (low Ca<sup>2+</sup> stimulates): increases bone resorption, renal Ca<sup>2+</sup> reabsorption and phosphate excretion, and renal <strong>1α-hydroxylation</strong> of vitamin D.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D (calcitriol):</strong> increases gut absorption of Ca<sup>2+</sup> and phosphate.</li><li><strong>Calcitonin</strong> (thyroid C cells): lowers Ca<sup>2+</sup> (minor role in humans).</li><li>Post-thyroidectomy <strong>hypocalcaemia</strong> (parathyroid damage): perioral tingling, Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs, laryngospasm, prolonged QT.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> adrenal zones; T<sub>3</sub> versus T<sub>4</sub>; PTH actions; nuclear versus membrane receptors.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning anaesthesia for a patient on long-term prednisolone, or recognising stridor after thyroidectomy.</li></ul></div>","endocrine-stress-glucose":"<h2 id=\"insulin-and-glucagon\">Insulin and glucagon</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Insulin</strong> (pancreatic β cells; released by glucose via GLUT2 uptake → ATP → closure of K<sub>ATP</sub> channels → depolarisation → Ca<sup>2+</sup> entry): the only hormone that lowers glucose. Promotes glucose uptake (GLUT4 in muscle and fat), glycogen, fat and protein synthesis; shifts K<sup>+</sup> into cells; inhibits lipolysis and ketogenesis.</li><li><strong>Counter-regulatory hormones</strong> raise glucose: <strong>glucagon</strong> (α cells: glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis), <strong>adrenaline</strong>, <strong>cortisol</strong>, <strong>growth hormone</strong>.</li><li>Normal fasting plasma glucose about 4–6 mmol/L. The brain needs about <strong>120 g of glucose a day</strong> (it can adapt to ketones in prolonged starvation). Red cells depend entirely on glucose.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-stress-response-to-surgery\">The stress response to surgery</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Change</th><th>Effects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hypothalamic–pituitary</td><td>↑ ACTH, GH, ADH, prolactin</td><td>↑ cortisol, water retention</td></tr><tr><td>Sympathetic / adrenal medulla</td><td>↑ catecholamines</td><td>Tachycardia, hypertension, hyperglycaemia</td></tr><tr><td>Pancreas</td><td>↑ glucagon, relative ↓ insulin, insulin resistance</td><td><strong>Hyperglycaemia</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone</td><td>↑</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup> and water retention, K<sup>+</sup> loss</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolic</td><td>Catabolism: protein breakdown, lipolysis, negative nitrogen balance</td><td>Muscle loss, delayed recovery</td></tr><tr><td>Inflammatory</td><td>IL-6 (peaks at about 24 h), acute phase proteins (CRP), fever</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The response is proportional to the size of surgery. It is reduced by <strong>neuraxial blockade</strong> (especially for lower body surgery, blocking afferent input), high-dose opioids and avoiding hypothermia; <strong>minimally invasive surgery</strong> mainly reduces the inflammatory and acute-phase response rather than the cortisol and catecholamine surge; <strong>etomidate</strong> blocks cortisol synthesis. Enhanced recovery aims to limit it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"starvation\">Starvation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Early (first 24 h):</strong> hepatic glycogen (about 100 g) is exhausted within about a day.</li><li><strong>Days:</strong> gluconeogenesis from amino acids (muscle protein), lactate and glycerol; lipolysis releases fatty acids; ketogenesis increases.</li><li><strong>Prolonged:</strong> the brain adapts to use <strong>ketones</strong>, sparing protein; metabolic rate falls.</li><li><strong>Refeeding syndrome:</strong> in malnourished patients, feeding drives insulin release → intracellular shift of <strong>phosphate</strong>, potassium and magnesium, and thiamine depletion → arrhythmias, respiratory failure, Wernicke's encephalopathy. Start feeding slowly with electrolyte monitoring and thiamine.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"preoperative-fasting-and-carbohydrate-loading\">Preoperative fasting and carbohydrate loading</h2>\n<ul><li>Standard adult elective fasting: <strong>6 hours for food</strong>, <strong>2 hours for clear fluids</strong> (many units now allow clear fluids until called to theatre, following newer guidance); breast milk 4 h in infants; for children, clear fluids up to 1 hour before is endorsed by consensus.</li><li>Carbohydrate drinks before surgery reduce insulin resistance and thirst (with caution in diabetes).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> hormones of the stress response; mechanism of insulin release; stages of starvation; refeeding.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> managing a diabetic patient's glucose and medication around a morning operation.</li></ul></div>","ent-shared-airway":"<h2 id=\"the-shared-airway\">The shared airway</h2>\n<p>The surgeon and anaesthetist work in the same space, so the airway is often out of reach, may be contaminated with blood and debris, and can be displaced by surgical manoeuvres. <strong>Plan together</strong>: device choice, tube fixation and position, throat packs (with a formal count and removal check), who controls the airway, and how you will manage problems.</p>\n<h2 id=\"devices\">Devices</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Reinforced (flexible) laryngeal masks</strong> for many tonsillectomies, nasal and dental procedures (with surgical cooperation), <strong>RAE preformed tubes</strong> (south-facing oral for tonsillectomy with a mouth gag; north-facing nasal for dental and maxillofacial surgery), <strong>reinforced tubes</strong>, <strong>nasal tubes</strong>, microlaryngeal tubes, <strong>laser-resistant tubes</strong>, and tubeless techniques (jet ventilation, high-flow nasal oxygen) for laryngeal surgery.</li><li><strong>Special surgical devices:</strong> mouth gags (Boyle–Davis) can compress or kink tubes; lasers (fire risk: low FiO<sub>2</sub>, saline-filled cuffs); microdebriders; vasoconstrictors on nasal packs (cocaine or adrenaline: hypertension and arrhythmias).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-procedures\">Common procedures</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Tonsillectomy:</strong> bleeding and airway protection; LMA or tube; multimodal analgesia (paracetamol, NSAIDs, dexamethasone, careful opioids — <strong>no codeine</strong> in children); PONV prophylaxis; smooth emergence in the lateral, head-down \"tonsil position\" or awake extubation.</li><li><strong>Nasal surgery:</strong> blood in the airway; a throat pack; emergence with the patient breathing through the mouth.</li><li><strong>Dental extractions:</strong> shared airway, throat packs, nasal tubes for longer procedures; many children and adults with learning disabilities (consent and capacity considerations).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-bleeding-tonsil\">The bleeding tonsil</h2>\n<ul><li>Primary (within 24 hours) or secondary (days later, often infection-related). Dangers: <strong>hypovolaemia</strong> (often underestimated in children, who swallow blood), a <strong>full stomach of blood</strong>, and a difficult view.</li><li><strong>Resuscitate first</strong> (IV access, fluids and blood, group and crossmatch), senior anaesthetist and ENT surgeon, <strong>RSI</strong> with two suction devices and a smaller tube ready (or inhalational induction in the head-down lateral position in some cases), empty the stomach with an orogastric tube before extubation, and extubate awake in the lateral position.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-obstructed-airway\">The obstructed airway</h2>\n<ul><li>Causes: tumours, infection (epiglottitis, abscess, Ludwig's angina), trauma, foreign body, haematoma, angioedema.</li><li>Principles: oxygen, sit the patient up, heliox or nebulised adrenaline as temporising measures, senior anaesthetic and ENT help, imaging if time allows, and a plan with the surgeon for <strong>awake tracheal intubation, awake tracheostomy (under local anaesthesia), or inhalational induction</strong> in theatre with the surgeon scrubbed and ready for front-of-neck access. Avoid sedation and neuromuscular blockade before the airway is secured unless part of a planned strategy.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> tube types and their uses; bleeding tonsil priorities; laser safety.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> plan the anaesthetic for a 7-year-old returning to theatre with a bleeding tonsil, and explain it to the ENT surgeon.</li></ul></div>","extubation-recovery":"<h2 id=\"extubation\">Extubation</h2>\n<ul><li>Extubation is an <strong>elective</strong> procedure: plan it (DAS extubation guidelines). <strong>Low-risk:</strong> fasted, uncomplicated airway, no general risk factors. <strong>At-risk:</strong> difficult airway at induction, airway surgery or oedema, restricted access, obesity/OSA, aspiration risk, cardiovascular or respiratory instability.</li><li><strong>Preparation:</strong> oxygenate (FiO<sub>2</sub> 1.0), suction under direct vision, reverse neuromuscular block (TOF ratio ≥ 0.9), analgesia, position (head-up in most adults).</li><li><strong>Awake extubation</strong> (eyes open, obeying commands, adequate spontaneous ventilation) is standard for most at-risk patients. <strong>Deep extubation</strong> avoids coughing (e.g. after some ENT and eye surgery) but risks airway obstruction and aspiration; it requires experience.</li><li>Advanced techniques for at-risk airways: <strong>remifentanil extubation</strong>, <strong>SGA exchange</strong> (Bailey manoeuvre), airway exchange catheters, postponing extubation or tracheostomy.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"laryngospasm\">Laryngospasm</h2>\n<ul><li>Reflex closure of the vocal cords, commonly in light planes of anaesthesia with airway stimulation (secretions, blood, suction, SGA removal), especially in children and smokers.</li><li><strong>Signs:</strong> stridor (partial) or silent obstruction (complete), paradoxical breathing, desaturation, bradycardia in children.</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> call for help, stop stimulation, <strong>100% O<sub>2</sub> with CPAP</strong> via a tight-fitting mask, jaw thrust (and <strong>Larson's point</strong> pressure behind the ear lobe), deepen anaesthesia with <strong>propofol</strong> (e.g. 0.5–1 mg/kg in adults), and if it persists, <strong>suxamethonium</strong> (small doses, e.g. 0.1–0.5 mg/kg IV, or IM if no access in children) with atropine for bradycardia; intubate if needed. Watch for <strong>negative-pressure pulmonary oedema</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"stridor-after-surgery\">Stridor after surgery</h2>\n<p>Causes: laryngospasm, laryngeal oedema (intubation, allergy), vocal cord palsy (recurrent laryngeal nerve after thyroid or neck surgery), haematoma (after thyroid/carotid surgery: <strong>open the wound</strong> — \"SCOOP\" the clips and sutures — then call for senior help), foreign body, residual block. Management includes oxygen, head-up, nebulised adrenaline, dexamethasone, heliox, and early senior airway expertise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"common-problems-in-recovery\">Common problems in recovery</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Think of</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Hypoxaemia</strong></td><td>Airway obstruction, hypoventilation (opioids, residual block, sedation), atelectasis, bronchospasm, aspiration, pulmonary oedema, pneumothorax, PE, shivering (↑ O<sub>2</sub> demand)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hypotension</strong></td><td>Hypovolaemia (bleeding), vasodilation (neuraxial, drugs), cardiac causes (ischaemia, arrhythmia), sepsis, anaphylaxis, tension pneumothorax</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hypertension</strong></td><td>Pain, full bladder, hypercapnia, hypoxia, withdrawal of antihypertensives, emergence agitation, pre-eclampsia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Confusion / delirium</strong></td><td>Hypoxia, hypercapnia, hypoglycaemia, hypotension, pain, full bladder, drugs (anticholinergics, benzodiazepines), sepsis, stroke, alcohol withdrawal, pre-existing cognitive impairment</td></tr><tr><td>Pain, <strong>PONV</strong>, shivering, urinary retention, hypothermia</td><td>See relevant notes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Also recognise which patients need <strong>higher-level care</strong> (level 2 or 3) afterwards: major surgery, significant comorbidity, instability, ongoing organ support.</p>\n<h2 id=\"discharge-from-recovery-typical-criteria\">Discharge from recovery (typical criteria)</h2>\n<p>Conscious and able to maintain their own airway, with protective reflexes; breathing adequately with acceptable SpO<sub>2</sub> (on oxygen if prescribed); cardiovascularly stable with no unexplained changes; pain and PONV controlled; normothermic; surgical site checked; prescriptions and instructions for the ward completed. <strong>Day-case discharge home</strong> also requires a responsible adult, oral intake as appropriate, mobilisation, and written advice. Follow up patients on the ward after anaesthesia where possible.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> laryngospasm drugs and doses; causes of each recovery problem; discharge criteria.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> called to recovery: a patient with an SpO<sub>2</sub> of 88% after a laparotomy — assess, treat and hand over.</li></ul></div>","flow-physics":"<h2 id=\"laminar-flow\">Laminar flow</h2>\n<ul><li>Smooth, parallel layers; fastest in the centre (parabolic profile).</li><li><strong>Hagen–Poiseuille:</strong> <strong>Q = (π P r<sup>4</sup>) / (8 η l)</strong>. Flow is proportional to the <strong>fourth power of radius</strong>, and inversely to <strong>viscosity (η)</strong> and length.</li><li>Doubling cannula radius increases flow <strong>16-fold</strong>; a short, wide cannula (e.g. 14G, 45 mm) is best for rapid infusion. Warming blood reduces viscosity and increases flow.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"turbulent-flow\">Turbulent flow</h2>\n<ul><li>Chaotic eddies; flow ∝ <strong>√pressure</strong> and inversely to <strong>density (ρ)</strong>; resistance is much higher.</li><li>Occurs above a critical velocity, at bends, branches, constrictions and irregularities (the larynx, kinked tubes, orifices).</li><li><strong>Reynolds number: Re = ρ v d / η</strong>. Below about <strong>2000</strong> flow is usually laminar; above about 2000 it becomes turbulent (the precise value depends on the geometry).</li><li><strong>Helium–oxygen (heliox):</strong> helium's <strong>low density</strong> reduces Re and resistance where flow is turbulent (upper airway obstruction), buying time. Its viscosity is similar to oxygen's, so it helps less where flow is laminar (small airways).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"bernoulli-and-venturi\">Bernoulli and Venturi</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Bernoulli:</strong> along a streamline, total energy is constant, so where fluid <strong>speeds up</strong> through a constriction, its <strong>pressure falls</strong>.</li><li><strong>Venturi effect:</strong> the low pressure at a constriction <strong>entrains</strong> a second fluid through a side port.</li><li><strong>Uses:</strong> <strong>Venturi (fixed-performance) oxygen masks</strong>, where a jet of oxygen entrains a fixed proportion of air to give a known FiO<sub>2</sub> (e.g. 24%, 28%, 35%, 40%, 60%) provided total flow exceeds the patient's peak inspiratory flow; nebulisers; suction devices; jet ventilation injectors; the <strong>Coanda effect</strong> (a jet tends to follow a curved surface: relevant to flow at vessel and airway bifurcations and some fluidic logic devices).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"measuring-flow\">Measuring flow</h2>\n<p>Variable-orifice flowmeters (rotameters): at low flows the bobbin's annular gap acts like a tube (laminar flow: depends on viscosity); at high flows it acts like an orifice (turbulent: depends on density). They are calibrated for a specific gas. See clinical measurement of flow.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Hagen–Poiseuille calculations; Reynolds number; why heliox helps; how a Venturi mask works.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining to a colleague why a large-bore peripheral cannula beats a triple-lumen central line for massive transfusion.</li></ul></div>","flow-volume-pft":"<h2 id=\"flow-and-volume-measurement\">Flow and volume measurement</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Principle</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Wright respirometer</strong></td><td>A vane rotates as gas passes through tangential slots; gears count volume</td><td>Measures <strong>volume</strong>; over-reads at high flows and under-reads at low flows (inertia); unidirectional</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pneumotachograph</strong> (Fleisch)</td><td>Gas flows through a <strong>laminar-flow resistor</strong>; the <strong>pressure drop is proportional to flow</strong> (Hagen–Poiseuille); integration gives volume</td><td>Heated to prevent condensation; calibrated for gas viscosity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pitot tube</strong></td><td>Pressure difference between openings facing towards and away from flow</td><td>Used in some anaesthetic spirometry (e.g. D-lite)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hot-wire anemometer</strong></td><td>Gas flow cools a heated wire; the current needed to keep its temperature constant relates to flow</td><td>ICU ventilators</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ultrasonic flowmeter</strong></td><td>Transit-time differences of ultrasound up- and downstream</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rotameter</strong></td><td>Variable-orifice flowmeter (see the anaesthetic machine)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Peak flow meter</td><td>Variable orifice (Wright peak flow meter)</td><td>Peak expiratory flow (L/min)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"spirometry\">Spirometry</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Vitalograph</strong> (bellows-type) or electronic spirometers record volume against time: <strong>FEV<sub>1</sub></strong> (forced expiratory volume in 1 s), <strong>FVC</strong>, and <strong>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC</strong>.</li><li><strong>Obstructive</strong> (asthma, COPD): <strong>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC &lt; 0.7</strong> (or below the lower limit of normal), FVC normal or reduced. <strong>Restrictive</strong> (fibrosis, chest wall disease, obesity): both reduced proportionally, ratio <strong>normal or high</strong>.</li><li><strong>Flow–volume loops:</strong> obstructive airways give a scooped expiratory limb. <strong>Fixed upper airway obstruction</strong> (e.g. tracheal stenosis) flattens <strong>both</strong> limbs; <strong>variable extrathoracic</strong> obstruction (e.g. vocal cord palsy) flattens the <strong>inspiratory</strong> limb; <strong>variable intrathoracic</strong> obstruction (e.g. tracheomalacia, a mid-tracheal tumour) flattens the <strong>expiratory</strong> limb.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"frc-and-diffusion\">FRC and diffusion</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>FRC and RV:</strong> helium dilution, nitrogen washout, or body plethysmography (see lung volumes).</li><li><strong>Transfer factor (DLCO / TLCO):</strong> single-breath carbon monoxide uptake (CO binds Hb avidly, so uptake is diffusion-limited). Reduced in emphysema, fibrosis, pulmonary vascular disease and anaemia; increased with polycythaemia and alveolar haemorrhage. KCO corrects for alveolar volume.</li><li><strong>Perioperative relevance:</strong> predicted postoperative FEV<sub>1</sub> and DLCO guide risk in lung resection (Final syllabus).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> principles of each flowmeter; spirometry patterns; flow–volume loop shapes; DLCO.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpreting spirometry and a flow–volume loop in a patient with a goitre before thyroidectomy.</li></ul></div>","fluids-osmolality":"<h2 id=\"compartments-70-kg-man\">Compartments (70 kg man)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Total body water ≈ 60% of body weight (~42 L)</strong>: less in women and older people (more fat), about 75–80% in term neonates.</li><li><strong>Intracellular ≈ 2/3 (~28 L)</strong>; <strong>extracellular ≈ 1/3 (~14 L)</strong>: interstitial ~10.5 L, plasma ~3 L, plus transcellular fluid.</li><li><strong>Blood volume ≈ 70 mL/kg</strong> in adults (about 80–90 mL/kg in infants).</li><li>Measured by dilution: total body water (deuterium oxide), ECF (inulin, bromide), plasma (Evans blue, labelled albumin).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"osmolality-and-tonicity\">Osmolality and tonicity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Osmolarity:</strong> osmoles per litre of solution. <strong>Osmolality:</strong> osmoles per kg of solvent (measured by freezing point depression). Normal plasma osmolality <strong>275–295 mOsm/kg</strong>.</li><li><strong>Calculated osmolality ≈ 2Na<sup>+</sup> + glucose + urea.</strong> An <strong>osmolar gap</strong> above about 10 suggests an unmeasured osmole (ethanol, methanol, ethylene glycol, mannitol).</li><li><strong>Tonicity</strong> is the effective osmolality: only solutes that cannot cross the membrane move water. Urea is an ineffective osmole; glucose is effective when insulin is lacking. <strong>5% dextrose is iso-osmolar in the bag but hypotonic in the body</strong> once the glucose is metabolised.</li><li><strong>Water distribution:</strong> ICF osmolality is held nearly constant, so water moves between ICF and ECF according to <strong>ECF osmolality, which is mainly sodium</strong>. Movement between plasma and interstitium follows the <a href=\"#notes/cvs-microcirculation\">Starling forces</a>.</li><li><strong>Regulation:</strong> hypothalamic <strong>osmoreceptors</strong> detect changes of about 1–2% in osmolality and drive <strong>thirst</strong> and <strong>ADH</strong> release (water reabsorption via aquaporin-2 in the collecting ducts).</li><li>The <strong>colligative properties</strong> (osmotic pressure, freezing point depression, boiling point elevation, vapour pressure depression) depend on the number of particles, not their nature.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"electrolytes\">Electrolytes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Ion</th><th>Normal plasma</th><th>Key roles and points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Na<sup>+</sup></td><td>135–145 mmol/L</td><td>Main ECF cation; determines ECF volume. Correct chronic hyponatraemia slowly (limit about 8–10 mmol/L per 24 h) to avoid osmotic demyelination</td></tr><tr><td>K<sup>+</sup></td><td>3.5–5.0 mmol/L</td><td>Main ICF cation (~150 mmol/L inside). Shifted into cells by insulin, β<sub>2</sub> agonists and alkalosis. Both high and low levels cause arrhythmias</td></tr><tr><td>Ca<sup>2+</sup></td><td>Total 2.2–2.6 mmol/L; ionised ~1.1–1.3</td><td>About 45% bound to albumin; alkalosis increases binding and lowers ionised Ca<sup>2+</sup>. Citrate in transfused blood binds Ca<sup>2+</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Mg<sup>2+</sup></td><td>0.7–1.0 mmol/L</td><td>Cofactor for ATP; NMDA and calcium channel antagonism; used in pre-eclampsia, asthma, torsades and arrhythmias. Potentiates neuromuscular block</td></tr><tr><td>Cl<sup>−</sup></td><td>95–105 mmol/L</td><td>Affects acid–base (Stewart)</td></tr><tr><td>PO<sub>4</sub><sup>3−</sup></td><td>0.8–1.5 mmol/L</td><td>ATP, 2,3-DPG; falls in refeeding syndrome</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"unit-conversions\">Unit conversions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>1% = 10 mg/mL</strong> (1 g per 100 mL). So 2% lidocaine = 20 mg/mL; 1:1000 adrenaline = 1 mg/mL; 1:200,000 = 5 µg/mL.</li><li>mmol = mass (mg) ÷ molecular weight. 1 g of NaCl ≈ 17 mmol Na<sup>+</sup>; 0.9% saline contains 154 mmol/L each of Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> compartment volumes; osmolality versus tonicity; calculation of osmolar gap; % and ratio conversions.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> calculating a safe dose of a drug expressed as a percentage, or explaining why 5% dextrose is a poor resuscitation fluid.</li></ul></div>","gas-analysis":"<h2 id=\"anaesthetic-agents-co-2-and-n-2-o-infrared-absorption\">Anaesthetic agents, CO<sub>2</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>O: infrared absorption</h2>\n<p>Molecules with <strong>two or more different atoms</strong> absorb infrared at characteristic wavelengths (CO<sub>2</sub> ~4.3 µm; N<sub>2</sub>O ~4.5 µm; volatile agents in the 8–12 µm range, allowing agent identification). O<sub>2</sub>, N<sub>2</sub> and helium (single element) do not absorb infrared.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen-analysers\">Oxygen analysers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Principle</th><th>Features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Paramagnetic</strong></td><td>O<sub>2</sub> is <strong>paramagnetic</strong> (attracted into a magnetic field, because of unpaired electrons). Classic: nitrogen-filled glass dumbbells in a non-uniform field rotate as O<sub>2</sub> concentration changes; modern: differential pressure in a switched magnetic field</td><td>Fast (breath-by-breath), accurate, used in anaesthetic monitors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fuel cell (galvanic cell)</strong></td><td>O<sub>2</sub> diffuses through a membrane and is reduced at a gold cathode with a lead anode, generating a <strong>current proportional to PO<sub>2</sub></strong>; no external power</td><td>Slower response (about 20 s); the cell is consumed over months; used in anaesthetic machine O<sub>2</sub> analysers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Polarographic (Clark) electrode</strong></td><td>Platinum cathode and silver/silver chloride anode with an applied voltage (~0.6 V); current ∝ PO<sub>2</sub></td><td>Blood gas analysers; needs temperature control at 37°C</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"blood-gas-electrodes\">Blood gas electrodes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>pH:</strong> a <strong>glass electrode</strong> with an H<sup>+</sup>-sensitive glass membrane; potential difference against a reference electrode (calomel or Ag/AgCl) relates to [H<sup>+</sup>] (Nernst equation). Maintained at 37°C.</li><li><strong>PCO<sub>2</sub>: Severinghaus electrode</strong> — a pH electrode behind a CO<sub>2</sub>-permeable membrane with a bicarbonate solution; CO<sub>2</sub> diffuses in and changes pH.</li><li><strong>PO<sub>2</sub>: Clark electrode</strong> (above).</li><li><strong>Derived values:</strong> bicarbonate (Henderson–Hasselbalch), base excess, saturation (unless co-oximetry measures it), anion gap.</li><li><strong>Co-oximetry:</strong> multi-wavelength spectrophotometry that measures oxy-, deoxy-, carboxy- and methaemoglobin separately.</li><li><strong>Temperature:</strong> analysers measure at 37°C. <strong>Alpha-stat</strong> management (reporting values at 37°C) is standard in adults during hypothermia; pH-stat corrects to the patient's temperature (adds CO<sub>2</sub>; used in some paediatric bypass).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-methods\">Other methods</h2>\n<p><strong>Mass spectrometry</strong> (ionised gas separated by mass-to-charge ratio; very fast, multiple gases, historically shared between theatres), <strong>Raman scattering</strong> (shift in wavelength of scattered laser light), <strong>piezoelectric</strong> quartz crystals coated with oil (volatile absorption changes resonant frequency), <strong>refractometry</strong> (calibration of vaporisers).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> which gases absorb infrared and why; paramagnetic versus fuel cell; Clark and Severinghaus electrodes.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the machine oxygen analyser and the monitor disagree: which do you believe and why?</li></ul></div>","gas-laws":"<h2 id=\"the-laws\">The laws</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>Statement</th><th>Clinical example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Boyle</strong></td><td>At constant temperature, <strong>PV = constant</strong></td><td>Estimating cylinder contents; plethysmography; gas expansion at altitude and in diving</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Charles</strong></td><td>At constant pressure, <strong>V ∝ T</strong> (kelvin)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gay-Lussac (third law)</strong></td><td>At constant volume, <strong>P ∝ T</strong></td><td>Cylinder pressure rises if the cylinder heats up</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ideal gas equation</strong></td><td><strong>PV = nRT</strong></td><td>1 mole of gas occupies <strong>22.4 L at STP</strong> (0°C, 101.3 kPa)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Avogadro</strong></td><td>Equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules</td><td>Calculating vapour volumes from liquid anaesthetic</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dalton</strong></td><td>Total pressure = sum of partial pressures</td><td>Alveolar gas equation; P<sub>H2O</sub> at 37°C = 6.3 kPa</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Henry</strong></td><td>At a given temperature, dissolved gas ∝ its partial pressure</td><td>Dissolved O<sub>2</sub> (0.023 mL/dL/kPa); decompression sickness</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Graham</strong></td><td>Rate of diffusion ∝ 1/√(molecular weight)</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> how much vapour does 1 mL of liquid isoflurane (MW 184.5, density 1.5 g/mL) produce? 1.5 g / 184.5 = 0.0081 mol × 22.4 L = about 0.18 L at STP (about 195 mL at 20°C).</p>\n<h2 id=\"critical-temperature-and-pressure\">Critical temperature and pressure</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Critical temperature:</strong> the temperature above which a gas <strong>cannot be liquefied by pressure alone</strong>. <strong>Critical pressure:</strong> the pressure needed to liquefy it at its critical temperature.</li><li>A <strong>gas</strong> is above its critical temperature; a <strong>vapour</strong> is below it.</li><li><strong>Oxygen</strong> (critical temperature −119°C) is a gas at room temperature: cylinder pressure falls <strong>in proportion to contents</strong> (Boyle), so the gauge shows how full it is.</li><li><strong>Nitrous oxide</strong> (critical temperature <strong>36.5°C</strong>) is a <strong>liquid in equilibrium with its vapour</strong> in the cylinder at room temperature: the gauge reads the <strong>saturated vapour pressure (~44 bar at 15°C, ~52 bar at 20°C)</strong> until almost all the liquid has gone, so <strong>contents must be judged by weight</strong>. Cylinders are filled to a <strong>filling ratio</strong> (mass of N<sub>2</sub>O / mass of water the cylinder could hold) of <strong>0.75</strong> in temperate climates (0.67 in the tropics) to allow for thermal expansion.</li><li><strong>Entonox</strong> (50:50 O<sub>2</sub>/N<sub>2</sub>O) has a <strong>pseudo-critical temperature of about −6°C</strong>: below it, N<sub>2</sub>O liquefies and separates (the Poynting effect keeps it mixed above this).</li><li><strong>Carbon dioxide</strong> (critical temperature 31°C) is also stored as a liquid.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> match laws to examples; calculations with PV = nRT and 22.4 L; critical temperatures; why an N<sub>2</sub>O gauge does not show contents.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> checking how long a size F oxygen cylinder will last on a transfer at 10 L/min.</li></ul></div>","gas-supply":"<h2 id=\"cylinders\">Cylinders</h2>\n<ul><li>Molybdenum steel (or aluminium/composite for lightweight cylinders). Test pressures and dates are stamped on the shoulder.</li><li><strong>UK colour coding</strong> (body white for medical gases, identified by shoulder colour): <strong>oxygen white</strong>; <strong>nitrous oxide blue</strong>; <strong>Entonox blue and white</strong> quarters; <strong>air black and white</strong> quarters; <strong>carbon dioxide grey</strong>; <strong>heliox brown and white</strong>.</li><li><strong>Pin index safety system</strong> on smaller cylinders (specific pin positions for each gas: oxygen 2–5, N<sub>2</sub>O 3–5, air 1–5, Entonox 7) prevents fitting the wrong cylinder to a yoke. Larger cylinders have bull-nose or handwheel valves. The Bodok seal makes the joint gas-tight.</li><li><strong>Full pressures:</strong> O<sub>2</sub> about <strong>137 bar</strong> (newer integral-valve cylinders up to 230 bar); N<sub>2</sub>O about <strong>44–52 bar</strong> (SVP; liquid in cylinder); Entonox 137 bar; air 137 bar.</li><li><strong>Filling ratio</strong> (mass of N<sub>2</sub>O in the cylinder ÷ mass of water it could hold) is <strong>0.75</strong> in the UK (0.67 in hot climates), leaving room for expansion. Cylinder pressure does not reflect N<sub>2</sub>O contents until all the liquid has gone: weigh it.</li><li><strong>Entonox</strong> separates below its pseudo-critical temperature. A standard cylinder then gives off the oxygen-rich gas above the liquid first and a <strong>hypoxic, N<sub>2</sub>O-rich mixture last</strong>; a cylinder with a <strong>dip tube</strong> (drawing from the bottom) delivers the hypoxic mixture first; store cylinders horizontally above 10°C and invert them several times if they may have been cold.</li><li><strong>Contents:</strong> an O<sub>2</sub> cylinder's contents are proportional to pressure. A size E O<sub>2</sub> cylinder holds about 680 L; a size F about 1360 L (check the label, as newer cylinders vary). At 10 L/min a 680 L cylinder lasts about an hour.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"measuring-pressure-the-bourdon-gauge\">Measuring pressure: the Bourdon gauge</h2>\n<p>A coiled, flattened tube of oval cross-section <strong>straightens as internal pressure rises</strong>, moving a pointer through gears. It reads <strong>gauge pressure</strong>, needs no power, and is robust. Aneroid gauges work similarly for lower pressures.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pressure-regulators\">Pressure regulators</h2>\n<p><strong>Pressure-reducing valves</strong> reduce high, falling cylinder pressure to a constant lower pressure (about <strong>4 bar</strong>) for the machine, using a diaphragm and spring. Flow restrictors protect against pipeline pressure surges.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pipelines-medical-gas-pipeline-system\">Pipelines (medical gas pipeline system)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygen supply:</strong> usually a <strong>vacuum-insulated evaporator (VIE)</strong> holding liquid oxygen at about −160°C and 5–10 bar, with a back-up cylinder manifold. Oxygen boils off as needed; a superheater warms it before it enters the pipes.</li><li>Delivered at <strong>4 bar</strong> (400 kPa) gauge; <strong>surgical air</strong> at 7 bar (for tools).</li><li>Safety: <strong>Schrader</strong> wall outlets (gas-specific collar indexing), <strong>non-interchangeable screw thread (NIST)</strong> connectors on the machine, colour-coded hoses, tug tests, and <strong>area valve service units (AVSUs)</strong> that isolate zones in fires or leaks. Regular testing and a gas identity check at installation (misconnection has caused deaths).</li><li><strong>Oxygen concentrators</strong> (zeolite molecular sieves adsorb nitrogen under pressure: pressure swing adsorption) produce about 90–95% oxygen with some argon; used in some hospitals, homes and low-resource settings.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> cylinder colours and pin indices; pressures; VIE; Bourdon gauge; safety features.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> pipeline oxygen failure alarm during an anaesthetic: immediate actions.</li></ul></div>","gi-drugs":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Onset and use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sodium citrate 0.3 M</strong> (30 mL)</td><td><strong>Non-particulate antacid</strong>: neutralises acid already in the stomach</td><td>Immediate; lasts ~20–30 min; before GA for caesarean section. Particulate antacids (magnesium trisilicate) cause lung injury if aspirated</td></tr><tr><td><strong>H<sub>2</sub> receptor antagonists</strong> (<strong>ranitidine</strong> withdrawn in the UK; <strong>famotidine</strong>, cimetidine)</td><td>Block histamine-stimulated acid secretion</td><td>Reduce volume and acidity of subsequent secretions; oral ~1–2 h before; cimetidine inhibits CYP450</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Proton pump inhibitors</strong> (omeprazole, lansoprazole, pantoprazole)</td><td>Irreversibly inhibit H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase (pro-drugs activated in acid)</td><td>Most effective suppression, but needs time (best given the evening before and morning of surgery); IV forms available</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metoclopramide</strong></td><td>D<sub>2</sub> antagonist (central and peripheral), 5-HT<sub>4</sub> agonist</td><td>↑ gastric emptying and LOS tone; antiemetic; extrapyramidal effects</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Domperidone</strong></td><td>Peripheral D<sub>2</sub> antagonist (does not cross the BBB well)</td><td>Prokinetic; QT prolongation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Erythromycin</strong></td><td>Motilin agonist</td><td>Prokinetic in critical care (feed intolerance)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antimuscarinics</strong> (glycopyrronium, atropine)</td><td>Reduce secretions</td><td>Reduce LOS tone</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"aspiration-prophylaxis-in-obstetrics-typical-practice\">Aspiration prophylaxis in obstetrics (typical practice)</h2>\n<p>For women at risk of needing general anaesthesia (e.g. high-risk labour), an <strong>H<sub>2</sub> antagonist or PPI</strong> is given regularly; before GA for caesarean section add <strong>sodium citrate</strong> and consider metoclopramide. Follow local policy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aspiration-of-gastric-contents\">Aspiration of gastric contents</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Mendelson's syndrome:</strong> chemical pneumonitis from acid aspiration (classically volume &gt; 25 mL and pH &lt; 2.5 were quoted as risk thresholds, from animal data).</li><li>Management: suction, head-down and lateral position if not intubated, secure the airway, bronchoscopy for particulate matter, supportive ventilation; antibiotics only if infection develops (not routinely); steroids are not indicated.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms and onset times; why particulate antacids are avoided; drugs affecting LOS tone.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning a GA for a category 1 caesarean section, including aspiration prophylaxis and why each drug is given.</li></ul></div>","imaging-interpretation":"<h2 id=\"a-systematic-approach-to-the-chest-x-ray\">A systematic approach to the chest X-ray</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Details:</strong> patient, date, projection (PA or AP: AP magnifies the heart), position (erect, supine: supine films hide pneumothorax and effusions).</li><li><strong>Quality:</strong> rotation (spinous processes midway between clavicle heads), inspiration (about 6 anterior ribs or 10 posterior ribs above the diaphragm), penetration.</li><li><strong>Lines and tubes:</strong> tracheal tube tip about <strong>5 cm above the carina</strong> (mid-trachea, between the clavicles and the carina in a neutral neck); <strong>central venous catheter tip at the cavo-atrial junction / lower SVC</strong> (around the level of the carina on the right); nasogastric tube below the diaphragm in the stomach, crossing the carina in the midline and deviating left; chest drains; pacing wires.</li><li><strong>Airway:</strong> trachea central? Carina, main bronchi.</li><li><strong>Breathing:</strong> lung fields (compare zones), pleura and costophrenic angles, apices, behind the heart.</li><li><strong>Circulation:</strong> heart size (<strong>cardiothoracic ratio &lt; 0.5 on a PA film</strong>), borders, mediastinal width, hila.</li><li><strong>Diaphragm:</strong> position (right usually higher), free gas under the diaphragm.</li><li><strong>Everything else:</strong> bones, soft tissues, <strong>review areas</strong> (apices, behind the heart, below the diaphragm).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"common-abnormalities\">Common abnormalities</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pneumothorax</strong></td><td>Visible lung edge with no markings beyond; deep sulcus sign on supine films; <strong>tension</strong>: mediastinal shift away (a clinical diagnosis — do not wait for a film)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pleural effusion</strong></td><td>Blunted costophrenic angle, meniscus</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Consolidation</strong></td><td>Air bronchograms; loss of borders (silhouette sign locates it: right heart border = middle lobe; left heart border = lingula; hemidiaphragm = lower lobe)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collapse</strong></td><td>Volume loss, shifted fissures and mediastinum; left lower lobe collapse gives a \"sail sign\" behind the heart</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pulmonary oedema</strong></td><td>Upper lobe diversion, Kerley B lines, bat's wing shadowing, effusions, cardiomegaly</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Endobronchial intubation</strong></td><td>Tube tip in the right main bronchus; collapse of the left lung or right upper lobe</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"airway-and-cervical-spine\">Airway and cervical spine</h2>\n<ul><li>Lateral neck and <strong>CT</strong> imaging can show tracheal deviation or compression (goitre, mediastinal mass), and <strong>flow–volume loops</strong> show the functional effect.</li><li><strong>Cervical spine trauma:</strong> CT is the first-line imaging in adults with significant head or neck injury (NICE NG232 and NG41 criteria); manual in-line stabilisation during intubation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"radiation-safety\">Radiation safety</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>IR(ME)R</strong> regulations define the roles of <strong>referrer, practitioner and operator</strong> and require justification of each exposure. Staff protection: <strong>time, distance (inverse square law), shielding</strong> (lead aprons, thyroid shields, screens), dosimeters.</li><li>Pregnancy: ask about the possibility of pregnancy before exposing people of childbearing potential; the fetus is most sensitive in the first trimester. Iodinated contrast risks: allergy-like reactions and contrast-associated nephropathy.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> tip positions; silhouette signs; features of the common films; IR(ME)R roles.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a post-intubation chest X-ray on ICU: talk through it and act on what you find.</li></ul></div>","immunity-anaphylaxis":"<h2 id=\"immunity\">Immunity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Innate:</strong> barriers, neutrophils, macrophages, NK cells, <strong>complement</strong> (classical, alternative and lectin pathways), mast cells and acute phase proteins. Rapid, non-specific, no memory.</li><li><strong>Adaptive:</strong> <strong>B cells</strong> (antibodies: humoral) and <strong>T cells</strong> (CD4 helper, CD8 cytotoxic: cellular). Specific, with memory.</li><li><strong>Antibodies:</strong> IgG (most abundant; crosses the placenta), IgM (first response, pentamer, fixes complement), IgA (mucosal), <strong>IgE</strong> (mast cells and basophils; allergy and parasites), IgD.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hypersensitivity-gell-and-coombs\">Hypersensitivity (Gell and Coombs)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I (immediate)</td><td><strong>IgE</strong> on mast cells → degranulation</td><td>Anaphylaxis, asthma, hay fever</td></tr><tr><td>II (cytotoxic)</td><td>IgG/IgM against cell surface antigens</td><td>ABO transfusion reaction, haemolytic disease of the newborn, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia</td></tr><tr><td>III (immune complex)</td><td>Antigen–antibody complexes deposit</td><td>Serum sickness, lupus</td></tr><tr><td>IV (delayed)</td><td>T cell-mediated</td><td>Contact dermatitis, chlorhexidine skin reactions, tuberculin test</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"anaphylaxis\">Anaphylaxis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Allergic (IgE-mediated) anaphylaxis</strong> requires prior sensitisation. <strong>Non-allergic anaphylaxis</strong> (previously \"anaphylactoid\") results from direct mast cell activation (e.g. via the MRGPRX2 receptor) or complement, and can occur on first exposure. Clinically they are managed identically.</li><li>Mediators: <strong>histamine</strong>, tryptase, leukotrienes, prostaglandins, PAF → vasodilation, capillary leak (up to half the plasma volume within minutes), bronchospasm, angioedema.</li><li>Perioperative presentation: <strong>hypotension</strong> is the commonest first feature; bronchospasm, rising airway pressure, falling ETCO<sub>2</sub>, rash (may be absent), and cardiac arrest. Most reactions occur within minutes of induction.</li><li><strong>NAP6</strong> (UK, 2018): incidence about <strong>1 in 10,000 anaesthetics</strong>; commonest triggers <strong>antibiotics</strong> (especially teicoplanin and co-amoxiclav), <strong>neuromuscular blockers</strong> (notably suxamethonium and rocuronium; atracurium less often) and <strong>chlorhexidine</strong>, then patent blue dye. Most deaths were in older patients with cardiovascular disease, often on β-blockers or ACE inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> adrenaline is first-line (IV titrated boluses under anaesthesia, e.g. 50 µg in adults, or IM 0.5 mg if no IV access), fluids, and remove triggers — follow the QRH and RCUK guidance. See the clinical note on managing anaphylaxis.</li><li><strong>Investigation:</strong> mast cell <strong>tryptase</strong> samples at about <strong>1 hour</strong> (up to 2 h) and <strong>at least 24 hours</strong> after the reaction (baseline), with an initial sample as soon as resuscitation allows; refer to an allergy clinic; tell the patient and GP and report to MHRA.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> antibody classes; hypersensitivity types; tryptase timing; NAP6 triggers.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> managing and then explaining an anaphylactic reaction to the patient afterwards, including follow-up and what they must tell future anaesthetists.</li></ul></div>","infusion-decontamination":"<h2 id=\"infusion-devices\">Infusion devices</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Syringe drivers:</strong> a lead screw pushes the plunger; accurate at low rates; used for TCI, drugs, PCA. <strong>Volumetric pumps:</strong> peristaltic mechanism; for larger volumes.</li><li><strong>Safety features:</strong> occlusion (pressure) alarms, air-in-line detection (volumetric), free-flow prevention, anti-siphon mechanisms, syringe size and brand recognition, drug libraries with dose limits (\"smart pumps\"), battery backup, locked settings (PCA, epidural).</li><li><strong>Siphonage:</strong> an unclamped syringe above the patient can empty under gravity if the plunger is not held (use anti-siphon valves). <strong>Bolus on release of an occlusion:</strong> clamp and relieve pressure before clearing. Place syringe drivers at the level of the patient.</li><li><strong>Epidural and neuraxial devices</strong> use dedicated yellow lines and non-Luer (NRFit) connectors to prevent wrong-route errors.</li><li><strong>Calculations:</strong> mg/kg/h → mL/h: rate (mL/h) = dose (mg/kg/h) × weight / concentration (mg/mL). Example: noradrenaline 4 mg in 50 mL = 80 µg/mL; 0.1 µg/kg/min for 70 kg = 7 µg/min = 420 µg/h ÷ 80 = <strong>5.25 mL/h</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"warming-devices\">Warming devices</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Forced-air warming</strong> (convection) is the most effective common method for surface warming. Also conductive (resistive) mattresses and blankets.</li><li><strong>Fluid warmers:</strong> counter-current or dry-heat plate warmers; high-capacity warmers for rapid infusion. They reduce heat loss from cold fluids (warming blood from 4°C to 37°C takes a lot of energy).</li><li><strong>Theatre ventilation (plenum systems):</strong> positive-pressure filtered air, about 15–25 air changes per hour; laminar flow ventilation (ultraclean) for arthroplasty.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"decontamination\">Decontamination</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Definition</th><th>Methods</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cleaning</strong></td><td>Physical removal of organic material</td><td>Detergent, washer-disinfectors</td><td>Always the first step</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disinfection</strong></td><td>Removes most micro-organisms but not all spores</td><td>Heat (pasteurisation, washer-disinfectors at 70–90°C), chemicals (alcohol, chlorine, peracetic acid, glutaraldehyde)</td><td>Semi-critical items (e.g. flexible endoscopes: high-level disinfection)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sterilisation</strong></td><td>Kills all micro-organisms including spores</td><td><strong>Autoclave</strong> (steam at 134°C for 3 min), ethylene oxide, gas plasma, irradiation</td><td>Critical items entering sterile tissue</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Spaulding classification:</strong> critical (sterile tissue: sterilise), semi-critical (mucous membranes: high-level disinfection or sterilise), non-critical (intact skin: clean or low-level disinfection).</li><li><strong>Prions</strong> resist conventional sterilisation: single-use equipment for high-risk procedures and quarantine of instruments when prion disease is suspected.</li><li>Single-use items must not be reused; follow manufacturer and local infection control guidance.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> infusion safety features; syringe driver calculations; sterilisation methods and levels.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> setting up and checking a PCA or a TIVA infusion so that it is safe.</li></ul></div>","inhalational-agents":"<h2 id=\"comparison-values-approximate\">Comparison (values approximate)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Sevoflurane</th><th>Desflurane</th><th>Isoflurane</th><th>Nitrous oxide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MAC (adult, % in O<sub>2</sub>)</td><td>~2.0</td><td>~6.0</td><td>~1.15</td><td>~104</td></tr><tr><td>Blood:gas</td><td>0.65</td><td>0.45</td><td>1.4</td><td>0.47</td></tr><tr><td>Oil:gas</td><td>47–55</td><td>19</td><td>91–98</td><td>1.4</td></tr><tr><td>Boiling point (°C)</td><td>58.5</td><td><strong>22.8</strong></td><td>48.5</td><td>−88</td></tr><tr><td>SVP at 20°C (kPa)</td><td>21</td><td><strong>89</strong></td><td>32</td><td>5200 (gas in cylinder)</td></tr><tr><td>Airway irritation</td><td><strong>Minimal</strong> (good for gas induction)</td><td><strong>Pungent</strong> (coughing, laryngospasm; avoid for induction)</td><td>Pungent</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Metabolised</td><td>3–5%</td><td>0.02%</td><td>0.2%</td><td>0.004%</td></tr><tr><td>Global warming potential (GWP<sub>100</sub>)</td><td>~130</td><td><strong>~2,500</strong></td><td>~510</td><td><strong>~270</strong>, long-lived, ozone depleting</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"common-effects-of-volatile-agents\">Common effects of volatile agents</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>CNS:</strong> dose-dependent unconsciousness; cerebral vasodilation (↑ CBF, may ↑ ICP) with reduced CMRO<sub>2</sub>; EEG suppression. Sevoflurane can cause epileptiform EEG at high concentrations and emergence agitation in children.</li><li><strong>CVS:</strong> ↓ SVR and BP (vasodilation; isoflurane and desflurane most), ↓ contractility, blunted baroreflex. <strong>Desflurane</strong> can cause sympathetic stimulation and tachycardia with rapid increases in concentration. Halothane sensitises the myocardium to catecholamines (arrhythmias).</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> ↓ tidal volume, ↑ rate, ↑ PaCO<sub>2</sub>; depress hypoxic and hypercapnic drives; bronchodilation; depress HPV slightly.</li><li>Uterine relaxation (dose-dependent: relevant at caesarean section), potentiation of neuromuscular block, triggers of <strong>malignant hyperthermia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Sevoflurane with CO<sub>2</sub> absorbent:</strong> compound A (nephrotoxic in rats) with low flows and strong bases; modern absorbents lack strong bases.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"nitrous-oxide\">Nitrous oxide</h2>\n<ul><li>Analgesic (NMDA antagonism, and endogenous opioid and noradrenergic mechanisms); weak anaesthetic (MAC &gt; 100%).</li><li><strong>34 times more soluble in blood than nitrogen</strong>, so it enters air-filled spaces faster than N<sub>2</sub> leaves: <strong>expands</strong> pneumothorax, bowel, middle ear, air emboli, intraocular gas bubbles (SF<sub>6</sub>, C<sub>3</sub>F<sub>8</sub>: contraindicated for weeks), and <strong>increases pressure</strong> in tracheal tube cuffs and LMA cuffs.</li><li><strong>Oxidises vitamin B<sub>12</sub></strong>, inactivating methionine synthase → megaloblastic changes and subacute combined degeneration with prolonged or repeated exposure (and recreational use); possible teratogenicity.</li><li>PONV, diffusion hypoxia. Large environmental impact, much from <strong>pipeline leakage</strong>: many UK hospitals have decommissioned central N<sub>2</sub>O manifolds.</li><li><strong>Entonox</strong> (50:50 O<sub>2</sub>/N<sub>2</sub>O): pseudo-critical temperature about −6°C (separates in the cold: the lower layer becomes rich in N<sub>2</sub>O).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"xenon\">Xenon</h2>\n<p>Noble gas; NMDA antagonist; MAC about 63–71%; blood:gas ~0.115 (very fast onset and offset); cardiostable, no MH trigger, environmentally neutral, but very expensive and needs special equipment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"environmental-impact\">Environmental impact</h2>\n<p>Anaesthetic gases are a major contributor to NHS carbon emissions. Strategies: avoid desflurane and N<sub>2</sub>O, use <strong>low fresh gas flows</strong>, consider <strong>TIVA</strong> and regional techniques.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> properties table; why desflurane needs a heated pressurised vaporiser; N<sub>2</sub>O effects on closed spaces and B<sub>12</sub>.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing a maintenance technique for a patient with an intraocular gas bubble, or discussing the environmental impact of your anaesthetic with a trainee.</li></ul></div>","inhalational-uptake":"<h2 id=\"mac\">MAC</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Minimum alveolar concentration:</strong> the alveolar concentration at <strong>steady state</strong> (1 atmosphere) that prevents movement in response to a standard surgical incision in <strong>50%</strong> of subjects. MAC-awake (about 0.3–0.5 MAC) and MAC-BAR (block of adrenergic response, about 1.5 MAC) are related concepts. MACs of different agents are roughly <strong>additive</strong>.</li><li><strong>Increased by:</strong> infants (peak at about 1–6 months), hyperthermia, hypernatraemia, chronic alcohol use, acute amphetamine or cocaine use, red hair (MC1R variants; modest).</li><li><strong>Decreased by:</strong> age (about 6% per decade after 40), hypothermia, pregnancy, opioids, benzodiazepines, α<sub>2</sub>-agonists, ketamine, N<sub>2</sub>O, acute alcohol intoxication, hypotension, hypoxia, anaemia, hyponatraemia, lithium.</li><li><strong>Not changed by:</strong> sex, duration of anaesthesia, hyper- or hypothyroidism (in practice), hyperkalaemia, weight.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"partition-coefficients\">Partition coefficients</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Blood:gas</strong> (solubility in blood) determines the <strong>speed of onset and offset</strong>: the lower it is, the faster the alveolar partial pressure (and so the brain) rises towards the inspired.</li><li><strong>Oil:gas</strong> correlates with <strong>potency</strong> (Meyer–Overton: MAC × oil:gas ≈ constant).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"uptake-what-makes-f-a-f-i-rise-fast\">Uptake: what makes F<sub>A</sub>/F<sub>I</sub> rise fast</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Low blood solubility</strong> (desflurane, N<sub>2</sub>O, sevoflurane rise fast; halothane slow).</li><li><strong>High alveolar ventilation</strong> (more delivery; especially important for soluble agents).</li><li><strong>Low cardiac output</strong> (less removal from the alveoli → faster rise in F<sub>A</sub>; so shocked patients may be overdosed quickly). High cardiac output slows induction.</li><li><strong>High inspired concentration</strong> (overpressure) and <strong>high fresh gas flow</strong> (reduces breathing system time constant).</li><li><strong>Concentration effect:</strong> at high inspired concentrations (only possible with N<sub>2</sub>O), uptake itself concentrates the remaining gas and draws in more gas.</li><li><strong>Second gas effect:</strong> rapid uptake of a large volume of N<sub>2</sub>O concentrates a second gas (volatile or O<sub>2</sub>) in the alveoli.</li><li>Right-to-left shunt slows inhalational induction (more so with insoluble agents); left-to-right shunt has little effect.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"elimination-and-emergence\">Elimination and emergence</h2>\n<ul><li>Largely by exhalation, the reverse of uptake; recovery is faster with insoluble agents and after short anaesthetics (tissues less saturated).</li><li>Metabolism: halothane ~20%, sevoflurane ~3–5% (fluoride; compound A with CO<sub>2</sub> absorbents), isoflurane ~0.2%, desflurane ~0.02%.</li><li><strong>Diffusion hypoxia:</strong> at the end of N<sub>2</sub>O anaesthesia, the rapid outflow of N<sub>2</sub>O into the alveoli dilutes oxygen: give 100% O<sub>2</sub> for several minutes.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> factors affecting MAC; blood:gas versus oil:gas; draw F<sub>A</sub>/F<sub>I</sub> curves for N<sub>2</sub>O, sevoflurane, isoflurane and halothane; second gas effect.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> gas induction in a child: explaining why it is quick with sevoflurane and what slows it (airway obstruction, low ventilation).</li></ul></div>","iv-fluids":"<h2 id=\"composition-per-litre-approximate\">Composition (per litre, approximate)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Fluid</th><th>Na<sup>+</sup></th><th>K<sup>+</sup></th><th>Cl<sup>−</sup></th><th>Other</th><th>Osmolarity (mOsm/L)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>0.9% sodium chloride</strong></td><td>154</td><td>0</td><td><strong>154</strong></td><td></td><td>308</td><td>Hyperchloraemic acidosis with large volumes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hartmann's (compound sodium lactate)</strong></td><td>131</td><td>5</td><td>111</td><td>Lactate 29, Ca<sup>2+</sup> 2</td><td>~278</td><td>Balanced; slightly hypotonic</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Plasma-Lyte 148</strong></td><td>140</td><td>5</td><td>98</td><td>Acetate 27, gluconate 23, Mg<sup>2+</sup> 1.5</td><td>~295</td><td>Balanced; no calcium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5% glucose</strong></td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>Glucose 50 g</td><td>278</td><td>Free water once metabolised</td></tr><tr><td>0.18% NaCl / 4% glucose</td><td>31</td><td>0</td><td>31</td><td>Glucose 40 g</td><td>284</td><td>Very hypotonic in the body: hyponatraemia (not for children)</td></tr><tr><td>Human albumin 4.5%</td><td>~140</td><td></td><td></td><td>Albumin 45 g</td><td>~300</td><td>Colloid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Plasma</strong></td><td>140</td><td>4</td><td>100</td><td>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 24</td><td>~290</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"crystalloids\">Crystalloids</h2>\n<ul><li>Distribute through the <strong>ECF</strong>: roughly a quarter or less stays intravascular after equilibration (less when the glycocalyx is damaged).</li><li><strong>Balanced solutions</strong> (Hartmann's, Plasma-Lyte) cause less hyperchloraemic acidosis than 0.9% saline and are generally preferred for resuscitation and replacement; saline is preferred in hypochloraemic alkalosis and is traditionally used with blood transfusion (calcium in Hartmann's can clot citrated blood in the same line) and in brain injury (Hartmann's is slightly hypotonic).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"colloids\">Colloids</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Albumin:</strong> used in specific settings (paracentesis, some critical care uses); expensive.</li><li><strong>Gelatins:</strong> short-acting; highest rate of anaphylaxis among colloids.</li><li><strong>Hydroxyethyl starches:</strong> associated with <strong>renal injury and increased mortality in sepsis</strong>; their marketing authorisations have been suspended in the UK and EU.</li><li>Colloids expand plasma volume more per mL initially, but trials have not shown outcome benefits over crystalloids in most settings.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"principles-nice-cg174-5-rs-resuscitation-routine-maintenance-replacement-redistribution-reassessment\">Principles (NICE CG174 \"5 Rs\": resuscitation, routine maintenance, replacement, redistribution, reassessment)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Routine maintenance (adults):</strong> about <strong>25–30 mL/kg/day of water</strong>, <strong>1 mmol/kg/day each of Na<sup>+</sup>, K<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup></strong>, and <strong>50–100 g/day glucose</strong>. Reduce in the elderly, frail, renal and cardiac failure.</li><li><strong>Resuscitation:</strong> a bolus of balanced crystalloid (e.g. 500 mL in under 15 minutes in adults), then reassess.</li><li><strong>Replacement:</strong> match ongoing losses (GI losses are rich in K<sup>+</sup>; gastric losses in H<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup>).</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (NICE NG29): isotonic fluids with glucose as needed for maintenance; boluses of 10 mL/kg (5 mL/kg in some groups) with reassessment.</li><li><strong>Avoid fluid overload</strong> (weight gain, oedema, anastomotic and pulmonary complications) as much as hypovolaemia.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> composition table; why saline causes acidosis; distribution volumes; maintenance requirements.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> prescribing postoperative fluids for a 60 kg woman who is nil by mouth after a laparotomy, and explaining each component.</li></ul></div>","iv-induction-agents":"<h2 id=\"the-ideal-induction-agent\">The ideal induction agent</h2>\n<p>Rapid, smooth onset in one arm–brain time; rapid recovery without accumulation; water-soluble and stable; painless on injection; minimal cardiovascular and respiratory depression; no excitatory effects, histamine release or allergy; analgesic; antiemetic; non-toxic; cheap; safe if injected intra-arterially.</p>\n<h2 id=\"comparison\">Comparison</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Propofol</strong></th><th><strong>Thiopental</strong></th><th><strong>Ketamine</strong></th><th><strong>Etomidate</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class</td><td>Phenol (2,6-diisopropylphenol)</td><td>Barbiturate (thiobarbiturate)</td><td>Phencyclidine derivative</td><td>Imidazole ester</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>GABA<sub>A</sub> potentiation</td><td>GABA<sub>A</sub> potentiation</td><td><strong>NMDA antagonist</strong></td><td>GABA<sub>A</sub> potentiation</td></tr><tr><td>Formulation</td><td>1% or 2% lipid emulsion (soya oil, egg lecithin)</td><td>Sodium salt powder, pH ~10.5</td><td>Racemic or S(+), aqueous</td><td>In lipid or propylene glycol</td></tr><tr><td>Adult induction dose</td><td>1.5–2.5 mg/kg</td><td>3–5 mg/kg</td><td>1–2 mg/kg IV (5–10 mg/kg IM)</td><td>0.3 mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Blood pressure</td><td><strong>↓↓</strong> (vasodilation)</td><td>↓</td><td><strong>↑</strong> (sympathomimetic)</td><td><strong>Stable</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Respiration</td><td>Apnoea common; ↓ airway reflexes (good for LMA)</td><td>Apnoea; laryngospasm</td><td>Airway reflexes largely preserved; bronchodilator</td><td>Apnoea</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>Antiemetic; pain on injection; propofol infusion syndrome</td><td>Anticonvulsant; ↓ ICP; porphyria trigger; intra-arterial injection causes gangrene</td><td><strong>Analgesic</strong>; emergence phenomena; ↑ secretions</td><td><strong>Adrenal suppression</strong> (11β-hydroxylase inhibition); myoclonus; PONV</td></tr><tr><td>Elimination</td><td>High clearance (exceeds hepatic flow: extrahepatic metabolism)</td><td>Slow; zero-order at high doses; accumulates</td><td>Hepatic (norketamine is active)</td><td>Ester hydrolysis</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Doses are adult starting points and must be reduced in the elderly, frail, hypovolaemic and those with cardiac disease; check the BNF and local guidance.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"clinical-points\">Clinical points</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Offset after a single bolus</strong> of propofol or thiopental is by <strong>redistribution</strong>, not metabolism.</li><li><strong>Propofol</strong> is the usual agent for day surgery and TIVA; <strong>propofol infusion syndrome</strong> (metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, cardiac failure) is associated with high doses (&gt; 4 mg/kg/h) for long periods, particularly in children and critical illness.</li><li><strong>Ketamine</strong> is useful in shocked patients (though a direct myocardial depressant in catecholamine-depleted patients), asthma, and analgesia at sub-anaesthetic doses; it causes hallucinations and dysphoria (reduced by benzodiazepines).</li><li><strong>Thiopental</strong> is still used in some units for RSI and status epilepticus; accidental intra-arterial injection → crystal formation and ischaemia (leave the cannula, give saline, heparin, vasodilators, consider sympathetic block).</li><li><strong>Etomidate:</strong> a single dose suppresses cortisol for up to about 24 hours; concern in sepsis.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms, formulations, cardiovascular effects, metabolism; why thiopental is dangerous intra-arterially; features of propofol infusion syndrome.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing an induction agent and dose for a shocked patient needing emergency laparotomy, and justifying it.</li></ul></div>","last-management":"<h2 id=\"presentation\">Presentation</h2>\n<ul><li>Usually within minutes of injection (intravascular), but can be delayed (up to an hour or more) after absorption from tissues.</li><li><strong>CNS:</strong> perioral tingling, tinnitus, metallic taste, light-headedness, agitation, confusion, muscle twitching, <strong>seizures</strong>, then coma.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> initially hypertension and tachycardia (or bradycardia), then <strong>arrhythmias</strong> (broad QRS, ventricular arrhythmias), hypotension, conduction block, <strong>cardiovascular collapse</strong> and asystole. Bupivacaine is particularly cardiotoxic; cardiac arrest may occur without CNS warning, especially under general anaesthesia or sedation.</li><li>Acidosis, hypoxia and hypercapnia worsen toxicity.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"prevention\">Prevention</h2>\n<ul><li>Use the lowest effective dose; know the maximum doses; <strong>ultrasound guidance</strong> (reduces vascular puncture and allows lower volumes); aspirate before and during injection; inject incrementally (for example 3–5 mL aliquots) with pauses; consider adrenaline-containing test doses where appropriate; monitor the patient during and after blocks.</li><li>Reduce doses in the frail, elderly, and those with cardiac, hepatic or renal disease, and in pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Stop Before You Block</strong> checks help prevent wrong-sided blocks (a different but related safety issue).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"management-follow-the-qrh-last-card\">Management (follow the QRH LAST card)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Stop injecting</strong>; call for help.</li><li>Airway, 100% O<sub>2</sub>, ventilate (avoid hypercapnia and acidosis).</li><li><strong>Control seizures</strong> with a benzodiazepine, propofol or thiopental in small doses.</li><li><strong>Circulatory arrest:</strong> start CPR (ALS); <strong>lipid emulsion</strong>; <strong>reduce adrenaline doses</strong> (smaller boluses) as recommended in LAST guidance; prolonged CPR may be needed (over an hour); consider ECMO.</li><li><strong>Lipid emulsion (20%)</strong> — as per the QRH card: an initial IV bolus of <strong>1.5 mL/kg</strong> over about a minute, then an infusion of <strong>15 mL/kg/h</strong>, with repeat boluses and a doubled infusion rate if instability persists (maximum cumulative dose about <strong>12 mL/kg</strong>). Mechanisms: a \"lipid sink\" for the lipophilic drug, and direct metabolic and inotropic effects.</li><li>Report and follow up; propofol is <strong>not</strong> a substitute for lipid emulsion (its lipid content is too low and it is cardiodepressant).</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> symptoms in order; the lipid emulsion regimen; factors worsening toxicity.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient becomes agitated and then seizes during an axillary block: lead the response.</li></ul></div>","liver-gi":"<h2 id=\"the-liver\">The liver</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Carbohydrate:</strong> glycogen storage, gluconeogenesis. <strong>Fat:</strong> β-oxidation, ketogenesis, lipoprotein and cholesterol synthesis. <strong>Protein:</strong> albumin, clotting factors (except VIII, made mainly in endothelium), deamination and the <strong>urea cycle</strong> (ammonia → urea).</li><li><strong>Drug metabolism</strong> (phase I and II), bilirubin conjugation and excretion, bile production (fat absorption), hormone inactivation, storage of vitamins A, D, B<sub>12</sub> and iron, and Kupffer cell immune function.</li><li><strong>Liver function tests:</strong> albumin and INR reflect <strong>synthetic function</strong>; transaminases (ALT, AST) hepatocellular injury; ALP and GGT cholestasis; bilirubin excretion.</li><li>Anaesthetic relevance of liver disease: coagulopathy, hypoalbuminaemia (more free drug), encephalopathy (sensitivity to sedatives), ascites, portal hypertension and varices, hepatorenal and hepatopulmonary syndromes, altered drug handling.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"gastric-physiology\">Gastric physiology</h2>\n<ul><li>Parietal cells secrete <strong>HCl</strong> (proton pump; stimulated by <strong>gastrin</strong>, histamine via H<sub>2</sub> receptors and ACh via M<sub>3</sub>) and intrinsic factor; chief cells secrete pepsinogen. About 2 L of gastric juice a day.</li><li><strong>Gastric emptying:</strong> clear fluids leave with a half-time of about 10–20 minutes; solids take hours, fatty meals longest. Emptying is delayed by opioids, pain, anxiety, labour, diabetes (autonomic neuropathy), trauma, bowel obstruction and pregnancy near term.</li><li><strong>Lower oesophageal sphincter</strong> tone protects against reflux (barrier pressure = LOS pressure − gastric pressure). Reduced by pregnancy, opioids, anticholinergics and volatile agents (propofol has little effect); increased by metoclopramide and suxamethonium (which also raises gastric pressure, so barrier pressure is maintained).</li><li><strong>Aspiration risk</strong> is increased by a full stomach, delayed emptying, reflux, obesity, pregnancy, obstruction and reduced consciousness. Strategies include fasting, antacids and H<sub>2</sub> blockers or PPIs, nasogastric drainage, RSI, and extubation awake.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"nausea-and-vomiting\">Nausea and vomiting</h2>\n<ul><li>Coordinated by the <strong>vomiting centre</strong> (the \"central pattern generator\" in the medulla, around the nucleus tractus solitarius), with inputs from:<ul><li>the <strong>chemoreceptor trigger zone</strong> in the <strong>area postrema</strong> (outside the blood–brain barrier; D<sub>2</sub>, 5-HT<sub>3</sub>, NK<sub>1</sub> and opioid receptors) → opioids, toxins;</li><li><strong>vagal afferents</strong> from the gut (5-HT<sub>3</sub>; serotonin release by enterochromaffin cells);</li><li>the <strong>vestibular system</strong> (H<sub>1</sub>, muscarinic) → motion;</li><li>the cortex (anticipation, smells, pain).</li></ul></li><li>Each antiemetic targets one or more of these receptors, which is why combinations work better.</li><li><strong>PONV risk factors (Apfel, adults):</strong> female sex, non-smoker, history of PONV or motion sickness, postoperative opioids. Also: volatile agents and nitrous oxide, longer surgery, certain operations (e.g. laparoscopic gynaecological, strabismus), and age (children over 3 and young adults).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> stimuli for acid secretion; factors affecting gastric emptying and LOS tone; receptors in the CTZ.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> assessing aspiration risk before an emergency laparotomy and explaining your plan; building a PONV prophylaxis plan.</li></ul></div>","local-anaesthetics":"<h2 id=\"structure\">Structure</h2>\n<p><strong>Lipophilic aromatic ring – intermediate chain – hydrophilic tertiary amine.</strong> The intermediate link defines the class:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Esters</strong> (–COO–)</th><th><strong>Amides</strong> (–NHCO–)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, cocaine, benzocaine</td><td>Lidocaine, prilocaine, bupivacaine, levobupivacaine, ropivacaine, articaine</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrolysed by <strong>plasma cholinesterase</strong> (short half-life)</td><td>Metabolised in the <strong>liver</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>PABA</strong> metabolite → more allergic reactions</td><td>Allergy very rare</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(Tip: amides have an \"i\" before the \"-caine\".)</p>\n<h2 id=\"mechanism\">Mechanism</h2>\n<p>Weak bases (pKa 7.6–8.1). The <strong>un-ionised</strong> form crosses the axon membrane; inside the cell the <strong>ionised</strong> form binds a site in the inner vestibule of the <strong>voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channel</strong>, blocking it from the inside. Binding is greater when channels are open or inactivated (<strong>use-dependent</strong> or frequency-dependent block).</p>\n<h2 id=\"determinants-of-the-block\">Determinants of the block</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Determines</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>pKa</strong></td><td><strong>Speed of onset</strong> (closer to pH 7.4 = more un-ionised = faster)</td><td>Lidocaine (7.9) faster than bupivacaine (8.1)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lipid solubility</strong></td><td><strong>Potency</strong></td><td>Bupivacaine &gt; lidocaine &gt; procaine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Protein binding</strong></td><td><strong>Duration</strong></td><td>Bupivacaine ~95%, lidocaine ~65%</td></tr><tr><td>Vasodilator activity</td><td>Shorter duration and more systemic absorption</td><td>Lidocaine is a vasodilator; cocaine a vasoconstrictor</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Site of injection affects <strong>systemic absorption</strong> (highest to lowest): intercostal / intravenous regional &gt; caudal &gt; epidural &gt; brachial plexus &gt; subcutaneous.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"commonly-quoted-maximum-doses-adult-guide\">Commonly quoted maximum doses (adult guide)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Plain</th><th>With adrenaline</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lidocaine</td><td>3 mg/kg</td><td>7 mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Prilocaine</td><td>6 mg/kg</td><td>8 mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Bupivacaine / levobupivacaine</td><td>2 mg/kg</td><td>2 mg/kg (UK guidance)</td></tr><tr><td>Ropivacaine</td><td>3 mg/kg</td><td>3 mg/kg</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>These are guides only; safe doses depend on site, patient (age, frailty, cardiac, hepatic and renal disease, pregnancy), and total dose from all sources. Always check the product information, BNF and local guidance.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"individual-agents\">Individual agents</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Bupivacaine:</strong> long-acting; high cardiotoxicity (binds Na<sup>+</sup> channels \"fast in, slow out\"); racemic. <strong>Levobupivacaine</strong> (S-enantiomer) and <strong>ropivacaine</strong> (S, propyl) are less cardiotoxic; ropivacaine has more sensory–motor separation.</li><li><strong>Prilocaine:</strong> least toxic amide; <strong>methaemoglobinaemia</strong> at high doses (o-toluidine); used for IV regional anaesthesia (Bier's block) and hyperbaric spinal.</li><li><strong>Chloroprocaine:</strong> short-acting ester for spinal day-case surgery.</li><li><strong>Cocaine:</strong> ester; blocks noradrenaline reuptake → vasoconstriction (nasal surgery), hypertension, arrhythmias.</li><li><strong>EMLA</strong> (eutectic mixture of lidocaine and prilocaine) and <strong>tetracaine gel</strong> for venepuncture in children.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"additives\">Additives</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Adrenaline</strong> (e.g. 1:200,000 = 5 µg/mL): vasoconstriction → prolongs block, reduces peak plasma level, marks intravascular injection (tachycardia). Avoid in end-artery areas.</li><li><strong>Bicarbonate:</strong> speeds onset (more un-ionised base). <strong>Opioids, clonidine, dexamethasone</strong> (perineural or IV) prolong or intensify blocks. <strong>Glucose</strong> makes spinal solutions hyperbaric.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ester versus amide; pKa, lipid solubility, protein binding and their clinical effects; maximum doses; absorption by site.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> calculating a safe volume of 0.5% levobupivacaine for a block in a 60 kg patient who has already had lidocaine infiltration.</li></ul></div>","measurement-principles":"<h2 id=\"static-characteristics\">Static characteristics</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> closeness to the true value. <strong>Precision:</strong> reproducibility (clustering of repeated measurements). A device can be precise but inaccurate (a consistent bias).</li><li><strong>Linearity:</strong> output is proportional to input across the range.</li><li><strong>Drift:</strong> change in output over time with a constant input: <strong>zero drift</strong> (offset shifts) or <strong>gain drift</strong> (slope changes). Two-point calibration corrects both.</li><li><strong>Hysteresis:</strong> the reading depends on whether the input is rising or falling.</li><li><strong>Sensitivity</strong> and <strong>resolution:</strong> smallest detectable change.</li><li><strong>Signal-to-noise ratio:</strong> improved by filtering, averaging and good technique.</li><li><strong>Calibration:</strong> comparing against known standards (one-point: zero; two-point: zero and a known value).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"dynamic-response\">Dynamic response</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Response time</strong>, <strong>time constant</strong> (time to reach 63% of a step change) and <strong>frequency response</strong>: a system must respond accurately to all frequency components of the signal. An arterial pressure waveform can be reconstructed from its fundamental frequency (heart rate) and harmonics up to about the <strong>8th–10th</strong>: at 120/min (2 Hz) that means accurate response up to <strong>~20 Hz</strong>.</li><li><strong>Resonance (natural frequency):</strong> every system oscillates most readily at its natural frequency. If it is close to the frequencies in the signal, the output is <strong>amplified</strong> (overshoot). For catheter–transducer systems, natural frequency is <strong>increased</strong> by a <strong>short, wide, stiff</strong> catheter, a stiff diaphragm and a low-density fluid, and <strong>decreased</strong> by long, narrow, compliant tubing and air bubbles.</li><li><strong>Damping:</strong> anything that dissipates energy and reduces oscillation (friction, air bubbles, clots, kinks, narrow tubing).<ul><li><strong>Damping coefficient (ζ)</strong>: <strong>critically damped ζ = 1</strong> (fastest response without overshoot, but too slow for waveforms); <strong>optimal damping ζ ≈ 0.64</strong> (fast response with minimal overshoot, ~7%; best frequency response).</li><li><strong>Underdamped</strong> (ζ &lt; 0.64): overshoot → <strong>overestimated systolic, underestimated diastolic</strong>, \"ringing\" waveform. <strong>Overdamped:</strong> flattened trace → <strong>underestimated systolic, overestimated diastolic</strong>. The <strong>mean pressure is relatively unaffected</strong>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Square-wave (fast flush) test:</strong> a brief flush produces a square wave followed by oscillations: counting and measuring these estimates the natural frequency and damping. One or two oscillations before settling indicates acceptable damping.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> definitions (drift, hysteresis, precision); natural frequency and damping; effects on systolic and diastolic readings.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpreting a \"ringing\" arterial trace and a flattened one, and fixing each.</li></ul></div>","mh-dantrolene":"<h2 id=\"pathophysiology\">Pathophysiology</h2>\n<ul><li>An inherited (autosomal dominant, variable penetrance) disorder, usually of the skeletal muscle <strong>ryanodine receptor (RYR1)</strong>, less often <strong>CACNA1S</strong> (dihydropyridine receptor) or STAC3.</li><li><strong>Triggers:</strong> all <strong>volatile anaesthetics</strong> and <strong>suxamethonium</strong>. (N<sub>2</sub>O, IV agents, opioids, local anaesthetics, non-depolarising NMBs are safe.)</li><li>Uncontrolled Ca<sup>2+</sup> release from the SR → sustained contraction and hypermetabolism → ↑ O<sub>2</sub> consumption and <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> production</strong>, heat, lactic acidosis, rigidity, rhabdomyolysis (<strong>hyperkalaemia</strong>, myoglobinuria, raised CK), arrhythmias, DIC.</li><li>Estimated incidence of reactions of about 1 in 10,000–250,000 anaesthetics; genetic prevalence much higher.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"recognising-a-crisis\">Recognising a crisis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Early signs:</strong> unexplained <strong>rising ETCO<sub>2</sub></strong> despite increased ventilation, tachycardia, masseter spasm after suxamethonium, generalised rigidity, rising O<sub>2</sub> requirement, soda lime heating up quickly.</li><li><strong>Later:</strong> hyperthermia (can be late), metabolic and respiratory acidosis, hyperkalaemia, arrhythmias, myoglobinuria.</li><li><strong>Differential diagnosis:</strong> light anaesthesia, sepsis, thyroid storm, phaeochromocytoma, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, serotonin syndrome, overheating, hypercapnia from laparoscopy or rebreathing.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"management-see-the-qrh-mh-card-and-association-of-anaesthetists-guideline\">Management (see the QRH MH card and Association of Anaesthetists guideline)</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Call for help</strong>; declare the emergency; get dantrolene and the MH kit.</li><li><strong>Stop triggers:</strong> turn off the vaporiser; high-flow 100% O<sub>2</sub>; insert <strong>activated charcoal filters</strong> in the breathing system (or change the machine); maintain anaesthesia with <strong>TIVA</strong>. Hyperventilate.</li><li><strong>Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV</strong>, then 1 mg/kg boluses as needed (up to about 10 mg/kg). Each vial is 20 mg (with 3 g of mannitol), reconstituted with <strong>60 mL sterile water</strong>: needs several people. A newer formulation (e.g. Ryanodex) is more concentrated.</li><li><strong>Treat complications:</strong> active cooling (stop when &lt; 38.5°C), hyperkalaemia (insulin–glucose, calcium), acidosis, arrhythmias (avoid calcium channel blockers with dantrolene); aim for urine output &gt; 2 mL/kg/h (myoglobinuria); check CK, coagulation.</li><li><strong>Transfer to ICU</strong>; watch for recrudescence over 24 h.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"dantrolene\">Dantrolene</h2>\n<p>Binds RyR1 and reduces Ca<sup>2+</sup> release from the SR (skeletal muscle only; no effect on the NMJ). Side effects: muscle weakness (respiratory in patients with poor reserve), phlebitis (high pH), hepatotoxicity with chronic oral use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"afterwards\">Afterwards</h2>\n<p>Refer the patient (and family) to the <strong>UK MH investigation unit (Leeds)</strong> for <strong>in vitro contracture testing</strong> of a muscle biopsy and/or genetic testing; counsel, document, and give an alert card. Known or suspected MH patients have trigger-free anaesthesia (vapour-free machine prepared per guidance or charcoal filters) and are first on the list where possible.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> triggers; genetics; dantrolene reconstitution and mechanism; early versus late signs.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the ETCO<sub>2</sub> is rising and the patient is tachycardic 30 minutes into a sevoflurane anaesthetic: lead the crisis.</li></ul></div>","monitoring-standards":"<h2 id=\"recommendations-for-standards-of-monitoring-2021-the-essentials\">Recommendations for standards of monitoring (2021) — the essentials</h2>\n<ul><li>The <strong>anaesthetist must be present</strong> throughout general anaesthesia (and sedation they provide), with a clear handover if they leave.</li><li><strong>Before induction:</strong> pulse oximetry, NIBP, ECG and capnography should be in use (induction may start before all are attached in uncooperative patients or children, but monitoring should follow promptly).</li><li><strong>During general anaesthesia:</strong> continuous <strong>pulse oximetry</strong>, <strong>NIBP</strong> (at least every 5 minutes), <strong>ECG</strong>, <strong>airway gases</strong> (oxygen, CO<sub>2</sub> with waveform capnography, and vapour concentration), <strong>airway pressure</strong> (for ventilated patients), <strong>peripheral nerve stimulator (quantitative) whenever neuromuscular blockers are used</strong>, <strong>temperature</strong> (for procedures over 30 minutes), and <strong>processed EEG (depth of anaesthesia) monitoring with TIVA when neuromuscular blockers are used</strong>.</li><li><strong>Sedation:</strong> capnography for moderate and deep sedation.</li><li><strong>Recovery:</strong> pulse oximetry, NIBP, capnography if the airway is still in place, and other monitoring as indicated; a trained member of staff for each patient until they can maintain their airway.</li><li><strong>Transfer:</strong> the same standard as in theatre, including waveform capnography for intubated patients.</li><li>Alarm limits should be set appropriately and alarms left on.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"point-of-care-near-patient-testing\">Point-of-care (near-patient) testing</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Principle</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Haemoglobin</strong> (e.g. HemoCue)</td><td>Spectrophotometry of converted haemoglobin (azide-methaemoglobin)</td><td>Capillary samples less accurate; technique</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Glucose</strong></td><td>Glucose oxidase or dehydrogenase electrochemical strips</td><td>Inaccurate with poor perfusion, extremes of haematocrit, some interfering substances</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Blood gases and electrolytes</strong></td><td>Electrodes (see gas analysis), ion-selective electrodes</td><td>Calibration and QC essential</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coagulation</strong>: ACT, INR meters, <strong>viscoelastic (TEG, ROTEM)</strong></td><td>Clot detection in whole blood</td><td>Operator training; quality control</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ketones</strong></td><td>β-hydroxybutyrate strips</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Lactate</td><td>Enzymatic electrode</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Point-of-care devices need training, quality control, and connection to the patient record; results should be interpreted in clinical context and confirmed by the lab when needed.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> what is mandatory and when; the 2021 additions (quantitative NMB monitoring, processed EEG with TIVA and paralysis).</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a colleague wants to start a case before the capnograph is working: what do you say?</li></ul></div>","neuraxial-basics":"<h2 id=\"indications-and-contraindications\">Indications and contraindications</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Uses:</strong> lower limb, pelvic, perineal and lower abdominal surgery; caesarean section; labour analgesia; postoperative analgesia.</li><li><strong>Absolute contraindications:</strong> patient refusal, infection at the site, raised ICP with mass effect, allergy to local anaesthetic, uncorrected hypovolaemia (relative in some texts).</li><li><strong>Relative:</strong> coagulopathy and anticoagulants (see timing guidance), systemic sepsis, fixed cardiac output states (severe aortic stenosis), spinal deformity or surgery, pre-existing neurological disease, inability to cooperate.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"consent\">Consent</h2>\n<p>Discuss: common (hypotension, itching with opioids, urinary retention, failed or incomplete block needing supplementation or GA), less common (<strong>post-dural puncture headache</strong> ~1 in 100–200 for spinals with fine pencil-point needles, higher after accidental dural puncture with an epidural needle), rare but serious (nerve damage: temporary about 1 in 1,000–10,000; permanent about 1 in 25,000–50,000 in the NAP3 range; epidural haematoma and abscess; meningitis; high or total spinal; LAST). Use local and national patient information.</p>\n<h2 id=\"technique\">Technique</h2>\n<p>Full asepsis (chlorhexidine 0.5% in alcohol allowed to dry, away from needles and drugs), sitting or lateral position, identify the space (below L2–3 for spinal; ultrasound if difficult), pencil-point 25–27G spinal needle, free flow of CSF before injection. Epidural: Tuohy needle, loss of resistance to saline or air, catheter 4–5 cm into the space, test dose, then incremental dosing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spread-of-spinal-anaesthesia\">Spread of spinal anaesthesia</h2>\n<p>Main factors: <strong>baricity</strong> of the solution relative to CSF (hyperbaric \"heavy\" solutions contain glucose and flow downhill), <strong>patient position</strong> during and after injection, and <strong>dose (mass)</strong> of drug. Others: CSF volume (reduced in pregnancy, obesity), height (minor), curvature of the spine, site of injection, speed of injection.</p>\n<h2 id=\"physiological-effects\">Physiological effects</h2>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic block</strong> (vasodilation → hypotension; bradycardia if above T4, removing cardiac accelerator fibres; Bezold–Jarisch reflex with severe hypovolaemia), reduced respiratory reserve with high blocks (intercostal paralysis), gut contraction (unopposed vagal tone), urinary retention.</p>\n<h2 id=\"complications\">Complications</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hypotension:</strong> fluids, vasopressors (phenylephrine or metaraminol; ephedrine if bradycardic), uterine displacement in pregnancy.</li><li><strong>High or total spinal:</strong> rising block with arm weakness, dyspnoea, difficulty speaking, bradycardia, hypotension, loss of consciousness: support airway and breathing (intubate), vasopressors, atropine, reassure; the block will recede.</li><li><strong>Accidental dural puncture and PDPH:</strong> postural (worse sitting or standing), fronto-occipital headache, neck stiffness, possible cranial nerve palsies (abducens), within days. Conservative management (analgesia, hydration, caffeine has limited evidence), then <strong>epidural blood patch</strong> for significant symptoms; exclude other causes (e.g. cerebral venous thrombosis, subdural haematoma, pre-eclampsia).</li><li><strong>Failed or inadequate block:</strong> test the block before surgery; options include repeat block, supplementation or GA.</li><li><strong>Serious:</strong> epidural haematoma and abscess — new or worsening leg weakness or numbness after block should have worn off, back pain, sphincter disturbance → <strong>urgent MRI and neurosurgical referral</strong> (decompression within hours). Monitor motor function after neuraxial blocks (neurological monitoring guidance).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> factors affecting spread; PDPH features and treatment; physiological effects; anticoagulant timing.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> consent a patient for a spinal for knee arthroplasty, then manage hypotension and a patient who complains of arm tingling.</li></ul></div>","neuro-ans":"<h2 id=\"anatomy\">Anatomy</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Sympathetic</th><th>Parasympathetic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Outflow</td><td><strong>Thoracolumbar (T1–L2)</strong></td><td><strong>Craniosacral</strong>: cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X and S2–4</td></tr><tr><td>Ganglia</td><td>Paravertebral chain (and prevertebral: coeliac, mesenteric) — close to the cord</td><td>In or near the target organ</td></tr><tr><td>Preganglionic fibre</td><td>Short, myelinated (B fibres), via <strong>white rami</strong></td><td>Long</td></tr><tr><td>Postganglionic fibre</td><td>Long, unmyelinated (C fibres), via <strong>grey rami</strong> to spinal nerves</td><td>Short</td></tr><tr><td>Ganglionic transmitter</td><td>ACh (nicotinic)</td><td>ACh (nicotinic)</td></tr><tr><td>Postganglionic transmitter</td><td><strong>Noradrenaline</strong> (except sweat glands: ACh, muscarinic)</td><td><strong>ACh</strong> (muscarinic)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The <strong>adrenal medulla</strong> is a modified sympathetic ganglion: preganglionic fibres release ACh onto chromaffin cells, which secrete mainly <strong>adrenaline</strong> (about 80%) into the blood.</li><li>The <strong>stellate ganglion</strong> (fused inferior cervical and first thoracic ganglia) lies anterior to the neck of the first rib at C7; blocking it causes <strong>Horner's syndrome</strong> (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) and can treat upper limb sympathetic pain.</li><li>Cardiac sympathetic fibres come from <strong>T1–T4</strong> (some texts include T5): a high neuraxial block above T4 removes cardiac accelerator fibres, allowing bradycardia.</li><li><strong>Lateralisation at the heart:</strong> the right vagus mainly supplies the SA node (rate) and the left vagus the AV node (conduction); right-sided sympathetic fibres mainly affect rate and left-sided fibres contractility.</li><li>Sympathetic stimulation is also <strong>lusitropic</strong>: faster relaxation, which protects diastolic filling time at high rates.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"receptors\">Receptors</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Receptor</th><th>Second messenger</th><th>Main effects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>α<sub>1</sub></td><td>G<sub>q</sub> → IP<sub>3</sub>/DAG → ↑ Ca<sup>2+</sup></td><td>Vasoconstriction, mydriasis, bladder sphincter contraction</td></tr><tr><td>α<sub>2</sub></td><td>G<sub>i</sub> → ↓ cAMP</td><td>Presynaptic inhibition of NA release; central sedation and analgesia (clonidine, dexmedetomidine)</td></tr><tr><td>β<sub>1</sub></td><td>G<sub>s</sub> → ↑ cAMP</td><td>↑ Heart rate, contractility, conduction; renin release</td></tr><tr><td>β<sub>2</sub></td><td>G<sub>s</sub> → ↑ cAMP</td><td>Bronchodilation, vasodilation (muscle), uterine relaxation, glycogenolysis, K<sup>+</sup> shift into cells</td></tr><tr><td>M<sub>2</sub></td><td>G<sub>i</sub></td><td>↓ Heart rate and AV conduction</td></tr><tr><td>M<sub>3</sub></td><td>G<sub>q</sub></td><td>Bronchoconstriction, secretions, gut motility, bladder contraction, endothelial NO (vasodilation)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"transmitter-handling\">Transmitter handling</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Noradrenaline:</strong> tyrosine → DOPA → dopamine → noradrenaline (→ adrenaline by PNMT in the adrenal medulla). Action ended mainly by <strong>reuptake (uptake 1)</strong> into the nerve terminal, then metabolism by <strong>MAO</strong> and <strong>COMT</strong> (urinary VMA).</li><li><strong>ACh:</strong> from choline and acetyl-CoA (choline acetyltransferase); hydrolysed by acetylcholinesterase.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"physiological-balance\">Physiological balance</h2>\n<p>Most organs have dual innervation; at rest the heart is under dominant <strong>vagal tone</strong> (intrinsic SA rate about 100/min falls to about 70/min), while vessels are under <strong>sympathetic tone</strong> (few blood vessels have parasympathetic innervation). Loss of sympathetic tone with neuraxial block or anaesthesia → vasodilation and hypotension.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> receptor subtypes and second messengers; transmitter synthesis and metabolism; anatomy of the chain and stellate ganglion.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining bradycardia and hypotension after a high spinal for caesarean section, and choosing between phenylephrine, ephedrine and atropine.</li></ul></div>","neuro-icp-cbf":"<h2 id=\"monro-kellie-doctrine\">Monro–Kellie doctrine</h2>\n<p>The skull is a rigid box containing <strong>brain (~80%), blood (~10%) and CSF (~10%)</strong>. An increase in one component (tumour, haematoma, oedema) must be offset by a decrease in another, first by displacing CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and venous blood out of the skull. Once compensation is exhausted, <strong>ICP rises steeply</strong> for small increases in volume (the exponential <strong>ICP–volume (elastance) curve</strong>).</p>\n<ul><li>Normal ICP: <strong>5–15 mmHg</strong> (supine adult).</li><li><strong>CPP = MAP − ICP</strong> (or − CVP if higher). Adult targets after severe head injury are usually a CPP of about <strong>60–70 mmHg</strong> and ICP under about <strong>20–22 mmHg</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"regulation-of-cerebral-blood-flow\">Regulation of cerebral blood flow</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> holds flow roughly constant over a range of MAP (classically 50–150 mmHg; wider variation between individuals than once thought), via myogenic and metabolic mechanisms.</li><li><strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub></strong>: the most potent short-term controller (vasodilation with hypercapnia). Hyperventilation lowers ICP within minutes but risks ischaemia; it is reserved for short-term rescue of impending herniation.</li><li><strong>PaO<sub>2</sub></strong>: flow rises sharply below about 6.7–8 kPa.</li><li><strong>Metabolic coupling:</strong> CMRO<sub>2</sub> about 3.5 mL/100 g/min; reduced by propofol, barbiturates, hypothermia; increased by seizures and fever.</li><li><strong>Volatile agents</strong> are cerebral vasodilators (dose-dependent, increasing flow while reducing metabolism: \"uncoupling\"); propofol preserves coupling and reduces flow and ICP. Ketamine was traditionally avoided but has little effect on ICP when ventilation is controlled.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"csf\">CSF</h2>\n<ul><li>About <strong>500 mL/day</strong> is produced, mainly by the <strong>choroid plexus</strong> (active secretion of Na<sup>+</sup>, Cl<sup>−</sup> and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>, with water following); total volume about <strong>150 mL</strong> (roughly half in the spinal compartment).</li><li>Circulation: lateral ventricles → foramina of Monro → third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → foramina of Magendie and Luschka → subarachnoid space → reabsorbed through <strong>arachnoid granulations</strong> into the venous sinuses.</li><li>Compared with plasma: similar Na<sup>+</sup>, <strong>higher Cl<sup>−</sup></strong>, <strong>lower glucose (~60% of plasma)</strong>, lower K<sup>+</sup>, very little protein, slightly lower pH. CSF pH is the stimulus for central chemoreceptors.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"signs-of-raised-icp-and-herniation\">Signs of raised ICP and herniation</h2>\n<p>Headache, vomiting, papilloedema, falling GCS; <strong>Cushing's response</strong> (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) is a late sign of brainstem compression; a fixed dilated pupil suggests uncal herniation compressing the oculomotor nerve.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the ICP–volume curve; CPP calculation; effect of anaesthetic drugs on CBF and CMRO<sub>2</sub>; CSF composition.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a head-injured patient before transfer: what physiological targets (PaO<sub>2</sub>, PaCO<sub>2</sub>, MAP, glucose, temperature, head position) protect the brain, and why?</li></ul></div>","neuro-membrane-ap":"<h2 id=\"resting-membrane-potential\">Resting membrane potential</h2>\n<ul><li>Set mainly by <strong>K<sup>+</sup> permeability</strong>: the resting membrane is far more permeable to K<sup>+</sup> than to Na<sup>+</sup>, so the potential (about <strong>−70 mV in neurones, −90 mV in muscle</strong>) lies close to the K<sup>+</sup> equilibrium potential.</li><li><strong>Nernst equation</strong> (one ion): <strong>E = (RT / zF) ln([ion]<sub>out</sub> / [ion]<sub>in</sub>)</strong> ≈ 61.5 log<sub>10</sub>([out]/[in]) mV at 37°C for a monovalent cation. E<sub>K</sub> ≈ −90 mV; E<sub>Na</sub> ≈ +60 mV.</li><li><strong>Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equation</strong> combines all permeant ions weighted by their permeability.</li><li>The <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase</strong> (3 Na<sup>+</sup> out, 2 K<sup>+</sup> in) maintains the gradients and is slightly electrogenic; it is not what generates most of the resting potential.</li><li><strong>Raising extracellular K<sup>+</sup></strong> makes the resting potential less negative (depolarises), because E<sub>K</sub> changes. Changing extracellular Na<sup>+</sup> has little effect at rest because the membrane is barely permeable to Na<sup>+</sup>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-action-potential\">The action potential</h2>\n<ol><li>A stimulus depolarises the membrane to <strong>threshold (~−55 mV)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> open (positive feedback): rapid depolarisation towards E<sub>Na</sub> (overshoot to about +30 mV).</li><li>Na<sup>+</sup> channels <strong>inactivate</strong> (the inactivation gate closes) within about 1 ms; <strong>voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> open more slowly → repolarisation and a brief <strong>after-hyperpolarisation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Absolute refractory period:</strong> Na<sup>+</sup> channels inactivated; no stimulus can trigger another action potential. <strong>Relative refractory period:</strong> a larger stimulus is needed.</li><li><strong>All-or-none:</strong> intensity is coded by frequency, not amplitude.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"conduction\">Conduction</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Fibre</th><th>Myelin</th><th>Diameter (µm)</th><th>Velocity (m/s)</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aα</td><td>Yes</td><td>12–20</td><td>70–120</td><td>Motor, proprioception</td></tr><tr><td>Aβ</td><td>Yes</td><td>5–12</td><td>30–70</td><td>Touch, pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Aγ</td><td>Yes</td><td>3–6</td><td>15–30</td><td>Muscle spindle motor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aδ</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>2–5</td><td>12–30</td><td><strong>Fast, sharp pain</strong>, cold</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>Yes (thin)</td><td>&lt; 3</td><td>3–15</td><td><strong>Preganglionic autonomic</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong></td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>0.4–1.2</td><td>0.5–2</td><td><strong>Slow, dull pain</strong>, warmth, postganglionic autonomic</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Velocity rises with <strong>diameter</strong> (lower internal resistance) and <strong>myelination</strong> (saltatory conduction between <strong>nodes of Ranvier</strong>, where Na<sup>+</sup> channels cluster).</li><li><strong>Local anaesthetic sensitivity:</strong> thin fibres are generally blocked before thick ones, and myelinated fibres before unmyelinated fibres of similar diameter (about three consecutive nodes must be blocked to stop conduction). Clinically the order is sympathetic (B) → temperature and pinprick (Aδ, C) → touch (Aβ) → motor (Aα), which is why sympathetic and sensory blocks extend higher than motor block after a spinal.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Nernst calculations; effect of changing extracellular K<sup>+</sup> or Na<sup>+</sup>; fibre types; differential block.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why a spinal block tested with cold spray is higher than with light touch, and what that means for surgery.</li></ul></div>","neuro-nmj-muscle":"<h2 id=\"the-neuromuscular-junction\">The neuromuscular junction</h2>\n<ol><li>The motor nerve action potential opens <strong>voltage-gated Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</strong> in the terminal.</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> entry triggers <strong>exocytosis of acetylcholine</strong> (quanta of about 5,000–10,000 molecules per vesicle) through <strong>SNARE proteins</strong> that fuse the vesicle with the membrane; <strong>botulinum toxin</strong> cleaves SNARE proteins and prevents release.</li><li>ACh crosses the cleft and binds the <strong>nicotinic receptor</strong> (a ligand-gated ion channel of five subunits: <strong>2α, β, δ, ε</strong> in adults; γ replaces ε in fetal and extrajunctional receptors). <strong>Both α subunits</strong> must be occupied to open the channel.</li><li>Na<sup>+</sup> in and K<sup>+</sup> out produce the <strong>end-plate potential</strong>; if it reaches threshold, a muscle action potential spreads.</li><li><strong>Acetylcholinesterase</strong> in the cleft hydrolyses ACh within about 1 ms.</li><li><strong>Large safety margin:</strong> about 70–80% of receptors must be blocked before twitch height falls, and over 90% for complete block.</li><li><strong>Prejunctional nicotinic receptors</strong> (α3β2) mobilise ACh during high-frequency stimulation; blocking them by non-depolarising drugs is thought to cause <strong>fade</strong>.</li><li><strong>Upregulation</strong> of extrajunctional (immature, fetal-type and α7) receptors after denervation, burns, prolonged immobility or upper motor neurone injury → exaggerated K<sup>+</sup> release with suxamethonium and resistance to non-depolarising drugs.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"skeletal-muscle-excitation-contraction-coupling\">Skeletal muscle excitation–contraction coupling</h2>\n<ul><li>The action potential travels down the <strong>T-tubules</strong>; the <strong>dihydropyridine receptor</strong> (voltage sensor) is <strong>mechanically coupled</strong> to the <strong>ryanodine receptor (RyR1)</strong> on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, releasing Ca<sup>2+</sup> (no calcium-induced release needed, unlike the heart).</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds <strong>troponin C</strong> → tropomyosin moves → cross-bridge cycling. ATP is needed both to detach myosin and to pump Ca<sup>2+</sup> back (SERCA).</li><li><strong>Malignant hyperthermia:</strong> abnormal RyR1 (or the dihydropyridine receptor, CACNA1S) → uncontrolled Ca<sup>2+</sup> release with volatile agents or suxamethonium.</li><li><strong>Motor unit:</strong> one motor neurone and all the fibres it supplies (few fibres for fine control, e.g. extraocular muscles; thousands in large limb muscles). Force is graded by recruitment and firing rate (summation, tetanus).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"smooth-muscle\">Smooth muscle</h2>\n<ul><li>No troponin; Ca<sup>2+</sup> binds <strong>calmodulin</strong>, which activates <strong>myosin light-chain kinase</strong>. Relaxation by myosin light-chain phosphatase.</li><li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> comes from the extracellular space and the SR (IP<sub>3</sub> receptors). Slow, sustained contraction (\"latch\" state) with low energy cost.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"disorders-relevant-to-anaesthesia\">Disorders relevant to anaesthesia</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Problem</th><th>Anaesthetic implications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Myasthenia gravis</td><td>Antibodies to postjunctional ACh receptors</td><td>Sensitive to non-depolarising NMBs; resistant to suxamethonium; postoperative respiratory weakness</td></tr><tr><td>Lambert–Eaton syndrome</td><td>Antibodies to presynaptic Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels (often small-cell lung cancer)</td><td>Sensitive to both depolarising and non-depolarising NMBs</td></tr><tr><td>Muscular dystrophies</td><td>Muscle membrane defects</td><td>Suxamethonium can cause hyperkalaemia and rhabdomyolysis; cardiomyopathy</td></tr><tr><td>Myotonic dystrophy</td><td>Abnormal chloride channels (myotonia)</td><td>Suxamethonium may cause sustained contraction; sensitive to respiratory depressants</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> receptor subunits; safety margin; how burns and denervation change responses; skeletal versus smooth versus cardiac coupling.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning neuromuscular block for a patient with myasthenia gravis, or explaining why suxamethonium is avoided two weeks after a major burn.</li></ul></div>","neuro-pain-physiology":"<h2 id=\"nociceptors-and-afferents\">Nociceptors and afferents</h2>\n<ul><li>Free nerve endings responding to mechanical, thermal and chemical stimuli. <strong>Aδ fibres</strong> (thinly myelinated): fast, sharp, well-localised \"first pain\". <strong>C fibres</strong> (unmyelinated): slow, dull, burning, poorly localised \"second pain\".</li><li>Inflammatory mediators (bradykinin, prostaglandins, H<sup>+</sup>, histamine, serotonin, nerve growth factor) sensitise nociceptors: <strong>peripheral sensitisation</strong> → primary hyperalgesia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-dorsal-horn\">The dorsal horn</h2>\n<ul><li>Afferents enter via the dorsal root, travel a segment or two in <strong>Lissauer's tract</strong> and synapse in the <strong>dorsal horn laminae</strong>: Aδ in laminae I and V; C fibres in laminae I and <strong>II (substantia gelatinosa)</strong>.</li><li>Transmitters: <strong>glutamate</strong> (AMPA receptors for fast transmission; <strong>NMDA</strong> receptors recruited with sustained input), <strong>substance P</strong> and <strong>CGRP</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gate control theory (Melzack and Wall):</strong> activity in large Aβ touch fibres activates inhibitory interneurones in lamina II that reduce nociceptive transmission (the basis for rubbing an injury and TENS).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ascending-pathways\">Ascending pathways</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Spinothalamic tract:</strong> second-order neurones <strong>cross within one or two segments</strong> in the anterior white commissure and ascend in the anterolateral cord to the <strong>thalamus</strong> (lateral: discriminative; medial: affective), then to the somatosensory and cingulate cortex and insula.</li><li>Spinoreticular and spinomesencephalic tracts contribute to arousal and emotional aspects.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"descending-modulation\">Descending modulation</h2>\n<p>From the <strong>periaqueductal grey</strong> and <strong>rostroventral medulla</strong> to the dorsal horn, using <strong>endogenous opioids</strong>, <strong>noradrenaline</strong> (α<sub>2</sub>) and <strong>serotonin</strong>. This is a target for opioids, and for tricyclics, SNRIs and α<sub>2</sub>-agonists.</p>\n<h2 id=\"central-sensitisation-and-chronic-pain\">Central sensitisation and chronic pain</h2>\n<ul><li>Repeated C-fibre input → <strong>wind-up</strong> (NMDA-dependent increase in dorsal horn response), expanded receptive fields, <strong>secondary hyperalgesia</strong> and <strong>allodynia</strong> (pain from normally non-painful stimuli).</li><li><strong>Neuropathic pain</strong> follows damage to the nervous system: ectopic discharges, sodium channel changes, loss of inhibition. Treated with gabapentinoids, tricyclics and SNRIs rather than simple analgesics.</li><li><strong>Visceral pain</strong> is poorly localised and <strong>referred</strong> to the dermatomes that share spinal segments (e.g. the heart to T1–T4 and the left arm).</li><li><strong>Labour pain:</strong> first stage via visceral afferents to <strong>T10–L1</strong>; second stage adds somatic pain from the perineum via the <strong>pudendal nerve (S2–4)</strong>.</li><li>Acute pain that is poorly controlled after surgery is a risk factor for <strong>chronic post-surgical pain</strong>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> fibre types and laminae; where the spinothalamic tract crosses; sites of action of analgesic classes; labour pain pathways.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining multimodal analgesia to a patient, or planning analgesia for a patient with established neuropathic pain having surgery.</li></ul></div>","nm-monitoring":"<h2 id=\"nerve-stimulation\">Nerve stimulation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Supramaximal</strong> stimulus (about 10–20% above the current needed to recruit all fibres; typically 50–60 mA), <strong>square wave</strong>, <strong>short duration (0.1–0.3 ms)</strong>, constant current. Usually at the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> (adductor pollicis), or the facial nerve (orbicularis oculi or corrugator supercilii) or posterior tibial nerve when the arm is inaccessible.</li><li>Muscles differ in sensitivity: the <strong>diaphragm and laryngeal muscles are more resistant</strong> (recover earlier) than the adductor pollicis; <strong>corrugator supercilii</strong> mirrors the laryngeal muscles (onset); orbicularis oculi may underestimate block.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"patterns\">Patterns</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Description</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Single twitch</strong></td><td>0.1–1 Hz</td><td>Needs a baseline; research</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Train-of-four (TOF)</strong></td><td>4 stimuli at <strong>2 Hz</strong></td><td><strong>TOF count</strong> (0–4) during deep–moderate block; <strong>TOF ratio</strong> (T4/T1) during recovery: <strong>≥ 0.9 before extubation</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Double burst stimulation (DBS)</strong></td><td>Two short bursts of 50 Hz tetanus 750 ms apart</td><td>Fade easier to feel than with TOF (but still unreliable above a ratio of ~0.6)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tetanic stimulation</strong></td><td>50 Hz for 5 s</td><td>Fade with non-depolarising block; painful; affects later readings at that site for minutes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Post-tetanic count (PTC)</strong></td><td>Single twitches at 1 Hz after 5 s tetanus</td><td><strong>Deep block</strong> when TOF count is 0; a lower PTC means deeper block</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Depolarising (phase I) block:</strong> reduced but equal responses, no fade, no post-tetanic potentiation. <strong>Non-depolarising block and phase II:</strong> fade and post-tetanic potentiation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"quantitative-monitors\">Quantitative monitors</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Acceleromyography</strong> (acceleration of the thumb ∝ force), <strong>electromyography</strong> (compound muscle action potential; not affected by movement), <strong>kinemyography</strong>, mechanomyography (the research standard).</li><li>The Association of Anaesthetists (2021) recommends <strong>quantitative monitoring whenever neuromuscular blocking drugs are used</strong>, from induction until recovery is confirmed with a TOF ratio ≥ 0.9.</li><li>Acceleromyography may overestimate the TOF ratio at baseline (normalising to baseline helps).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> stimulus characteristics; TOF, DBS, PTC; site differences; depolarising versus non-depolarising patterns.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the TOF count is 0 twenty minutes before the end of a laparoscopic case: what are your reversal options?</li></ul></div>","nmb-nondepolarising":"<h2 id=\"mechanism\">Mechanism</h2>\n<p><strong>Competitive antagonists</strong> at the postjunctional nicotinic receptor (binding one α subunit is enough to prevent opening). Block at the prejunctional receptor reduces ACh mobilisation, causing <strong>fade</strong> on train-of-four and tetanic stimulation. They are <strong>quaternary ammonium</strong> compounds: they do not cross the BBB or placenta and have small volumes of distribution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"classes\">Classes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Aminosteroids</strong></th><th><strong>Benzylisoquinoliniums</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Drugs</td><td>Rocuronium, vecuronium, pancuronium</td><td>Atracurium, cisatracurium, mivacurium</td></tr><tr><td>Histamine release</td><td>Minimal</td><td>Atracurium and mivacurium (flushing, hypotension)</td></tr><tr><td>Vagolytic</td><td>Pancuronium (tachycardia)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Reversal by sugammadex</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (rocuronium &gt; vecuronium)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Elimination</td><td>Hepatic/biliary and renal</td><td><strong>Hofmann degradation and esterases</strong> (atracurium, cisatracurium); plasma cholinesterase (mivacurium)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"individual-drugs-typical-adult-intubating-doses\">Individual drugs (typical adult intubating doses)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Rocuronium</strong> 0.6 mg/kg (1–1.2 mg/kg for RSI, giving intubating conditions in about 60 s); duration ~30–40 min at 0.6 mg/kg; largely unchanged in bile; prolonged in liver failure.</li><li><strong>Vecuronium</strong> 0.1 mg/kg; powder; active 3-desacetyl metabolite accumulates in renal failure.</li><li><strong>Atracurium</strong> 0.5 mg/kg; Hofmann degradation (slowed by hypothermia and acidosis) and ester hydrolysis; <strong>laudanosine</strong> metabolite; histamine release.</li><li><strong>Cisatracurium</strong> 0.15 mg/kg; a single stereoisomer, ~4× as potent; mainly Hofmann; minimal histamine release; ideal in hepatic and renal failure; slower onset.</li><li><strong>Mivacurium:</strong> short-acting; hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase (prolonged in cholinesterase deficiency).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Check the BNF and local guidance for doses.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"onset-the-bowman-principle\">Onset: the Bowman principle</h2>\n<p><strong>Less potent drugs have a faster onset</strong>, because a larger dose (more molecules) is given, so more molecules reach the junction quickly (law of mass action). Rocuronium is about one sixth as potent as vecuronium and has a faster onset. Onset is also faster with higher dose and cardiac output, and in muscles with high blood flow (the diaphragm and larynx are affected before the adductor pollicis but are more resistant).</p>\n<h2 id=\"factors-potentiating-block\">Factors potentiating block</h2>\n<p>Volatile agents; <strong>aminoglycosides</strong>, magnesium, lithium, calcium channel blockers, local anaesthetics; hypothermia; acidosis; hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia, hypermagnesaemia; myasthenia gravis and Lambert–Eaton; neonates (for some drugs); hepatic or renal failure (drug-dependent).</p>\n<p><strong>Resistance:</strong> burns, denervation, prolonged immobility (upregulated receptors); anticonvulsants (chronic phenytoin, carbamazepine); hypercalcaemia.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> structures and elimination routes; Bowman principle; potentiating factors; Hofmann degradation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing a neuromuscular blocker for a patient with renal and hepatic failure, or for an RSI where suxamethonium is contraindicated.</li></ul></div>","nmb-reversal":"<h2 id=\"anticholinesterases\">Anticholinesterases</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Neostigmine</strong> (a quaternary carbamate) inhibits acetylcholinesterase, raising ACh at the junction to outcompete the blocker. Typical adult dose about 50 µg/kg up to 2.5–5 mg (check local guidance), with an <strong>antimuscarinic</strong> (glycopyrronium, or atropine) to block the muscarinic effects.</li><li><strong>Ceiling effect:</strong> once acetylcholinesterase is fully inhibited, more neostigmine adds nothing. It <strong>cannot reverse deep block</strong> and should be given only when there is evidence of recovery (e.g. <strong>at least 2 and ideally 4 twitches</strong> on TOF). Peak effect takes about <strong>10 minutes</strong>.</li><li><strong>Muscarinic effects:</strong> bradycardia, bronchospasm, secretions, gut and bladder stimulation, PONV. Excessive doses may cause a paradoxical depolarising-type weakness.</li><li><strong>Prolongs suxamethonium</strong> (inhibits plasma cholinesterase too).</li><li>Other anticholinesterases: <strong>edrophonium</strong> (short-acting, reversible; formerly used to diagnose myasthenia), <strong>pyridostigmine</strong> (oral, myasthenia gravis), <strong>physostigmine</strong> (tertiary; crosses BBB; central anticholinergic syndrome), and irreversible <strong>organophosphates</strong> (nerve agents, insecticides → cholinergic crisis; treat with atropine, oximes, benzodiazepines).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sugammadex\">Sugammadex</h2>\n<ul><li>A modified <strong>γ-cyclodextrin</strong> that <strong>encapsulates</strong> aminosteroid molecules (rocuronium &gt; vecuronium; not benzylisoquinoliniums) in plasma in a 1:1 complex, creating a concentration gradient that draws drug away from the junction. Excreted renally as the complex.</li><li>Dose by depth of block (per product information): lower dose for moderate block, higher for deep block, and a high dose for <strong>immediate reversal</strong> shortly after rocuronium in a \"can't intubate\" scenario (but reversal of paralysis does not solve an obstructed airway).</li><li>No muscarinic effects, so no anticholinergic needed. Issues: hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis, bradycardia (rare), reduced efficacy of <strong>hormonal contraceptives</strong> (advise extra contraception for 7 days), interference with some coagulation assays, not recommended in severe renal impairment. Re-blocking with rocuronium soon afterwards requires higher doses or a benzylisoquinolinium.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"adequate-recovery\">Adequate recovery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Quantitative monitoring</strong> is recommended whenever neuromuscular blockers are used (Association of Anaesthetists 2021): extubate only with a <strong>TOF ratio ≥ 0.9</strong> at the adductor pollicis.</li><li>Clinical tests (head lift, grip) and tactile or visual assessment of fade are unreliable: fade is rarely detectable once the TOF ratio exceeds about 0.4.</li><li><strong>Residual block</strong> (TOF &lt; 0.9) impairs pharyngeal function and the hypoxic ventilatory response, increases aspiration risk and postoperative pulmonary complications, and is distressing for the patient.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanism and ceiling effect of neostigmine; sugammadex mechanism and interactions; TOF targets.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient in recovery who is weak and anxious after an anaesthetic with rocuronium: assessment and management.</li></ul></div>","nmb-suxamethonium":"<h2 id=\"mechanism\">Mechanism</h2>\n<ul><li>Two acetylcholine molecules joined together; a <strong>depolarising agonist</strong> at nicotinic receptors. It opens the channel, causing <strong>fasciculations</strong>, then the end-plate stays depolarised and perijunctional Na<sup>+</sup> channels stay <strong>inactivated</strong>, so the muscle cannot respond: <strong>phase I block</strong>.</li><li>Onset about <strong>30–60 s</strong> at 1–1.5 mg/kg; offset in about <strong>5–10 minutes</strong> by diffusion away from the junction and hydrolysis by <strong>plasma cholinesterase</strong> (butyrylcholinesterase) in plasma (there is little at the junction).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"phase-i-versus-phase-ii-block\">Phase I versus phase II block</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Phase I (depolarising)</th><th>Phase II</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>When</td><td>Normal dose</td><td>Large or repeated doses, infusions, abnormal cholinesterase</td></tr><tr><td>Train-of-four</td><td>Reduced equally, <strong>no fade</strong></td><td><strong>Fade</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Post-tetanic facilitation</td><td>Absent</td><td>Present</td></tr><tr><td>Anticholinesterase</td><td><strong>Worsens</strong> block</td><td>May partly reverse (unpredictable)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"side-effects\">Side effects</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hyperkalaemia:</strong> normally a rise of about <strong>0.5 mmol/L</strong>; life-threatening rises with <strong>upregulated extrajunctional receptors</strong>: burns, denervation (spinal cord injury, stroke), prolonged immobility, muscular dystrophies, and severe crush or abdominal sepsis. Risk begins about 24–48 h after injury.</li><li><strong>Bradycardia</strong> (muscarinic stimulation of the SA node), especially after a second dose and in children; tachycardia can also occur.</li><li><strong>Myalgia</strong> (young adults, early mobilisation), <strong>raised intraocular, intragastric and intracranial pressure</strong> (small, transient), <strong>masseter spasm</strong>.</li><li><strong>Malignant hyperthermia trigger.</strong></li><li><strong>Anaphylaxis</strong> (one of the commoner NMB triggers in NAP6).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"suxamethonium-apnoea\">Suxamethonium apnoea</h2>\n<ul><li>Caused by reduced plasma cholinesterase <strong>activity</strong>:<ul><li><strong>Inherited:</strong> variant genes (e.g. atypical dibucaine-resistant, fluoride-resistant, silent). Homozygotes for the atypical gene may be paralysed for hours; silent homozygotes longer.</li><li><strong>Acquired:</strong> pregnancy, liver disease, renal failure, malnutrition, cardiopulmonary bypass, plasmapheresis, drugs (<strong>neostigmine</strong>, echothiophate, cyclophosphamide, oral contraceptives, ester local anaesthetics competing, esmolol).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Dibucaine number:</strong> the % inhibition of the enzyme by dibucaine; normal about <strong>80</strong>, atypical homozygote about <strong>20</strong>, heterozygote about <strong>40–60</strong>.</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> keep the patient <strong>anaesthetised and ventilated</strong> (awareness is a real risk) until neuromuscular function has recovered on a nerve stimulator; FFP would provide enzyme but is rarely needed. Test the patient and family, warn them, and document (\"alert card\").</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"contraindications\">Contraindications</h2>\n<p>Known MH susceptibility; history of suxamethonium apnoea; hyperkalaemia or risk of hyperkalaemic response (see above); some myopathies; allergy.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanism; phase II features; causes of reduced cholinesterase; dibucaine numbers; conditions with hyperkalaemic response.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient still paralysed after a short procedure: diagnosis, safe management, and explaining it to the patient and family afterwards.</li></ul></div>","nutrition-metabolism":"<h2 id=\"cellular-energy\">Cellular energy</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Glycolysis</strong> (cytoplasm): glucose → 2 pyruvate, net <strong>2 ATP</strong>, no oxygen needed.</li><li><strong>Aerobic:</strong> pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → <strong>Krebs cycle</strong> (mitochondrial matrix) → NADH and FADH<sub>2</sub> → <strong>oxidative phosphorylation</strong> (electron transport chain, inner mitochondrial membrane) → about <strong>30–32 ATP</strong> per glucose in total.</li><li><strong>Cyanide</strong> (smoke inhalation, sodium nitroprusside toxicity) blocks <strong>cytochrome c oxidase (cytochrome a<sub>3</sub>)</strong>, the last step of the electron transport chain: cells cannot use oxygen, so venous PO<sub>2</sub> stays high and lactate rises.</li><li><strong>Anaerobic:</strong> pyruvate → <strong>lactate</strong> (regenerating NAD<sup>+</sup> so glycolysis can continue): only 2 ATP per glucose.</li><li><strong>Lactate</strong> is cleared mainly by the liver (Cori cycle) and kidneys. Raised lactate: <strong>type A</strong> (tissue hypoxia: shock, hypoperfusion) and <strong>type B</strong> (no hypoxia: adrenaline via β<sub>2</sub> stimulation of glycolysis, liver failure, metformin, drugs, malignancy). In sepsis, raised lactate reflects both.</li><li>Fats (β-oxidation) yield the most energy per gram; ketone bodies supply the brain in starvation.</li><li><strong>Glycogen</strong> is stored in muscle (about three quarters of the total, for local use only) and liver (released as glucose; about 24 hours' supply). <strong>Gluconeogenesis</strong> in the liver (and kidney) makes glucose from lactate, amino acids (mainly alanine and glutamine) and glycerol; fatty acids cannot be converted to glucose.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"energy-requirements\">Energy requirements</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Substrate</th><th>Energy (kcal/g)</th><th>Respiratory quotient (CO<sub>2</sub> produced / O<sub>2</sub> consumed)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Carbohydrate</td><td>~4</td><td><strong>1.0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Protein</td><td>~4</td><td>~0.8</td></tr><tr><td>Fat</td><td>~9</td><td><strong>~0.7</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Mixed diet</td><td>—</td><td>~0.8</td></tr><tr><td>Overfeeding / lipogenesis</td><td>—</td><td><strong>&gt; 1.0</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Basal requirement roughly <strong>25 kcal/kg/day</strong> (more with stress, sepsis, burns); protein about 1–1.5 g/kg/day in critical illness. Water about 25–30 mL/kg/day.</li><li><strong>Measurement:</strong> indirect calorimetry (O<sub>2</sub> consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> production) or predictive equations (e.g. Harris–Benedict).</li><li><strong>Overfeeding</strong> with carbohydrate raises CO<sub>2</sub> production (RQ &gt; 1), which can hinder weaning from ventilation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"perioperative-nutrition\">Perioperative nutrition</h2>\n<ul><li>Malnutrition (weight loss, low BMI, poor intake) predicts complications, poor wound healing and infection. Screen (e.g. MUST) and optimise before major elective surgery.</li><li><strong>Enteral feeding</strong> is preferred to parenteral when the gut works (maintains mucosal integrity). Early oral intake after surgery is a principle of enhanced recovery.</li><li><strong>Parenteral nutrition:</strong> central access, risk of line infection, hyperglycaemia, hepatic dysfunction. Watch for <strong>refeeding syndrome</strong> (see starvation).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ATP yields; RQ values; causes of type A and B lactic acidosis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a raised lactate in a patient on an adrenaline infusion: interpreting it and deciding what to do.</li></ul></div>","obstetric-basics":"<h2 id=\"labour-analgesia\">Labour analgesia</h2>\n<ul><li>Options: non-pharmacological, <strong>Entonox</strong>, IM opioids (pethidine, diamorphine), <strong>remifentanil PCA</strong> (needs one-to-one midwifery, SpO<sub>2</sub> monitoring and oxygen), and <strong>epidural or combined spinal–epidural</strong> (the most effective).</li><li>Neuraxial analgesia: low-concentration local anaesthetic with opioid (e.g. levobupivacaine or bupivacaine 0.1% with fentanyl 2 µg/mL), given by programmed intermittent boluses or infusion with patient-controlled boluses. It does not increase the caesarean section rate; it may prolong the second stage and increase instrumental delivery.</li><li>Contraindications and cautions as for neuraxial blocks (coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia — check platelets in pre-eclampsia, anticoagulant timing).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"caesarean-section\">Caesarean section</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Categories of urgency:</strong> 1 immediate threat to the life of the woman or fetus; 2 maternal or fetal compromise that is not immediately life-threatening; 3 no compromise but needs early delivery; 4 at a time to suit the woman and team. Category 1 is traditionally achieved within 30 minutes (a decision-to-delivery interval target, not a rule).</li><li><strong>Spinal anaesthesia</strong> is preferred (safer than GA): hyperbaric bupivacaine with intrathecal opioid (fentanyl and/or diamorphine), a <strong>T4 block to touch</strong> bilaterally before surgery, <strong>phenylephrine infusion</strong> to maintain blood pressure near baseline, left uterine displacement.</li><li><strong>Epidural top-up</strong> if a labour epidural is working well.</li><li><strong>General anaesthesia</strong> when regional is contraindicated or too slow: antacid prophylaxis, head-up pre-oxygenation, RSI (propofol or thiopental, rocuronium or suxamethonium), smaller tube, <strong>follow the OAA/DAS obstetric difficult airway guideline</strong> (including a plan to wake or proceed), volatile maintenance (keep ≥ 1 MAC until delivery then consider reducing to limit uterine relaxation), awareness risk.</li><li><strong>Uterotonics:</strong> oxytocin (a slow IV bolus, e.g. 3 units, and/or infusion — causes hypotension and tachycardia if given fast), then ergometrine (avoid in hypertension), carboprost (avoid in asthma), misoprostol.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"pre-eclampsia\">Pre-eclampsia</h2>\n<ul><li>New hypertension after 20 weeks with proteinuria or organ dysfunction (renal, liver, neurological, haematological, uteroplacental). <strong>Eclampsia</strong> = seizures.</li><li>Management (NICE NG133): antihypertensives (labetalol, nifedipine, hydralazine), <strong>magnesium sulphate</strong> for severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia (seizure prevention and treatment; watch reflexes, respiratory rate, urine output and levels; potentiates neuromuscular blockers), careful fluid restriction, check platelets and coagulation before neuraxial block, attenuate the hypertensive response to laryngoscopy if GA is needed (e.g. remifentanil, alfentanil or labetalol). Delivery is the definitive treatment.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"major-obstetric-haemorrhage\">Major obstetric haemorrhage</h2>\n<ul><li>Causes: <strong>4 Ts</strong> — tone (atony, the commonest), trauma, tissue (retained placenta, abnormal placentation), thrombin (coagulopathy).</li><li><strong>Call for help</strong> (major obstetric haemorrhage protocol), large-bore access, warm fluids and blood, uterotonics, <strong>tranexamic acid</strong>, fibrinogen guided by viscoelastic testing or Clauss fibrinogen, cell salvage, surgical and radiological control, and a clear communication loop with the lab.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"maternal-cardiac-arrest\">Maternal cardiac arrest</h2>\n<p>Modified ALS: <strong>manual left uterine displacement</strong>, early airway management by the most experienced person, and <strong>resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem caesarean section)</strong> within about <strong>5 minutes</strong> of arrest if gestation is over about 20 weeks (to improve maternal resuscitation). Consider the causes (haemorrhage, thromboembolism, amniotic fluid embolism, sepsis, pre-eclampsia, LAST, high block, anaphylaxis, cardiac disease). <strong>MBRRACE-UK</strong> reports describe the leading causes of maternal death.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CS categories; spinal dose principles; magnesium; uterotonic side effects; 4 Ts.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining the risks and benefits of regional versus general anaesthesia to a woman needing a category 2 caesarean section.</li></ul></div>","opioids":"<h2 id=\"receptors-and-mechanism\">Receptors and mechanism</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>μ (MOP)</strong>, <strong>κ (KOP)</strong>, <strong>δ (DOP)</strong> and the nociceptin receptor (NOP), all <strong>G<sub>i</sub>-coupled</strong>: ↓ cAMP, <strong>open K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong> (hyperpolarisation) and <strong>close voltage-gated Ca<sup>2+</sup> channels</strong> (less transmitter release).</li><li><strong>Sites:</strong> dorsal horn (pre- and postsynaptic), periaqueductal grey and rostroventral medulla (activating descending inhibition), and peripheral nerve endings in inflamed tissue.</li><li>μ: analgesia, respiratory depression, euphoria, miosis, constipation, dependence. κ: analgesia, sedation, dysphoria, diuresis. δ: analgesia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"comparison\">Comparison</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Relative potency (morphine = 1)</th><th>pKa / notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Morphine</strong></td><td>1</td><td>Glucuronidated to <strong>M6G (active, accumulates in renal failure)</strong> and M3G (neuroexcitatory); histamine release</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Diamorphine</strong></td><td>~2–3</td><td>Pro-drug; lipid-soluble → rapid onset; deacetylated to 6-monoacetylmorphine and morphine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oxycodone</strong></td><td>~1.5–2 (oral)</td><td>High oral bioavailability; CYP3A4/2D6</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fentanyl</strong></td><td>~100</td><td>Very lipid-soluble; pKa 8.4; accumulates with infusions; CYP3A4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alfentanil</strong></td><td>~10–20</td><td>pKa 6.5 (fast onset); small Vd; CYP3A4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Remifentanil</strong></td><td>~100–200</td><td>Esterase metabolism; CSHT 3–4 min regardless of duration; hyperalgesia on withdrawal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tramadol</strong></td><td>~0.1</td><td>Weak μ agonist plus <strong>serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibition</strong>; CYP2D6 → active metabolite; seizures, serotonin syndrome risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Codeine</strong></td><td>~0.1</td><td>Pro-drug via <strong>CYP2D6</strong> → morphine; contraindicated for analgesia after tonsillectomy in under-18s</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Buprenorphine</strong></td><td>~30</td><td><strong>Partial μ agonist</strong>, high affinity and slow dissociation (hard to reverse with naloxone)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Methadone</strong></td><td>~variable</td><td>NMDA antagonist too; long, unpredictable half-life; QT prolongation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pethidine</strong></td><td>~0.1</td><td>Norpethidine (seizures); serotonin syndrome with MAOIs; anticholinergic</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Doses and conversions vary with patient and context: check the BNF and local guidance.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"side-effects\">Side effects</h2>\n<p>Respiratory depression (↓ rate &gt; ↓ tidal volume; synergy with sedatives), sedation, nausea and vomiting (CTZ), pruritus (especially neuraxial), constipation and ileus, urinary retention, miosis, muscle rigidity (chest wall rigidity with rapid high-dose fentanyl/remifentanil), bradycardia, <strong>hyperalgesia</strong>, tolerance and dependence, immunosuppression.</p>\n<h2 id=\"naloxone\">Naloxone</h2>\n<p>Competitive antagonist at all opioid receptors; titrate IV in small increments (for example 40–100 µg in adults) to avoid abrupt return of severe pain, hypertension and pulmonary oedema. Its duration (~30–60 min) is shorter than many opioids: <strong>re-narcotisation</strong> is common, so observe and consider an infusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"patient-controlled-analgesia\">Patient-controlled analgesia</h2>\n<p>Settings: bolus dose, <strong>lockout interval</strong> (long enough for each dose to reach peak effect), optional background infusion (increases respiratory depression; usually avoided in opioid-naïve adults), and a maximum dose limit. Safety: dedicated line or anti-reflux valve, monitoring of sedation and respiratory rate, standardised concentrations. Only the patient presses the button.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> receptor mechanisms; pharmacokinetic differences (pKa, Vd, metabolism); pro-drugs and pharmacogenetics; naloxone kinetics.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient on PCA morphine with a respiratory rate of 7 and sedation score 3: immediate management, then an explanation to the nurse and patient.</li></ul></div>","oxygen-delivery-devices":"<h2 id=\"oxygen-delivery-devices\">Oxygen delivery devices</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Performance</th><th>Approximate FiO<sub>2</sub></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nasal cannulae (1–4 L/min)</td><td>Variable</td><td>~0.24–0.4 (roughly +0.03–0.04 per L/min)</td></tr><tr><td>Simple face mask (Hudson) (5–10 L/min)</td><td>Variable</td><td>~0.3–0.6</td></tr><tr><td>Non-rebreathe mask with reservoir (15 L/min)</td><td>Variable</td><td>~0.6–0.85</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Venturi masks</strong></td><td><strong>Fixed</strong>: entrain a fixed ratio of air</td><td>0.24, 0.28, 0.35, 0.40, 0.60 (colour-coded)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>High-flow nasal oxygen (HFNO)</strong></td><td>Fixed (flow exceeds inspiratory demand)</td><td>Up to ~1.0 at 30–70 L/min</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Variable performance</strong> devices deliver an FiO<sub>2</sub> that depends on the patient's inspiratory flow and pattern (more air is entrained when breathing fast and deep).</li><li><strong>Fixed performance</strong> requires the total gas flow to exceed the patient's peak inspiratory flow (about 30 L/min at rest, more when distressed).</li><li><strong>HFNO</strong> delivers warmed, humidified gas: washes out anatomical dead space, provides a little PEEP (a few cmH<sub>2</sub>O with the mouth closed), improves comfort, and is used for pre-oxygenation and apnoeic oxygenation (THRIVE) and in respiratory failure.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hazards-of-oxygen\">Hazards of oxygen</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Absorption atelectasis:</strong> high FiO<sub>2</sub> washes out nitrogen, and alveoli behind closed or narrow airways collapse as their oxygen is absorbed. This is why FiO<sub>2</sub> is often reduced below 1.0 after pre-oxygenation and for emergence where safe.</li><li><strong>Oxygen-induced hypercapnia</strong> in some patients with chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure: aim for SpO<sub>2</sub> 88–92% (see <a href=\"#notes/resp-control\">control of breathing</a>).</li><li><strong>Toxicity:</strong> pulmonary (prolonged high FiO<sub>2</sub>), CNS (hyperbaric), retinopathy of prematurity; fire risk.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"suction\">Suction</h2>\n<ul><li>A vacuum source (pipeline at about −53 kPa or more negative, or a portable pump) with a <strong>reservoir jar</strong>, <strong>bacterial filter</strong> and <strong>overflow protection</strong> (a float valve), and a regulator to set negative pressure (lower settings for airway suction in children and for chest drains).</li><li>Flow depends on the tubing and catheter (Hagen–Poiseuille): <strong>wide-bore Yankauer</strong> suckers for vomit; fine catheters for tracheal suction (catheter diameter ideally no more than half the tube's internal diameter to avoid atelectasis and hypoxia).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"scavenging\">Scavenging</h2>\n<ul><li>Reduces staff exposure to waste anaesthetic gases (UK workplace exposure limits (8-hour time-weighted averages): N<sub>2</sub>O <strong>100 ppm</strong>, isoflurane 50 ppm, halothane 10 ppm, enflurane 50 ppm).</li><li><strong>Active (typical UK AGSS):</strong> the APL valve and ventilator exhaust connect via 30 mm connectors (distinct from 22/15 mm breathing system connectors) to a receiving system with a reservoir and <strong>air breaks</strong> (open to atmosphere) so that neither positive nor negative pressure is transmitted to the patient. A fan draws gas out of the building at high flow (e.g. 75–130 L/min).</li><li><strong>Passive:</strong> gas vented outside by the patient's own expiratory effort (limited by tube length and wind).</li><li><strong>Other measures:</strong> theatre air changes (about 15–20 per hour), low-flow anaesthesia, avoiding N<sub>2</sub>O, careful filling of vaporisers, and cuffed tubes/LMAs. Scavenging does not remove the environmental impact: waste gases still reach the atmosphere.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> fixed versus variable performance; HFNO physiology; scavenging components and exposure limits.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> pre-oxygenation strategy for an obese patient using HFNO, explained to the ODP.</li></ul></div>","paeds-basics":"<h2 id=\"preparation\">Preparation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> history from child and parent, previous anaesthetics, prematurity, syndromes, airway, <strong>loose teeth</strong>, recent infections, bleeding tendency, weight.</li><li><strong>Psychological preparation:</strong> play specialists, explanation in age-appropriate language, parental presence at induction where appropriate, distraction.</li><li><strong>Fasting (UK consensus):</strong> solids and formula 6 h, <strong>breast milk 4 h</strong>, <strong>clear fluids until 1 h</strong> before anaesthesia.</li><li><strong>Premedication:</strong> topical local anaesthetic cream (EMLA, tetracaine) for cannulation; oral midazolam or intranasal dexmedetomidine/clonidine for anxious children.</li><li><strong>Consent:</strong> from a person with parental responsibility; <strong>Gillick competence</strong> in under-16s who understand the treatment; a young person's refusal may be overridden in limited circumstances (legal advice).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"upper-respiratory-tract-infection-urti\">Upper respiratory tract infection (URTI)</h2>\n<p>Increases the risk of <strong>perioperative respiratory adverse events</strong> (laryngospasm, bronchospasm, desaturation), especially with airway surgery, intubation, young age, passive smoking and moist cough/fever. <strong>Postpone elective surgery</strong> if the child is systemically unwell, febrile, with a productive cough or chest signs; otherwise weigh the risks (airway hyper-reactivity lasts for weeks). Using an SGA rather than a tube, avoiding desflurane, and experienced anaesthetists reduce the risk.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conduct-of-anaesthesia-children-over-5-in-stage-1\">Conduct of anaesthesia (children over 5 in Stage 1)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Induction:</strong> IV (with topical anaesthesia) or <strong>inhalational with sevoflurane</strong> (in oxygen ± N<sub>2</sub>O). Beware of laryngospasm in light planes.</li><li><strong>Equipment:</strong> appropriately sized masks, airways, SGAs (by weight), tracheal tubes (cuffed tubes now common; check leak or cuff pressure), T-piece (Mapleson F) for small children, warming.</li><li><strong>Drugs by weight</strong>, with double-checking: for example atropine 20 µg/kg, suxamethonium 1–2 mg/kg (higher in infants), paracetamol and NSAIDs by weight (check BNF for Children), fluids 10 mL/kg boluses.</li><li><strong>Analgesia:</strong> paracetamol, NSAIDs, opioids by weight, and regional techniques such as the <strong>caudal block</strong> (e.g. levobupivacaine 0.2%, 0.5–1 mL/kg depending on the height needed, keeping within the maximum dose — see the BNF for Children and local guidance), penile block, ilioinguinal block, and wound infiltration.</li><li><strong>PONV:</strong> common after age 3; ondansetron and dexamethasone.</li><li><strong>Emergence:</strong> sevoflurane agitation (exclude pain, hypoxia, full bladder); dexmedetomidine or clonidine can reduce it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-sick-child\">The sick child</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Recognition:</strong> ABCDE with paediatric normal values; tachypnoea, recession, grunting, poor feeding, lethargy, prolonged capillary refill, tachycardia; bradycardia and hypotension are late signs.</li><li><strong>Airway emergencies:</strong> <strong>croup</strong> (viral; barking cough; nebulised adrenaline, dexamethasone), <strong>epiglottitis</strong> (toxic, drooling, sitting forward; do not upset the child; senior anaesthetist and ENT; gas induction in theatre), <strong>inhaled foreign body</strong> (rigid bronchoscopy; spontaneous ventilation strategy often preferred).</li><li><strong>Paediatric life support (RCUK):</strong> 5 rescue breaths, compressions at 15:2, adrenaline 10 µg/kg, defibrillation 4 J/kg; know the local escalation and transfer pathway (retrieval teams).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> fasting times; URTI decisions; caudal dose principles; croup versus epiglottitis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining to a worried parent why their child's tonsillectomy should be postponed, or planning induction for an anxious 7-year-old.</li></ul></div>","paeds-physiology":"<h2 id=\"airway\">Airway</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Large head and occiput</strong> (neck flexion when supine: a shoulder roll helps in infants), <strong>large tongue</strong>, floppy U-shaped epiglottis, high <strong>anterior larynx</strong> (C3–4 in infants; C5–6 in adults).</li><li>Traditionally the <strong>cricoid</strong> was described as the narrowest point; imaging suggests the glottis may be narrower in the sagittal plane, but the cricoid ring is the narrowest <strong>complete, non-distensible</strong> ring, so an oversized tube causes subglottic oedema. Resistance ∝ 1/r<sup>4</sup>: 1 mm of oedema greatly narrows a small airway.</li><li>Short trachea (about 4–5 cm in neonates) → easy endobronchial intubation. <strong>Obligate nasal breathers</strong> for the first few months.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"respiratory\">Respiratory</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygen consumption about 6–8 mL/kg/min</strong> (roughly double the adult value) with a small FRC → <strong>rapid desaturation</strong>.</li><li>Horizontal ribs, compliant chest wall and fatigue-prone diaphragm (fewer type I fibres): increased work of breathing. Minute ventilation is driven by <strong>rate</strong> (tidal volume ~7 mL/kg is similar to adults).</li><li>Closing capacity may exceed FRC in young infants.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cardiovascular\">Cardiovascular</h2>\n<ul><li>Stroke volume is relatively fixed (stiff, less compliant ventricles): <strong>cardiac output is rate-dependent</strong>, and <strong>bradycardia is dangerous</strong> (often a sign of hypoxia).</li><li>Dominant parasympathetic tone: vagal reflexes (laryngoscopy, suxamethonium, oculocardiac reflex).</li><li>Blood volume: <strong>neonate ~90 mL/kg, infant ~80 mL/kg, child ~70–75 mL/kg</strong>.</li><li>Hypotension is a <strong>late</strong> sign of hypovolaemia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"fluids-glucose-and-temperature\">Fluids, glucose and temperature</h2>\n<ul><li>Maintenance (the \"4-2-1\" rule): <strong>4 mL/kg/h for the first 10 kg, 2 mL/kg/h for the next 10 kg, 1 mL/kg/h thereafter</strong> (i.e. 100/50/20 mL/kg/day). Use <strong>isotonic</strong> fluids (NICE NG29); hypotonic fluids risk hyponatraemia because of ADH release.</li><li>Neonates and infants have limited glycogen: risk of <strong>hypoglycaemia</strong> with prolonged fasting.</li><li>High surface area to volume ratio, little subcutaneous fat, brown fat thermogenesis only → <strong>rapid heat loss</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"drugs\">Drugs</h2>\n<ul><li>Larger ECF and total body water → larger volume of distribution for water-soluble drugs (e.g. suxamethonium dose is higher per kg in infants).</li><li>Immature hepatic enzymes and renal function in neonates → reduced clearance of many drugs. MAC is highest in infants (about 1–6 months).</li><li><strong>Weight estimates (APLS, ages 1–10):</strong> (age + 4) × 2 kg is a common simple formula; use measured weight when possible.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"useful-formulae-starting-points-only\">Useful formulae (starting points only)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Uncuffed tracheal tube internal diameter (mm):</strong> age/4 + 4; <strong>cuffed:</strong> age/4 + 3.5 (cuffed tubes are now widely used).</li><li><strong>Oral tube length (cm):</strong> age/2 + 12; nasal: age/2 + 15.</li><li><strong>Spinal cord</strong> ends at about <strong>L3 at birth</strong>, reaching the adult level (L1–2) by about 1 year; the dural sac ends at about S3–4 in neonates (S1–2 in adults).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> airway differences; blood volumes; maintenance fluids; drug handling in neonates.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> preparing equipment and drug doses for a 5-year-old having tonsillectomy, and explaining the plan to a parent.</li></ul></div>","paracetamol-nsaids":"<h2 id=\"cyclo-oxygenase\">Cyclo-oxygenase</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>COX</strong> converts arachidonic acid (released from membranes by phospholipase A<sub>2</sub>) to prostaglandin H<sub>2</sub>, the precursor of prostaglandins, <strong>thromboxane A<sub>2</sub></strong> (platelets: aggregation, vasoconstriction) and <strong>prostacyclin</strong> (endothelium: anti-aggregation, vasodilation).</li><li><strong>COX-1</strong> is constitutive (gastric protection, platelets, renal blood flow). <strong>COX-2</strong> is induced by inflammation (also constitutive in the kidney and brain).</li><li>Blocking COX diverts arachidonic acid to the <strong>lipoxygenase pathway</strong> → <strong>leukotrienes</strong> → bronchospasm in <strong>aspirin-/NSAID-exacerbated respiratory disease</strong> (asthma with nasal polyps).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"paracetamol\">Paracetamol</h2>\n<ul><li>Analgesic and antipyretic with little anti-inflammatory action. Mechanism not fully understood: central COX inhibition (in the low-peroxide environment of the CNS), serotonergic descending pathways, and the active metabolite AM404 acting via TRPV1 and cannabinoid receptors.</li><li>Well absorbed orally (bioavailability ~80–90%); IV and rectal forms.</li><li>Metabolism: mainly glucuronidation and sulphation; a small fraction by CYP2E1 to <strong>NAPQI</strong>, detoxified by <strong>glutathione</strong>. In overdose (or glutathione depletion: malnutrition, chronic alcohol, low weight), NAPQI causes <strong>hepatic necrosis</strong>. Treatment: <strong>acetylcysteine</strong> (replenishes glutathione).</li><li><strong>Dose</strong> by weight in low-weight adults (under 50 kg) and children; check the BNF and local guidance.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"nsaids\">NSAIDs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Non-selective</strong> (ibuprofen, diclofenac, naproxen, ketorolac) inhibit COX-1 and COX-2; <strong>COX-2-selective</strong> (celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib) spare platelets and cause less GI ulceration.</li><li><strong>Side effects:</strong> GI ulceration and bleeding; <strong>renal impairment</strong> (loss of prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilatation, especially with hypovolaemia, ACE inhibitors or diuretics); platelet dysfunction (non-selective); bronchospasm in susceptible asthmatics; fluid retention and hypertension; increased cardiovascular thrombotic risk (especially COX-2 inhibitors and high-dose diclofenac); possible impairment of bone healing (debated).</li><li><strong>Cautions / contraindications:</strong> active peptic ulcer or bleeding, significant renal impairment, hypovolaemia, severe heart failure, NSAID-sensitive asthma, third trimester of pregnancy (premature closure of the ductus arteriosus).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"aspirin\">Aspirin</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Irreversibly acetylates COX</strong>; in platelets (no nucleus, cannot make new enzyme) this lasts the <strong>7–10-day life of the platelet</strong>. Low dose (75 mg) is antiplatelet; high doses are analgesic and anti-inflammatory.</li><li>Avoid in children under 16 (<strong>Reye's syndrome</strong>). Overdose: respiratory alkalosis then metabolic acidosis, tinnitus, hyperthermia.</li><li>Usually continued perioperatively for secondary prevention (except some neurosurgery, spinal and ophthalmic procedures).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> COX pathways and their products; paracetamol toxicity mechanism and treatment; NSAID renal effects; why aspirin's antiplatelet action lasts a week.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning multimodal analgesia for an asthmatic patient with mild CKD, justifying each drug choice.</li></ul></div>","pd-receptors":"<h2 id=\"receptor-types\">Receptor types</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Response time</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Ligand-gated ion channels</strong></td><td>Milliseconds</td><td>Nicotinic ACh, GABA<sub>A</sub>, NMDA, 5-HT<sub>3</sub>, glycine</td></tr><tr><td><strong>G-protein coupled receptors</strong></td><td>Seconds</td><td>Adrenoceptors, muscarinic, opioid, dopamine; G<sub>s</sub> (↑ cAMP), G<sub>i</sub> (↓ cAMP), G<sub>q</sub> (IP<sub>3</sub>/DAG, ↑ Ca<sup>2+</sup>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Enzyme-linked (tyrosine kinase)</strong></td><td>Minutes</td><td>Insulin, growth factors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intracellular / nuclear</strong></td><td>Hours</td><td>Steroids, thyroid hormones</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other targets: <strong>voltage-gated ion channels</strong> (Na<sup>+</sup>: local anaesthetics; Ca<sup>2+</sup>: dihydropyridines; K<sup>+</sup>), <strong>transporters/pumps</strong> (Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase: digoxin; H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase: PPIs; reuptake transporters: SSRIs), and <strong>enzymes</strong> (acetylcholinesterase, COX, phosphodiesterase, ACE).</p>\n<h2 id=\"dose-response-relationships\">Dose–response relationships</h2>\n<ul><li>Plotting response against <strong>log(dose)</strong> gives a sigmoid curve with a linear central portion.</li><li><strong>Affinity:</strong> how strongly a drug binds (K<sub>D</sub>, the concentration occupying 50% of receptors; the affinity constant K<sub>A</sub> = 1/K<sub>D</sub>).</li><li><strong>Occupancy theory:</strong> response ∝ fractional occupancy × intrinsic activity. Signal amplification through second messengers means a maximal response can occur with only a fraction of receptors occupied (<strong>spare receptors</strong>, as at the neuromuscular junction).</li><li><strong>Efficacy (intrinsic activity):</strong> the ability to produce a response once bound: the <strong>maximum</strong> of the curve.</li><li><strong>Potency:</strong> the dose needed for a given effect, reflected by <strong>EC<sub>50</sub> / ED<sub>50</sub></strong> (position along the x-axis). Potency is not the same as efficacy.</li><li><strong>Full agonist:</strong> maximal efficacy. <strong>Partial agonist:</strong> submaximal efficacy even at full occupancy, and can antagonise a full agonist (buprenorphine at the μ receptor). <strong>Inverse agonist:</strong> reduces constitutive receptor activity (e.g. some benzodiazepine-site ligands).</li><li><strong>Therapeutic index:</strong> LD<sub>50</sub>/ED<sub>50</sub> (or TD<sub>50</sub>/ED<sub>50</sub>).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"antagonists\">Antagonists</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Competitive reversible:</strong> binds the same site; can be overcome by more agonist → <strong>parallel rightward shift</strong> of the log dose–response curve, same maximum (e.g. non-depolarising NMBs, naloxone, atropine).</li><li><strong>Irreversible (or non-competitive):</strong> reduces the <strong>maximum</strong> response; the curve flattens (e.g. phenoxybenzamine at α receptors).</li><li><strong>Allosteric modulators</strong> bind elsewhere and change the receptor's response (benzodiazepines, propofol and barbiturates enhance GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor function).</li><li><strong>Physiological antagonism:</strong> opposing effects via different receptors (adrenaline versus histamine in anaphylaxis). <strong>Chemical antagonism:</strong> direct binding (protamine–heparin, sugammadex–rocuronium).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"receptor-regulation-and-tachyphylaxis\">Receptor regulation and tachyphylaxis</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Down-regulation / desensitisation</strong> with prolonged agonist exposure (β receptors in heart failure and with β-agonist therapy). <strong>Up-regulation</strong> with antagonists or denervation (β-blocker withdrawal → rebound tachycardia; extrajunctional ACh receptors).</li><li><strong>Tachyphylaxis:</strong> a rapidly diminishing response to repeated doses, e.g. <strong>ephedrine</strong> (depletion of noradrenaline stores), GTN (nitrate tolerance), desmopressin. <strong>Tolerance</strong> develops more slowly (opioids).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"enzymes-as-targets\">Enzymes as targets</h2>\n<p>Michaelis–Menten kinetics describe the rate of enzyme action. Drugs may inhibit <strong>competitively</strong> (edrophonium, neostigmine: reversible carbamylation), <strong>irreversibly</strong> (organophosphates, aspirin acetylating COX in platelets for their lifespan), or allosterically.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> draw dose–response curves showing competitive versus non-competitive antagonism and partial agonism; receptor types and second messengers; examples of tachyphylaxis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why naloxone may not fully reverse buprenorphine, or why ephedrine stops working after repeated doses.</li></ul></div>","peripheral-blocks":"<h2 id=\"safety-first\">Safety first</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Consent</strong> (benefits, risks: failure, nerve injury, LAST, block-specific complications), <strong>\"Stop Before You Block\"</strong> immediately before needle insertion (re-confirm the site and side), aseptic technique, monitoring, IV access, resuscitation drugs and lipid emulsion available.</li><li><strong>Ultrasound guidance</strong> (with or without nerve stimulation): see the needle tip, avoid intraneural injection (stop if high injection pressure, pain or paraesthesia), hydrodissect, inject incrementally with aspiration.</li><li>Document the block, the drug, dose and volume, and give the patient instructions on protecting the numb limb.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"stage-1-blocks\">Stage 1 blocks</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Target and view</th><th>Indications</th><th>Key complications</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Interscalene</strong></td><td>C5–6 roots between scalenus anterior and medius (\"traffic lights\")</td><td>Shoulder and proximal humerus surgery</td><td><strong>Ipsilateral phrenic nerve palsy</strong> (avoid in poor respiratory reserve), Horner's, hoarseness, vertebral artery/epidural/intrathecal injection, pneumothorax (rare)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Axillary</strong></td><td>Median, ulnar and radial nerves around the axillary artery; musculocutaneous in coracobrachialis</td><td>Elbow, forearm and hand surgery</td><td>Vascular puncture, LAST; tourniquet pain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Distal (forearm/elbow/wrist)</strong></td><td>Individual median, ulnar and radial nerves</td><td>Hand surgery, rescue of incomplete proximal blocks</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Femoral</strong></td><td>Lateral to the femoral artery, deep to fascia iliaca</td><td>Femoral shaft and knee surgery, hip fracture analgesia</td><td>Quadriceps weakness (falls risk)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fascia iliaca</strong></td><td>Deep to fascia iliaca (infra- or supra-inguinal)</td><td><strong>Hip fracture</strong> analgesia (recommended in UK guidance)</td><td>Volume-dependent spread; LAST with large volumes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"complications-of-peripheral-nerve-blocks\">Complications of peripheral nerve blocks</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Nerve injury:</strong> transient symptoms are common (a few per cent); persistent injury is rare. Risk factors: intraneural injection, pre-existing neuropathy, surgical and positioning factors, tourniquets.</li><li><strong>LAST</strong>, vascular puncture and haematoma, infection, pneumothorax (supraclavicular), wrong-site block, falls (lower limb blocks).</li><li><strong>Rebound pain</strong> when a single-shot block wears off: plan multimodal analgesia to start before this.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> anatomy seen on ultrasound; which block for which operation; complications.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> consent a patient for an interscalene block, including explaining the effect on breathing.</li></ul></div>","pk-absorption":"<h2 id=\"absorption\">Absorption</h2>\n<p>Passage across membranes depends on <strong>lipid solubility</strong>, <strong>ionisation</strong> (only the un-ionised form crosses readily), molecular size, the concentration gradient, blood flow and surface area at the site, and transporters (e.g. P-glycoprotein efflux in the gut).</p>\n<h2 id=\"bioavailability\">Bioavailability</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>F = AUC<sub>oral</sub> / AUC<sub>IV</sub></strong> (dose-corrected): the fraction of an administered dose reaching the systemic circulation unchanged.</li><li>Reduced by incomplete absorption, destruction in the gut, and <strong>first-pass metabolism</strong> in the gut wall and liver (e.g. oral morphine ~20–40%; oral GTN almost nil; lidocaine too low to be useful orally).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"routes\">Routes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Drawbacks / examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oral</td><td>Simple, cheap</td><td>First pass, variable absorption, delayed gastric emptying, needs a working gut</td></tr><tr><td>Sublingual / buccal</td><td>Avoids first pass; rapid</td><td>GTN, buprenorphine</td></tr><tr><td>Rectal</td><td>Partly avoids first pass (lower rectal veins drain systemically)</td><td>Paracetamol, diclofenac; erratic</td></tr><tr><td>Intranasal</td><td>Rapid, avoids first pass, needle-free</td><td>Fentanyl, diamorphine, midazolam, dexmedetomidine in children</td></tr><tr><td>Transdermal</td><td>Steady levels, avoids first pass</td><td>Fentanyl, buprenorphine patches: slow onset and offset, depot in skin; heat increases absorption</td></tr><tr><td>Inhalational</td><td>Rapid onset and control</td><td>Volatiles, bronchodilators</td></tr><tr><td>Intramuscular / subcutaneous</td><td>No IV access needed</td><td>Absorption depends on perfusion (poor in shock)</td></tr><tr><td>Intravenous</td><td>100% bioavailable, immediate</td><td>Most risk of error and toxicity</td></tr><tr><td>Neuraxial / perineural</td><td>Direct to site of action, low doses</td><td>Opioids and LAs; specific toxicities</td></tr><tr><td>Intraosseous</td><td>Rapid access in emergencies</td><td>Equivalent to IV for drugs and fluids</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"drug-delivery-systems\">Drug delivery systems</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Modified release</strong> (sustained release, enteric coating) spreads absorption over time; do not crush.</li><li><strong>Iontophoresis</strong> uses a small electric current to drive ionised drug across skin (e.g. fentanyl systems).</li><li><strong>Oral absorption of weak acids and bases</strong> depends on pH and pKa, but in practice most absorption occurs in the small intestine because of its enormous surface area, even for weak acids.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculating bioavailability; first-pass effect; factors affecting transdermal absorption.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> converting a patient's oral morphine to a parenteral equivalent, or explaining why a fentanyl patch should not be started for acute postoperative pain.</li></ul></div>","pk-basics":"<h2 id=\"core-definitions\">Core definitions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Volume of distribution (Vd)</strong> = amount of drug in the body / plasma concentration. An apparent volume: lipophilic, tissue-bound drugs have huge Vd (fentanyl about 4 L/kg); ionised drugs, and drugs bound more to plasma proteins than to tissues, have a small Vd (neuromuscular blockers about 0.2 L/kg; warfarin about 0.1 L/kg). High plasma protein binding alone does not guarantee a small Vd: propofol is about 98% bound yet has a very large Vd because tissue binding is greater still.</li><li><strong>Bioavailability (F):</strong> the fraction of a dose reaching the systemic circulation unchanged (IV = 1), reduced by incomplete absorption and first-pass metabolism in gut wall and liver.</li><li><strong>Loading dose</strong> = target concentration × Vd.</li><li><strong>Clearance (CL)</strong>: volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time. <strong>Rate of elimination = CL × concentration.</strong> Clearances add: CL<sub>total</sub> = CL<sub>hepatic</sub> + CL<sub>renal</sub> + others.</li><li><strong>Maintenance rate</strong> = target concentration × CL.</li><li><strong>Elimination rate constant k = CL / Vd.</strong></li><li><strong>Half-life t<sub>½</sub> = 0.693 × Vd / CL</strong> (0.693 = ln 2). It therefore <strong>rises if Vd rises or clearance falls</strong>.</li><li><strong>Time constant τ = Vd / CL = 1/k = 1.44 × t<sub>½</sub></strong>: the time it would take to eliminate all the drug if the initial rate continued.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"exponential-processes\">Exponential processes</h2>\n<ul><li>In <strong>first-order</strong> kinetics a constant <strong>fraction</strong> is eliminated per unit time: C = C<sub>0</sub> e<sup>−kt</sup>.</li><li>After one time constant, concentration falls to <strong>37%</strong> (a fall of 63%); after 3τ to 5%, after 5τ to under 1%.</li><li>After one half-life 50% remains; after <strong>4–5 half-lives</strong> about 94–97% is gone. Steady state during a constant infusion is reached after about 4–5 half-lives (without a loading dose).</li><li><strong>Wash-in</strong> (e.g. of oxygen during pre-oxygenation, or volatile in a circle) follows the reverse (exponential approach to a plateau): 63% at 1τ, 95% at 3τ. For a breathing system or the FRC, <strong>τ = volume / flow</strong>.</li><li>Plotting <strong>ln(concentration)</strong> against time turns an exponential decline into a straight line with slope −k.</li><li><strong>AUC</strong> (area under the concentration–time curve) = dose × bioavailability / CL. Used to calculate bioavailability and clearance.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hepatic-clearance\">Hepatic clearance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>CL<sub>H</sub> = Q<sub>H</sub> × E</strong> (hepatic blood flow × extraction ratio).</li><li><strong>High extraction drugs</strong> (E &gt; 0.7: propofol, lidocaine, morphine, fentanyl): clearance is <strong>flow-limited</strong> — reduced by low cardiac output and hepatic blood flow, little affected by enzyme induction or protein binding.</li><li><strong>Low extraction drugs</strong> (E &lt; 0.3: warfarin, phenytoin, diazepam): clearance is <strong>capacity-limited</strong> — sensitive to enzyme induction and inhibition and to free fraction.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"compartment-models\">Compartment models</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>One-compartment:</strong> instantaneous distribution; single exponential decline.</li><li><strong>Two-compartment:</strong> a rapid <strong>distribution (α) phase</strong> followed by a slower <strong>elimination (β) phase</strong>. After an IV bolus of propofol or thiopental, <strong>redistribution</strong> from the brain to muscle and then fat ends the clinical effect, not elimination.</li><li><strong>Three-compartment:</strong> central (V1), fast peripheral (V2, muscle) and slow peripheral (V3, fat). The basis of TCI (see context-sensitive half-time).</li><li><strong>Physiological (and non-compartmental) models</strong> use real organ volumes and flows, or statistical moments (mean residence time).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculations of Vd, loading dose, half-life and time constants; percentage remaining after n half-lives; high versus low extraction drugs.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why a patient in low-output cardiac failure needs less lidocaine or propofol by infusion.</li></ul></div>","pk-csht-tci":"<h2 id=\"key-facts\">Key facts</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Context-sensitive half-time (CSHT):</strong> the time for the <strong>plasma</strong> concentration to fall by <strong>50%</strong> after stopping an infusion that has maintained a <strong>steady plasma concentration</strong>, where the \"context\" is the <strong>duration of the infusion</strong>.</li><li>It is <strong>not</strong> the elimination half-life. After a short infusion, the fall in plasma concentration is driven mainly by <strong>redistribution</strong> to peripheral compartments. After a long infusion, those compartments are full and drug returns from them to plasma, so the fall slows.</li><li>CSHT depends on the <strong>ratio of elimination to redistribution</strong>, not on either alone.</li><li><strong>Remifentanil</strong>: CSHT is about <strong>3–4 minutes and almost independent of infusion duration</strong>, because non-specific plasma and tissue esterases give it a very high clearance relative to its small volume of distribution.</li><li><strong>Fentanyl</strong>: CSHT rises steeply with duration (large, lipid-soluble peripheral stores). <strong>Alfentanil</strong> plateaus at a moderate value. <strong>Propofol</strong> rises only modestly over several hours because of its high clearance.</li><li>CSHT does not predict waking directly: the decrement needed for recovery may be more or less than 50% (the <strong>decrement time</strong> is the general concept, e.g. 80% decrement time).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"compartment-models-and-tci\">Compartment models and TCI</h2>\n<ul><li>Three-compartment model: central volume V1 (plasma and highly perfused tissue), a fast peripheral compartment V2 (muscle) and a slow peripheral compartment V3 (fat), linked by rate constants k12, k21, k13, k31, with elimination k10 from V1.</li><li>A <strong>target-controlled infusion (TCI)</strong> pump uses a population PK model to calculate the bolus and the decreasing infusion rates needed to reach and hold a chosen <strong>plasma (Cp)</strong> or <strong>effect-site (Ce)</strong> target. Effect-site targeting adds an equilibration constant, <strong>ke0</strong>.</li><li>Propofol models: <strong>Marsh</strong> (weight only; plasma targeting) and <strong>Schnider</strong> (age, weight, height and lean body mass; usually effect-site targeting). <strong>Eleveld</strong> is a newer general-purpose model covering a wide range of ages and weights. Remifentanil: <strong>Minto</strong>.</li><li>TCI is <strong>open-loop</strong>: the pump never measures the actual concentration. With propofol and remifentanil together, start the propofol first, as remifentanil reaches effect much faster.</li><li>Predicted concentrations are population estimates. Real concentrations vary considerably between patients, so titrate to clinical effect and depth-of-anaesthesia monitoring where appropriate.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"clinical-relevance\">Clinical relevance</h2>\n<ul><li>Choose drugs whose offset suits the case: remifentanil for long cases needing rapid, predictable emergence; avoid long fentanyl infusions if early extubation matters.</li><li>Plan <strong>transition analgesia</strong> before stopping remifentanil, because its analgesic effect ends within minutes.</li><li>TIVA safety: a dedicated cannula that is visible throughout, anti-reflux valves, correct model and patient data, and syringe labelling. Disconnection or extravasation risks awareness, as NAP5 highlighted.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CSHT definitions; comparing offset of propofol, fentanyl, alfentanil and remifentanil; model inputs; why Ce and Cp differ.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"Your supervisor asks why you chose remifentanil TIVA for a 6-hour operation. Explain, and tell them what you will do about analgesia at the end.\" Expect to sketch CSHT curves.</li></ul></div>","pk-metabolism":"<h2 id=\"phase-i-and-phase-ii\">Phase I and phase II</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Phase I (functionalisation):</strong> oxidation (mainly <strong>cytochrome P450</strong>, in the smooth ER), reduction and hydrolysis. May produce <strong>active metabolites</strong> (e.g. morphine-6-glucuronide is formed in phase II; codeine → morphine by CYP2D6 in phase I; diazepam → desmethyldiazepam).</li><li><strong>Phase II (conjugation):</strong> glucuronidation, sulphation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation → water-soluble products excreted in urine or bile.</li><li><strong>Pro-drugs</strong> need metabolism to become active: codeine, tramadol (partly), clopidogrel, parecoxib (→ valdecoxib), enalapril.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cytochrome-p450\">Cytochrome P450</h2>\n<p>The main enzymes: <strong>CYP3A4</strong> (about half of all drugs, including midazolam, fentanyl, alfentanil, lidocaine, ciclosporin), <strong>CYP2D6</strong> (codeine, tramadol, many antidepressants and β-blockers), CYP2C9 (warfarin, NSAIDs), CYP2E1 (volatile agents, ethanol, paracetamol toxicity), CYP1A2 (caffeine, theophylline).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inducers (↑ metabolism; onset over days)</th><th>Inhibitors (↓ metabolism; rapid onset)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, rifampicin, St John's wort, chronic alcohol, smoking (CYP1A2)</td><td>Erythromycin and clarithromycin, azole antifungals, ritonavir, amiodarone, cimetidine, grapefruit juice, SSRIs (CYP2D6), acute alcohol</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Example: erythromycin inhibits CYP3A4 → prolonged midazolam and alfentanil.</p>\n<h2 id=\"elimination-without-liver-enzymes\">Elimination without liver enzymes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hofmann degradation:</strong> spontaneous, pH- and temperature-dependent breakdown in plasma (<strong>atracurium</strong>, <strong>cisatracurium</strong>). Slowed by hypothermia and acidosis. <strong>Laudanosine</strong> is a metabolite (CNS excitation at very high levels).</li><li><strong>Ester hydrolysis:</strong> <strong>plasma cholinesterase</strong> (suxamethonium, mivacurium, ester local anaesthetics); <strong>non-specific tissue and red cell esterases</strong> (<strong>remifentanil</strong>, esmolol, atracurium partly).</li><li><strong>Renal excretion</strong> of water-soluble drugs and metabolites (filtration, tubular secretion, pH-dependent reabsorption); <strong>biliary</strong> (vecuronium, rocuronium in part); <strong>exhalation</strong> (volatiles); breast milk and sweat (minor).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"factors-that-alter-elimination\">Factors that alter elimination</h2>\n<p>Age (neonates and elderly), hepatic and renal disease, cardiac output (flow-limited drugs), hypothermia, pregnancy, obesity, interactions, genetics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pharmacogenetics\">Pharmacogenetics</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Variation</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Plasma cholinesterase variants</strong> (e.g. atypical, silent genes; dibucaine number)</td><td>Prolonged apnoea after suxamethonium or mivacurium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CYP2D6 poor metabolisers</strong> (~7–10% of white Europeans)</td><td>Little analgesia from codeine/tramadol</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolisers</strong> (up to about 30% in parts of East Africa such as Ethiopia; also common in North African and Middle Eastern populations)</td><td>Morphine toxicity from codeine (deaths in children after tonsillectomy)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Slow acetylators</strong> (NAT2; about half of Europeans)</td><td>Toxicity with hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Malignant hyperthermia susceptibility</strong> (RYR1, CACNA1S)</td><td>MH with volatiles or suxamethonium</td></tr><tr><td><strong>G6PD deficiency</strong></td><td>Haemolysis with oxidant drugs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Acute porphyrias</strong></td><td>Attacks precipitated by enzyme inducers (e.g. barbiturates)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> inducers and inhibitors; which drugs undergo Hofmann degradation or esterase metabolism; genetic causes of prolonged drug effect.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient still paralysed an hour after suxamethonium: diagnosis, management, investigation and counselling.</li></ul></div>","pregnancy-physiology":"<h2 id=\"cardiovascular\">Cardiovascular</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Size (by term)</th><th>Implication</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cardiac output</td><td>↑ <strong>~40–50%</strong> (rate and stroke volume); further ↑ in labour and immediately after delivery (autotransfusion)</td><td>Risk for women with cardiac disease, especially at delivery</td></tr><tr><td>SVR</td><td>↓ ~20–30% (progesterone, prostacyclin, low-resistance placenta)</td><td>BP falls in the second trimester</td></tr><tr><td>Blood volume</td><td>↑ <strong>~40–50%</strong> (plasma more than red cells)</td><td>Physiological (dilutional) anaemia</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aortocaval compression</strong></td><td>Clinically important from about 20 weeks when supine (can begin earlier)</td><td>Hypotension, reduced uteroplacental flow: use <strong>left lateral tilt</strong> or uterine displacement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"respiratory\">Respiratory</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Minute ventilation ↑ ~45–50%</strong> (mainly tidal volume; progesterone increases CO<sub>2</sub> sensitivity) → <strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub> about 4.0–4.2 kPa</strong>, with renal compensation (bicarbonate ~18–21 mmol/L).</li><li><strong>FRC ↓ ~20%</strong> (more when supine) and <strong>O<sub>2</sub> consumption ↑ ~20–30%</strong> → rapid desaturation during apnoea.</li><li><strong>Airway:</strong> mucosal oedema and capillary engorgement (bleeding with nasal instrumentation; use smaller tracheal tubes), breast enlargement, weight gain. Failed intubation is more common than in the general population: follow the <strong>OAA/DAS obstetric guidelines</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"haematological\">Haematological</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hypercoagulable:</strong> ↑ fibrinogen (often 4–6 g/L at term) and factors VII, VIII, X and vWF; ↓ protein S; reduced fibrinolysis → <strong>VTE risk</strong> (a leading direct cause of maternal death).</li><li>Fibrinogen below about 2 g/L in obstetric haemorrhage predicts severe bleeding.</li><li>Mild gestational thrombocytopenia is common.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Oxygen carriage:</strong> the respiratory alkalosis would shift the maternal curve left, but a rise in 2,3-DPG (about 30%) produces a net <strong>right shift</strong>, helping oxygen unloading to the placenta. Uterine blood flow rises to about 10–12% of cardiac output at term.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gastrointestinal\">Gastrointestinal</h2>\n<ul><li>Progesterone lowers <strong>LOS tone</strong>; the gravid uterus raises intragastric pressure and displaces the stomach. Gastric emptying is normal in pregnancy but <strong>delayed in labour, especially with opioids</strong>. Hence antacid prophylaxis and RSI for general anaesthesia from about the second trimester onwards (practice varies).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-systems\">Other systems</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Renal:</strong> renal plasma flow and <strong>GFR ↑ ~50%</strong> → normal pregnant creatinine is lower (a \"normal\" non-pregnant creatinine may indicate renal impairment).</li><li><strong>CNS:</strong> <strong>MAC falls by up to ~30–40%</strong> (progesterone, endorphins); local anaesthetic requirement for neuraxial block is reduced (engorged epidural veins reduce the CSF volume, hormonal effects).</li><li><strong>Plasma cholinesterase</strong> falls by about 25% (little clinical effect on suxamethonium).</li><li><strong>Endocrine:</strong> insulin resistance (gestational diabetes), raised thyroid-binding globulin.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> percentage changes; blood gas values in pregnancy; coagulation changes; MAC.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explain why you would use a smaller tube, head-up pre-oxygenation and left uterine displacement for a general anaesthetic for category 1 caesarean section.</li></ul></div>","preop-assessment":"<h2 id=\"structure\">Structure</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Presenting condition and planned surgery</strong> (urgency, extent, position, expected blood loss).</li><li><strong>Anaesthetic history:</strong> previous anaesthetics and problems (difficult airway, PONV, awareness, suxamethonium apnoea), <strong>family history</strong> (MH, cholinesterase deficiency).</li><li><strong>Medical history by system</strong>, focusing on <strong>functional capacity</strong> (can the patient climb two flights of stairs? — roughly ≥ 4 METs), cardiorespiratory disease, diabetes, renal, liver, neurological, rheumatoid disease, OSA (STOP-BANG), reflux.</li><li><strong>Drugs and allergies</strong> (what reaction?); anticoagulants and antiplatelets; alcohol, smoking, recreational drugs.</li><li><strong>Examination:</strong> cardiovascular, respiratory, <strong>airway</strong>, and relevant neurology; sites for cannulation and regional blocks.</li><li><strong>Investigations</strong> as indicated, not routine (NICE NG45: by ASA grade and surgical severity).</li><li><strong>Plan and discussion:</strong> technique, risks and alternatives, fasting, medication instructions, postoperative care level (ward, enhanced care, critical care). Document it and escalate concerns.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"classifications\">Classifications</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>ASA physical status:</strong> I healthy; II mild systemic disease; III severe systemic disease; IV severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life; V moribund, not expected to survive without surgery; VI brain-dead organ donor; \"E\" for emergency. Simple and widely used, but subjective and not a full risk score (updated statement 2025).</li><li><strong>NCEPOD classification of intervention:</strong> <strong>immediate</strong> (life- or limb-saving, within minutes), <strong>urgent</strong> (acute onset or deterioration, within hours), <strong>expedited</strong> (stable but needs early intervention, within days), <strong>elective</strong> (planned).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"risk-stratification\">Risk stratification</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Cardiac:</strong> the <strong>Revised Cardiac Risk Index</strong> (high-risk surgery, ischaemic heart disease, heart failure, cerebrovascular disease, insulin-treated diabetes, creatinine &gt; 177 µmol/L); functional capacity; BNP/NT-proBNP in some pathways (ESC 2022).</li><li><strong>General mortality:</strong> P-POSSUM, <strong>SORT</strong>, NELA risk score (emergency laparotomy), and frailty scores.</li><li><strong>Pulmonary:</strong> ARISCAT and similar; OSA screening.</li><li>Higher-risk patients may need CPET, echocardiography or specialist review; in Stage 1 the key skill is <strong>recognising risk and telling a senior</strong> (EPA 1): severe comorbidity (ASA III+), previous anaesthetic problems, and a known or predicted difficult airway.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"emergency-versus-elective\">Emergency versus elective</h2>\n<p>Emergency patients may be hypovolaemic, septic, in pain, not fasted and with less time to optimise. Resuscitate and optimise in parallel with preparation; do not delay life-saving surgery for tests that will not change management.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fasting-and-aspiration-risk\">Fasting and aspiration risk</h2>\n<p>Standard adult fasting: 6 h for food, 2 h for clear fluids (see local policy, which may allow clear fluids until called). Assess factors that increase aspiration risk (see RSI).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> ASA and NCEPOD definitions; RCRI factors; which tests NICE NG45 recommends.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a preassessment role play: take a focused history, identify the risks, explain the plan to the patient and escalate appropriately.</li></ul></div>","pressure-measurement":"<h2 id=\"non-invasive-oscillometric-blood-pressure\">Non-invasive (oscillometric) blood pressure</h2>\n<ul><li>A cuff inflates above systolic then deflates in steps; a transducer detects <strong>oscillations</strong> in cuff pressure caused by arterial pulsation.</li><li><strong>Mean arterial pressure = the point of maximum oscillation</strong> (the most accurately measured value). Systolic and diastolic are <strong>calculated</strong> by algorithms (proprietary).</li><li><strong>Cuff size:</strong> bladder width about <strong>40% of the arm circumference</strong> and length about 80%. <strong>Too small a cuff overestimates</strong> blood pressure; too large underestimates.</li><li><strong>Errors:</strong> arrhythmias (AF), shivering and movement, very low or high pressures, external compression. Tends to overestimate at low pressures and underestimate at high pressures. Complications: ulnar nerve injury, skin damage, compartment syndrome with frequent cycling.</li><li>Manual methods: <strong>Korotkoff sounds</strong> (phase I systolic; phase V disappearance = diastolic); Riva-Rocci; von Recklinghausen oscillotonometer (historical).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"invasive-arterial-pressure\">Invasive arterial pressure</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Components:</strong> arterial cannula → non-compliant, fluid-filled tubing → <strong>transducer</strong> (a diaphragm moving a <strong>strain gauge</strong> arranged in a <strong>Wheatstone bridge</strong>, which converts changes in resistance to a voltage) → amplifier and display. A pressurised flush (about 300 mmHg, ~3 mL/h) keeps the line patent.</li><li><strong>Zeroing:</strong> open the transducer to air to set atmospheric pressure as zero. <strong>Levelling:</strong> position the transducer at the <strong>level of the heart</strong> (the phlebostatic axis: 4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line) — or at the external auditory meatus for cerebral perfusion pressure in neurosurgery. A transducer <strong>10 cm below</strong> the heart reads <strong>~7.5 mmHg too high</strong>.</li><li><strong>Waveform information:</strong> systolic, diastolic and mean; <strong>dicrotic notch</strong> (aortic valve closure); slope of upstroke (contractility, roughly); area under the systolic curve (stroke volume, used by pulse contour devices); <strong>pulse pressure and systolic pressure variation</strong> with ventilation (fluid responsiveness); rhythm. The waveform gets <strong>narrower and taller distally</strong> (systolic amplification), while mean pressure falls slightly.</li><li><strong>Indications:</strong> beat-to-beat monitoring (major surgery, haemodynamic instability, vasoactive drugs), frequent blood sampling, and when NIBP is impossible.</li><li><strong>Complications:</strong> thrombosis and distal ischaemia (check collateral flow; Allen's test is unreliable), haemorrhage from disconnection, infection, <strong>inadvertent intra-arterial drug injection</strong> (label clearly), air embolism, false aneurysm, nerve damage.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> oscillometry principle; cuff size errors; Wheatstone bridge; zeroing versus levelling; waveform features.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the arterial line reads 60/30 but the patient looks well and the NIBP is 120/70: work through the causes.</li></ul></div>","procoagulants-blood-products":"<h2 id=\"drugs\">Drugs</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Uses and notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Tranexamic acid</strong></td><td>Lysine analogue: blocks plasminogen binding to fibrin (antifibrinolytic)</td><td>Trauma (<strong>within 3 hours</strong> of injury: CRASH-2), obstetric haemorrhage (WOMAN), major orthopaedic and cardiac surgery; seizures at high doses; reduce in renal impairment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vitamin K (phytomenadione)</strong></td><td>Cofactor for γ-carboxylation of II, VII, IX, X</td><td>Warfarin reversal (IV acts within 6–12 h)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Protamine</strong></td><td>Basic protein binding acidic heparin</td><td>Hypotension, pulmonary hypertension, anaphylaxis (fish allergy, previous exposure, protamine insulin); excess is itself anticoagulant</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Desmopressin (DDAVP)</strong></td><td>V<sub>2</sub> agonist: releases vWF and factor VIII</td><td>Type 1 von Willebrand disease, mild haemophilia A, uraemic platelet dysfunction; hyponatraemia; tachyphylaxis</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prothrombin complex concentrate</strong></td><td>Factors II, VII, IX, X (± proteins C and S)</td><td>Rapid warfarin reversal; small volume; thrombotic risk</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fibrinogen concentrate</strong></td><td>Fibrinogen</td><td>Hypofibrinogenaemia (e.g. obstetric haemorrhage guided by FIBTEM or Clauss)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Recombinant factor VIIa</strong></td><td>Activates X on platelets at the site of injury</td><td>Haemophilia with inhibitors; off-label rescue</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"blood-components-in-haemorrhage\">Blood components in haemorrhage</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>FFP:</strong> all factors; 12–15 mL/kg; needs thawing (about 20–30 min).</li><li><strong>Cryoprecipitate:</strong> fibrinogen, VIII, vWF, XIII; about 2 pools raise fibrinogen by about 1 g/L in an adult.</li><li><strong>Platelets:</strong> aim &gt; 75 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L in active major bleeding (&gt; 100 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L in multiple trauma or CNS injury).</li><li><strong>Major haemorrhage protocols:</strong> early high ratios of FFP (and platelets) to red cells in trauma; goal-directed replacement with laboratory or viscoelastic results; correct <strong>hypothermia, acidosis and hypocalcaemia</strong> (the lethal triad plus calcium).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"blood-conservation\">Blood conservation</h2>\n<p>Treat preoperative anaemia; stop antithrombotic drugs appropriately; surgical technique and haemostasis; positioning; tranexamic acid; <strong>cell salvage</strong> (intraoperative collection, washing and reinfusion of red cells; acceptable to many Jehovah's Witnesses in a continuous circuit; caution in malignancy and contamination — leucocyte filters help); acute normovolaemic haemodilution; restrictive transfusion thresholds; controlled hypotension in selected cases.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms and indications; composition of FFP versus cryoprecipitate versus PCC; tranexamic acid timing.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> leading a major obstetric haemorrhage: drugs, products and targets, and communicating with the lab and team.</li></ul></div>","pulse-oximetry":"<h2 id=\"principle\">Principle</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Beer–Lambert law:</strong> absorbance depends on the concentration of the absorbing substance (<strong>Beer</strong>) and the path length (<strong>Lambert</strong>).</li><li>Two LEDs emit <strong>red (660 nm)</strong> and <strong>infrared (940 nm)</strong> light, rapidly alternating (with a period with both off to correct for ambient light); a photodetector on the other side measures transmitted light.</li><li><strong>Deoxyhaemoglobin absorbs more red light</strong>; <strong>oxyhaemoglobin absorbs more infrared</strong>. The <strong>isobestic points</strong> (where both absorb equally, e.g. <strong>~805 nm</strong> and ~590 nm) are used in some devices as references.</li><li>Only the <strong>pulsatile (AC) component</strong> is due to arterial blood; the constant (DC) component represents tissue, venous and capillary blood. The ratio <strong>R = (AC<sub>660</sub>/DC<sub>660</sub>) / (AC<sub>940</sub>/DC<sub>940</sub>)</strong> is converted to SpO<sub>2</sub> using a <strong>calibration curve derived from healthy volunteers</strong> (who can only be desaturated safely to about 70–80%; below this readings are extrapolated and less accurate). R = 1 corresponds to about 85%; R ≈ 0.4 to 100%.</li><li>Accuracy is about ±2% in the range 70–100%.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sources-of-error\">Sources of error</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Carboxyhaemoglobin</strong></td><td>Reads as oxyhaemoglobin → <strong>falsely high</strong> SpO<sub>2</sub></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Methaemoglobin</strong></td><td>Absorbs both wavelengths equally → SpO<sub>2</sub> tends towards <strong>~85%</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Methylene blue, indocyanine green</strong></td><td>Falsely <strong>low</strong> readings (transient)</td></tr><tr><td>Low perfusion, vasoconstriction, cold, hypotension</td><td>Poor or absent signal</td></tr><tr><td>Motion, shivering</td><td>Artefact (reduced by signal processing)</td></tr><tr><td>Venous pulsation (tricuspid regurgitation, tight probe)</td><td>Falsely low</td></tr><tr><td>Ambient light, nail varnish (especially blue, black, green)</td><td>Error</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Skin pigmentation</strong></td><td><strong>Overestimation</strong> in people with darker skin, increasing the risk of undetected hypoxaemia — be cautious and check arterial gases when in doubt</td></tr><tr><td>Delay</td><td>Response lags behind arterial changes (finger probes more than ear or forehead probes)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>SpO<sub>2</sub> is not a measure of ventilation (a patient on high FiO<sub>2</sub> may have normal SpO<sub>2</sub> despite severe hypercapnia) or of oxygen content (anaemia).</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> wavelengths; Beer–Lambert; why methaemoglobin gives 85%; calibration; sources of error.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> SpO<sub>2</sub> reads 100% in a patient rescued from a house fire: why you do not trust it and what you do.</li></ul></div>","renal-gfr":"<h2 id=\"renal-blood-flow\">Renal blood flow</h2>\n<ul><li>About <strong>1.2 L/min (20–25% of cardiac output)</strong>; plasma flow about 650 mL/min.</li><li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> keeps flow and GFR fairly constant between a MAP of about <strong>80–180 mmHg</strong> (quoted ranges vary), by:<ul><li>the <strong>myogenic response</strong> of the afferent arteriole (stretch → constriction);</li><li><strong>tubuloglomerular feedback</strong>: the <strong>macula densa</strong> senses NaCl delivery to the distal tubule; increased delivery → afferent vasoconstriction (adenosine) and less renin.</li></ul></li><li>Sympathetic stimulation, catecholamines and angiotensin II reduce flow. Angiotensin II constricts the <strong>efferent</strong> arteriole preferentially, supporting GFR when perfusion is low; this is why ACE inhibitors and ARBs can precipitate AKI in hypovolaemia or renal artery stenosis. NSAIDs remove prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilatation.</li><li>The <strong>outer cortex</strong> is well perfused; the <strong>medulla</strong> receives about 10% of flow and runs a low PO<sub>2</sub> (countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta), making it vulnerable to hypoxic injury.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"glomerular-filtration\">Glomerular filtration</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>GFR ≈ 125 mL/min (180 L/day)</strong> in a young adult; <strong>filtration fraction</strong> (GFR / renal plasma flow) ≈ <strong>20%</strong>.</li><li>Starling forces across the glomerular capillary: <strong>GFR = Kf [(P<sub>GC</sub> − P<sub>BS</sub>) − (π<sub>GC</sub> − π<sub>BS</sub>)]</strong>. Glomerular capillary pressure is high (about 45–60 mmHg; figures vary between sources) because the efferent arteriole provides downstream resistance.</li><li>The filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, podocyte slit diaphragms) excludes molecules above about 70 kDa; albumin (~69 kDa, negatively charged) is largely retained.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"clearance\">Clearance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Clearance = (U × V) / P</strong>: the volume of plasma completely cleared of a substance per minute.</li><li><strong>Inulin</strong> is freely filtered, neither reabsorbed nor secreted: its clearance = GFR (the gold standard).</li><li><strong>Creatinine</strong> clearance slightly <strong>overestimates</strong> GFR because a little creatinine is secreted.</li><li><strong>Para-aminohippuric acid (PAH)</strong> is filtered and almost completely secreted in one pass: its clearance ≈ effective renal plasma flow.</li><li><strong>eGFR</strong> (e.g. CKD-EPI) estimates GFR from plasma creatinine, age and sex; it is unreliable when creatinine is not at steady state (AKI) or muscle mass is abnormal. <strong>Cockcroft–Gault</strong> estimates creatinine clearance and is still used for some drug dosing.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"urea-and-creatinine\">Urea and creatinine</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Creatinine</strong> comes from muscle creatine at a fairly constant rate; plasma level rises only after GFR has fallen substantially and depends on muscle mass.</li><li><strong>Urea</strong> is filtered and partly reabsorbed (more when flow is low, so a high urea:creatinine ratio suggests hypovolaemia); it also rises with protein load, GI bleeding, catabolism and steroids.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"acute-kidney-injury\">Acute kidney injury</h2>\n<ul><li>Defined (KDIGO, as adopted by NICE) by a rise in creatinine of ≥ 26 µmol/L within 48 h or ≥ 1.5 × baseline within 7 days, or urine output &lt; 0.5 mL/kg/h for more than 6 hours.</li><li>Perioperative risk factors: CKD, age, diabetes, heart failure, sepsis, hypovolaemia and hypotension, nephrotoxins (NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, contrast), major and emergency surgery.</li><li>Prevention: maintain perfusion and volume, avoid nephrotoxins, review ACE inhibitors and diuretics, monitor urine output and creatinine.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> clearance calculations; why creatinine overestimates GFR; autoregulation mechanisms; drugs that impair autoregulation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> an oliguric patient in recovery: assess volume status, catheter, perfusion and drugs, and explain your plan.</li></ul></div>","renal-tubule":"<h2 id=\"the-nephron-segment-by-segment\">The nephron segment by segment</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Key functions</th><th>Transporters / notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Proximal convoluted tubule</strong></td><td>Reabsorbs <strong>~65%</strong> of filtered Na<sup>+</sup> and water (isotonically), nearly all glucose and amino acids, ~80–90% of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>; secretes organic acids and bases (many drugs)</td><td>Na<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> exchanger; carbonic anhydrase (acetazolamide acts here); SGLT2 (glucose; SGLT2 inhibitors)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thin descending limb</strong></td><td>Permeable to water, not solutes</td><td>Water leaves into the hypertonic medulla</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thick ascending limb</strong></td><td>Reabsorbs <strong>~25%</strong> of Na<sup>+</sup>; <strong>impermeable to water</strong> (\"diluting segment\")</td><td><strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/2Cl<sup>−</sup> co-transporter</strong> (loop diuretics)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Distal convoluted tubule</strong></td><td>~5–8% of Na<sup>+</sup>; Ca<sup>2+</sup> reabsorption (PTH)</td><td><strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>−</sup> co-transporter</strong> (thiazides)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collecting duct</strong></td><td>Fine control: Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption and K<sup>+</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> secretion (<strong>aldosterone</strong>, principal and intercalated cells); water reabsorption (<strong>ADH</strong>)</td><td>ENaC (amiloride); mineralocorticoid receptor (spironolactone); aquaporin-2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"concentrating-urine-the-countercurrent-multiplier\">Concentrating urine: the countercurrent multiplier</h2>\n<ul><li>The thick ascending limb pumps NaCl into the interstitium without water, creating a gradient that increases towards the papilla (to about <strong>1200–1400 mOsm/kg</strong> in humans). <strong>Urea</strong> recycling from the medullary collecting duct adds to it; the <strong>vasa recta</strong> act as countercurrent exchangers that preserve the gradient.</li><li>With <strong>ADH</strong>, aquaporin-2 channels are inserted into the collecting duct, water follows the gradient, and urine can be concentrated to about 1200 mOsm/kg; without ADH it can be diluted to about 50 mOsm/kg.</li><li>Minimum obligatory urine volume ≈ solute load / maximal concentration (about 600 mOsm/day ÷ 1200 ≈ <strong>0.5 L/day</strong>).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"water-balance-adh-vasopressin\">Water balance: ADH (vasopressin)</h2>\n<ul><li>Made in the <strong>supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei</strong>, released from the <strong>posterior pituitary</strong>.</li><li>Released for <strong>raised plasma osmolality</strong> (osmoreceptors respond to changes of 1–2%) and for <strong>hypovolaemia/hypotension</strong> (less sensitive, but can override osmolality when large). Also by pain, nausea, surgery (stress), opioids, positive pressure ventilation.</li><li>V<sub>2</sub> receptors (collecting duct, cAMP → aquaporin-2): water retention. V<sub>1</sub> receptors: vasoconstriction.</li><li><strong>SIADH</strong> (surgery, pneumonia, intracranial pathology, drugs) → hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine. <strong>Diabetes insipidus</strong> (pituitary surgery, head injury, brainstem death) → dilute polyuria and hypernatraemia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"renal-prostaglandins-and-nsaids\">Renal prostaglandins and NSAIDs</h2>\n<ul><li>Prostaglandins (PGE<sub>2</sub>, PGI<sub>2</sub>) dilate the afferent arteriole and protect renal blood flow when it is threatened (hypovolaemia, heart failure, cirrhosis, high angiotensin II or noradrenaline). They also oppose ADH in the medulla.</li><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong> remove this protection: in a hypovolaemic or \"triple whammy\" patient (NSAID + ACE inhibitor/ARB + diuretic) they can precipitate acute kidney injury, and they cause sodium and water retention and hyperkalaemia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sodium-balance\">Sodium balance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Aldosterone</strong> (adrenal zona glomerulosa; stimulated by angiotensin II and <strong>raised K<sup>+</sup></strong>): increases Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption and K<sup>+</sup> and H<sup>+</sup> secretion in the collecting duct.</li><li><strong>ANP/BNP</strong> (atrial stretch): natriuresis and vasodilation.</li><li>The sympathetic system and angiotensin II also increase proximal Na<sup>+</sup> reabsorption.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"potassium\">Potassium</h2>\n<p>Most filtered K<sup>+</sup> is reabsorbed proximally; urinary K<sup>+</sup> is determined by <strong>distal secretion</strong>, increased by aldosterone, high distal Na<sup>+</sup> delivery and flow (loop and thiazide diuretics → hypokalaemia) and alkalosis.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> where each diuretic acts; the countercurrent multiplier; stimuli for ADH and aldosterone; SIADH versus DI.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a confused patient with a sodium of 121 mmol/L after surgery: assess volume and osmolality, and explain the likely cause and safe correction.</li></ul></div>","resp-altitude":"<h2 id=\"altitude\">Altitude</h2>\n<ul><li>Barometric pressure falls roughly exponentially with height: about <strong>half at 5,500 m</strong>. FiO<sub>2</sub> remains 0.21, but inspired PO<sub>2</sub> falls.</li><li><strong>Acute response:</strong> hypoxic stimulation of the carotid bodies → hyperventilation → respiratory alkalosis, which initially limits the ventilatory response.</li><li><strong>Acclimatisation (days to weeks):</strong> renal bicarbonate excretion corrects the alkalosis and ventilation rises further; <strong>2,3-DPG</strong> increases (right shift); <strong>erythropoietin</strong> raises haemoglobin over weeks; capillary density and mitochondrial changes.</li><li><strong>Illness:</strong> acute mountain sickness, high-altitude cerebral oedema and high-altitude pulmonary oedema (uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction).</li><li><strong>Vaporisers at altitude:</strong> a variable-bypass vaporiser delivers roughly the same <strong>partial pressure</strong> of agent regardless of altitude, because saturated vapour pressure depends only on temperature. Since anaesthetic effect depends on partial pressure, the dial setting gives a similar clinical effect, although the percentage delivered is higher.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"diving-and-increased-pressure\">Diving and increased pressure</h2>\n<ul><li>Every <strong>10 m of seawater adds about 1 atmosphere</strong> (≈ 100 kPa).</li><li><strong>Boyle's law:</strong> gas volumes in closed spaces shrink on descent and expand on ascent → barotrauma of ears, sinuses and lungs; arterial gas embolism if a diver ascends holding their breath.</li><li><strong>Henry's law:</strong> more nitrogen dissolves at depth; if ascent is too fast it comes out of solution → <strong>decompression sickness</strong>.</li><li><strong>Nitrogen narcosis</strong> (below about 30 m) and <strong>oxygen toxicity</strong> (CNS toxicity with seizures at high PO<sub>2</sub>; pulmonary toxicity with prolonged exposure).</li><li><strong>Gas density</strong> rises with pressure (air at 4 atmospheres is four times as dense), increasing the work of breathing. Deep divers breathe <strong>helium–oxygen</strong> mixtures, which are less dense and avoid nitrogen narcosis.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hyperbaric-oxygen\">Hyperbaric oxygen</h2>\n<ul><li>Breathing 100% O<sub>2</sub> at 2–3 atmospheres raises dissolved oxygen enough to supply resting needs without haemoglobin (at about 3 atm absolute, dissolved O<sub>2</sub> is roughly 6 mL/dL).</li><li>Uses include decompression sickness, arterial gas embolism, severe carbon monoxide poisoning (shortens COHb half-life), necrotising soft-tissue infections and radiation injury.</li><li>Anaesthetic issues: monitoring and ventilator function under pressure, air-filled cuffs (fill with saline), pneumothorax must be drained, limited access.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate inspired PO<sub>2</sub> at altitude; vaporiser performance at altitude; gas laws applied to diving.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning the transfer of a patient with a pneumothorax by air.</li></ul></div>","resp-co2-transport":"<h2 id=\"how-co-2-is-carried-in-arterial-blood\">How CO<sub>2</sub> is carried in arterial blood</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Share of total (approx.)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Bicarbonate</strong></td><td>~90%</td><td>Formed in red cells by <strong>carbonic anhydrase</strong>: CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌ H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> ⇌ H<sup>+</sup> + HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Carbamino compounds</strong></td><td>~5%</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub> bound to terminal amine groups, mostly on haemoglobin</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dissolved</strong></td><td>~5%</td><td>CO<sub>2</sub> is about 20 times more soluble than O<sub>2</sub></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <em>arteriovenous difference</em> in CO<sub>2</sub> content is carried in different proportions: carbamino compounds account for a much larger share (around a third) of CO<sub>2</sub> unloaded in the lungs than of the total.</p>\n<h2 id=\"chloride-hamburger-shift\">Chloride (Hamburger) shift</h2>\n<p>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> formed in red cells diffuses out into plasma in exchange for Cl<sup>−</sup> (via band 3 protein). Venous red cells therefore contain more chloride, and they swell slightly because of the extra osmotically active particles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-haldane-effect\">The Haldane effect</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Deoxygenated haemoglobin carries more CO<sub>2</sub></strong> than oxyhaemoglobin at the same PCO<sub>2</sub>. It is a better buffer for H<sup>+</sup> (a weaker acid) and forms carbamino compounds more readily.</li><li>In the tissues, oxygen unloading helps load CO<sub>2</sub>; in the lungs, oxygenation helps release CO<sub>2</sub>.</li><li>This is why venous pH is only slightly lower than arterial pH despite the added CO<sub>2</sub> (\"isohydric\" transport).</li><li>Clinical relevance: giving high-concentration oxygen to some patients with chronic CO<sub>2</sub> retention can raise PaCO<sub>2</sub>, partly through the Haldane effect and partly through worsening V/Q mismatch (release of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), rather than solely through loss of \"hypoxic drive\".</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-bohr-effect-for-contrast\">The Bohr effect (for contrast)</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Bohr effect</strong> is the effect of CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sup>+</sup> on <strong>oxygen</strong> binding (a right shift of the O<sub>2</sub> curve). The <strong>Haldane effect</strong> is the effect of <strong>oxygen</strong> on CO<sub>2</sub> carriage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"co-2-dissociation-curve\">CO<sub>2</sub> dissociation curve</h2>\n<p>Nearly <strong>linear</strong> over the physiological range and much steeper than the O<sub>2</sub> curve. So hyperventilating healthy lung units can offload extra CO<sub>2</sub> to compensate for poorly ventilated units, whereas they cannot add extra oxygen. This is why V/Q mismatch causes hypoxaemia well before hypercapnia.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the proportions; chloride shift; Haldane versus Bohr; the shape of the CO<sub>2</sub> curve.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpreting a raised PaCO<sub>2</sub> in a patient with COPD given high-flow oxygen, and targeting saturations of 88–92% as in the BTS guideline.</li></ul></div>","resp-control":"<h2 id=\"the-controller\">The controller</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Respiratory centres in the medulla</strong> (dorsal and ventral respiratory groups; the pre-Bötzinger complex generates rhythm), modulated by the <strong>pons</strong> (pneumotaxic and apneustic centres) and by the cortex (voluntary control).</li><li><strong>Effectors:</strong> diaphragm (phrenic nerve, C3–5), intercostals, accessory muscles, upper airway dilators.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sensors\">Sensors</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Central chemoreceptors</th><th>Peripheral chemoreceptors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Site</td><td>Ventrolateral surface of the medulla</td><td><strong>Carotid bodies</strong> (main; glossopharyngeal nerve) and aortic bodies (vagus)</td></tr><tr><td>Stimulus</td><td><strong>H<sup>+</sup> in CSF</strong>, generated from CO<sub>2</sub> diffusing across the blood–brain barrier</td><td><strong>Falling PaO<sub>2</sub></strong> (steeply below about 8 kPa), rising PaCO<sub>2</sub>, and H<sup>+</sup> (carotid bodies; the aortic bodies respond little to pH)</td></tr><tr><td>Share of CO<sub>2</sub> response</td><td>~80%</td><td>~20%, but faster</td></tr><tr><td>Acid–base</td><td>Much more sensitive to a <strong>respiratory</strong> than a metabolic acidosis: CO<sub>2</sub> crosses the blood–brain barrier freely, H<sup>+</sup> does not</td><td>Respond to metabolic acidosis (carotid bodies)</td></tr><tr><td>Adaptation</td><td>CSF bicarbonate is adjusted over 24–48 h, resetting the response in chronic hypercapnia</td><td>Rapid</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other inputs: stretch receptors (Hering–Breuer reflex, weak in adult humans), irritant and J receptors, joint and muscle receptors during exercise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ventilatory-responses\">Ventilatory responses</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>CO<sub>2</sub> response:</strong> ventilation rises roughly linearly with PaCO<sub>2</sub> (about 2–3 L/min per mmHg, or 15–20 L/min per kPa, in healthy adults). Hypoxia steepens the slope.</li><li><strong>Hypoxic response:</strong> little change until PaO<sub>2</sub> falls below about 8 kPa, then a steep rise. The carotid bodies respond to <strong>PaO<sub>2</sub>, not oxygen content</strong>, so anaemia and CO poisoning do not stimulate them.</li><li><strong>Apnoeic threshold:</strong> the PaCO<sub>2</sub> below which spontaneous breathing stops. After hyperventilation under anaesthesia, breathing will not resume until PaCO<sub>2</sub> rises above it.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Oxygen in chronic hypercapnia:</strong> giving high-flow oxygen to some patients with COPD raises PaCO<sub>2</sub>. A loss of \"hypoxic drive\" is only a small part of it; worsening V/Q mismatch (release of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction) and the Haldane effect matter more. Titrate oxygen to a target saturation of 88–92% in those at risk.</p>\n<h2 id=\"effects-of-drugs\">Effects of drugs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Opioids</strong> shift the CO<sub>2</sub> response curve to the right and flatten it, and reduce respiratory rate more than tidal volume.</li><li><strong>Volatile agents and propofol</strong> depress the CO<sub>2</sub> response, and the hypoxic response is depressed even at sub-anaesthetic concentrations (around 0.1 MAC). This matters in recovery.</li><li><strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> combined with opioids are synergistic.</li><li><strong>Doxapram</strong> stimulates peripheral (and at higher doses central) chemoreceptors.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> CO<sub>2</sub> response curves (draw them with the effects of opioids and hypoxia); the carotid body's stimulus; the apnoeic threshold.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the drowsy patient in recovery with a respiratory rate of 6 after morphine: assess, support, and consider naloxone, with the physiology behind it.</li></ul></div>","resp-ippv":"<h2 id=\"respiratory-effects\">Respiratory effects</h2>\n<ul><li>Normal breathing is by <strong>negative</strong> intrapleural pressure; IPPV reverses this, pushing gas in with positive airway pressure.</li><li><strong>V/Q:</strong> ventilation goes preferentially to non-dependent, more compliant lung, while perfusion stays dependent → increased V/Q mismatch and <strong>dead space</strong>. Zone 1 may appear.</li><li><strong>Atelectasis</strong> in dependent regions (reduced FRC, absorption with high FiO<sub>2</sub>); PEEP and recruitment help.</li><li><strong>Ventilator-induced lung injury:</strong> volutrauma (overdistension), barotrauma (pneumothorax), atelectrauma (cyclical opening and closing), biotrauma (inflammatory mediators). Protective ventilation: tidal volume <strong>~6 mL/kg predicted body weight</strong> in ARDS (6–8 mL/kg in theatre), plateau pressure under 30 cmH<sub>2</sub>O, and appropriate PEEP.</li><li>Loss of mucociliary clearance, and risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia when prolonged.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"cardiovascular-effects\">Cardiovascular effects</h2>\n<ul><li>Raised intrathoracic pressure <strong>reduces venous return</strong> and right ventricular preload → lower cardiac output and blood pressure, especially if hypovolaemic (the basis of pulse pressure variation as a marker of fluid responsiveness).</li><li><strong>Increases RV afterload</strong> at high lung volumes (compression of alveolar vessels).</li><li><strong>Reduces LV afterload</strong> (lower transmural pressure) — helpful in LV failure, which is one reason CPAP helps acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-organs\">Other organs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Renal:</strong> reduced renal blood flow and GFR, raised ADH and renin–angiotensin–aldosterone activity → fluid retention.</li><li><strong>Hepatic and splanchnic:</strong> reduced blood flow.</li><li><strong>Cerebral:</strong> raised intrathoracic pressure impedes cerebral venous drainage and can raise ICP; hyperventilation lowers PaCO<sub>2</sub> and cerebral blood flow.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the cardiovascular effects of PEEP; causes of dead space during IPPV; protective ventilation targets.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> hypotension immediately after intubation in a septic, hypovolaemic patient: explain the physiology and your immediate management.</li></ul></div>","resp-lung-volumes":"<h2 id=\"key-facts\">Key facts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Volume or capacity</th><th>Typical adult value (70 kg)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tidal volume (VT)</td><td>500 mL (about 7 mL/kg)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Inspiratory reserve volume</td><td>~3 L</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)</td><td>~1.2 L</td><td>Falls when supine, with obesity and pregnancy</td></tr><tr><td>Residual volume (RV)</td><td>~1.2 L</td><td>Cannot be measured by spirometry</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Functional residual capacity (FRC)</strong> = ERV + RV</td><td><strong>~2.5 L upright</strong>, falls by roughly 0.5–1 L supine</td><td>The lungs' oxygen reserve during apnoea</td></tr><tr><td>Vital capacity</td><td>~4.5 L (≈ 60–70 mL/kg)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Total lung capacity</td><td>~6 L</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>FRC</strong> is the volume at the end of a normal expiration, where the inward recoil of the lung balances the outward recoil of the chest wall.</li><li>FRC matters for four reasons: it is the <strong>oxygen store</strong> during apnoea; keeping it above closing capacity <strong>prevents airway closure</strong>; lung <strong>compliance</strong> is greatest near FRC; and <strong>PVR</strong> is lowest at FRC.</li><li><strong>RV, FRC and TLC</strong> cannot be measured by simple spirometry: use <strong>helium dilution</strong> (measures communicating gas only), <strong>nitrogen washout</strong>, or <strong>body plethysmography</strong> (measures all intrathoracic gas, including trapped gas).</li><li><strong>Closing capacity (CC)</strong> is the lung volume at which small airways in dependent zones begin to close. It <strong>rises with age</strong>; it equals FRC when supine at about 44 years and when upright at about 66 years. When CC exceeds FRC, airway closure occurs during normal breathing, causing V/Q mismatch and shunt.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-reduces-frc\">What reduces FRC</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Posture:</strong> supine (abdominal contents push the diaphragm up), Trendelenburg.</li><li><strong>General anaesthesia:</strong> loss of diaphragmatic and chest wall tone reduces FRC by around 15–20%, whichever technique is used; atelectasis forms within minutes, especially with high FiO<sub>2</sub>.</li><li><strong>Obesity, pregnancy (term), abdominal distension, pneumoperitoneum.</strong></li><li><strong>Restrictive lung disease.</strong></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"clinical-relevance\">Clinical relevance</h2>\n<ul><li>Pre-oxygenation fills the FRC with oxygen (denitrogenation). An FRC of 2.5 L at FeO<sub>2</sub> 0.9 holds over 2 L of O<sub>2</sub>; at resting consumption of about 250 mL/min, this is why a healthy adult can tolerate several minutes of apnoea. Obese and pregnant patients desaturate much faster: smaller FRC, higher oxygen consumption.</li><li>Head-up positioning, CPAP/PEEP during pre-oxygenation and high-flow nasal oxygen help maintain FRC and oxygen reserves.</li><li>Atelectasis after induction is reduced by avoiding unnecessarily high FiO<sub>2</sub> once intubated, recruitment manoeuvres and PEEP.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> definitions; which volumes spirometry cannot measure; helium dilution versus plethysmography; effect of age and posture on closing capacity.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> \"Why did this patient with a BMI of 45 desaturate so quickly at induction, and what would you do differently?\" Link FRC, oxygen consumption and closing capacity to a practical plan.</li></ul></div>","resp-mechanics":"<h2 id=\"compliance\">Compliance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Compliance = ΔV / ΔP.</strong> Normal total respiratory system compliance is about <strong>100 mL/cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong> (lung ~200 and chest wall ~200 mL/cmH<sub>2</sub>O, which add as reciprocals: 1/C<sub>total</sub> = 1/C<sub>lung</sub> + 1/C~chest wall~).</li><li><strong>Static compliance</strong> is measured with no gas flow (plateau pressure), so it reflects elastic properties only. <strong>Dynamic compliance</strong> is measured during flow (peak pressure) and is lower because it includes airway resistance.</li><li>The <strong>pressure–volume curve</strong> of the lung is sigmoid: compliance is lowest at very low volumes (alveolar collapse) and near TLC (overdistension). Normal breathing at FRC sits on the steep part.</li><li>Compliance is reduced by pulmonary fibrosis, oedema, ARDS, atelectasis, obesity (chest wall), and increased in emphysema and old age (loss of elastic tissue).</li><li><strong>Hysteresis:</strong> the inflation and deflation limbs differ, because of surfactant and alveolar recruitment.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"surface-tension-and-surfactant\">Surface tension and surfactant</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Laplace's law</strong> for a sphere: <strong>P = 2T / r</strong>. Without surfactant, small alveoli would empty into large ones.</li><li><strong>Surfactant</strong> (dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine, from type II pneumocytes) lowers surface tension, more so as alveoli shrink. This stabilises alveoli, increases compliance, reduces the work of breathing and helps keep alveoli dry (less fluid is drawn in from capillaries).</li><li>Deficiency: neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (surfactant production matures from about 34 weeks' gestation).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"airway-resistance\">Airway resistance</h2>\n<ul><li>Laminar flow: resistance ∝ <strong>η·l / r<sup>4</sup></strong> (Hagen–Poiseuille). Halving the radius increases resistance 16-fold.</li><li>Most resistance in normal lungs is in the <strong>medium-sized bronchi</strong>, not the small airways, because small airways are so numerous that their total cross-sectional area is huge.</li><li>Resistance falls as lung volume increases (radial traction), so it rises at low lung volumes, with bronchospasm, secretions, and narrow or kinked tracheal tubes.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"work-of-breathing\">Work of breathing</h2>\n<ul><li>Work = pressure × volume. It has an <strong>elastic</strong> component (overcome compliance; greater with large tidal volumes) and a <strong>resistive</strong> component (overcome airway and tissue resistance; greater with high flow rates and fast breathing).</li><li>At rest breathing uses only about 1–2% of total oxygen consumption; this can rise dramatically in respiratory disease.</li><li>Patients naturally choose a respiratory rate that minimises work: stiff lungs → rapid shallow breathing; obstructed airways → slow deep breathing.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculating compliance from ventilator pressures; static versus dynamic compliance; Laplace; effect of radius on resistance.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> interpreting a rising peak pressure with a normal plateau pressure (resistance problem: kinked tube, bronchospasm, secretions) versus rising peak and plateau (compliance problem: endobronchial tube, pneumothorax, abdominal insufflation, light anaesthesia with chest wall rigidity).</li></ul></div>","resp-o2-transport":"<h2 id=\"oxygen-content-and-delivery\">Oxygen content and delivery</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Oxygen content:</strong> <strong>CaO<sub>2</sub> = (1.34 × Hb × SaO<sub>2</sub>) + (0.023 × PaO<sub>2</sub>)</strong>, with Hb in g/dL, PaO<sub>2</sub> in kPa and content in mL/dL. (The Hüfner constant is often quoted between 1.31 and 1.39.)</li><li>Normal CaO<sub>2</sub> ≈ 20 mL/dL; CvO<sub>2</sub> ≈ 15 mL/dL. Dissolved oxygen is small: about 0.3 mL/dL at a normal PaO<sub>2</sub>, but it matters in hyperbaric therapy.</li><li><strong>Delivery: DO<sub>2</sub> = CO × CaO<sub>2</sub></strong> ≈ 5 L/min × 200 mL/L ≈ <strong>1000 mL/min</strong>. <strong>Consumption VO<sub>2</sub></strong> ≈ <strong>250 mL/min</strong> at rest, so resting <strong>oxygen extraction ≈ 25%</strong> and mixed venous saturation ≈ 75%.</li><li>DO<sub>2</sub> falls with anaemia, hypoxaemia and low cardiac output. Of these, the body compensates first by increasing cardiac output and extraction.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-oxygen-cascade\">The oxygen cascade</h2>\n<p>Inspired (dry air) ≈ 21 kPa → humidified tracheal ≈ 19.8 kPa → alveolar ≈ 13.3 kPa → arterial ≈ 12–13 kPa (venous admixture) → capillary → mitochondria, where PO<sub>2</sub> may be only about 0.1–1 kPa.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-dissociation-curve\">The dissociation curve</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Sigmoid</strong> because of <strong>cooperative binding</strong>: binding of each O<sub>2</sub> increases the affinity of the remaining haem groups (T to R state).</li><li>Reference points: <strong>P50 ≈ 3.5 kPa</strong> (26.6 mmHg); venous point ≈ 5.3 kPa / 75%; arterial ≈ 13.3 kPa / 97–98%.</li><li>The flat upper part protects saturation despite falls in PaO<sub>2</sub>; the steep part allows large unloading for small falls in tissue PO<sub>2</sub>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Right shift (↓ affinity, ↑ P50, better unloading)</th><th>Left shift (↑ affinity, ↓ P50)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>↑ H<sup>+</sup> (↓ pH), ↑ PCO<sub>2</sub> (Bohr effect)</td><td>Alkalosis, hypocapnia</td></tr><tr><td>↑ temperature</td><td>Hypothermia</td></tr><tr><td>↑ 2,3-DPG (chronic hypoxia, anaemia, altitude)</td><td>↓ 2,3-DPG (stored blood)</td></tr><tr><td>Pregnancy (maternal curve)</td><td>Fetal haemoglobin, carboxyhaemoglobin, methaemoglobin</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Carbon monoxide</strong> binds haemoglobin with over 200 times the affinity of oxygen: it reduces content <strong>and</strong> shifts the curve left. Standard pulse oximetry reads COHb as oxyhaemoglobin.</li><li><strong>Placental exchange:</strong> fetal haemoglobin has a P50 of about 2.4–2.7 kPa. As fetal CO<sub>2</sub> passes to the mother, the maternal curve shifts right (unloading) and the fetal curve left (loading): the <strong>double Bohr effect</strong> (with a double Haldane effect for CO<sub>2</sub>).</li><li><strong>Myoglobin</strong> has a hyperbolic curve with a very low P50 (≈ 0.3 kPa); it stores oxygen in muscle.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate content and delivery; the effect of anaemia versus hypoxaemia; causes of curve shifts; why stored blood has low 2,3-DPG.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explain oxygen delivery to a relative or colleague in the context of a bleeding, anaemic patient; draw and annotate the curve.</li></ul></div>","resp-pulmonary-circulation":"<h2 id=\"pressures-and-resistance\">Pressures and resistance</h2>\n<ul><li>A low-pressure, high-flow, highly compliant circuit: PA pressure about <strong>25/10 mmHg (mean ~15)</strong>, left atrial pressure ~5–8 mmHg.</li><li><strong>PVR = (mean PAP − LAP) / CO × 80</strong> dyn·s·cm<sup>−5</sup>; normal about <strong>100–200 dyn·s·cm<sup>−5</sup></strong> (about one tenth of SVR).</li><li>PVR <strong>falls as cardiac output rises</strong>, by <strong>recruitment</strong> of closed capillaries and <strong>distension</strong> of open ones, so pressure rises little with exercise.</li><li>PVR is lowest at <strong>FRC</strong>: at low lung volumes extra-alveolar vessels narrow; at high lung volumes alveolar vessels are compressed.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"west-zones-upright-lung\">West zones (upright lung)</h2>\n<p>Gravity makes perfusion greatest at the base. Comparing alveolar (P<sub>A</sub>), arterial (P<sub>a</sub>) and venous (P<sub>v</sub>) pressures:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Pressures</th><th>Flow</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 (apex)</td><td>P<sub>A</sub> &gt; P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub></td><td>None: alveolar dead space. Not seen normally; appears with hypotension or high airway pressure (PEEP, IPPV)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>A</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub></td><td>Intermittent; driven by arterial minus alveolar pressure (the \"waterfall\")</td></tr><tr><td>3 (base)</td><td>P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub> &gt; P<sub>A</sub></td><td>Continuous; driven by the arteriovenous difference</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A pulmonary artery catheter tip should sit in zone 3 for the wedge pressure to reflect left atrial pressure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"bronchial-circulation\">Bronchial circulation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Bronchial arteries</strong> (from the thoracic aorta) supply the airways down to the terminal bronchioles at systemic pressure.</li><li>Part of their venous return drains into the <strong>pulmonary veins</strong>: deoxygenated blood joins oxygenated blood, forming part of the normal <strong>anatomical shunt</strong> (with the Thebesian veins). This is why left ventricular output slightly exceeds right.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hypoxic-pulmonary-vasoconstriction-hpv\">Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV)</h2>\n<ul><li>Pulmonary arterioles constrict in response to <strong>low alveolar PO<sub>2</sub></strong> (and to a lesser extent low mixed venous PO<sub>2</sub>), diverting blood to better-ventilated lung and reducing shunt.</li><li>Mechanism: inhibition of oxygen-sensitive potassium channels in smooth muscle → depolarisation → calcium entry.</li><li>Enhanced by acidosis; inhibited by alkalosis, vasodilators (nitroprusside, GTN), and (weakly, dose-dependently) by volatile anaesthetics. Largely preserved with propofol.</li><li>Relevant in one-lung ventilation; global HPV (altitude, chronic hypoxia) causes pulmonary hypertension.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"non-respiratory-functions\">Non-respiratory functions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Filter:</strong> traps small emboli, thrombi and air bubbles.</li><li><strong>Reservoir:</strong> holds about 500 mL of blood, which can move into the systemic circulation.</li><li><strong>Metabolic:</strong> the endothelium converts <strong>angiotensin I to angiotensin II</strong> (ACE), inactivates <strong>bradykinin</strong>, and removes much of circulating <strong>serotonin</strong> and <strong>noradrenaline</strong> and some prostaglandins. Adrenaline, histamine, isoprenaline and angiotensin II pass through largely unchanged.</li><li><strong>Drug handling:</strong> first-pass uptake of basic lipophilic drugs such as fentanyl and lidocaine.</li><li><strong>Defence:</strong> mucociliary clearance, alveolar macrophages, IgA.</li><li>Surfactant production; heat and water loss; acid–base regulation via CO<sub>2</sub>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> calculate PVR; West zones; the bronchial circulation and physiological shunt; which substances are metabolised in the lung; factors affecting HPV.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining the management of hypoxaemia during one-lung ventilation, including why HPV matters.</li></ul></div>","resp-vq":"<h2 id=\"distribution-of-ventilation-and-perfusion\">Distribution of ventilation and perfusion</h2>\n<ul><li>In the upright lung <strong>both</strong> ventilation and perfusion increase towards the base, but perfusion increases more steeply. So V/Q is <strong>high at the apex (~3)</strong> and <strong>low at the base (~0.6)</strong>; overall about <strong>0.8</strong> (alveolar ventilation ~4 L/min, cardiac output ~5 L/min).</li><li><strong>West zones:</strong> zone 1 (P<sub>A</sub> &gt; P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub>) has no flow: alveolar dead space, which appears only if alveolar pressure is raised (IPPV) or pulmonary pressure falls (hypovolaemia). Zone 2 (P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>A</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub>): flow depends on the arterial–alveolar difference. Zone 3 (P<sub>a</sub> &gt; P<sub>v</sub> &gt; P<sub>A</sub>): continuous flow.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"causes-of-hypoxaemia\">Causes of hypoxaemia</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>A–a gradient</th><th>Response to raising FiO<sub>2</sub></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low inspired PO<sub>2</sub> (altitude, hypoxic gas mixture)</td><td>Normal</td><td>Corrects</td></tr><tr><td>Hypoventilation</td><td>Normal</td><td>Corrects (but CO<sub>2</sub> still rises)</td></tr><tr><td>V/Q mismatch (low V/Q)</td><td>Raised</td><td>Improves</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Shunt</strong> (V/Q = 0)</td><td>Raised</td><td><strong>Little improvement</strong> once shunt exceeds ~30%</td></tr><tr><td>Diffusion impairment</td><td>Raised</td><td>Improves</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The <strong>alveolar gas equation</strong>: <strong>P<sub>A</sub>O<sub>2</sub> = F<sub>I</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (P<sub>B</sub> − P<sub>H2O</sub>) − P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> / RQ</strong>. Breathing air at sea level: 0.21 × (101 − 6.3) − 5.3/0.8 ≈ 13.3 kPa.</li><li>A normal A–a gradient is under about 2 kPa in young adults, rising with age.</li><li><strong>Shunt lowers PaO<sub>2</sub> but barely raises PaCO<sub>2</sub>:</strong> chemoreceptors increase ventilation, and because the CO<sub>2</sub> dissociation curve is nearly linear, over-ventilated units can shed the extra CO<sub>2</sub>. They cannot add much extra oxygen, because blood leaving them is already almost saturated.</li><li><strong>High V/Q units</strong> (hypotension, PE, high airway pressure) add to alveolar dead space and widen the arterial–end-tidal CO<sub>2</sub> gap.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"shunt\">Shunt</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Shunt equation:</strong> <strong>Qs/Qt = (CcO<sub>2</sub> − CaO<sub>2</sub>) / (CcO<sub>2</sub> − CvO<sub>2</sub>)</strong>, where Cc is end-capillary content (calculated from P<sub>A</sub>O<sub>2</sub>).</li><li>Normal physiological shunt is 2–5% (bronchial and Thebesian veins). Pathological shunt: atelectasis, pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, intracardiac right-to-left shunt, one-lung ventilation.</li><li>Oxygen fails to correct shunt because shunted blood never meets the enriched gas, and blood from well-ventilated units is already nearly saturated, so it cannot carry much extra.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"dead-space\">Dead space</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Physiological dead space = anatomical + alveolar.</strong> Anatomical about <strong>2 mL/kg</strong> (~150 mL).</li><li><strong>Bohr equation:</strong> <strong>V<sub>D</sub>/V<sub>T</sub> = (P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> − P<sub>E</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>) / P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub></strong> (using mixed expired CO<sub>2</sub>). Normal V<sub>D</sub>/V<sub>T</sub> is 0.2–0.35.</li><li>Anatomical dead space is measured by <strong>Fowler's method</strong> (single-breath nitrogen washout).</li><li>Alveolar dead space increases with pulmonary embolism, low cardiac output and hypovolaemia, and high airway pressures, and widens the P<sub>a</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>–E<sub>T</sub>CO<sub>2</sub> gap.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> alveolar gas equation calculations; the effect of FiO<sub>2</sub> on shunt versus V/Q mismatch; Bohr and Fowler.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a desaturating patient in recovery: work through the causes systematically (FiO<sub>2</sub>, ventilation, V/Q and shunt, diffusion, low mixed venous content) and act.</li></ul></div>","respiratory-drugs":"<h2 id=\"bronchodilators-and-anti-inflammatories\">Bronchodilators and anti-inflammatories</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Examples</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>β<sub>2</sub>-agonists</strong></td><td>Salbutamol (short), salmeterol and formoterol (long)</td><td>G<sub>s</sub> → ↑ cAMP → smooth muscle relaxation</td><td>Tremor, tachycardia, <strong>hypokalaemia</strong>, lactic acidosis</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Antimuscarinics</strong></td><td>Ipratropium (short), tiotropium (long)</td><td>M<sub>3</sub> blockade</td><td>Especially useful in COPD</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Methylxanthines</strong></td><td>Aminophylline, theophylline</td><td>PDE inhibition, adenosine antagonism</td><td><strong>Narrow therapeutic index</strong>; arrhythmias, seizures; CYP1A2 interactions (smoking lowers, erythromycin/ciprofloxacin raise levels)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Corticosteroids</strong></td><td>Inhaled (beclometasone, budesonide); systemic (prednisolone, hydrocortisone)</td><td>Anti-inflammatory; ↑ β-receptor expression</td><td>Take hours to act</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Leukotriene antagonists</strong></td><td>Montelukast</td><td>Block CysLT<sub>1</sub></td><td>Aspirin-sensitive asthma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Magnesium sulphate</strong></td><td></td><td>Ca<sup>2+</sup> antagonism in smooth muscle</td><td>Acute severe asthma (single IV dose)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Volatile anaesthetics</strong></td><td>Sevoflurane, isoflurane</td><td>Direct relaxation, reduced vagal tone</td><td>Refractory bronchospasm</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ketamine</strong></td><td></td><td>Sympathomimetic, direct relaxation</td><td>Induction in severe asthma</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"bronchospasm-under-anaesthesia\">Bronchospasm under anaesthesia</h2>\n<p>Stop the stimulus (light anaesthesia, airway instrumentation, a drug), 100% O<sub>2</sub>, deepen anaesthesia with volatile, <strong>salbutamol</strong> (inhaled into the circuit or IV), consider adrenaline, magnesium, aminophylline and ketamine; exclude mimics (tube problems, endobronchial intubation, anaphylaxis, pneumothorax, aspiration). Allow a long expiratory time to avoid gas trapping (dynamic hyperinflation → hypotension). The QRH has a bronchospasm card.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oxygen-as-a-drug\">Oxygen as a drug</h2>\n<ul><li>Prescribe to a <strong>target saturation</strong>: <strong>94–98%</strong> for most acutely ill patients; <strong>88–92%</strong> for those at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure (BTS guideline).</li><li><strong>Toxicity:</strong> absorption atelectasis, pulmonary toxicity with prolonged high FiO<sub>2</sub>, CNS toxicity under hyperbaric conditions, retinopathy of prematurity, hypercapnia in susceptible patients, and fire risk.</li><li><strong>Devices:</strong> variable performance (nasal cannulae, simple face masks, non-rebreathe masks) versus fixed performance (<strong>Venturi masks</strong>; high-flow nasal oxygen).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"other-drugs\">Other drugs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Respiratory stimulants:</strong> doxapram (peripheral chemoreceptors).</li><li><strong>Pulmonary vasodilators:</strong> inhaled nitric oxide, prostacyclin (epoprostenol, iloprost), sildenafil (PDE5), bosentan (endothelin antagonist).</li><li><strong>Mucolytics:</strong> acetylcysteine, dornase alfa (cystic fibrosis). <strong>Surfactant</strong> for neonatal RDS.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> mechanisms; theophylline interactions; oxygen targets and devices.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> managing severe bronchospasm after intubation in an asthmatic, and explaining your stepwise plan.</li></ul></div>","resuscitation-als":"<h2 id=\"the-als-algorithm-follow-the-current-rcuk-2025-guideline\">The ALS algorithm (follow the current RCUK 2025 guideline)</h2>\n<ul><li>Recognise arrest; call the team; start <strong>high-quality chest compressions</strong> (5–6 cm deep, 100–120/min, full recoil, <strong>minimal interruptions</strong>), ventilate with oxygen (30:2 until the airway is secured, then continuous compressions with ventilation at about 10/min — <strong>avoid hyperventilation</strong>, which reduces venous return and coronary perfusion).</li><li><strong>Shockable (VF/pulseless VT):</strong> shock, resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes, reassess. <strong>Adrenaline 1 mg IV/IO</strong> after the third shock, then every 3–5 minutes; <strong>amiodarone 300 mg</strong> after the third shock (150 mg after the fifth). Consider lidocaine as an alternative.</li><li><strong>Non-shockable (PEA/asystole):</strong> adrenaline 1 mg as soon as access is available, then every 3–5 minutes; look for reversible causes.</li><li><strong>IV or intraosseous</strong> access (IO when IV is not quickly available).</li><li><strong>Waveform capnography:</strong> confirms tube placement, shows CPR quality, and a sudden rise suggests ROSC.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"reversible-causes-4-hs-and-4-ts\">Reversible causes: 4 Hs and 4 Ts</h2>\n<p><strong>Hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hypo-/hyperkalaemia and metabolic, hypothermia</strong>; <strong>thrombosis (coronary or pulmonary), tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins</strong>. Point-of-care ultrasound may help identify some causes (without interrupting compressions).</p>\n<h2 id=\"special-circumstances\">Special circumstances</h2>\n<p>Anaphylaxis, asthma, pregnancy, trauma (treat reversible causes: haemorrhage, hypoxia, tension, tamponade), drowning, hypothermia (no drugs below 30°C; prolonged resuscitation), local anaesthetic toxicity (lipid), hyperkalaemia (calcium, insulin–glucose), poisoning, and <strong>perioperative cardiac arrest</strong> (NAP7: incidence about 3 per 10,000 anaesthetics, commonly from major haemorrhage, bradyarrhythmia, cardiac ischaemia and anaphylaxis; an anaesthetist is usually present, so causes can often be identified and treated early).</p>\n<h2 id=\"post-resuscitation-care\">Post-resuscitation care</h2>\n<p>ABCDE; <strong>targeted oxygenation</strong> (SpO<sub>2</sub> 94–98%) and <strong>normocapnia</strong>; blood pressure (MAP &gt; 65 mmHg as a minimum); 12-lead ECG and coronary angiography when a cardiac cause is likely; <strong>temperature control</strong> (avoid fever); glucose control; seizure management; prognostication after at least 72 hours with a multimodal approach; investigations for the cause.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ethical-and-legal-aspects\">Ethical and legal aspects</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>DNACPR</strong> decisions and <strong>treatment escalation plans</strong> (e.g. ReSPECT) should be discussed with the patient (or those close to them) and documented; they do not mean \"do not treat\".</li><li>Allow relatives to be present during resuscitation if they wish, with a staff member supporting them.</li><li>Stopping resuscitation is a senior team decision.</li><li><strong>Debrief</strong> the team afterwards (hot and cold debriefs) and support relatives compassionately.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"recognising-deterioration\">Recognising deterioration</h2>\n<p><strong>NEWS2</strong> (respiratory rate, SpO<sub>2</sub> and oxygen, systolic BP, heart rate, consciousness including new confusion, temperature) triggers escalation. Critical care scores (APACHE, SOFA) quantify severity of illness.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> drug doses and timings; 4 Hs and 4 Ts; why hyperventilation is harmful; post-ROSC targets.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> lead a cardiac arrest simulation, then break bad news or debrief the team.</li></ul></div>","rsi-aspiration":"<h2 id=\"indications\">Indications</h2>\n<p>Patients at increased risk of regurgitation and aspiration: <strong>inadequate fasting</strong>, emergency surgery, bowel obstruction or ileus, <strong>pregnancy</strong> (from about the second trimester), symptomatic reflux or hiatus hernia, trauma and opioid administration (delayed gastric emptying), diabetic gastroparesis, reduced consciousness, GLP-1 agonist therapy with residual contents, obesity (in some circumstances).</p>\n<h2 id=\"preparation\">Preparation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Team brief</strong> with the airway plan (A–D) and roles; a skilled assistant; working suction under the pillow; drugs drawn up and labelled; a range of tubes, a bougie and a video laryngoscope; second-generation SGA; front-of-neck access kit.</li><li><strong>Position:</strong> head-up or ramped (improves FRC and view; reduces passive regurgitation).</li><li><strong>Nasogastric tube:</strong> aspirate before induction if one is present (can leave in place).</li><li><strong>Pre-oxygenation</strong> to an end-tidal O<sub>2</sub> ≥ 0.9 (typically 3 minutes of tidal breathing or 8 vital capacity breaths); consider HFNO or nasal cannulae for apnoeic oxygenation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"technique\">Technique</h2>\n<ul><li>Rapid injection of a <strong>predetermined dose</strong> of induction agent followed immediately by a fast-onset neuromuscular blocker: <strong>suxamethonium 1–1.5 mg/kg</strong> or <strong>rocuronium 1–1.2 mg/kg</strong> (with sugammadex available).</li><li><strong>Cricoid pressure</strong> (10 N awake, 30 N after loss of consciousness) is traditional; it may worsen the laryngoscopic view or mask ventilation, so reduce or release it if it does; do not release if active vomiting occurs without first turning the patient/tilting head-down and suctioning.</li><li><strong>Gentle facemask ventilation</strong> (e.g. inflation pressures &lt; 20 cmH<sub>2</sub>O) before intubation is increasingly accepted when hypoxaemia is a risk.</li><li>Intubate, inflate the cuff, confirm with <strong>waveform capnography</strong>, then release cricoid pressure.</li><li><strong>Titrate drugs in the shocked patient</strong> (reduced doses of induction agent; vasopressor ready): \"modified RSI\" is common in practice.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"aspiration\">Aspiration</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Risk:</strong> about 1 in 3,000–4,000 anaesthetics overall (higher in emergencies), commonest at induction and emergence.</li><li><strong>Recognition:</strong> gastric contents in the pharynx, laryngospasm, bronchospasm, hypoxaemia, rising airway pressures, later infiltrates on the chest X-ray.</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> head-down and lateral tilt if not intubated, <strong>suction</strong>, 100% O<sub>2</sub>, <strong>intubate and suction the trachea before ventilating</strong> if appropriate, bronchoscopy for particulate matter, PEEP, supportive care. Antibiotics only if pneumonia develops; steroids are not indicated. Admit to an appropriate care area if significant.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> indications; cricoid pressure force; RSI drug doses; features of aspiration.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> lead the team brief and conduct of an RSI for a patient with small bowel obstruction, and manage vomiting at induction.</li></ul></div>","safety-human-factors":"<h2 id=\"thinking-about-error\">Thinking about error</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Reason's Swiss cheese model:</strong> harm occurs when holes in multiple layers of defence align; most incidents have <strong>latent conditions</strong> (system problems) as well as active failures.</li><li><strong>Error types:</strong> slips and lapses (skill-based), mistakes (rule- or knowledge-based), violations. Fixation error, confirmation bias and task saturation are common in crises.</li><li><strong>Safety-I</strong> (learning from what goes wrong) and <strong>Safety-II</strong> (understanding how things usually go right, with adaptation).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"non-technical-skills-ants-framework\">Non-technical skills (ANTS framework)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Elements</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Task management</strong></td><td>Planning and preparing, prioritising, maintaining standards, using resources</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Team working</strong></td><td>Coordinating, exchanging information, using authority and assertiveness, assessing capabilities, supporting others</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Situation awareness</strong></td><td>Gathering information, recognising and understanding, anticipating</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Decision making</strong></td><td>Identifying options, balancing risks and selecting, re-evaluating</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Useful tools: <strong>closed-loop communication</strong>, <strong>SBAR</strong> handovers, \"10 seconds for 10 minutes\", speaking up (graded assertiveness, e.g. \"PACE\" or \"CUS\"), allocating a leader and roles, <strong>cognitive aids</strong> (the QRH) with a reader.</p>\n<h2 id=\"safety-systems-in-theatre\">Safety systems in theatre</h2>\n<ul><li>The <strong>WHO Surgical Safety Checklist</strong> (sign in, time out, sign out) with <strong>team briefings and debriefings</strong>; <strong>Stop Before You Block</strong>; the <strong>NatSSIPs/LocSSIPs</strong> standards for invasive procedures.</li><li><strong>Drug safety:</strong> see drug errors (labelling, NRFit connectors, checking, standardisation). Prescribe clearly and safely, with allergies checked.</li><li><strong>Fatigue</strong>, hunger, interruptions and production pressure reduce performance.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"reporting-and-learning\">Reporting and learning</h2>\n<p>Report incidents (local systems; national learning systems) without blame; investigate systemically (e.g. under the Patient Safety Incident Response Framework in England); share learning through morbidity and mortality meetings; the <strong>National Audit Projects (NAPs)</strong> have shaped UK practice (NAP4 airway, NAP5 awareness, NAP6 anaphylaxis, NAP7 cardiac arrest).</p>\n<h2 id=\"sustainability\">Sustainability</h2>\n<p>Healthcare is a major source of carbon emissions, and anaesthetic gases contribute significantly. Choose lower-impact techniques (<strong>avoid desflurane and nitrous oxide</strong>, use low-flow anaesthesia, TIVA and regional anaesthesia where appropriate), reduce waste (reusables where safe, avoid opening unnecessary equipment), and consider medicines waste.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Swiss cheese model; ANTS categories; checklist components; environmental impact of agents.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a professional conversation with a senior colleague about a drug error you nearly made, or leading a team brief before a complex case.</li></ul></div>","sepsis-critical-illness":"<h2 id=\"definitions-sepsis-3\">Definitions (Sepsis-3)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Sepsis:</strong> life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection (an acute rise in <strong>SOFA score ≥ 2</strong>).</li><li><strong>Septic shock:</strong> sepsis needing <strong>vasopressors to keep MAP ≥ 65 mmHg</strong> with <strong>lactate &gt; 2 mmol/L</strong> despite adequate fluid resuscitation (mortality over 40%).</li><li>Screening tools: NEWS2 and the NICE NG51 risk stratification; qSOFA (RR ≥ 22, altered mentation, systolic BP ≤ 100) is less sensitive.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"early-management\">Early management</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Recognise, escalate and treat within an hour</strong> for high-risk patients: cultures (without delaying antibiotics), <strong>broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong> per local policy (then de-escalate), <strong>fluid resuscitation</strong> with balanced crystalloids guided by reassessment (the Surviving Sepsis Campaign suggests up to 30 mL/kg in the first hours for hypoperfusion, individualised), <strong>lactate</strong>, <strong>source control</strong> (surgery, drainage), oxygen, urine output monitoring.</li><li><strong>Vasopressors:</strong> <strong>noradrenaline</strong> first-line (peripheral administration is possible initially), adding vasopressin; corticosteroids (hydrocortisone) for ongoing vasopressor requirement.</li><li><strong>Anaesthesia for the septic patient</strong> (e.g. for source control): expect profound hypotension at induction (reduced doses, vasopressor ready, arterial line), aspiration risk, coagulopathy.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"critical-care\">Critical care</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Admission</strong> decisions balance potential benefit, the patient's wishes and prognosis; involve senior clinicians. Levels of care: 1 (ward with outreach), 2 (single organ support, e.g. vasopressors or NIV), 3 (advanced respiratory support or two or more organ supports).</li><li><strong>Invasive procedures:</strong> arterial and central lines, chest drains (see anatomy), renal replacement access — performed with full asepsis and ultrasound guidance.</li><li><strong>Communication:</strong> clear, compassionate, regular updates to patients and families; honest discussion of uncertainty and ceilings of care; handover and discharge summaries to ward teams.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"end-of-life-and-organ-donation\">End of life and organ donation</h2>\n<ul><li>Withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment focuses on comfort, dignity and family support, with a clear plan documented.</li><li><strong>Brainstem death testing</strong> (UK Academy of Medical Royal Colleges code): preconditions (a known cause of irreversible brain damage; exclusion of reversible causes such as drugs, hypothermia, and metabolic and endocrine disturbances), then two sets of tests by two doctors (one a consultant; both registered for more than 5 years): absent pupillary, corneal, vestibulo-ocular, gag and cough reflexes, no motor response in the cranial nerve distribution, and <strong>apnoea test</strong> (no respiratory effort with PaCO<sub>2</sub> ≥ 6.5 kPa and pH &lt; 7.40). Death is confirmed at the completion of the first set.</li><li>Early involvement of the <strong>specialist nurse for organ donation</strong> in every potential donor.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> Sepsis-3 definitions; first-hour actions; brainstem death preconditions and tests.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> an ICU referral conversation about a septic patient on the ward, or explaining to a relative why their father is being admitted to ICU.</li></ul></div>","si-units-mechanics":"<h2 id=\"si-base-units\">SI base units</h2>\n<p>Metre (length), kilogram (mass), second (time), ampere (current), kelvin (temperature), mole (amount of substance), candela (luminous intensity).</p>\n<h2 id=\"derived-units\">Derived units</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Unit</th><th>In base units</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Force</td><td>newton (N)</td><td>kg·m·s<sup>−2</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Pressure</td><td>pascal (Pa)</td><td>N/m<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Energy, work, heat</td><td>joule (J)</td><td>N·m</td></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td>watt (W)</td><td>J/s</td></tr><tr><td>Charge</td><td>coulomb (C)</td><td>A·s</td></tr><tr><td>Potential difference</td><td>volt (V)</td><td>W/A (J/C)</td></tr><tr><td>Resistance</td><td>ohm (Ω)</td><td>V/A</td></tr><tr><td>Capacitance</td><td>farad (F)</td><td>C/V</td></tr><tr><td>Frequency</td><td>hertz (Hz)</td><td>s<sup>−1</sup></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"pressure\">Pressure</h2>\n<p><strong>Pressure = force / area.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Equivalent to 1 atmosphere</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>kPa</td><td><strong>101.3</strong></td></tr><tr><td>mmHg</td><td><strong>760</strong></td></tr><tr><td>cmH<sub>2</sub>O</td><td><strong>~1033</strong></td></tr><tr><td>bar</td><td>1.013</td></tr><tr><td>psi</td><td>14.7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Useful conversions: <strong>1 kPa ≈ 7.5 mmHg ≈ 10.2 cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong>; <strong>1 mmHg ≈ 1.36 cmH<sub>2</sub>O</strong>.</li><li><strong>Bourdon gauge:</strong> a coiled, flattened tube that straightens as pressure rises, moving a pointer; used for high pressures such as cylinder contents (and, filled with liquid, in dial thermometers).</li><li><strong>Gauge pressure</strong> is measured relative to atmospheric pressure (what cylinder gauges and most clinical monitors read). <strong>Absolute pressure = gauge + atmospheric.</strong> A \"full\" oxygen cylinder at 137 bar gauge is 138 bar absolute.</li><li>Pressure in a column of liquid: <strong>P = ρ g h</strong> (hence a transducer must be zeroed and levelled; every 10 cm of height difference in blood produces about 7.5 mmHg).</li><li><strong>Syringe pressure:</strong> the same force on a smaller syringe gives a higher pressure (P = F/A) — why a 2 mL syringe can generate very high pressures (relevant to injection pressure during nerve blocks and to ruptured cuffs).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"mechanics\">Mechanics</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Work = force × distance</strong> (for gases, work = pressure × volume change; the basis of the work of breathing).</li><li><strong>Power = work / time</strong> (e.g. the power of breathing).</li><li><strong>Energy:</strong> kinetic (½mv<sup>2</sup>), potential (mgh).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> unit conversions; gauge versus absolute pressure; derived units; P = ρgh.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining why moving the arterial transducer below the patient changes the reading, and how much.</li></ul></div>","stats-data-descriptive":"<h2 id=\"types-of-data\">Types of data</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Subtype</th><th>Examples</th><th>Summary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Categorical (qualitative)</strong></td><td><strong>Nominal</strong> (no order)</td><td>Blood group, sex, type of surgery</td><td>Counts, proportions, mode</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>Ordinal</strong> (ordered, unequal intervals)</td><td>ASA grade, pain score, Mallampati</td><td>Median, IQR</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Numerical (quantitative)</strong></td><td><strong>Discrete</strong> (counts)</td><td>Number of attempts</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>Continuous</strong> — interval (arbitrary zero, e.g. °C) or ratio (true zero, e.g. weight, Kelvin)</td><td>Blood pressure, weight</td><td>Mean and SD if normally distributed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-normal-distribution\">The normal distribution</h2>\n<ul><li>Symmetrical and bell-shaped; <strong>mean = median = mode</strong>; defined by its mean and standard deviation.</li><li><strong>68%</strong> of values lie within ±1 SD, <strong>95%</strong> within <strong>±1.96 SD</strong>, 99.7% within ±3 SD.</li><li><strong>Skewed distributions:</strong> positive (right) skew has a long tail to the right and <strong>mean &gt; median &gt; mode</strong> (e.g. length of stay); negative skew the reverse. Use the <strong>median and interquartile range</strong>, or transform the data (e.g. logarithms).</li><li>Other distributions: binomial (two outcomes), Poisson (counts of rare events), t distribution (small samples), χ<sup>2</sup>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"descriptive-statistics\">Descriptive statistics</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Central tendency:</strong> mean (affected by outliers), median (middle value), mode (most frequent).</li><li><strong>Spread:</strong> range, <strong>interquartile range</strong> (25th–75th centiles), <strong>variance</strong> (mean squared deviation), <strong>standard deviation</strong> (√variance; describes the spread of the data).</li><li><strong>Standard error of the mean (SEM) = SD / √n</strong>: describes the precision of the sample mean as an estimate of the population mean. It falls as sample size rises.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"probability-and-confidence-intervals\">Probability and confidence intervals</h2>\n<ul><li>Probability ranges from 0 to 1. For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B); for mutually exclusive events, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).</li><li><strong>95% confidence interval</strong> for a mean ≈ <strong>mean ± 1.96 × SEM</strong>: if the study were repeated many times, 95% of such intervals would contain the true population value. A confidence interval shows both the size of an effect and its precision; if the 95% CI for a difference excludes zero (or for a ratio excludes 1), the result is significant at p &lt; 0.05.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> classify data types; properties of the normal distribution; SD versus SEM; interpreting confidence intervals.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> explaining to a patient what \"a 1 in 100 risk\" means, or interpreting the results table of a trial.</li></ul></div>","stats-hypothesis-tests":"<h2 id=\"hypothesis-testing\">Hypothesis testing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Null hypothesis (H<sub>0</sub>):</strong> no difference or no association. The <strong>p value</strong> is the probability of obtaining the observed result (or one more extreme) <strong>if the null hypothesis were true</strong>. It is <strong>not</strong> the probability that the null hypothesis is true.</li><li>Conventionally p &lt; 0.05 is \"statistically significant\" (α = 0.05). Statistical significance is not the same as <strong>clinical importance</strong>.</li><li><strong>Multiple comparisons</strong> inflate the chance of a false positive (e.g. 20 tests at α = 0.05 → about 64% chance of at least one false positive): correct with methods such as Bonferroni (α / number of tests).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"errors-and-power\">Errors and power</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>H<sub>0</sub> true</th><th>H<sub>0</sub> false</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Reject H<sub>0</sub></strong></td><td><strong>Type I error (α)</strong>: false positive</td><td>Correct (power)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Accept H<sub>0</sub></strong></td><td>Correct</td><td><strong>Type II error (β)</strong>: false negative</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Power = 1 − β</strong>: the probability of detecting a true effect of a given size; usually set at <strong>80–90%</strong>.</li><li><strong>Sample size</strong> depends on α, power, the <strong>minimum clinically important difference</strong> (smaller → more patients) and the <strong>variability</strong> of the outcome (larger SD → more patients). Underpowered studies risk type II errors.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-test\">Choosing a test</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Two independent groups</th><th>Paired / repeated</th><th>More than two groups</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Continuous, normally distributed (parametric)</td><td><strong>Unpaired Student's t-test</strong></td><td><strong>Paired t-test</strong></td><td><strong>ANOVA</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Ordinal or non-normal (non-parametric)</td><td><strong>Mann–Whitney U</strong></td><td><strong>Wilcoxon signed-rank</strong></td><td><strong>Kruskal–Wallis</strong> (independent), Friedman (repeated)</td></tr><tr><td>Categorical (proportions)</td><td><strong>Chi-squared (χ<sup>2</sup>)</strong>, or <strong>Fisher's exact</strong> when expected counts are small (&lt; 5)</td><td><strong>McNemar's</strong></td><td>χ<sup>2</sup></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Correlation</strong> (Pearson r for normally distributed data; Spearman ρ for ranks) measures the strength of a linear association (−1 to +1); it does not imply causation. <strong>Regression</strong> predicts one variable from another (linear, logistic for binary outcomes, Cox for time-to-event).</li><li><strong>Bland–Altman plots</strong> compare two methods of measurement (agreement, bias and limits of agreement); correlation is inappropriate for this.</li><li><strong>Survival analysis:</strong> Kaplan–Meier curves, log-rank test, hazard ratios.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"diagnostic-tests\">Diagnostic tests</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Disease present</th><th>Disease absent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Test positive</td><td>True positive (a)</td><td>False positive (b)</td></tr><tr><td>Test negative</td><td>False negative (c)</td><td>True negative (d)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Sensitivity = a/(a + c)</strong> (proportion of diseased correctly identified; SnNout: a highly <strong>s</strong>ensitive test, when <strong>n</strong>egative, rules <strong>out</strong>). <strong>Specificity = d/(b + d)</strong> (SpPin).</li><li><strong>Positive predictive value = a/(a + b)</strong>; <strong>negative predictive value = d/(c + d)</strong>. Predictive values <strong>depend on prevalence</strong>; sensitivity and specificity do not.</li><li><strong>ROC curve:</strong> sensitivity against (1 − specificity) across cut-off values; area under the curve summarises discrimination (0.5 = chance, 1 = perfect).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"measures-of-effect\">Measures of effect</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Absolute risk reduction (ARR)</strong> = control event rate − experimental event rate. <strong>Number needed to treat (NNT) = 1/ARR.</strong></li><li><strong>Relative risk (RR)</strong> = risk in the treated / risk in controls; <strong>relative risk reduction</strong> = 1 − RR. <strong>Odds ratio</strong> approximates RR when events are rare.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> definitions of p, α, β and power; choose the right test for a scenario; calculate sensitivity, specificity, PPV and NNT from a 2 × 2 table.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> critically appraising a trial abstract presented by a colleague.</li></ul></div>","stats-study-design":"<h2 id=\"study-designs\">Study designs</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Design</th><th>Description</th><th>Strengths / weaknesses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Case report / series</td><td>Descriptions of patients</td><td>Hypothesis-generating; no comparison</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cross-sectional</strong></td><td>Snapshot at one time</td><td>Prevalence; cannot show causation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Case–control</strong></td><td>Start with outcome (cases vs controls), look back at exposures</td><td>Good for rare outcomes; <strong>recall bias</strong>; gives <strong>odds ratios</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cohort</strong> (prospective or retrospective)</td><td>Follow exposed and unexposed groups over time</td><td>Incidence and <strong>relative risk</strong>; confounding; long and costly for rare outcomes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Randomised controlled trial</strong></td><td>Random allocation to intervention or control</td><td>Controls known <strong>and unknown</strong> confounders; the best design to test interventions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Systematic review and meta-analysis</strong></td><td>Systematic search and pooling of studies</td><td>Highest level of evidence if studies are good; <strong>heterogeneity</strong>, <strong>publication bias</strong> (funnel plot)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"key-concepts-in-trials\">Key concepts in trials</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Randomisation</strong> (with <strong>allocation concealment</strong>) prevents selection bias. <strong>Blinding</strong> (single, double) prevents performance and detection bias.</li><li><strong>Intention-to-treat analysis</strong> keeps patients in their allocated groups (preserves randomisation); per-protocol analysis may exaggerate effects.</li><li><strong>Bias</strong> is systematic error (selection, performance, detection, attrition, reporting). <strong>Confounding</strong> is a third factor associated with both exposure and outcome. <strong>Chance</strong> is addressed by statistics.</li><li><strong>Outcome measures</strong> should be clinically meaningful and precisely defined; composite outcomes, surrogate outcomes (e.g. blood pressure instead of stroke) and subjective measures introduce uncertainty. Core outcome sets (e.g. for perioperative medicine) improve comparability.</li><li><strong>Equivalence and non-inferiority trials</strong> test whether a new treatment is not worse by more than a set margin.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-based-medicine-and-meta-analysis\">Evidence-based medicine and meta-analysis</h2>\n<ul><li>Combining the best research evidence with clinical expertise and patient values.</li><li><strong>Hierarchy (roughly):</strong> systematic reviews of RCTs → RCTs → cohort → case–control → case series → expert opinion. GRADE rates the certainty of evidence.</li><li><strong>Forest plot:</strong> each study's effect estimate and CI (square size reflects weight), the pooled estimate (diamond) and the line of no effect. I<sup>2</sup> describes heterogeneity.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"audit-research-and-quality-improvement\">Audit, research and quality improvement</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Research</th><th>Audit</th><th>Quality improvement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Question</td><td>What is best practice? (generates new knowledge)</td><td>Are we meeting a standard?</td><td>How can we improve this system?</td></tr><tr><td>Method</td><td>Protocol, often randomisation; ethics approval</td><td>Measure against a standard; re-audit (the audit cycle)</td><td>Iterative small tests of change (<strong>PDSA cycles</strong>), measured over time (<strong>run charts</strong>, statistical process control)</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>Data for research</td><td><strong>Data for assurance</strong> (judgement against a standard)</td><td><strong>Data for improvement</strong> (frequent, \"good enough\", to learn)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>The <strong>Model for Improvement</strong> asks: what are we trying to accomplish; how will we know a change is an improvement; what changes can we make? Then PDSA (plan–do–study–act).</li><li>Deming's <strong>System of Profound Knowledge</strong>: appreciation of a system, knowledge about variation (common-cause versus special-cause), theory of knowledge, and psychology.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> match designs to questions; bias versus confounding; intention to treat; forest plots; audit versus QI.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> describing a QI project you would run to reduce hypothermia in recovery, including your measures.</li></ul></div>","steroids-thyroid-drugs":"<h2 id=\"corticosteroids\">Corticosteroids</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Glucocorticoid potency</th><th>Mineralocorticoid potency</th><th>Equivalent dose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hydrocortisone</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>20 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Prednisolone</td><td>4</td><td>0.8</td><td>5 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Methylprednisolone</td><td>5</td><td>~0.5</td><td>4 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Dexamethasone</td><td>25–30</td><td>~0</td><td>0.75 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Fludrocortisone</td><td>~10</td><td>125–250</td><td>(mineralocorticoid replacement)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Uses in anaesthesia:</strong> PONV prophylaxis and analgesic adjunct (dexamethasone), airway oedema, cerebral oedema around tumours (dexamethasone), anaphylaxis (adjunct), asthma and COPD exacerbations, adrenal insufficiency and septic shock (hydrocortisone), prolonging nerve blocks.</li><li><strong>Long-term side effects:</strong> HPA axis suppression, hyperglycaemia, hypertension, fluid retention, hypokalaemia, osteoporosis, myopathy, thin skin and poor wound healing, infection, peptic ulcer, cataracts, psychiatric effects, Cushingoid features.</li><li><strong>Perioperative steroid cover:</strong> patients taking <strong>≥ 5 mg prednisolone (or equivalent) daily for more than 4 weeks</strong> are at risk of adrenal insufficiency. Continue their usual dose and give additional <strong>hydrocortisone</strong> according to the size of surgery (per the 2020 joint guideline from the Association of Anaesthetists and endocrine societies); give IV equivalents if nil by mouth.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"thyroid-drugs\">Thyroid drugs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> <strong>levothyroxine (T<sub>4</sub>)</strong>; long half-life (~7 days), so a missed dose or two is unimportant. <strong>Liothyronine (T<sub>3</sub>)</strong> acts faster (myxoedema coma, with caution).</li><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism:</strong><ul><li><strong>Carbimazole</strong> and <strong>propylthiouracil</strong> inhibit thyroid peroxidase (PTU also blocks peripheral T<sub>4</sub> → T<sub>3</sub> conversion); agranulocytosis (sore throat → check FBC).</li><li><strong>β-blockers</strong> (propranolol) for symptoms; <strong>iodide</strong> (Lugol's) briefly before surgery reduces gland vascularity and hormone release.</li><li><strong>Thyroid storm:</strong> β-blockade, PTU or carbimazole, then iodide, hydrocortisone, cooling and supportive care.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Amiodarone</strong> can cause either hypo- or hyperthyroidism.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> potency ratios; who needs steroid cover; mechanisms of antithyroid drugs.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> a patient with Crohn's disease on prednisolone 10 mg daily for a bowel resection: steroid plan and explanation to the surgical team.</li></ul></div>","sympathomimetics":"<h2 id=\"receptor-profiles\">Receptor profiles</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Drug</th><th>α<sub>1</sub></th><th>β<sub>1</sub></th><th>β<sub>2</sub></th><th>Other</th><th>Main effect / use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Adrenaline</strong></td><td>++ (high dose)</td><td>++</td><td>++</td><td></td><td>Anaphylaxis, cardiac arrest, low output; low doses β-predominant; raises lactate and glucose</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Noradrenaline</strong></td><td>+++</td><td>+</td><td>–</td><td></td><td><strong>Vasodilatory shock</strong> (first-line in sepsis)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dopamine</strong></td><td>+ (high dose)</td><td>++</td><td></td><td>D<sub>1</sub></td><td>Rarely used now; arrhythmias</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dobutamine</strong></td><td>(+)</td><td>+++</td><td>+</td><td></td><td>Inotrope; vasodilation may lower BP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Isoprenaline</strong></td><td>–</td><td>+++</td><td>+++</td><td></td><td>Bradycardia / heart block (chronotrope)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phenylephrine</strong></td><td>+++ (direct)</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td></td><td>Pure vasoconstrictor; reflex bradycardia; obstetric spinal hypotension</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metaraminol</strong></td><td>++ (direct and indirect)</td><td>+</td><td></td><td></td><td>Bolus/infusion vasopressor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ephedrine</strong></td><td>+</td><td>+</td><td>+</td><td>Indirect NA release</td><td>Anaesthetic hypotension; <strong>tachyphylaxis</strong>; interactions with MAOIs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"catecholamines\">Catecholamines</h2>\n<ul><li>Contain a catechol ring: <strong>not active orally</strong> (COMT and MAO in gut and liver), short half-lives, given by infusion (central line preferred for high concentrations; peripheral infusion of dilute noradrenaline is increasingly used with safeguards).</li><li>Synthesis: tyrosine → DOPA → dopamine → noradrenaline → adrenaline (PNMT, adrenal medulla, cortisol-dependent).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"direct-versus-indirect\">Direct versus indirect</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Direct</strong> agents act on receptors; <strong>indirect</strong> agents release noradrenaline from nerve terminals (ephedrine partly, amphetamines, cocaine by blocking reuptake). Indirect agents show <strong>tachyphylaxis</strong> and exaggerated responses with <strong>MAOIs</strong> (hypertensive crisis).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"non-adrenergic-agents\">Non-adrenergic agents</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Phosphodiesterase III inhibitors</strong> (<strong>milrinone</strong>, enoximone): ↑ cAMP independent of β receptors → <strong>inodilators</strong> (inotropy with vasodilation), useful in β-receptor downregulation; hypotension, arrhythmias; renal excretion.</li><li><strong>Levosimendan:</strong> calcium sensitiser (troponin C) and K<sub>ATP</sub> opener.</li><li><strong>Vasopressin:</strong> V<sub>1</sub> vasoconstriction; second-line in septic shock (relative vasopressin deficiency); <strong>terlipressin</strong> for varices and hepatorenal syndrome.</li><li><strong>Methylene blue</strong> (inhibits NO–cGMP) and <strong>angiotensin II</strong> in refractory vasoplegia.</li><li><strong>Glucagon</strong> for β-blocker overdose; <strong>calcium</strong> for calcium channel blocker overdose and hyperkalaemia.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-an-agent\">Choosing an agent</h2>\n<p>Think about the cause: <strong>vasodilation</strong> (sepsis, anaesthesia, neuraxial block) → vasoconstrictor; <strong>pump failure</strong> → inotrope (and treat ischaemia, rhythm, loading); <strong>hypovolaemia</strong> → volume first. Match heart rate: bradycardic and hypotensive → ephedrine; tachycardic → phenylephrine or metaraminol.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> receptor profiles; direct versus indirect action; mechanism of PDE inhibitors; catecholamine synthesis.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing a vasopressor for hypotension after a spinal for caesarean section versus for a septic patient in ICU, and explaining the choice.</li></ul></div>","temperature-measurement":"<h2 id=\"scales\">Scales</h2>\n<p><strong>Kelvin</strong> (SI; absolute zero = 0 K = −273.15°C; the triple point of water = 273.16 K); <strong>Celsius</strong> (0°C = 273.15 K).</p>\n<h2 id=\"thermometers\">Thermometers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Principle</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Liquid-in-glass (mercury, alcohol)</td><td>Thermal expansion</td><td>Mercury: wide range, slow; alcohol for low temperatures</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bimetallic strip / Bourdon gauge</strong></td><td>Different expansion of two metals; or pressure of a fluid in a coiled tube</td><td>Dial thermometers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Resistance thermometer (platinum wire)</strong></td><td>Resistance <strong>rises linearly</strong> with temperature</td><td>Accurate, stable; used in Wheatstone bridges</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thermistor</strong></td><td>Semiconductor bead whose resistance usually <strong>falls non-linearly</strong> with temperature (negative temperature coefficient)</td><td>Small, fast, sensitive; used in probes and pulmonary artery catheters</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thermocouple</strong></td><td><strong>Seebeck effect</strong>: a voltage is generated at the junction of two dissimilar metals (e.g. copper and constantan), proportional to the temperature difference between measuring and reference junctions</td><td>Small, fast; needs a reference junction</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Infrared (tympanic, forehead)</strong></td><td>Emitted infrared power rises with the <strong>fourth power of absolute temperature</strong> (Stefan–Boltzmann: E = σT<sup>4</sup>)</td><td>Very fast; tympanic reflects hypothalamic blood supply but depends on technique; forehead readings are less reliable</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid crystal (skin strips)</td><td>Colour change</td><td>Skin, not core, temperature</td></tr><tr><td>Zero-heat-flux sensors</td><td>A heated forehead sensor creates an isothermal tunnel to deeper tissue</td><td>Continuous non-invasive core estimate</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"sites-for-core-temperature\">Sites for core temperature</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Pulmonary artery</strong> (the reference standard), <strong>distal oesophagus</strong> (lower third, close to the heart), <strong>nasopharynx</strong>, <strong>tympanic membrane</strong>, <strong>bladder</strong> (lags with changes; depends on urine flow), <strong>rectum</strong> (slow, affected by faeces and cold blood from the legs).</li><li>Axillary and skin temperatures underestimate core temperature.</li><li>NICE CG65 requires core temperature measurement (or a validated estimate) before induction and every 30 minutes during anaesthesia.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> thermistor versus thermocouple versus platinum resistance; Seebeck effect; sites and their lag.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing a temperature monitoring site for a long laparotomy and explaining why.</li></ul></div>","thermoregulation":"<h2 id=\"normal-regulation\">Normal regulation</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Core temperature</strong> is about 37°C (range about 36.5–37.5°C), with a circadian variation. The <strong>hypothalamus</strong> integrates input from peripheral and central thermoreceptors (cold via Aδ, warmth via C fibres).</li><li>The <strong>anterior hypothalamus</strong> (preoptic area) drives heat loss (sweating, vasodilation); the <strong>posterior hypothalamus</strong> drives heat conservation and production (vasoconstriction, shivering).</li><li><strong>Countercurrent exchange</strong> between parallel arteries and veins in the limbs returns heat to the core; skin vasoconstriction greatly reduces heat loss.</li><li>The <strong>interthreshold range</strong> (between the thresholds for sweating and vasoconstriction) is normally only about <strong>0.2–0.4°C</strong>.</li><li><strong>Heat loss responses:</strong> cutaneous vasodilation, sweating. <strong>Heat conservation and gain:</strong> vasoconstriction, behavioural changes, <strong>shivering</strong> (can double or triple heat production and oxygen consumption), and <strong>non-shivering thermogenesis</strong> in <strong>brown fat</strong> (important in neonates; uncoupling protein, stimulated by noradrenaline).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-anaesthesia-causes-hypothermia\">How anaesthesia causes hypothermia</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Redistribution (first hour):</strong> anaesthetic-induced vasodilation lets heat flow from the core to the cooler periphery. Core temperature typically falls by <strong>about 1–1.5°C in the first hour</strong>. Pre-warming reduces this.</li><li><strong>Linear phase (2–3 hours):</strong> heat loss exceeds metabolic heat production, which falls by about 20–30% under anaesthesia (no muscle tone or shivering, less work of breathing, no behavioural responses).</li><li><strong>Plateau:</strong> core temperature stabilises when vasoconstriction returns at a lower threshold (not with neuraxial block, which prevents it in the blocked area).</li><li>General anaesthesia <strong>widens the interthreshold range to about 2–4°C</strong> (the vasoconstriction threshold falls, the sweating threshold rises).</li><li><strong>Mechanisms of heat loss:</strong> <strong>radiation</strong> (the largest, about 40%), <strong>convection</strong> (~30%), <strong>evaporation</strong> (~15–20%, more with open body cavities), <strong>respiration</strong> (~10%: humidifying and warming inspired gas) and <strong>conduction</strong> (small, ~5%).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"consequences-of-hypothermia-core-36-c\">Consequences of hypothermia (core &lt; 36°C)</h2>\n<p>Increased surgical site infection, coagulopathy and blood loss, prolonged drug action (e.g. neuromuscular blockers), shivering and increased oxygen consumption, myocardial ischaemia, patient discomfort, longer recovery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prevention-nice-cg65\">Prevention (NICE CG65)</h2>\n<ul><li>Measure temperature before induction, every 30 minutes during anaesthesia, and in recovery. Do not start anaesthesia (unless urgent) if the core temperature is below 36°C.</li><li><strong>Forced-air warming</strong> for procedures over 30 minutes (or at higher risk); warm IV fluids and blood above 500 mL; warm irrigation fluids; theatre temperature at least 21°C while the patient is exposed.</li><li>Neonates and infants lose heat especially fast (large surface area to volume ratio, thin skin, little subcutaneous fat).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> the three phases; mechanisms of heat loss and their proportions; brown fat.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> planning temperature management for a long laparotomy in an elderly patient, or explaining shivering in recovery.</li></ul></div>","trauma-transfer":"<h2 id=\"primary-survey-abcde\">Primary survey (&lt;C&gt;ABCDE)</h2>\n<p><strong>Catastrophic haemorrhage</strong> control (tourniquets, pressure, haemostatic dressings) → <strong>Airway with cervical spine protection</strong> → <strong>Breathing</strong> (tension pneumothorax, open chest wound, flail, haemothorax) → <strong>Circulation</strong> (haemorrhage control, access including <strong>intraosseous</strong>, blood-based resuscitation, <strong>tranexamic acid within 3 hours</strong>, permissive hypotension in penetrating trauma without head injury) → <strong>Disability</strong> (GCS, pupils, glucose) → <strong>Exposure</strong> (avoid <strong>hypothermia</strong>, part of the lethal triad with acidosis and coagulopathy). Then the secondary survey (head-to-toe, history \"AMPLE\").</p>\n<p><strong>Imaging:</strong> whole-body CT for major trauma in adults; FAST ultrasound as an adjunct; plain films in the resuscitation room.</p>\n<h2 id=\"airway-in-trauma\">Airway in trauma</h2>\n<ul><li>RSI with <strong>manual in-line stabilisation</strong> (remove the front of the collar); video laryngoscopy; expect blood and vomit; plan for failed intubation.</li><li><strong>Indications for intubation</strong> after head injury include GCS ≤ 8, loss of protective reflexes, inadequate ventilation or oxygenation, seizures, significant facial injury, agitation needing sedation for imaging, and before transfer if deterioration is likely.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"head-injury-preventing-secondary-brain-injury\">Head injury: preventing secondary brain injury</h2>\n<p><strong>Glasgow Coma Scale</strong> (eyes 4, verbal 5, motor 6). Targets: <strong>SpO<sub>2</sub> ≥ 94% / PaO<sub>2</sub> &gt; 13 kPa</strong>, <strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub> 4.5–5.0 kPa</strong>, <strong>MAP ≥ 80 mmHg</strong> (or as locally advised to keep CPP ≥ 60), normoglycaemia, normothermia, head-up 30° with the neck neutral and ties loose, adequate sedation, treat seizures; osmotherapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline) for signs of raised ICP while arranging neurosurgery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transfer\">Transfer</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Indications and planning:</strong> the benefits must outweigh the risks. Stabilise before transfer (\"secure the airway before you move\").</li><li><strong>Personnel:</strong> a doctor with airway and critical care skills (at least a trained anaesthetist or intensivist for ventilated patients) plus an assistant.</li><li><strong>Equipment and monitoring:</strong> as in theatre, including <strong>waveform capnography</strong>, invasive BP for unstable patients, a transport ventilator, enough oxygen (calculate with a 50% reserve), infusion pumps with batteries, a drugs bag including controlled drugs (documented), and communication equipment.</li><li><strong>Documentation and communication:</strong> a transfer record, handover to the receiving team (SBAR), consent where possible, and informing relatives.</li><li><strong>Hazards:</strong> equipment failure, limited space and light, acceleration/deceleration effects on haemodynamics, vibration, noise, cold, and the agitated or aggressive patient (sedation, restraint within the law). Intra-hospital transfers (to CT, theatres, ICU) carry the same risks.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> GCS; head injury physiological targets; oxygen calculations; transfer monitoring standards.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> prepare and hand over a ventilated head-injured patient for transfer to the regional neurosurgical centre.</li></ul></div>","ultrasound-doppler":"<h2 id=\"ultrasound\">Ultrasound</h2>\n<ul><li>Sound above <strong>20 kHz</strong>; medical imaging uses about <strong>2–15 MHz</strong>. Generated and received by <strong>piezoelectric crystals</strong>, which vibrate when a voltage is applied and generate a voltage when struck by returning echoes.</li><li>Speed in soft tissue ≈ <strong>1540 m/s</strong>; the machine calculates depth from echo return time.</li><li><strong>Acoustic impedance</strong> (density × speed) differences at tissue interfaces cause reflection. Very large differences (tissue–air, tissue–bone) reflect almost everything (hence gel, and why bone and air cast shadows).</li><li><strong>Frequency trade-off:</strong> <strong>higher frequency → better resolution but less penetration</strong> (more attenuation). Use high-frequency linear probes (e.g. 6–15 MHz) for superficial nerves and vessels; low-frequency curvilinear probes (2–5 MHz) for deep structures.</li><li><strong>Echogenicity:</strong> hyperechoic (bright: fascia, bone surface, needles), hypoechoic, anechoic (black: fluid, vessels).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"artefacts\">Artefacts</h2>\n<p><strong>Acoustic shadowing</strong> (behind bone or calculi), <strong>posterior acoustic enhancement</strong> (behind fluid-filled structures, e.g. deep to arteries), <strong>reverberation</strong> (between parallel reflectors, e.g. a needle shaft: comet-tail), <strong>mirror image</strong>, <strong>anisotropy</strong> (a nerve or tendon changes brightness with probe angle), <strong>bayonet artefact</strong> (needle appears bent because of differing tissue speeds).</p>\n<h2 id=\"needle-guidance\">Needle guidance</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>In-plane:</strong> the whole needle shaft is visible; keep it aligned with the narrow beam. <strong>Out-of-plane:</strong> the needle appears as a dot; the tip can be missed.</li><li>Steep angles reduce needle visibility; echogenic needles and a shallow approach help. Always see the tip before injecting; hydrodissect with small volumes.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"doppler\">Doppler</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Doppler shift:</strong> the frequency of reflected sound changes when the reflector moves: <strong>Δf = 2 f<sub>0</sub> v cos θ / c</strong>. Velocity is estimated from the shift; accuracy depends on the <strong>angle of insonation</strong> (cos θ; errors grow above about 60°, and at 90° there is no shift).</li><li><strong>Colour Doppler</strong> shows direction and mean velocity (conventionally red towards and blue away from the probe: \"BART\"); <strong>pulsed-wave</strong> measures velocity at a chosen depth (limited by aliasing); <strong>continuous-wave</strong> measures high velocities without depth information.</li><li>Uses: vascular identification, echocardiography, <strong>oesophageal Doppler cardiac output</strong> (stroke distance × aortic cross-sectional area), transcranial Doppler, fetal monitoring.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"optical-fibres\">Optical fibres</h2>\n<p>Light travels along glass fibres by <strong>total internal reflection</strong> (a core with a higher refractive index than its cladding, with light striking the boundary beyond the critical angle). Used in fibreoptic bronchoscopes (coherent bundles transmit an image; incoherent bundles carry light) and some sensors.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> frequency versus resolution; speed of sound; artefacts; the Doppler equation and angle; total internal reflection.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> setting up the ultrasound for an axillary brachial plexus block and explaining what you see.</li></ul></div>","vaporisers":"<h2 id=\"variable-bypass-plenum-vaporiser-e-g-tec-5-and-7\">Variable-bypass (plenum) vaporiser (e.g. Tec 5 and 7)</h2>\n<ul><li>Fresh gas is split at the <strong>splitting valve</strong>: a small fraction passes through the <strong>vaporising chamber</strong>, where it becomes <strong>saturated</strong> with vapour (large surface area from wicks and baffles); the rest bypasses. They recombine at the outlet.</li><li>The dial controls the <strong>splitting ratio</strong>. Output ≈ (chamber flow / total flow) × SVP / (atmospheric − SVP) as a fraction, calibrated for a specific agent.</li><li><strong>Temperature compensation:</strong> as liquid cools with vaporisation, SVP falls. A <strong>bimetallic strip</strong> (or expanding bellows) at the bypass narrows the bypass as temperature falls, directing more gas through the chamber. A heat sink (copper, large metal mass) limits temperature change.</li><li><strong>Flow compensation:</strong> accurate over about <strong>0.5–15 L/min</strong>. Below that, gas may not pick up enough vapour; at very high flows, it may not be fully saturated.</li><li><strong>Pumping effect:</strong> back-pressure from IPPV or oxygen flush can compress gas in the chamber and push extra vapour into the bypass, raising output. Reduced by a long inlet tube, check valves and baffles.</li><li><strong>Altitude:</strong> the dial gives about the same partial pressure (and so the same effect), although the percentage delivered is higher.</li><li>Filling systems are <strong>agent-specific</strong> (keyed fillers); a <strong>interlock</strong> prevents two vaporisers being on at once; vaporisers are sited outside the breathing system (VOC) on the back bar.</li><li>Filling with the wrong agent: a higher-SVP agent in a lower-SVP vaporiser (e.g. sevoflurane in an isoflurane vaporiser → higher output than dialled; in practice keyed fillers prevent this).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"desflurane-tec-6-type\">Desflurane (Tec 6-type)</h2>\n<p>Desflurane's SVP is near atmospheric at room temperature, so it is <strong>heated to 39°C</strong> in a sump, raising its pressure to about <strong>2 atmospheres (~200 kPa)</strong>. Vapour is injected into the fresh gas flow through a <strong>pressure-regulating valve</strong> that matches a differential pressure transducer, controlled by the dial. It needs electrical power and warm-up, and alarms if it fails. At altitude, the percentage output stays the same, so the <strong>partial pressure falls</strong> — the dial must be turned up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-types\">Other types</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Draw-over vaporisers</strong> (e.g. OMV, EMO) have <strong>low internal resistance</strong> so the patient's breathing (or a self-inflating bag) draws air through them; used in field and resource-limited settings. They sit within the breathing system (VIC).</li><li><strong>Direct injection / electronic vaporisers</strong> (e.g. Aladin cassette, and some modern workstations) inject liquid agent under computer control.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> plenum vaporiser principles; temperature and flow compensation; pumping effect; desflurane vaporiser; altitude effects.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> the vaporiser is empty and you cannot refill it during a case: what do you do, and why is it dangerous?</li></ul></div>","vapours-humidity":"<h2 id=\"vapours\">Vapours</h2>\n<ul><li>A <strong>vapour</strong> is the gaseous phase of a substance below its critical temperature.</li><li><strong>Saturated vapour pressure (SVP):</strong> the partial pressure of vapour in equilibrium with its liquid in a closed container. It depends <strong>only on temperature</strong> (and the substance), not on ambient pressure.</li><li><strong>Boiling point:</strong> the temperature at which SVP equals ambient pressure. So at altitude liquids boil at lower temperatures, and desflurane (boiling point 22.8°C) boils in a warm room.</li><li><strong>Maximum concentration</strong> of a vapour at 1 atmosphere = SVP / atmospheric pressure (e.g. isoflurane 32/101 ≈ 32%): hence the need for vaporisers that dilute to a safe output.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"latent-heat\">Latent heat</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Specific latent heat of vaporisation:</strong> the heat needed to convert 1 kg of liquid to vapour <strong>without a change in temperature</strong>. It falls as temperature rises (zero at the critical temperature).</li><li>Vaporisation takes heat from the remaining liquid, cooling it and lowering its SVP — why vaporisers need a heat sink (copper, a large mass) and <strong>temperature compensation</strong>, and why an N<sub>2</sub>O cylinder in heavy use gets cold (and ice forms on the outside).</li><li><strong>Specific heat capacity:</strong> heat to raise 1 kg by 1 K (water ~4.2 kJ/kg/K; blood similar). Warming 1 L of fluid from room temperature to body temperature takes about 70 kJ — why cold fluids cool patients.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"humidity\">Humidity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Absolute humidity:</strong> mass of water vapour per unit volume of gas (g/m<sup>3</sup>). Air saturated at 37°C contains about <strong>44 g/m<sup>3</sup></strong> (SVP 6.3 kPa).</li><li><strong>Relative humidity:</strong> actual mass / mass needed to saturate at that temperature (%). Warming gas lowers its relative humidity without changing absolute humidity; cooling it produces condensation (dew point).</li><li><strong>Measurement:</strong> hair hygrometer (hair lengthens with humidity), wet and dry bulb hygrometer (evaporative cooling), Regnault's hygrometer (dew point), electrical humidity sensors.</li><li>The <strong>isothermic saturation boundary</strong> (where gas reaches 37°C, 100% RH) normally lies just below the carina. Tracheal intubation bypasses the nose and upper airway; dry gas causes mucosal damage, thick secretions, tube blockage and heat loss.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"humidifiers\">Humidifiers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Heat and moisture exchanger (HME)</strong></td><td>Passive; hygroscopic material retains exhaled heat and water; about 60–70% efficiency; adds dead space and resistance; can be combined with a filter (HMEF); can block with secretions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hot water bath humidifier</strong></td><td>Active; can achieve full saturation at 37°C; risk of scalding, infection, water in the tubing (heated wires reduce condensation)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nebulisers</strong> (gas-driven, ultrasonic)</td><td>Produce droplets that can deliver water or drugs; risk of over-humidification and infection</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> SVP and boiling point; latent heat and cooling; absolute versus relative humidity; HME principles.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> choosing humidification for a patient ventilated on ICU with thick secretions.</li></ul></div>","ventilators":"<h2 id=\"classification\">Classification</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Power source:</strong> pneumatic, electric or both.</li><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> <strong>flow generators</strong> deliver a set flow regardless of the patient's compliance (so tidal volume is preserved when compliance falls, but pressure rises); <strong>pressure generators</strong> deliver a set pressure (so tidal volume falls when compliance falls or resistance rises).</li><li><strong>Cycling</strong> (inspiration to expiration): <strong>time-cycled</strong> (most), volume-, pressure- or flow-cycled.</li><li><strong>Bag-in-bottle ventilators</strong> (e.g. on many anaesthetic machines): driving gas compresses a bellows containing breathing-system gas; ascending bellows (rise on expiration) are safer because a disconnection is obvious.</li><li><strong>Minute volume dividers</strong> (e.g. the Manley) use the fresh gas flow itself to power ventilation.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"modes\">Modes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Set</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Volume control</strong></td><td>Tidal volume, rate, I:E</td><td>Guaranteed VT; pressure varies with compliance — watch peak and plateau pressures</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pressure control</strong></td><td>Inspiratory pressure, rate, I:E</td><td>Decelerating flow; VT varies with compliance (watch minute ventilation)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pressure-regulated volume control</strong></td><td>Target VT</td><td>Adjusts pressure breath by breath</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SIMV</strong></td><td>Mandatory breaths synchronised with spontaneous effort</td><td>Weaning</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pressure support (PSV)</strong></td><td>Pressure assist triggered by the patient, flow-cycled</td><td>Spontaneous mode for weaning or under LMA</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CPAP / PEEP</strong></td><td>End-expiratory pressure</td><td>Increases FRC and oxygenation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"other-ventilators\">Other ventilators</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Transport ventilators:</strong> compact, gas-driven, with limited modes; know their oxygen consumption (driving gas) when calculating cylinder needs.</li><li><strong>High-frequency jet ventilation:</strong> a jet via a narrow catheter at high rates entrains gas (Venturi); used in laryngeal and tracheal surgery. Risk of <strong>barotrauma</strong> if exhalation is obstructed.</li><li><strong>High-frequency oscillatory ventilation:</strong> very small tidal volumes at 3–15 Hz with active expiration.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"disconnection-and-alarms\">Disconnection and alarms</h2>\n<p><strong>Low pressure / low volume alarms</strong> (the pressure fails to reach a threshold within a set time), <strong>capnography</strong> (the most reliable disconnection monitor), high pressure alarms, apnoea alarms and, on ICU ventilators, minute volume alarms.</p>\n<div class=\"callout-box exam\"><p class=\"callout-title\">In the exam</p><ul><li><strong>AKT:</strong> flow versus pressure generators; the effect of reduced compliance in each mode; jet ventilation.</li><li><strong>CASE:</strong> tidal volumes fall suddenly in pressure control during laparoscopy: what has happened and what will you do?</li></ul></div>"};
